\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
Category Theory (CT) can be thought of as a general theory of mathematical structures and their relations. Recently, there has been a surging interest in studying Machine Learning (ML) 
%subfields 
from a CT perspective, to leverage the unifying power of the latter (thus unifying apparently unrelated ML subfields), and to obtain more profound theoretical results.


For example, \citet{stehlik2012category} and \citet{song2024describe} survey how to frame statistical learning models into a CT framework. \citet{shiebler2021categorytheorymachinelearning} do the same for gradient-based methods, probabilistic methods, and invariant and equivariant learning. Furthermore, \citet{cornish2025stochasticneuralnetworksymmetrisation} expands on the latter by studying the problem of symmetrizing a neural network along a group homomorphism. That is, given two groups $H$ and $G$, they study a procedure that converts $H$-equivariant neural networks to $G$-equivariant ones. More in general, CT approaches to probability and statistics (which play a pivotal role in modern-day ML) are active fields of study, see e.g. \citet{fritz2025empiricalmeasuresstronglaws,bohinen2025categoricalalgebraconditionalprobability,ensarguet2024categoricalprobabilityspacesergodic,Ackerman_2024,Matache_2022}, and are widely discussed in the \href{https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/category-theoretic+approaches+to+probability+theory}{nLab} and in the \href{https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2020/06/statistics_for_category_theori.html}{n-Category Café}.

Conformal Prediction (CP, introduced formally in Section \ref{full-cp}) is a popular ML prediction tool. Suppose we are given an $n$-sized exchangeable training data $y^n=(y_1,\ldots,y_n)$ where each observation $y_i$ belongs to the state space $Y$, a confidence level $\alpha \in [0,1]$, and a non-conformity score $\psi$ (i.e. a function that measures ``how different'' a new observation is with respect to the ones in the training set). CP, then, returns a prediction set, called the Conformal Prediction Region (CPR), that contains the ``true value'' $y_{n+1}$ with high probability $1-\alpha$ \citep{vovk2005algorithmic,shafer2008tutorial,vovk-trans,angelopoulos2024theoreticalfoundationsconformalprediction}.\footnote{For extensions, and more modern references, see e.g. \citep{caprio-conformalized,alireza,gibbs2023conformal,barber2023conformal,papadopoulos2008inductive}.} In \citet[Section 5.1]{cp-ip}, the authors show how CP can be seen as a correspondence (i.e. a set-valued function), thus highlighting a connection between ML and functional analysis (FA, \cite{aliprantis}). Other such connections have been explored in the literature, see e.g. \citet{Korolev}. 
%In this paper, we build on this functional-analytic intuition to show that 

This paper builds on the CP-as-a-correspondence intuition to develop a category-theoretic approach to CP,
%\footnote{As a result, it hints at a bridge between the CT paths to ML, probability, and statistics that we discussed earlier, and that to functional analysis \citep{GhezLimaRoberts1985,Castillo2010,Kock1983}.} 
which allows us to expand on how we understand the structural underpinnings of distribution-free uncertainty quantification.\footnote{Throughout the paper, we do not use the form ``we/our'' as a form of pluralis majestatis, but rather to comply to the convention of scientific papers of writing in plural form.} We cast CP as a morphism (embedded in a commuting diagram) of two newly-defined categories, and we are also able to frame a CPR as a functor image. 

%allows us to discover some important intrinsic properties of Conformal Prediction.

\textbf{Contributions.} This category-theoretic approach grants us three joys, which are briefly summarized next.
%The joys of categorical Conformal Prediction that we present are the following.

\begin{itemize}
    \item CP is not only an uncertainty representation, but also an uncertainty {\em quantification} tool: The scholars who improperly referred to CP as an Uncertainty Quantification (UQ) methodology, were right all along.
    \item CP bridges Bayesian, frequentist, and imprecise probabilistic approaches to predictive statistical reasoning, thus formalizing an intuition first put forth by \citet{martin2022valid}.
    %\item Framing a CPR as a functor image is important for AI privacy \citep{fang2025trustworthyaisafetybias}, as privacy noise introduced locally does not break coverage. It also allows us to conclude that, in a federated learning setting \citep{LIU2024128019}, each agent only needs to share a summary object (the credal set $\mathcal{M}$ that we introduce in the following paragraphs) rather than raw data. 
    \item Seeing the Conformal Prediction Region as the image of a covariant functor directly benefits AI privacy \citep{fang2025trustworthyaisafetybias}: (i) Any locally added privacy noise leaves coverage guarantees intact, and (ii) In federated learning \citep{LIU2024128019}, each agent need only share a summary object (the credal set $\mathcal{M}$ that we introduce in the following paragraphs) instead of raw data.
\end{itemize}

As we can see, our Categorical Conformal Prediction approach exhibits many boons, and little to no banes (minimal assumptions on $Y$, choice of $\psi$, and $\alpha$).







