Conformal Prediction is associated with a special case of upper probability $\overline{P}$,  called a {\em possibility function} \citep{cuzzolin2020geometry}. The latter is an upper probability for which there exists a function $\pi:Y\rightarrow [0,1]$ such that 
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\sup_{y\in\mathbb{Y}}\pi(y)=1$;
    \item $\overline{P}(A)=\sup_{y\in A} \pi(y)$, $A\in \Sigma_Y$.
\end{itemize}

\citet{cella2022validity} show that, under the so-called {\em consonance assumption}, i.e. if $\sup_{y_{n+1} \in Y} \pi(y_{n+1},y^n)=1$, then conformal transducer $\pi$ induces a possibility function $\overline{\Pi}(A)\coloneqq \sup_{y_{n+1} \in A} \pi(y_{n+1},y^n)$, for all $A\in \Sigma_Y$, and in turn a credal set $\mathcal{M}(\overline{\Pi}) \coloneqq \{P : P(A) \leq \overline{\Pi}(A), \forall A\in \Sigma_Y\}$. Such a credal set can be seen as the image of the correspondence $\CRED:Y^n \times \F \rightrightarrows \Prob$, where $\F$ is the collection of functions in \eqref{funct-coll}, and $(y^n,\psi) \mapsto \CRED(y^n,\psi) \coloneqq $

\begin{align}\label{cred-corresp}
    \Bigg\{P \in \Prob : P(A) \leq \underbrace{\sup_{y_{n+1}\in A} \left(\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} \mathbbm{1}\left[\psi \left(y^{n+1}_{-i},y_i \right)  \geq \psi \left(y^{n+1}_{-(n+1)},y_{n+1} \right)\right]\right)}_{\equiv \sup_{y_{n+1} \in A} \pi(y_{n+1},y^n) \eqqcolon \overline{\Pi}(A)} \text{, } \forall A \in \Sigma_Y\Bigg\}.
\end{align}

The consonance assumption is needed to ensure that $\CRED(y^n,\psi)\in\Ccal$. In the remainder of the paper, we sometimes write $\pi(y,y^n,\psi)\equiv \pi_\psi(y,y^n)$ for notational convenience (we drop the $n+1$ subscript for $y_{n+1}$), and to explicitly acknowledge that the conformal transducer $\pi$ depends on the choice of the non-conformity measure $\psi$. 

We also consider a slight modification of $\CRED$, that will considerably simplify our proving endeavors in Sections \ref{main}, \ref{main2}, and \ref{unifying}; We denote it by $\CRED_\text{cl}(y^n,\psi)\coloneqq \{P \in \Delta_Y: P(A) \leq \overline{\Pi}(A) \text{, } \forall A \in \Sigma_Y \text{ closed}\}$. As we can see, $\CRED_\text{cl}(y^n,\psi) \supseteq \mathcal{M}(\overline{\Pi})$, because it imposes less constraints on the $P$'s: They only have to be setwise dominated on closed sets. The upper probability $\overline{\Pi}$ and its conjugate lower probability $\underline{\Pi}$ (the only characteristics of the credal sets that we use) stay the same for both $\CRED$ and $\CRED_\text{cl}$: This justifies our simplifying choice. To avoid notation complications, in the remainder of this work we do not distinguish between $\CRED(y^n,\psi)$ and $\CRED_\text{cl}(y^n,\psi)$.




