Theorem \ref{conf-pred-diagr} clarifies the nature of Conformal Prediction: Although often billed as an \emph{uncertainty quantification} tool, CP furnishes a \emph{representation} of predictive
uncertainty, but not a \emph{quantification} in the cardinal sense.
The distinction is subtle yet essential: The diameter
$\text{diam}(\mathscr R)$ of a Conformal Prediction Region (CPR)
$\mathscr R\subseteq Y$ is only defined up to strictly \emph{order-preserving}
(monotone) transformations.\footnote{Having assumed a metric $d_Y$ on $Y$, $\text{diam}(A)=\sup_{y_1,y_2 \in A} d_Y(y_1,y_2)$, $A\subseteq Y$. A similar argument holds if we consider the volume in place of the diameter of the CPR.}  Therefore,
\[
  \text{diam}\bigl(\mathscr R_1\bigr)=
  3\,\text{diam}\bigl(\mathscr R_2\bigr)
  \;\;\;\centernot\Longrightarrow\;\;\;
  \text{``$\mathscr R_1$ is three times more uncertain than $\mathscr R_2$''.}
\]
Conformal regions support \emph{ordinal} comparisons
(``larger'' versus ``smaller'') but not the \emph{cardinal} arithmetic needed
for expected-length-type calibration that variance-based approaches
provide.  Interpreting region width as a direct proxy for probability
mass is therefore as misleading as treating a ranking as if it were a
probability distribution.
%in fact provides an \emph{uncertainty representation} via the Conformal Prediction Region (CPR). For instance, if two CPRs $\mathscr R_1$ and $\mathscr R_2$ satisfy $\text{diam}(\mathscr R_1)=3\,\text{diam}(\mathscr R_2)$, where $\text{diam}(\cdot)$ denotes the diameter operator, we can only rank their uncertainties \emph{ordinally}. We cannot claim a three-fold increase in uncertainty for $\mathscr R_1$. 

The commuting diagram, however, reveals a hidden layer: Computing a CP region is equivalent to (i) forming the credal set $\mathcal{M}(\overline{\Pi})$ and (ii) extracting its IHDR.
Because $\mathcal{M}(\overline{\Pi})$ lives in the space of probability measures, existing metrics---e.g. those in \citet{abellan,Hullermeier2021Aleatoric,alireza2,hofman2024quantifyingaleatoricepistemicuncertainty}---endow it with a \emph{cardinal} scale that is able to quantify (different types of) uncertainty, in particular, reducible (epistemic) and irreducible (aleatoric) \citep{alireza,javanmardi2025optimalconformalpredictionepistemic,cabezas2025epistemicuncertaintyconformalscores,10.1145/3649329.3663512,hanselle2025conformal}. This will be ``marginal uncertainty'',\footnote{That is, averaged over the possible realization $y^n$.} thus reflecting the marginal guarantee of the Conformal Prediction Region (see equation \eqref{eq_imp6}). We conjecture that the analyst-controlled choices---the significance level $\alpha$, which fixes the tolerated error, and the non-conformity score $\psi\in{\mathscr F}$, which embodies modeling assumptions---are primarily associated with \emph{epistemic} uncertainty. On the other hand, \emph{aleatoric} uncertainty stems from the intrinsic variability of the exchangeable process that we study, encoded in $\mathfrak{P}$.



Thus, Conformal Prediction’s capacity for predictive uncertainty quantification is not an add-on, but a built-in consequence of the categorical factorization resulting from Theorem \ref{conf-pred-diagr}’s commuting diagram. The scholars who have been calling Conformal Prediction a UQ method were right all along (under the stated assumptions), and Theorem \ref{conf-pred-diagr} explains why. This expands the work initiated by \citet{cella2022validity,cella2023possibility,cp-ip}, who found UQ capabilities of CP methods through an imprecise probabilistic route.


