\subsection{Full Conformal Prediction}\label{conf-pred}

As pointed out in \citet[Section 5.1]{cp-ip}, for any given $n\in\mathbb{N}$, we can write (Full) Conformal Prediction as a correspondence
$$\kappa: [0,1] \times Y^n \times \mathscr{F} \rightrightarrows Y,$$


%Unless otherwise stated, throughout the paper we consider a slight modification to \eqref{kappa-definition}, i.e. ``$\geq \alpha$'' in place of ``$>\alpha$''. This does not change the interpretation of the Conformal Prediction procedure, and it allows us to verify that, under minimal assumptions, $\kappa$ is upper hemicontinuous. 


%\footnote{Notice also that, with minor tweaks, we can revert to the ``$>\alpha$'' definition, and our results will remain valid for a lower hemicontinuous $\kappa$.}
%due to their desirable properties, particularly when dealing with optimization problems and equilibrium analysis.



In the Conformal Prediction literature, $\kappa(\alpha,y^n,\psi)$ is often denoted as $\mathscr{R}_\alpha^\psi(y^n)$, and referred to as the ($\alpha$-level) Conformal Prediction Region. Also, map $y_{n+1} \mapsto \pi(y_{n+1},y^n)$ is called {\em conformal transducer}. We now ask ourselves under which conditions is the Full Conformal Prediction correspondence upper hemicontinuous.


\begin{theorem}[Upper Hemicontinuous Conformal Prediction]\label{conf-pred-uhc}
If $Y$ is a compact Hausdorff topological space and $\mathscr{F} \subseteq C(Y^{n+1}, \mathbb{R})$ is endowed with the compact-open topology, then the (Full) Conformal Prediction correspondence $\kappa$ is upper hemicontinuous.
\end{theorem}

Here $C(Y^{n+1}, \mathbb{R})$ is the set of jointly continuous functions from \( Y^{n+1} \) to \( \mathbb{R} \). 


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{conf-pred-uhc}]
The map
\[
  \operatorname{ev}:\mathscr{F}\times Y^{n+1}\rightarrow\mathbb{R},\qquad
  (\psi,z)\longmapsto\psi(z),
\]
is continuous (because $Y^{n+1}$ is compact Hausdorff). This is immediate from the definition of the compact-open topology, which is the coarsest topology making all evaluations continuous.

We now define a function
\[
  p:Y\times Y^{n}\times\mathscr{F}\rightarrow[0,1]
\]
that is upper semicontinuous (u.s.c.). For each $i=1,\dots,n+1$ define the continuous map
\[
  g_i(y^{n+1},\psi)
  =\psi\bigl(y^{n+1}_{-i},y_i\bigr)
   -\psi\bigl(y^{n+1}_{-(n+1)},y_{n+1}\bigr)
\]
Its super-level set $\{g_i\ge0\}$ is closed, hence the indicator
$h_i\coloneqq\mathbbm{1}[\,\{g_i\ge0\}]$ is u.s.c..  
Because a finite sum and a positive scalar multiple of u.s.c. functions is again u.s.c.,
\[
  p(y,y^n,\psi)=\frac1{n+1}\sum_{i=1}^{n+1}h_i
\]
is u.s.c.. Fix now $(\alpha,y^{n},\psi)$. By our previous argument, we have that $y_{n+1}\mapsto p(y_{n+1},y^{n},\psi)$ is u.s.c.. Hence the upper-contour set
\[
  \kappa(\alpha,y^{n},\psi)
  =\bigl\{y_{n+1}:\,p(y_{n+1},y^{n},\psi)\ge\alpha\bigr\}
\]
is closed in $Y$.  Thus $\kappa$ has closed values. Define
\[
  \Phi:[0,1]\times Y^{n}\times\mathscr{F}\times Y\rightarrow\mathbb{R},\qquad
  \Phi(\alpha,y^{n},\psi,y_{n+1})
  =p(y_{n+1},y^{n},\psi)-\alpha.
\]
Because $p$ is u.s.c. and $\alpha\mapsto-\alpha$ is continuous,
$\Phi$ is u.s.c..  Its super-level set
\[
  \operatorname{Gr}\kappa
  =\bigl\{(\alpha,y^{n},\psi,y_{n+1}):\Phi(\alpha,y^{n},\psi,y_{n+1})\ge0\bigr\}
\]
is therefore closed, i.e.\ $\kappa$ has a closed graph. Upper hemicontinuity, then, follows from the Closed Graph Theorem \citep[Theorem 17.11]{aliprantis}.
\end{proof}


