\section{Further Properties of our Categories}\label{further-props}

In this section, we ask ourselves whether we can further characterize the categories that we introduced earlier in Sections \ref{main} and \ref{main2}. Throughout the section, if restrictions have to be imposed on the objects or morphisms of a category, we will report them as superscript and subscripts of the ensuing subcategory, respectively.


\subsection{Monoidal Categories}

The first, natural question is whether they are monoidal.

Alas, $\mathbf{UHCont}$ is not, as upper hemicontinuity is not preserved under product in general unless the correspondences are compact-valued. We can define a slight strengthening of $\mathbf{UHCont}$, though, which is monoidal.  

\begin{definition}[Structure \textbf{UHCont}$_\text{c}$]\label{structure-uhcont-comp}
Define a structure $\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}$ as follows,
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Objects:} Topological spaces $(X, \tau_X)$.
  \item \textbf{Morphisms:} {\em Compact-valued}, upper hemicontinuous correspondences $\Phi : X \rightrightarrows Y$.
%  \item \textbf{Composition:} Given $\Phi : X \rightrightarrows Y$ and $\Psi : Y \rightrightarrows Z$, define the composition $\Psi \circ \Phi : X \rightrightarrows Z$ by:
%  \[
%  (\Psi \circ \Phi)(x) \coloneqq \bigcup_{y \in \Phi(x)} \Psi(y).
%  \]
%  \item \textbf{Identities:} For each object $X$, define $\mathrm{id}_X : X \rightrightarrows X$ by $\mathrm{id}_X(x) \coloneqq \{x\}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[$\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}$ is a Monoidal Category]\label{monoidal1}
The structure \(\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}\) is a monoidal category under the Cartesian product of topological spaces.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We verify the monoidal category structure.

\textbf{(1) Tensor Product of Objects and Morphisms.} For objects \(X, Y\), define \(X \otimes Y \coloneqq X \times Y\), the Cartesian product with the product topology.

Given morphisms \(\Phi_1: X \rightrightarrows X^\prime\) and \(\Phi_2: Y \rightrightarrows Y^\prime\), define their tensor product as
\[
(\Phi_1 \otimes \Phi_2)(x,y) \coloneqq \Phi_1(x) \times \Phi_2(y).
\]

Since compact sets are preserved under finite products, \(\Phi_1(x) \times \Phi_2(y)\) is compact for all \((x,y) \in X \times Y\). Also, the product of upper hemicontinuous correspondences with compact values is upper hemicontinuous with compact values \citep[Theorem 17.28]{aliprantis}. Thus, \(\Phi_1 \otimes \Phi_2\) is a morphism in \(\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}\).

\textbf{(2) Unit Object.} Let the unit object be the singleton space \(I \coloneqq \{*\}\), with the trivial topology. For any object \(X\), the canonical identifications
\[
\lambda_X: I \times X \rightarrow X, \quad \rho_X: X \times I \rightarrow X
\]
are homeomorphisms. They define morphisms in \(\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}\) via single-valued, continuous correspondences (e.g., \( (x, *) \mapsto x \)) with compact singleton values.

\textbf{(3) Associativity.} Define the associator as the canonical homeomorphism,
\[
\alpha_{X,Y,Z} : (X \times Y) \times Z \rightarrow X \times (Y \times Z).
\]
Its graph defines a single-valued, continuous correspondence with compact-valued singleton fibers. Hence, it is a morphism in the category.

\textbf{(4) Coherence Conditions.} The coherence diagrams (pentagon and triangle) commute strictly, as they correspond to natural identifications of Cartesian products in the category of topological spaces. Thus, the associators and unitors satisfy the coherence conditions.
\end{proof}

Given our previous proofs, it is easy to see that, under conditions \ref{H1}-\ref{H5}, the Full Conformal Prediction correspondence $\kappa$ is a morphism of $\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}$. This is because $Y$ is compact Hausdorff, and the graph of $\kappa$ is closed by the Proof of Theorem \ref{conf-pred-diagr}. By the Closed Graph Theorem (Lemma \ref{lem:CG}), this means that $\kappa$ is upper hemicontinuous and closed-valued; But $Y$ is compact Hausdorff, which implies that $\kappa$ is compact-valued.
%it is easy to see that, for the Full Conformal Prediction correspondence $\kappa$ to be a morphism of category $\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}$, we need to require that $Y$ is a compact Hausdorff space, and that $\mathscr{F}\subseteq C(Y^{n+1},\mathbb{R})$ is endowed with the compact-open topology.
%, and that its elements are coercive in the last variable. These conditions are slightly stronger than those in Corollary \ref{cor-imp1}, but they ensure that Full CP is a morphism of a monoidal category.

As perhaps expected from the proof of Theorem \ref{monoidal1}, the category $\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$ is monoidal.

\begin{theorem}[$\mathbf{WMeas}_{\text{uc}}$ is a Monoidal Category]
The structure \(\mathbf{WMeas}_{\text{uc}}\) forms a monoidal category under the Cartesian product.
\end{theorem}

% and take the unit object to be $(\{\ast\},\{\emptyset,\{\ast\}\})$.
% Then $(\mathbf{WMeas}_{\mathrm{uc}},\otimes,\{\ast\})$ is a
% \emph{(strict) symmetric monoidal category}.
% \end{theorem}

\begin{proof}

Let the unit object be the singleton space \(I \coloneqq \{*\}\), with the trivial topology, and
\[
(X,\Sigma_X)\,\otimes\,(X',\Sigma_{X'})
    \;\coloneqq\;(X\times X',\,\Sigma_X\!\otimes\!\Sigma_{X'}),
\qquad
(F\otimes G)(x,x')\;\coloneqq\;F(x)\times G(x').
\]

\textbf{1. Tensor is a bifunctor.}
For morphisms $F:X\!\Rightarrow\!Y$ and $G:X'\!\Rightarrow\!Y'$ the map
$(F\otimes G)(x,x')=F(x)\times G(x')$ is:

\emph{Uniformly compact.}  If $K_F\subset Y$ and $K_G\subset Y'$
witness compactness, $K_F\times K_G$ is compact by Tychonoff, and
$(F\otimes G)(x,x')\subset K_F\times K_G$ for all $(x,x')$.

\emph{Weakly measurable.}  Let
$O=\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb N}(B_k\times C_k)$ be an open subset of
$Y\times Y'$ written as a countable union of basic rectangles
(recall that $Y,Y'$ are second-countable).
Then,
\[
  (F\otimes G)^{-1}(O)
   \;=\;
   \bigcup_{k\in\mathbb N}
     \bigl(\{x:F(x)\cap B_k\!\neq\emptyset\}
            \times
            \{x':G(x')\cap C_k\!\neq\emptyset\}\bigr),
\]
a countable union of measurable rectangles, hence measurable in
$\Sigma_X\!\otimes\!\Sigma_{X'}$.

Functoriality is immediate:
\((G_1\!\circ\!F_1)\otimes(G_2\!\circ\!F_2)
  =(G_1\otimes G_2)\!\circ\!(F_1\otimes F_2)\) because product
distributes over union, and $1_X\otimes1_{X'}=1_{X\times X'}$.

\textbf{2. Associativity, unit, symmetry.}
Because $\otimes$ is the cartesian product on objects and the
point-wise cartesian product on sets, we have equalities (not merely
isomorphisms),
\[
  (X\otimes Y)\otimes Z = X\otimes(Y\otimes Z),
  \qquad
  X\otimes\{\ast\}=X=\{\ast\}\otimes X.
\]
The associator and unitors are therefore identities, so the monoidal
structure is \emph{strict}.  The symmetry is the flip map
$\sigma_{X,Y}:(x,y)\mapsto(y,x)$; naturality and the hexagon condition
hold because the cartesian product is symmetric in the category \textbf{Set}, and every
construction is inherited verbatim.

\textbf{3. Coherence.}  Since all coherence morphisms are identities,
MacLane’s pentagon and triangle commute trivially.

Hence
\((\mathbf{WMeas}_{\mathrm{uc}},\otimes,\{\ast\})\) is a strict
symmetric monoidal category.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Functors Between Categories}


Another question that we ask ourselves is whether there is a canonical functor between $\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$ and $\mathbf{UHCont}$ (or vice versa). The answer, as the attentive reader may expect, is no. The reason is that there exists a non-functorial gap between measurability and continuity, two different concepts that also motivated us to consider categories $\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$ and $\mathbf{UHCont}$ to begin with. 

Functors do exist if we focus on restrictions of $\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$ and $\mathbf{UHCont}$. In particular, call
%it is easy to see that, if we consider the category  
$\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc,uhc}\subset \mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$ the category whose objects are measurable Polish spaces with the Borel $\sigma$-algebra, and whose morphisms are uniformly compact-valued, weakly measurable, {\em and upper hemicontinuous} correspondences. Then, the following holds.


\begin{theorem}[Faithful Functor between $\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc,uhc}$ and $\mathbf{UHCont}$]\label{thm-funct1}
There exists a faithful functor
\[
\mathcal{F} : \mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc,uhc} \rightarrow \mathbf{UHCont}
\]
defined by
\begin{itemize}
  \item \(\mathcal{F}(X, \tau_X, \mathcal{B}(\tau_X)) \coloneqq (X, \tau_X)\),
  \item \(\mathcal{F}(\Phi) \coloneqq \Phi\) (viewed as a topological correspondence).
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We verify that \(\mathcal{F}\) is a functor.

{\bf (1) Identity.} The identity correspondence \(\mathrm{id}_X(x) = \{x\}\) is trivially compact-valued, upper hemicontinuous, and weakly measurable. Thus, \(\mathcal{F}(\mathrm{id}_X) = \mathrm{id}_X\) is a valid morphism in \(\mathbf{UHCont}\).

{\bf (2) Composition.} Let \(\Phi : X \rightrightarrows Y\) and \(\Psi : Y \rightrightarrows Z\) be morphisms in \(\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc,uhc}\). Then,
\begin{itemize}
  \item Both are uniformly compact-valued and upper hemicontinuous;
  \item Their composition \(\Psi \circ \Phi\) is also uniformly compact-valued \citep[Page 558 and (a consequence of) Example 17.24]{aliprantis} and upper hemicontinuous \citep[Theorem 17.23]{aliprantis};
  \item Weak measurability is preserved under composition (by the Proof of Theorem \ref{meas-corr-cat}).
\end{itemize}
So \(\Psi \circ \Phi\) is a morphism in \(\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc,uhc}\), and \(\mathcal{F}(\Psi \circ \Phi) = \mathcal{F}(\Psi) \circ \mathcal{F}(\Phi)\).

{\bf(3) Faithfulness.} If \(\Phi \neq \Psi\) as correspondences in \(\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc,uhc}\), then \(\mathcal{F}(\Phi) \neq \mathcal{F}(\Psi)\) in \(\mathbf{UHCont}\). So \(\mathcal{F}\) is injective on morphisms. Therefore, \(\mathcal{F}\) is a well-defined faithful functor.
\end{proof}


Functor \(\mathcal{F}\) in Theorem \ref{thm-funct1} ``forgets'' the measurable structure but preserves the topological one. The condition of weak measurability ensures that the image \(\Phi\) remains a valid morphism in both categories. 

It is also worth mentioning that, in general, $\mathcal{F}$ does not have a limit in $\mathbf{UHCont}$ \citep[Definition 3.1.5]{perrone2024starting}. This is because the representability conditions fail: Universal lifting of upper hemicontinuous cones is obstructed by the loss of measurability information via $\mathcal{F}$. This is a consequence of the incompatibility between continuity-based and measurability-based structures, exactly the same structural gap that made defining functor $\mathcal{F}$ nontrivial in the first place.


Let now $\mathbf{UHCont}_\text{c}^\text{Pa} \subset \mathbf{UHCont}$ be the category whose objects are {\em Polish} topological spaces, and whose morphisms are upper hemicontinuous, 
%(with respect to the Polish topologies), 
{uniformly compact-valued} correspondences having {\em analytic graphs}. Then, the following holds.

\begin{theorem}[Faithful Functor between $\mathbf{UHCont}_{\mathrm{c}}^{\mathrm{Pa}}$ and $\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$]\label{thm-funct2}
There exists a faithful functor
\[
\mathcal{G} : \mathbf{UHCont}_{\mathrm{c}}^{\mathrm{Pa}} \rightarrow \mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}
\]
defined by
\begin{itemize}
  \item \(\mathcal{G}(X, \tau_X) \coloneqq (X, \tau_X, \mathcal{B}(\tau_X))\),
  \item \(\mathcal{G}(\Phi) \coloneqq \Phi\), viewing the correspondence as a measurable one.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
First, notice that if \(X\) is a Polish space with topology \(\tau_X\), then \((X, \tau_X, \mathcal{B}(\tau_X))\) is a measurable Polish space, a valid object in \(\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}\). 

Also, let \(\Phi: X \rightrightarrows Y\) be a morphism in \(\mathbf{UHCont}_{\mathrm{c}}^{\mathrm{Pa}}\), i.e. uniformly compact-valued, upper hemicontinuous, and with analytic graph. Then, since \(\Phi\) is compact-valued, it follows 
%(see Castaing-Valadier, or Srivastava) 
that \(\Phi\) is weakly measurable \citep[Theorem 18.10]{aliprantis}. Thus \(\Phi\) is a valid morphism in \(\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}\).
 % \[
%  \forall \text{ open } O \subseteq Y,\quad \Phi^\ell(O) = \{ x \in X : \Phi(x) \cap O \neq \emptyset \} \in \mathcal{B}(X).
 % \]

We now verify the functorial properties.

\textbf{(1) Identity.}  The identity correspondence \(x \mapsto \{x\}\) has analytic graph, is trivially upper hemicontinuous and (uniformly) compact-valued, hence maps to the identity in \(\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}\).

{\bf (2) Composition.} The composition of two (uniformly) compact-valued, upper hemicontinuous, analytic-graph correspondences again satisfies all three properties (using standard results from multivalued analysis), so \(\mathcal{G}(\Psi \circ \Phi) = \mathcal{G}(\Psi) \circ \mathcal{G}(\Phi)\).\footnote{The graph of the composite
\(
  G_{\Psi\circ\Phi}
  =\bigl\{(x,z):\exists y, \,(x,y)\in G_\Phi,\;(y,z)\in G_\Psi\bigr\}
\)
is the analytic image (Souslin projection) of the analytic set
\(G_\Phi\times Z\cap X\times G_\Psi\); Hence it is analytic.}

\textbf{(3) Faithfulness.}  
Since \(\mathcal{G}\) is the identity on morphisms, it is injective on \(\operatorname{Hom}\)-sets.
\end{proof}


Notice that the analytic graph condition in Theorem \ref{thm-funct2} is essential. Upper hemicontinuity alone does not imply weak measurability; Hence, \(\mathbf{UHCont}_{\mathrm{c}}^{\mathrm{Pa}}\) is the minimal natural subcategory for which functor $\mathcal{G}$ is defined.

We also mention that, just as continuity cannot capture all measurable behavior, measurability cannot reconstruct topological properties like upper hemicontinuity. Therefore, $\mathcal{G}$ typically fails to admit a limit object in \(\mathbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}\) \citep[Definition 3.1.5]{perrone2024starting} representing $\text{Cone}(-,\mathcal{G})$, for precisely the same reason $\mathcal{F}$ does: The two categories encode fundamentally distinct structures.


