\section{Conclusion}\label{concl}
In this paper, we took a Category Theory route to show that 
%showed that, by taking a Category Theory route, we are able to show that 
Full Conformal Prediction exhibits intrinsic UQ capabilities. This is a result of framing it as a morphism of two categories, $\textbf{UHCont}$ and $\textbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$, and of showing that it is part of a diagram that commutes. As byproduct, we were able to prove that CP methods bridge Bayesian, frequentist, and imprecise approaches to statistical prediction. We also studied the properties of such categories, and we pointed out how a Conformal Prediction Region can be seen as a functor image.

This work opens up the study of Categorical Conformal Prediction, and hints at many unsolved problems. For example, it is an open question how to leverage the structure of the functional-analysis-flavored categories $\textbf{UHCont}$ and $\textbf{WMeas}_\text{uc}$ to learn further aspects of the CP methodology.

In addition, it is left to inspect whether studying CP as a morphism of different categories can allow us to remove the need for assumptions altogether, in particular compactness of the state-space $Y$.

Finally, it is left for the future to show whether our results extend to Split Conformal Prediction, and to any other extension of classical Conformal Prediction that ML researchers introduced in recent years. 


