According to Eqs.~(\ref{Eq:NC-AFM-Freq-2}) and (\ref{Eq:NC-AFM-Phase}), both $\delta f/f^{\,}_0$ and $\delta \varphi$, measured in two different modes, are proportional to the same geometrical factor $F'_z({\bf r}^{\,}_0)$, where ${\bf r}^{\,}_0$ is the position of the apex of the MFM probe. This makes possible to estimate independently the $Q$-factor of the cantilever. Indeed, the profiles of $\delta f(x,y)$ and $\delta \varphi(x,y)$ along the lines running via the centers of the vortices (figure \ref{Fig-240-df-dphi}c) after normalization have the same shape, therefore
    \begin{eqnarray}
    Q \simeq \frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{\max |\delta \varphi|}{\max \left|\delta f/f^{\,}_0\right|} \simeq  \frac{1}{2}\cdot \frac{2.6\cdot (\pi/180)}{1/(66.9\cdot 10^3)} \simeq 1.5\cdot 10^3.
    \end{eqnarray}
The factor $\pi/180$ serves to convert the phase shift expressed in degrees into the equivalent value in radians. It is easy to see that the estimated $Q$-value is close to that found previously by the fitting of the frequency responses (figure~\ref{Fig03-freq-response}).


