\title{The Szilard's engine Revisited: The Piston as Intrinsic Memory}

\author{[Your Name]}
\affiliation{[Your Institution]}
\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
We present a novel reinterpretation of the Szilard's engine, proposing that the piston itself acts as a physical memory register encoding the particle's position. This perspective embeds information storage within the engine's working substance, eliminating the need for an external observer or demon. We demonstrate that the expansion phase increases total entropy by \(  \log_2 2\), which reset the piston to its initial state offsets at a thermodynamic cost of \(  T \log_2 2\), consistent with Landauer's principle. Designed for educational clarity, this approach simplifies the classic thought experiment, offering a tangible illustration of information-thermodynamics for teaching while connecting to autonomous systems in modern physics.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
The Szilard's engine, introduced by Leo Szilard in 1929~\cite{Szilard1929}, is a cornerstone of information-thermodynamics, extending Maxwell's demon to a single-particle ideal gas. By measuring the particle's position in a box coupled to a heat bath at temperature \(T\), the engine extracts work, traditionally relying on an external demon to record this information, with erasure costing \(  T \log_2 2\)~\cite{Landauer1961} to resolve the apparent second-law violation. This interplay of information and energy has made the Szilard's engine a staple in physics education and research~\cite{LeffRex2003}. Yet, the demon's role has long faced scrutiny. Smoluchowski argued fluctuations thwart its control~\cite{Smoluchowski1912}, Feynman's ratchet and pawl illustrated how thermal noise undermines such mechanisms~\cite{Feynman1963}, Popper and Feyerabend questioned its necessity for work extraction~\cite{Feyerabend1966}, and Norton suggested external reservoirs, not information, drive the process~\cite{Norton2013}. These objections, while insightful, remain vague—lacking precise thermodynamic accounting—leaving the engine's mechanics obscured for students.

Conventionally, memory is an abstract, external device, complicating physical intuition. Here, we propose a simpler, more concrete view: the piston itself serves as the memory register, encoding the particle's position through its displacement. This eliminates the demon, embedding information processing within the engine's mechanics. We show that expansion increases entropy, whereas resetting the piston balances at Landauer's cost, offering a pedagogically powerful alternative that clarifies the thermodynamics of feedback control with explicit entropy changes—addressing the vagueness of prior critiques.

