\newpage

\section{Introduction}

Ever since the foundational work of Savage (1954), the theory of decision
under uncertainty has been based on the notion of preferences over
state-contingent acts. Given a known set of states of the world, an act
associates an outcome to each such state.

However, the state-act-outcome approach has been subject to fundamental
challenges from two directions. First, theories of bounded and differential
awareness (Halpern and Rego, 2008, Heifetz, Meier and Schipper, 2006, 2008,
Karni and Viero, 2013, Grant and Quiggin, 2013), have challenged the
assumption of a commonly known set of states of the world. In these
theoretical frameworks, agents may be completely unaware of some possible
states of the world, or may be unaware of relevant distinctions between
states. The former is referred to as \emph{restricted} awareness, and the
latter \emph{coarse} awareness.

Second, the use of a semantic representations based on ``states of
nature'' has been criticized as being less natural than a syntactic
representation based on languages with appropriate properties (Grant and
Quiggin, 2013, Halpern, 2015, Piermont and Zuazo-Garin, 2024). Notably,
Halpern (2015) writes: ``This problem is relatively minor in the case of a
single agent, aware of all possible states and events." In this case,
Stone's (1936) representation theorem establishes a correspondence between
syntactic (language based) and semantic (state space) descriptions of the
world (languages). States correspond to models which assign a truth value to
every proposition in the language. Every proposition in the language can
thus be associated with an event, and every event corresponds to an
equivalence class of propositions.

For the case of two agents with bounded and differential awareness, 
working directly with syntax is natural,
as inter-agent communication must be mediated by language.%
\footnote{
Existing formulations of differential awareness via hierarchies of state spaces, as in Heifetz, Meier and Schipper (2006) and
Grant and Quiggin (2013) are ill-suited to represent communication or interpretation, as the modeling takes place at the level of a common state-space, rather than from the perspective of the agents.}
However, each agent's subjective understanding of the state-space is represented by
a distinct language, and so, we must consider the
problem of translation: how is one agent's description of an event (i.e., an event according his own subjective understanding of the state-space) interpreted by another agent.
Because some propositions in one language are not expressible in the other,
translation may only \emph{approximate} the original meaning. We address this
problem by introducing the idea of inner and outer translations.

When translating a proposition $l$ from a source language to a target language, the inner translation of $l$ will be a statement in the target language more specific than $l$, in particular, it is the most general proposition
in the target language which implies $l$.\footnote{A hyponym is a word which represents a special
case of another word: ``dog'' is a hyponyms of ``animal''. 
A hypernym is a word which subsumes the meaning of another word: ``animal''
is a hypernym of ``dog''. The inner translation is the disjunction of all $l$'s hyponyms in the target language.} 
Conversely, the outer translation will be more general than $l$  -- the most specific proposition in the target language which is implied by $l$.\footnote{The general formulation of the outer translation operator is more involved, to account for cases where agents are aware of distinct sets of states. The important aspect for this discussion is that the outer translation is always a more general proposition subject to the agent's awareness.}

If a statement $l$ describes an event both agents are aware of, then the inner and outer 
translations of $l$ coincide. Moreover, translating the translation back to the original language results in $l$ again. However, this does not hold in general: under differential awareness, the inner and outer translations do not always coincide, and back and forth translation can result is linguistic drift.
Our notion of translation thus formalizes Halpern's (2015) idea that ``different agents
may react differently to the same expression."

When the inner and  outer translations do not coincide, i.e., under differential awareness, the proposition $l$ corresponds to a \emph{range} of possible meanings in the target language: the `true' meaning of $l$ is determined only insofar as being more general than its inner translation and more specific than its outer translation. The gap between inner and outer translations can be understood as ambiguity (in the colloquial sense of the word) and this imprecision is a consequence of differential awareness.

When considered from the semantic perspective, the events expressed by the inner and outer
translations correspond to events contained in, and containing, the event referred to by the original proposition; specifically, these are lower and upper approximations in the sense of  Dempster (1967). If the agents entertain a probability over the state-space, then the probability of that $l$ being true is equal to the probability of the event it describes---so, when $l$ is translated, the speaker of the target language will entertain a range of probabilities for $l$.\footnote{The lower-bound probability being the likelihood of the event corresponding to the inner translation of $l$ (i.e., lower approximation)  and the upper-bound that of the event corresponding to the outer translation (i.e., upper approximation).} Hence, translations serves as a bridge between differential awareness and ambiguity (in the decision theoretic sense).


While translation will not in general preserve all logical relations, e.g., tautologies or negation,
out translation operators are characterized by three desirable
properties. First, both inner and outer translations preserve logical
contradictions. Second, the outer translation preserves disjunctions, while
the inner translation preserves conjunctions. Third, the translation
operators satisfy ``consistency of translation'': if a translation of a
proposition $\bar{l}$ from language $\bar{L}$ and a proposition $l$ from
language $L$ refer to some common contingencies, then so should $l$ and the translation of $\bar{l}$. This weakens exact
preservation of meaning, while ensuring that the operators can nevertheless
be interpreted as translations.

Finally, while the inner and outer translation satisfy duality when
awareness takes the form of coarsening; with restriction, only a restricted
notion of duality holds. In particular, each of the translation operators
carries additional information not contained in the other.

Having established these results, we consider an external observer who has
access to the languages used by two agents, as well as to the translation
operators. We ask the following questions. First, can the individual
languages be embedded into a \emph{joint language} which preserves
implications between propositions from the same language, but also
determines implications between propositions from distinct languages? Can
the agents' perception of uncertainty be viewed as bounded awareness of a 
\emph{joint state space}? Second, can one \emph{identify} the joint state
space and the joint language? Finally, is it possible to \emph{compare the
awareness} of agents relative to the joint state space?

Our main result shows that the existence of a joint language and the
existence of a joint state space obtain iff the outer and inner translation
operators satisfy the three properties identified above (preservation of
contradictions, preservation of disjunction / conjunction, and consistency
of translation), together with restricted duality. The joint state space and
the corresponding joint language can be explicitly constructed, and each
agent can be seen as exhibiting bounded awareness w.r.t. the joint state
space. They are also minimally unique in that they do not refer to
contingencies of which none of the two agents is aware, and do not
distinguish between contingencies which are indistinguishable to both
agents. Finally, we show how consistency of translation can be used to
characterize comparative awareness based on the translation operators, even
when the languages used by two agents are non-nested.

\section{Literature}

The standard decision-theoretic model of uncertainty, both for individual
decisions and for games, assumes a commonly-known state space (Savage,
1954). This assumption is maintained in generalizations of the model, such
as rank-dependent utility (Quiggin, 1982), cumulative prospect theory
(Tversky and Kahneman, 1992) and choice under ambiguity (Gilboa and
Schmeidler, 1989, Klibanoff, Marinacci and Mukerji, 2005).

Tversky and Koehler (1994) are among the first to note that the proposition
used to refer to an event may bias its perception. They discuss the impact
of such misperception on probabilistic judgments. Minardi and Savochkin
(2019) use the change in preferences due to updating to derive a subjective
state space, which may differ from the objective one and also discuss
implications for probabilistic judgments.

Halpern (2003) describes the ``states of the world" approach as semantic,
distinguishing this from syntactic, or propositional representations. The
agents' different views of the world are naturally expressed by a language
and its propositions. Halpern (2015) points out the advantages of the
syntactic approach, which is also defended by Feinberg (2000, p. 128).

Blume, Easley and Halpern (2021) axiomatize expected utility maximization
whereby the state-space is endogenously derived from preferences over
programs described in terms of syntactic propositions. Their framework
allows for misperception of the ``objective'' state space as described by
Tversky and Koehler (1994).

The relationship between syntax and semantics becomes more complex under
bounded awareness. In the case of a single agent, the primary problem is to
compare the model of the world held by the agent with that which they might
hold if they were fully aware. A crucial distinction is that between coarse
awareness and restricted awareness.\footnote{%
This distinction has been pointed out by a number of authors, beginning with
Carnap (1971, pp. 87-94), but has not been fully appreciated in the
literature on unawareness} It is most naturally expressed in syntactic
terms: an agent displays coarse awareness if their language does not include
a sufficiently rich set of propositions to distinguish between two (or more)
possibilities. Restricted awareness arises when an agent ignores some
possibility, implicitly assuming propositions expressing that possibility to
be false.\footnote{%
An important implication of restricted awareness is that agents' probability
assignments for some propositions must be incorrect. By contrast, coarse
awareness does not, in itself, imply incorrect probability judgements. We
address this issue in other work.}

Multiple works have demonstrated the relevance of differential awareness in economic applications. 
Heifetz, Meier and Schipper (2006) address the issue of interactions between
agents with coarse awareness. In syntactic terms, coarse awareness is
represented by the idea that each agent has access to a language derived
from a subset of the propositions in a descriptively complete language
sufficiently rich to discriminate between all relevant possibilities. Common
awareness is generated by the intersection of the individual languages.
Heifetz et al. (2008) and Halpern and Rego (2008) provide axiomatizations
for this model. Although their model introduces a mapping (projection) from
the events of which one agent is aware to those of which another agent is
aware, these mappings are only defined for agents whose awareness is
comparable in the sense of coarsening / refinement, but not in the general
case.

Although their formal model is one of coarse awareness, Heifetz, Meier and
Schipper (2006) consider a market interaction between two agents each with
restricted awareness of propositions relevant to the market value of a firm.
In this example, bounded awareness leads to willingness to trade, even
though a no-trade result would hold under full awareness. Similar problems
of differential restricted analysis are considered by Gabaix and Laibson
(2006) (modelling competitive markets), Filiz-Ozbay (2012) (contracts), and
Li, Peltz and Zhao (2014) (duopoly). All of these papers use a semantic
framework, although propositional labels may be attached to states to
enhance intuition. Piermont (2017) demonstrates that bounded and
differential awareness may lead to preferences for flexibility, as well as
to strategic concealment of actions, which in turn gives rise to incomplete
contracts. 

The issue of translation between different languages is to our knowledge
first introduced by Carnap (1937, p. 224): he studies mappings which
preserve logical implication and examines the preservation of other logical
operations. In our setting, his analysis applies to the case of coarsening.%
\footnote{%
Carnap (1937) uses the term ``sublanguage'' to refer to a language which is less expressive. 
Unawareness in terms of restriction corresponds to a ``sublanguage of the fourth type" in Carnap (1971).} For the case of
restriction, Carnap (1971, pp. 93-97) states that translation does not
preserve logical equivalence and notes the challenge of dealing with
contradictions, which might emerge based on empirically observable facts.

When agents' languages differ in expressiveness, they are represented by
distinct sub-algebras of the joint state space. Translation consists in
finding the best approximation of the event expressed by a proposition,
which may not be measurable in the algebra of the target language. This is
formally similar to the problem of learning based on imprecise evidence, as
in Dempster (1967) and Shafer (1976). The upper and lower approximations of
an event give rise to the outer and inner translation operator in our model,
similarly to the inner and outer measures in the theory of imprecise
probabilities. It is well-known that the inner and outer measures satisfy a
duality property: specifying one of them uniquely pinpoints the other. Under
common awareness, a similar duality exists between the notions of knowledge
/ belief and possibility in the theory of epistemic knowledge. In contrast,
as already observed by Modica and Rustichini (1999), duality does not hold
for the inner and outer translation when awareness takes the form of
restriction. They suggest a restricted notion of duality conditional on
awareness. In the framework of Heifetz, Meier and Schipper (2006), Fukuda
(2023) shows that with this notion of duality, and given an awareness
operator, there is a unique correspondence between belief and possibility.
This is also the case in our model, whenever the awareness of the two agents
takes the form of coarsening. In contrast, with restriction, the two
translation operators are not mutually redundant. Thus, using both is necessary
for a comprehensive characterization of the joint state space.

In an economic context, translation is discussed by Viero (2023) who is
interested in how an external observer can recognize the fact that a
decision maker cannot distinguish two contingencies. Grant, Kline and
Quiggin (2018, p. 181) consider the case of contracting where both parties
are fully aware, but use different languages. Although the contractual
language is common, the two parties may disagree about its interpretation,
which may result in contractual disputes. Kops and Tsakas (2024) introduce
an interpretation functional between an \textquotedblleft
objective\textquotedblright\ and a \textquotedblleft
subjective\textquotedblright\ state space, which is similar to our
translation operator, but does not involve translation across different
subjective state spaces. The main property of their operator is preservation
of inclusions, whereas in our model this is only satisfied by the inner
translation, but not by the outer translation. Piermont (2021) shows how an
agent can incorporate new statements/events into their subjective
state-space using a mapping between the objective and the subjective state
space. In the terminology of the current paper, the probability of the novel
event is restricted to reside between the probability of the inner and outer
translations.

\section{Syntactic and Semantic Representation of Bounded Awareness}

Human agents reason about the world using natural languages, such as French
and English. Interpretation of natural languages requires attention to
issues such as ambiguity, Empson (1973) and implicature, Grice (1989) and 
may go beyond the formal content of a proposition, in apparent contradiction to the laws of formal logic.
The formal syntactic analysis used here allows us to capture some of these aspects (e.g., ambiguity), while preserving the assumption of logical omniscience within the agents' subjective view of the world. 
We consider languages as sets of propositions related by logical
connectives. Semantic analysis based on the idea of a state space takes the
abstraction a step further. State spaces are typically represented as subsets
of the natural numbers $\mathbb{N}$, or the reals $\mathbb{R}$. Although
states are often labelled for convenience, as in Savage's canonical example
of making an omelette, they are formally indistinguishable, except with
respect to probability or measure. The higher level of abstraction
associated with the state space allows us a more tractable representation of
the idea of translation between formal languages, while the syntactic
representation allows for a better understanding of issues of bounded and
differential awareness.

\subsection{Syntax}

Let $\Lambda ^{e}$ be a finite set of elementary propositions. The language $%
\Lambda $ is the closure of $\Lambda ^{e}$ under negation $\lnot $ and
(finite) disjunction $\vee $ and conjunction $\wedge $. A \emph{truth
assignment} for $\Lambda $ is an assignment of truth values to each
proposition:%
\begin{equation*}
w:\Lambda \rightarrow \left \{ 0,1\right \}
\end{equation*}

such that for all $\lambda ,\lambda ^{\prime }\in \Lambda$,

\begin{enumerate}
\item $w\left( \lnot \lambda \right) =1-w\left( \lambda\right) $,

\item $w\left( \lambda \vee \lambda ^{\prime }\right) =\max \left \{ w\left(
\lambda \right) ,w\left( \lambda ^{\prime }\right) \right \} $, and

\item $w\left( \lambda \wedge \lambda ^{\prime }\right) =\min \left \{
w\left( \lambda \right) ,w\left( \lambda ^{\prime }\right) \right \} $.
\end{enumerate}

Let $W\left( \Lambda \right) $ collect all truth assignments for the
language $\Lambda $.

An \emph{axiomatized language} is a pair $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) 
$ where $\Lambda $ is a language and $\mathcal{L}\subseteq \Lambda $ is a
set of \emph{axioms}, i.e., propositions that are assumed to be true due to
their nature, or due to available reliable information, or due to limited
awareness. The axiomatized language $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ is
free of logical contradiction if there exists a truth assignment $w:\Lambda
\rightarrow \left \{ 0,1\right \} $ such that $w\left( \lambda \right) =1$
for all $\lambda \in \mathcal{L}$. Such a $w$ is called a \textquotedblleft
model" for $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ and is fully characterized
by the truth values of the elementary propositions $\left \{ w\left( \lambda
^{e}\right) \right \} _{\lambda ^{e}\in \Lambda ^{e}}$.

Let 
\begin{equation*}
W\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) =\left \{ w\in W(\Lambda )\mid w\left(
\lambda \right) =1\text{ for all }\lambda \in \mathcal{L}\right \}
\end{equation*}%
collect the set of models for a given axiomatized language. Notice that $%
W\left( \Lambda \right) =W\left( \Lambda ,\emptyset \right) $, i.e., the set
of all truth assignments coincides with the set of models for the theory
with no axioms.

For two propositions $\lambda ,\lambda ^{\prime }\in \Lambda$, say that $\lambda $ 
\textit{implies} $\lambda ^{\prime }$ in $\Lambda$ if $w\left( \lambda \right)
\leq w\left( \lambda ^{\prime }\right) $ for all $w\in W\left( \Lambda ,%
\mathcal{L}\right) $. That is, for any model where $\lambda $ is true, $%
\lambda ^{\prime }$ is true as well \footnote{%
Logical implication can be defined in purely syntactic terms by defining
inference rules. We work directly with the semantic definition as the
equivalence is well understood.}. We write this as $\lambda \Rightarrow
_{(\Lambda ,\mathcal{L})}\lambda ^{\prime }$.

Further, say that $\lambda $ and $\lambda ^{\prime }$ are \textit{logically
equivalent} in $\Lambda$, written $\lambda \Leftrightarrow _{\left( \Lambda ,%
\mathcal{L}\right) }\lambda ^{\prime }$, if both $\lambda \Rightarrow
_{\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) }\lambda ^{\prime }$ and $\lambda
^{\prime }\Rightarrow _{\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) }\lambda $. The
operator $\Leftrightarrow _{\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) }$ defines an
equivalence relation on $\Lambda$.

The resulting quotient space, $L\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) ={%
\raisebox{.1em}{$L$}\left/ 
\raisebox{-.1em}{$\Leftrightarrow_{(\Lambda,
\mathcal{L})}$}\right. }$ represents the set of logically independent
propositions in $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $, or, alternatively,
the set of logical equivalence classes for $\Lambda $, given the axioms. Let 
$l(\lambda )\in L\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right)$ represent the equivalence class containing $\lambda \in
\Lambda $. In a slight abuse of notation, we will often refer to the set of
equivalence classes as $L$, when $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ is
obvious from the context.

A proposition $\lambda $ is a \textit{tautology} in $\left( \Lambda ,%
\mathcal{L}\right) $, if $w(\lambda )=1$ for all $w\in W\left( \Lambda ,%
\mathcal{L}\right) $. The equivalence class of tautologies is denoted by $t$%
. The negation of a tautology is a \textit{contradiction}, with equivalence
class denoted by $f=\lnot t$. In particular, any axiom is a tautology, and
its negation is a contradiction: if $\lambda \in \mathcal{L}$ then $l\left(
\lambda \right) =t$ and $\lnot l\left( \lambda \right) =f$.

Finally, for some $w\in W\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $, let 
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}=\bigwedge \left \{ l(\lambda ^{e})\mid w(\lambda ^{e})=1,\lambda
^{e}\in \Lambda ^{e}\right \} \bigwedge \left \{ \lnot l(\lambda ^{e})\mid
w(\lambda ^{e})=0,\lambda ^{e}\in \Lambda ^{e}\right \}
\end{equation*}%
denote the \textquotedblleft most specific" equivalence class in $L$ that is
true at $w$.

\subsubsection{Leading Example}

In this section we present an example showcasing our model. We will
repeatedly return to this example as we introduce new technical elements.

\begin{example}
\label{example-farming} Consider a farmer who wishes to settle at a given
location. The state of the world describes the natural resources available
for farming: soil fertility, conditions for raising life stock and, risk of
disease. The relevant characteristics are given by the presence of earth
worms for soil fertilization, the presence of parasitic worms as carriers of
illness, and the existence of beneficial conditions for raising sheep.
Consider two individuals, a subsistence farmer who speaks French and
produces and uses meat, wool and milk and a commercial farmer who speaks
English and produces milk and meat only.

The subsistence farmer's language contains the elementary propositions:%
\footnote{%
In French, vers= worms, moutons= sheep, blancs= white, noirs= black. In
sheep, the colors black and white are expressions of the same gene, with
black being recessive. White wool is finer than black and easier to dye
different colors. However, black wool is often preferred for craft knitting
and weaving.} 
\begin{equation*}
\Lambda ^{e}=\{\lambda _{1}^{e}=\text{\textquotedblleft
vers\textquotedblright },\lambda _{2}^{e}=\text{\textquotedblleft moutons
blancs\textquotedblright },\lambda _{3}^{e}=\text{\textquotedblleft moutons
noirs\textquotedblright }\}
\end{equation*}%
Other propositions such as \textquotedblleft moutons\textquotedblright =$%
\lambda _{2}^{e}\vee \lambda _{3}^{e}$ may be derived by closing the set of
elementary propositions under negation and disjunction. Furthermore, axioms
may be taken to represent accumulated wisdom or known facts. We take as
axioms $\mathcal{L}=\left\{ \lambda _{1}^{e}\Rightarrow _{L}\lambda
_{2}^{e}\vee \lambda _{3}^{e}\text{, }\vee _{k=1}^{3}\lambda
_{k}^{e}\right\} $: \textquotedblleft if worms are present then at least one
type of sheep thrive\textquotedblright\ (for instance because the soil
provides plentiful grass), and \textquotedblleft at least one of the
elementary propositions is true\textquotedblright . This defines the
language $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ and its equivalence classes $L
$.

The language of the commercial farmer contains the propositions: 
\begin{equation*}
\bar{\Lambda}^{e}=\{\bar{\lambda}_{1}^{e}=\text{\textquotedblleft earth
worms"},\bar{\lambda}_{2}^{e}=\text{\textquotedblleft parasitic
worms\textquotedblright , }\bar{\lambda}_{3}^{e}=\text{\textquotedblleft
sheep\textquotedblright }\}
\end{equation*}%
Other propositions such as \textquotedblleft worms\textquotedblright =$\bar{%
\lambda}_{1}^{e}\vee \bar{\lambda}_{2}^{e}$ can be derived. While a direct
translation from French to English would associate \textquotedblleft
worms\textquotedblright\ with \textquotedblleft vers\textquotedblright ,
this word may have different meanings in $\Lambda $ and $\bar{\Lambda}$ if
the subsistence farmer is unaware of parasitic worms. Further, $\bar{\Lambda}
$ does not distinguish the color of sheep. The commercial farmer may hold
true that \textquotedblleft if there are earth worms, but no parasitic
worms, sheep thrive\textquotedblright , \textquotedblleft if parasitic worms
are present, sheep cannot thrive\textquotedblright , and \textquotedblleft
at least one of the elementary propositions is true\textquotedblright . Thus
the axioms are: 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{\bar{L}}=\left\{ \bar{\lambda}_{1}^{e}\wedge \lnot \bar{\lambda}%
_{2}^{e}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\bar{\lambda}_{3}^{e},\text{ }\bar{\lambda}%
_{2}^{e}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\lnot \bar{\lambda}_{3}^{e}\text{, }\vee
_{k=1}^{3}\lambda _{k}^{e}\right\} 
\end{equation*}%
We obtain the language $\left( \bar{\Lambda},\mathcal{\bar{L}}\right) $ and
its equivalence classes $\bar{L}$.

A model for a language assigns to each elementary proposition a truth value
with \textquotedblleft true" interpreted as \textquotedblleft presence of /
conditions beneficial for...". This assignment has to be consistent with the
axioms. E.g., the subsistence farmer may describe the world as: $w\left(
\lambda _{1}^{e}\right) =0$, $w\left( \lambda _{2}^{e}\right) =1$, $w\left(
\lambda _{3}^{e}\right) =0$ to indicate the absence of worms, beneficial
conditions for white sheep, but not for black sheep.$\square $
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Properties of Quotient Algebras}

We assume that agents are logically omniscient given the language $\left(
\Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ they are endowed with. Thus, within $\left(
\Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ the agents treat logically equivalent
propositions identically: as such, we will often work directly with $L={%
\raisebox{.1em}{$L$}\left/ 
\raisebox{-.1em}{$\Leftrightarrow_{(\Lambda,
\mathcal{L})}$}\right. }$.

It is well known that $L$ is a Boolean algebra that inherits its algebraic
structure from $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $. As such, we extend our
logical operators (negation, disjunction, conjunction) to $L$. Thus,
implication can be lifted to $L$: for $l$, $l^{\prime }\in L$, $l\Rightarrow
_{L}l^{\prime }$ iff $\lambda \Rightarrow _{\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}%
\right) }\lambda ^{\prime }$ for any (hence all) $\lambda \in l$ and $%
\lambda ^{\prime }\in l^{\prime }$.

Similarly, truth valuations are extended to $L$ by setting $w\left( l\right)
=w\left( \lambda \right) $ for $\lambda \in l$. Let $W\left( L\right) $
denote the set of all truth valuations defined on $L$.\footnote{%
Taking the Boolean algebra $L$ as a primitive object, we can define $W\left(
L\right) $ to be the set of Boolean homomorphisms into the two element
Boolean algebra $\left \{ 0,1\right \} $. It is easy to show that this set
coincides with the set of models of any axiomatized language with quotient
algebra $L$.}

Not only is the quotient of an axiomatized language, $L$, a Boolean algebra,
but in fact, every Boolean algebra can be characterized this way: every
Boolean algebra isomorphic to the quotient algebra of some axiomatized
language:

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-algebra-to-lang}Let $BA$ be a finite Boolean algebra. Then
there exists some axiomatized language $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $
such that $BA$ is isomorphic to $L\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right)$.%
\footnote{%
See for example, Theorem 14.4 of Sikorski (1969).}
\end{lemma}

Lemma \ref{lemma-algebra-to-lang} implies that using the set of equivalence
classes $L$ as a primitive is equivalent to using axiomatized languages $%
\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ (under the maintained assumption of
logical omniscience). We thus slightly abuse terminology and refer to $L$ as
a language and to $l\in L$ as propositions.

\subsection{Semantics}

In the semantic interpretation of the language, a model corresponds to a
(subjective, to the speaker of the language) state. That is for $L$, the
quotient algebra for some $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ that is free
of contradiction, the corresponding set of models $W\left( L\right) $
defines a set of possible worlds, or a state space. The definition of $%
W\left( L\right) $ and the rules of logic further imply that $W\left(
L\right) $ satisfies the intrinsic properties of a Savagean state space:
exhaustiveness and exclusivity, i.e., one and exactly one of the states $%
w\in W\left( L\right) $ is realized. When the language and the axioms can be
unambiguously inferred, we will use $W$ to denote the state space.

Denote by $\mathcal{B}\left( L\right) $ the algebra of subsets of $W\left(
L\right) $. For each $l\in L$ let $b_{l}\in \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) $
denote the (possibly trivial) event on which $l$ is true: 
\begin{equation*}
b_{l}=\left \{ w\in W\left( L\right) \mid w\left( l\right) =1\right \}
\end{equation*}

Every event in $\mathcal{B}\left( L\right) $ is the truth set for a logical
equivalence class in $L$ and \textit{vice versa}. Formally:

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-proposition-event-mapping}The mapping $\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L}:L
\rightarrow \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) $, defined such that $\mathcal{\hat{B}%
}_{L}: l \mapsto b_{l}$, is an isomorphism. Moreover, $l\Rightarrow_{L}
l^{\prime }$ iff $b_{l}\subseteq b_{l^{\prime }}$
\end{lemma}

So every language can be captured entirely by a state-space. This
association runs the other way, as well:

\begin{corollary}
For any finite state space $W$ there exists a finite axiomatized language $%
\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ with quotient algebra $L$ such that $%
\left( W\left( L\right) ,\mathcal{B}\left( L\right) \right) $ is isomorphic
to $\left( W,2^{W}\right) $.
\end{corollary}

Thus, with each language, $L$, we can identify a state space, and,
conversely, with each state space, we can associate a language.

