\subsection{\label{common-language}A Joint Language and a Joint State Space}

We now define a joint language and a joint state space for two agents who
have differential bounded awareness and thus use different languages.

\subsubsection{A Joint Language}

Consider two languages, $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ and $\left( 
\bar{\Lambda},\mathcal{\bar{L}}\right) $ with quotients $L$ and $\bar{L}$,
respectively. A joint language for these two languages denoted by $\left(
\Lambda ^{\ast },\mathcal{L}^{\ast }\right) $, with quotient $L^{\ast }$, is
defined as follows.

\begin{definition}
\label{definition-joint-language}The language $L^{\ast }$ is a \textbf{joint
language} for $L$ and $\bar{L}$, if there exists two (injective) embeddings $%
\pi :L\rightarrow L^{\ast }$ and $\bar{\pi}:\bar{L}\rightarrow L^{\ast }$
such that

\begin{itemize}
\item for all $l$, $l^{\prime }\in L$, $\pi \left( l\vee l^{\prime }\right)
=\pi \left( l\right) \vee \pi \left( l^{\prime }\right) $ and $\pi \left(
l\wedge l^{\prime }\right) =\pi \left( l\right) \wedge \pi \left( l^{\prime
}\right) $ (and analogously for $\bar{\pi}$ and $\bar{L}$);

\item $\pi (f)=\bar{\pi}(\bar{f})=f^{\ast }$
\end{itemize}

where $t,\bar{t},t^{\ast }$ and $f,\bar{f},f^{\ast }$ are the equivalence
classes of tautologies and contradictions, respectively, in the
corresponding languages.
\end{definition}

A joint language must contain (images of) each of the individual languages.
The first condition requires that disjunction and conjunction are preserved,
but note that this does not imply that negation is preserved, and indeed it
will generally not be in cases of restricted awareness. The second condition
requires all contradictions map to the same equivalence class.

It follows from the first condition that logical implication in each of the
individual languages is exactly preserved:

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-joint-lang-implication}If $L^{\ast }$ is a joint language for $%
L $ and $\bar{L}$, then for all $l,l^{\prime }\in L$: 
\begin{equation*}
l\Rightarrow _{L}l^{\prime }\text{ if and only if }\pi \left( l\right)
\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\pi \left( l^{\prime }\right) ,
\end{equation*}%
and analogously for $\bar{\pi}$ and $\bar{L}$.
\end{lemma}

A joint language is not unique (that is, not unique even as a Boolean
algebra). In general, this multiplicity lies in the fact that while each
individual language embeds into $L^{\ast }$, there may be additional
contingencies in the joint language that are not expressed by either of the
individual ones. By requiring that the joint language to be as succinct as
possible, we obtain an (up to isomorphism) minimal representation.

\begin{definition}
Let $L^{\ast }$ be a joint language for $L$ and $\bar{L}$, with embeddings $%
\pi :L\rightarrow L^{\ast }$ and $\bar{\pi}:\bar{L}\rightarrow L^{\ast }$.
Call $L^{\ast }$ \textbf{minimal} if for all $l^{\ast }\in L^{\ast }$, there
exists $l\in L$ and an $\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$ such that 
\begin{equation*}
l^{\ast }=\big(\pi \left( l\right) \wedge \lnot \bar{\pi}\left( \bar{t}%
\right) \big)\vee \big(\lnot \pi \left( t\right) \wedge \pi \left( \bar{l}%
\right) \big)\vee \big(\pi \left( l\right) \wedge \bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}%
\right) \big).
\end{equation*}%
