\left( l\right) \wedge \bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}\right) \big)$, that both $l$
and $\bar{l}$ are true.

Notice, in particular for a minimal joint language we have 
\begin{align*}
t^{\ast }&\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\big(\pi \left( t\right) \wedge \lnot \bar{%
\pi}\left( \bar{t}\right) \big)\vee \big(\lnot \pi \left( t\right) \wedge
\pi \left( \bar{t}\right) \big)\vee \big(\pi \left( t\right) \wedge \bar{\pi}%
\left( \bar{t}\right) \big) \\ 
&=\pi \left( t\right) \vee \bar{\pi}\left( \bar{t}%
\right) ,
\end{align*}%
so there are no additional contingencies that are incompatible with the
axioms of both individual languages.

Notice that the maps $\pi ,\bar{\pi}$ contain all the information about how
the two languages relate to each other. The structure of these embeddings
(and indeed their existence) crucially depends on the set of axioms $%
\mathcal{L}^{\ast }$ in the joint language. These axioms tacitly relate the
meaning of the propositions in the two languages.

A minimal joint language always exists, and further, the set of all joint
languages can be characterized explicitly. However, we postpone to Section %
\ref{section-existence} the task of formalizing these results, as they are
laid bare by the re-characterization of joint languages via joint
state-spaces, introduced next.

\addtocounter{example}{-1}

\begin{example}[resumed]
The languages of the two farmers are distinct in terms of words used
(English vs. French), their meaning, and expressivity. An agricultural
biologist (who speaks English) may formulate a language $\left( \Lambda
^{\ast },\mathcal{L}^{\ast }\right) $ with corresponding equivalence classes 
$L^{\ast }$ which is able to express both world views. Write $l_{k}^{e}$
(respectively $\bar{l}_{k}^{e} $) for the equivalence classes corresponding
to $\lambda _{k}^{e}$ ($\bar{\lambda}_{k}^{e}$) in language $L$ ($\bar{L}$).

The joint language comprises equivalence classes $\left( l_{k}^{\ast
}\right) _{k=1}^{4}$, which correspond to propositions in $L$ and $\bar{L}$.
The mappings $\pi $ and $\bar{\pi}\,$satisfy: $\pi \left( l_{k}^{e}\right)
=l_{k-1}^{\ast }$ for $k=2,3$ ($l_{1}^{\ast }$ and $l_{2}^{\ast }$ are
\textquotedblleft white\textquotedblright \ and \textquotedblleft black
sheep\textquotedblright ), $\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}_{k}^{e}\right)
=l_{k+2}^{\ast }$ for $k=1,2$, ($l_{3}^{\ast }$, $l_{4}^{\ast }$ stand for
\textquotedblleft earth\textquotedblright \ and \textquotedblleft parasitic
worms\textquotedblright ). Each proposition from the individual languages
can now be mapped to a proposition in $L^{\ast }$. E.g., $\pi \left(
l_{1}^{e}\right) =l_{3}^{\ast }\wedge \left( \lnot l_{4}^{\ast }\right) $, $%
\pi \left( l_{2}^{e}\right) =l_{1}^{\ast }\wedge \left( \lnot l_{4}^{\ast
}\right) $, $\pi \left( l_{3}^{e}\right) =l_{2}^{\ast }\wedge \left( \lnot
l_{4}^{\ast }\right) $: \textquotedblleft vers\textquotedblright \ is mapped
to ``earth worms and no parasitic worms\textquotedblright ; similarly,
\textquotedblleft moutons blancs (noirs)\textquotedblright \ is mapped to
\textquotedblleft white (black) sheep and no parasitic
worms\textquotedblright , capturing unawareness of parasitic worms.
\textquotedblleft Sheep\textquotedblright \ in $\bar{L}$ is mapped to
\textquotedblleft white or black sheep\textquotedblright , $\bar{\pi}\left( 
\bar{l}_{3}^{e}\right) =l_{1}^{\ast }\vee l_{2}^{\ast }$, representing
coarsening of this category.

Associate with each of the equivalence classes $l_{k}^{\ast }$, an
elementary proposition $\lambda _{k}^{\ast e}$. Set $\Lambda ^{\ast e}=\left
\{ \left( \lambda _{k}^{\ast e}\right) _{k=1}^{4}\right \} $ and impose the
axioms: 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{L}^{\ast }=\left \{ \lambda _{3}^{\ast e}\wedge \left( \lnot
\lambda _{4}^{\ast e}\right) \Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\lambda _{1}^{\ast
e}\vee \lambda _{2}^{\ast e}\text{, }\lambda _{4}^{\ast e}\Rightarrow
_{L^{\ast }}\lnot \left( \lambda _{1}^{\ast e}\vee \lambda _{2}^{\ast
e}\right) \text{, }\vee _{k=1}^{4}\lambda _{k}^{\ast e}\right \}
\end{equation*}%
It is easy to check that the resulting joint language is minimal for $L$ and 
$\bar{L}$. E.g., $l_{1}^{\ast }=\left( \pi \left( l_{1}^{e}\right) \wedge
\lnot \pi \left( t\right) \right) \vee \left( \pi \left( l_{1}^{e}\right)
\wedge \pi \left( \bar{l}_{1}^{e}\right) \right) $, $l_{3}^{\ast }=\pi
\left( l_{2}^{e}\right) \wedge \pi \left( \bar{t}\right) $.
\end{example}

\subsubsection{A Joint State Space}

The conditions for $L^{\ast }$ to be a joint language for $L$ and $\bar{L}$
can be equivalently captured via conditions on their state-spaces / algebra
of events. Write $W=W\left( L\right) $ and $\bar{W}=W\left( \bar{L}\right) $
for the state spaces of the two languages.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-joint-state-space-map}A language $L^{\ast }$ is a joint
language for $L$ and $\bar{L}$ if and only if there exist two embeddings $%
\sigma :\mathcal{B}\left( L\right) \rightarrow \mathcal{B}\left( L^{\ast
}\right) $ and $\bar{\sigma}:\mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}\right) \rightarrow 
\mathcal{B}\left( L^{\ast }\right) $ such that

\begin{itemize}
\item for all $E$, $E^{\prime }\in \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) $, $\sigma
\left( E\cup E^{\prime }\right) =\sigma \left( E\right) \cup \sigma \left(
E^{\prime }\right) $ and $\sigma \left( E\cap E^{\prime }\right) =\sigma
\left( E\right) \cap \sigma \left( E^{\prime }\right) $ (and analogously for 
$\bar{\sigma}$ and $\bar{L}$); 
% \item $\sigma(W(\L)) \cup \bar{\sigma}(W(\bar{\L})) = W(\L^*)$

\item $\sigma (\emptyset )=\bar{\sigma}(\emptyset )=\emptyset $.
\end{itemize}

Moreover $L^{\ast }$ is minimal if in addition

\begin{itemize}
\item $\sigma \left( \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) \right) \cup \sigma \left( 
\mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}\right) \right) $ generates the algebra $\mathcal{B}%
\left( L^{\ast }\right) $.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

Let $Q$ denote the state space described by the joint language. The set $%
\sigma \left( \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) \right) =\left \{ \sigma \left(
b_{l}\right) \in \mathcal{B}\left( L^{\ast }\right) \mid l\in L\right \} $
collects the events in the joint state-space that the language $L$ can
describe. As such, we can interpret $\sigma \left( \mathcal{B}\left(
L\right) \right) $ as the set of events that (the speaker of) language $L$
is aware of. Likewise, the set $A=\sigma \left( W\right) =\bigcup \sigma
\left( \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) \right) $ collects the set of states that
are (implicitly) considered possible by $L$. We say `implicitly' because it
is possible that a state consistent with $L$, i.e., $q\in \sigma (W)$,
cannot be expressed exactly in $L$, i.e., $\left \{ q\right \} \notin \sigma
\left( \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) \right) $. The set of subjective states
defines a partition of $A$, $\left \{ \sigma \left( w\right) _{w\in
W}\right
\} $.

Call $l\in L$ and $\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$ state-equivalent if $\sigma \left(
b_{l}\right) =\bar{\sigma}\left( b_{\bar{l}}\right) $, or, equivalently, $%
\pi \left( l\right) \Leftrightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\pi \left( \bar{l}\right) $.
State-equivalence is in general not preserved under negation within the
individual languages: $\sigma \left( b_{l}\right) =\bar{\sigma}\left( b_{%
\bar{l}}\right) $ does not imply $\sigma \left( \lnot b_{l}\right) =\bar{%
\sigma}\left( \lnot b_{\bar{l}}\right) $.

