\subsubsection{Coarsening and Restriction}

We now make precise the terms coarsening and restriction and characterize
them using the implication relation $\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}$ in $L^{\ast }$. An alternative
characterization in terms of the joint state space will be stated in Section %
\ref{section-comparative-awareness} below.

Coarsening arises when an agent fails to distinguish between events that are
in fact different. Say that $L$ is a \emph{pure} \emph{coarsening} of $\bar{L%
}$ if $\pi \left( t\right) =\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{t}\right) =t^{\ast }$ and,
for all $l\in L$, there is an $\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$ s.t. $\pi \left( l\right)
\Leftrightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}\right) $. Thus, every
proposition in $L$ can be expressed by a proposition in $\bar{L}$, but not
vice versa. In semantic terms, the languages agree on the universal event: $%
\sigma \left( W\right) =\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{W}\right) $, and the the
subjective state space $W$ partitions $\bar{W}$, i.e., $\left \{ \sigma
\left( w\right) _{_{w\in W}}\right \} $ is a partition of $\left \{ \bar{%
\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) _{\bar{w}\in \bar{W}}\right \} $.

Restriction refers to a situation in which an agent is unaware of events
that are in fact possible. Say that $L$ is a \emph{pure} \emph{restriction}
of $\bar{L}$ if $\pi \left( t\right) \Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\bar{t}$ and $%
\pi \circ \bar{\pi}^{-1}$ is a bijection between $L$ and $\{ \bar{l}\in \bar{%
L}\mid b_{\bar{l}}\subseteq \sigma \left( W\right) \}$. Every proposition
considered possible in $L$ is thus considered possible in $\bar{L}$, but not
vice versa. In semantic terms, $\sigma \left( W\right) \subseteq \bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{W}\right) $: $L$ considers possible a subset of the \emph{states}
considered by $\bar{L}$. Furthermore, there is a 1-to-1 mapping between the
states in $W$ and those states in $\bar{W}$ of which $L$ is aware.

Say that $L$ is a \emph{combination of coarsening and restriction} of $\bar{L%
}$, if there is a language $\tilde{L}$ such that $\tilde{L}$ is a pure
restriction of $\bar{L}$ and $L$ is a pure coarsening of $\tilde{L}$.
Clearly, if $L^{\ast }$ is a joint language of $L$ and $\bar{L}$, then each $%
L$ and $\bar{L}$ are combinations of coarsening and restriction of $L^{\ast
} $. In particular, for language $L$, the set $A=\sigma \left( W\right) $
determines the restriction, whereas the coarsening is captured by $\left \{
\sigma \left( w\right) _{w\in W}\right \} $, which is a partition of $A$.

Note that in both cases of pure coarsening and pure restriction, we have
that $\sigma \left( \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) \right) \subseteq \bar{\sigma}%
\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}\right) \right) $. It is easy to see that
the reverse is also true: if $\sigma \left( \mathcal{B}\left( L\right)
\right) \subseteq \bar{\sigma}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}\right)
\right) $, then $L$ is a combination of coarsening and restriction of $\bar{L%
}$.

The partial order on axiomatized languages defined by ``$L$ is a combination
of a coarsening and restriction of $\bar{L}$", can in fact be characterized
through the relation of $\bar{L}$ being a joint language, as shown by the
following result.

\begin{corollary}
\label{corollary-comparative-unawareness-1}Consider two languages $\bar{L}$
and $L$. Then $L$ is a combination of coarsening and restriction of $\bar{L}$
if and only if $\bar{L}$ serves as a minimal joint language for the pair.
\end{corollary}

For example, consider the case where $L$ and $\bar{L}$ are the quotient
algebras of $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}\right) $ and $\left( \bar{\Lambda},%
\mathcal{\bar{L}}\right) $, respectively, with $\bar{L}^{e}\subseteq L^{e}$
and $\mathcal{\bar{L}}\supseteq \mathcal{L}$. Then Corollary \ref%
{corollary-comparative-unawareness-1} establishes that $L$ serves as a joint
state-space. In particular, we have that if $\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{\bar{L}}$
then $\bar{L}$ is a pure coarsening of $L$, whereas if $L^{e}=\bar{L}^{e}$,
then $\bar{L}$ is a pure restriction.

\addtocounter{example}{-1}

\begin{example}[resumed]
The languages of the two farmers provide examples of both coarsening and
restriction. The subsistence farmer is unaware of the existence of parasitic
worms. Thus, \textquotedblleft vers\textquotedblright\ in $L$, $l_{1}^{e}$,
is mapped to \textquotedblleft earth worms\textquotedblright =$l_{3}^{\ast
}{}^{e}$ in $L^{\ast }$, but no proposition corresponding to
\textquotedblleft worms\textquotedblright =$l_{3}^{\ast }{}^{e}\vee
l_{4}^{\ast e}$ exists in $L$. The state space $W$ excludes states in which
earth worms are present, but sheep cannot thrive. It is a pure restriction
of the joint state space $Q=\left\{ q_{1}...q_{8}\right\} $: $A=\sigma
\left( W\right) =\left\{ q_{2},q_{3},q_{4},q_{6},q_{7},q_{8}\right\} \subset
Q$.

The commercial farmer fails to distinguish between sheep of different color: 
$\bar{l}_{3}^{e}$ is a coarsening of $l_{1}^{\ast e}$ and $l_{2}^{\ast e}$.
The state space $\bar{W}$ is a pure coarsening of $Q$, $\bar{A}=\bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{W}\right) =Q$, and merges states where white or black sheep are
present:\ 
\begin{equation*}
\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}_{1}\right) =\left\{ q_{1}\right\} ,\bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{w}_{2}\right) =\left\{ q_{2},q_{3},q_{4}\right\} ,\bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{w}_{3}\right) =\left\{ q_{5}\right\} ,\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}%
_{4}\right) =\left\{ q_{6},q_{7},q_{8}\right\} \text{. }\square 
\end{equation*}
\end{example}

\section{Translation Between Languages}

We now consider the process of translation between languages, which
typically differ in their vocabulary (elementary propositions), expressive
power and in their axiomatic assumptions. The languages may be associated
with different agents, or with a given agent whose awareness changes over
time. A translation operator is a mapping between the equivalence classes of
two languages:

\begin{definition}
\label{def-translation-operator}A translation operator from $L$ to $\bar{L}$
is a function 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L \rightarrow \bar{L}}: L \to \bar{L}.
\end{equation*}
\end{definition}

The translation operator introduced here exploits the interplay between
syntax and semantics in a way that is similar to the way translation takes
place between speakers of initially incommensurable languages. On their
initial encounter, speakers of different languages may begin by indicating
and naming objects, before building up to a complete translation.

In doing so, they may use the previously defined objects of the joint
language $\left( \Lambda ^{\ast },\mathcal{L}^{\ast }\right) $ and the
resulting $L^{\ast }$, which, in this section, we take as given. Based on
it, we define two translation operators:

The inner translation operator $\mathcal{T}_{L \rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ is
defined as:

\begin{definition}
For $l\in L$, 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l\right) =\bigvee \left \{ \bar{%
l}\in \bar{L}\mid \pi \left( l\right) \Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\bar{\pi}%
\left( \bar{l}\right) \right \}  \label{inner-translation-implication}
\end{equation}%
(and symmetrically for $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}%
\right) $).
\end{definition}

The outer translation operator is $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$:

\begin{definition}
For $l\in L$, 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l\right) =\bigwedge \left \{ 
\bar{l}\in \bar{L}\mid \bar{\pi}\left( \lnot \bar{l}\right) \Rightarrow
_{L^{\ast }}\lnot \pi \left( l\right) \right \}
\label{outer-translation-implication}
\end{equation}%
(and symmetrically for $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}(\bar{l})$).
\end{definition}

For a given proposition $l$ in $L$, the inner translation identifies the
most general proposition in $\bar{L}$, which implies $l$ according to $%
\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}$. The outer translation determines the least
specific proposition in $\bar{L}$ the negation of which implies that $l$ is
not true according to $\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}$.

The inner and outer translation operators defined here have their
counterparts in the theory of natural languages. The outer translation
operator corresponds to the least generic hypernym of the original
proposition in the target language, whereas the inner translation is the
disjunction of all hyponyms of the original proposition in the target
language.

Note that if $\pi \left( l\right) \Leftrightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\pi \left( 
\bar{l}\right) $ then the inner and the outer translations of $l$ coincide
and result in $\bar{l}$. If each proposition in $L$ has a $\Leftrightarrow
_{L^{\ast }}$-equivalent proposition in $\bar{L}$ and vice-versa, then the
joint state space coincides with the individual ones and the translation
operator is an isomorphism.

Given the joint language $L^{\ast }$ and the identified joint state space $%
W^{\ast }$, we can restate the translation operators in terms of states. For 
$E\in \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) $, define the inner and outer events in $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}\right) $, as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( E\right) &=&\left \{ {\bar{w}%
\mid \bar{w}\in }\bar{W}\text{,{\ }}\bar{\sigma}\left( {\bar{w}}\right) {%
\subseteq \sigma }\left( {E}\right) \right \}  \label{inner-event} \\
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( E\right) &=&\left \{ {\bar{w}%
\mid \bar{w}\in \bar{W}}\text{, }\bar{\sigma}\left( {\bar{w}}\right) \cap
\sigma \left( E\right) {\not=\varnothing }\right \}  \label{outer-event}
\end{eqnarray}

The inner event of $E$, $\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left(
E\right) $, is the largest event in the state space of the language $\bar{L}$%
, $\mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}\right) $, which is ``included'' in the image of
event $E$, $\sigma \left( E\right) $. The outer event of $E$, $\mathcal{E}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( E\right) $, is the collection of all
states in $\bar{W}$ which have a non-empty intersection with the image of $E$%
. In the terminology of Dempster (1967), these are the lower and the upper
approximations of the event $E$ in the space $\mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}%
\right) $.

By Lemma \ref{lemma-proposition-event-mapping}, it is immediate to restate
the translation operators in terms of events as:

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-translation-in-states}The inner translation operator $\mathcal{T%
}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ satisfies 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l\right) =\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{%
\bar{L}}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left(
b_{l}\right) \right)  \label{inner-translation}
\end{equation}%
whereas the outer translation operator $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}$ satisfies: 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l\right) =\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{%
\bar{L}}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left(
b_{l}\right) \right)  \label{outer-translation}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\addtocounter{example}{-1}

\begin{example}[resumed]
When there is a one-to-one correspondence between propositions in the two
languages, e.g. \textquotedblleft worms\textquotedblright\ in $L$
corresponds to \textquotedblleft earth worms\textquotedblright\ in $\bar{L}$%
: $\pi \left( l_{1}^{e}\right) =\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}_{1}^{e}\right)
=l_{3}^{\ast e}$, both translation operators map $l_{1}^{e}$ to $\bar{l}%
_{1}^{e}$ and vice-versa. $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{%
l}_{1}^{e}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}%
_{1}^{e}\right) =l_{1}^{e}$. In general, the correspondence between the two
languages is not one-to-one, and translation has to approximate the meaning
of propositions. E.g., $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}%
_{1}^{e}\vee \bar{l}_{2}^{e}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{1}^{e}\vee \bar{l}_{2}^{e}\right) =l_{1}^{e}$
(\textquotedblleft worms\textquotedblright\ in $\bar{L}$ are translated to
\textquotedblleft vers\textquotedblright\ in $L$, generating a biased
meaning, because the subsistence farmer is unaware of parasitic worms).
Translating \textquotedblleft moutons blancs\textquotedblright , $l_{2}^{e}$%
, to $\bar{L}$ gives distinct outer and inner translations. The outer
translation is the most specific hypernym, \textquotedblleft
sheep\textquotedblright , $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left(
l_{2}^{e}\right) =\bar{l}_{3}^{e}$, whereas the inner translation is the
contradiction, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left(
l_{2}^{e}\right) =f$, because $\bar{L}$ lacks a hyponym (a more specific
term) for a \textquotedblleft white sheep\textquotedblright . $\square $
\end{example}

\subsection{Duality of the Translation Operators}

Consider two languages $L\ $and $\bar{L}$ and let $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}$ be a translation operator. The dual of $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}$, $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{d}$, is defined by: 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{d}\left( l\right) =\lnot \left( \mathcal{%
T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( \lnot l\right) \right)
\end{equation*}

The inner and outer translation are dual if $L$ and $\bar{L}$ are pure
coarsenings of a joint language. With restriction, duality holds only in a
restricted sense:

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-restricted-duality}The inner and the outer translation
operators between $L$ and $\bar{L}$ satisfy restricted duality:%
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l\right) &=&\lnot \mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( \lnot l\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right)  \label{restricted-duality} \\
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}\right) &=&\lnot 
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \lnot \bar{l}\right) \wedge 
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right)  \notag
\end{eqnarray}

In particular, when $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right) =%
\bar{t}$ ($\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) =t$),
then $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}^{-}$ ($\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$) are dual.
\end{lemma}

Example \ref{example-farming} shows the failure of duality in the case of
restriction:

\addtocounter{example}{-1}

\begin{example}[resumed]
Consider the inner translation of $l_{\left\{ w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}\right\} }$
to $\bar{L}$, given by $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left(
l_{\left\{ w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}\right\} }\right) =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}$ and
the outer translation of $\lnot l_{\left\{ w_{1}...w_{3}\right\} }$ given by 
$\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( \lnot l_{\left\{
w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}\right\} }\right) =\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}\left( l_{\left\{ w_{4}...w_{6}\right\} }\right) =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{4}}$%
. Note that 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l_{\left\{
w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}\right\} }\right) =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\not=\bar{l}%
_{\left\{ \bar{w}_{1},\bar{w}_{2},\bar{w}_{3}\right\} }=\lnot \mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( \lnot l_{\left\{ w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}\right\}
}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
Thus, duality fails. However, restricted duality as in Lemma \ref%
{lemma-restricted-duality} holds:%
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l_{\left\{
w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}\right\} }\right) & =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}} \\
& =\bar{l}_{\left\{ \bar{w}_{1},\bar{w}_{2},\bar{w}_{3}\right\} }\wedge \bar{%
l}_{\left\{ \bar{w}_{2},\bar{w}_{4}\right\} } \\
& =\lnot \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( \lnot l_{\left\{
w_{1},w_{2},w_{3}\right\} }\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{-}\left( t\right) \text{. }\square 
\end{align*}
\end{example}

We show below that the failure of duality implies that the two translation
operators provide complementary information about the underlying joint state
space. Using both is thus necessary for identifying the joint state space and the
joint language.

\subsection{Preservation of Meaning}

In natural languages, translation should preserve the original meaning of
the proposition. In this section, we examine in how far the translation
operators defined above satisfy this desideratum.

If two propositions, $l\in L$ and $\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$ are equivalent, $%
l\Leftrightarrow _{L^{\ast }}\bar{l}$, then both translation operators map $%
l $ to $\bar{l}$: $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l\right) =%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l\right) =\bar{l}$ (and
symmetrically for $\bar{L}\rightarrow L$). Recall $l\Leftrightarrow
_{L^{\ast }}\bar{l}$ whenever $l$ and $\bar{l}$ are state-equivalent, $%
\sigma \left( b_{l}\right) =\bar{\sigma}\left( b_{\bar{l}}\right) $. For
state-equivalent propositions, inverting the translation gives back the
original proposition: 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L%
}}^{+}\left( l\right) \right) &=&l  \label{outer-pres-meaning} \\
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L%
}}^{-}\left( l\right) \right) &=&l  \label{inner-pres-meaning}
\end{eqnarray}

If (\ref{outer-pres-meaning}), respectively, (\ref{inner-pres-meaning})
holds, we say that the outer, respectively, inner translation exactly
preserves meaning.

In general, a proposition is not state-equivalent to its translation. The
translation operators preserve original meaning only in as far as this is
compatible with the expressive power of the target language. The following
lemma shows that the outer and the inner translation operators preserve
meaning in a weaker sense. When the languages represent a pure coarsening of
the full-awareness state space, and thus, the tautologies in the two
languages are equivalent ($\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{%
t}\right) =t$), the outer translation of the outer translation of $l$ into
the original language $L$ is always implied by $l$. In the general case,
this property only holds for those propositions of which both languages are
implicitly aware, $l\wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{%
t}\right) $. The inner translation of the inner translation of $l$ into the
original language $L$ always implies $l$.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-restricted-preservation-of-meaning}The translation operators
defined in (\ref{inner-translation}) and (\ref{outer-translation}) satisfy
restricted preservation of meaning: for every $l\in L$,%
\begin{eqnarray}
l\wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{t}\right)
&\Rightarrow &_{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l\wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+}\left( \bar{t}\right) \right) \right)
\label{outer-preservation-meaning} \\
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L%
}}^{-}\left( l\right) \right) &\Rightarrow &_{L}l
\label{inner-preservation-meaning}
\end{eqnarray}
\end{lemma}

Lemma \ref{lemma-restricted-preservation-of-meaning} clarifies the extent to
which the translation operators bias the meaning of propositions due to the
limited expressiveness of the language. Nevertheless, for any two languages
based on a joint language, there are subsets of propositions $%
L^{dp}\subseteq L$ and $\bar{L}^{dp}\subseteq \bar{L}$ for which meaning is
exactly preserved by both translation operators.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-exact-preservation-of-meaning-L*}There exist non-empty subsets $%
L^{p}\subseteq L$ and $\bar{L}^{p}\subseteq \bar{L}$ such that each of the
translation operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{%
T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ is a bijection between $L^{p}$ and $\bar{L}%
^{p}$ and satisfies exact preservation of meaning as in (\ref%
{outer-pres-meaning}) on this set.

Similarly, there exist non-empty subsets $L^{dp}\subseteq L$ and $\bar{L}%
^{dp}\subseteq \bar{L}$ such that each of the translation operators $%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}$ is a bijection between $L^{dp}$ and $\bar{L}^{dp}$ and satisfies
exact preservation of meaning as in (\ref{inner-pres-meaning}) on this set.

Furthermore, $L^{dp}\subseteq L^{p}$ and $\bar{L}^{dp}\subseteq \bar{L}^{p}$.
\end{lemma}

E.g., for two languages which are pure coarsenings of a joint language (and
thus, of a joint state space), the sets $L^{p}=L^{dp}$ and $\bar{L}^{p}=\bar{%
L}^{dp}$ are exactly those that correspond to the events in the finest
common coarsening of the partitions $\sigma \left( \mathcal{B}\left(
L\right) \right) $ and $\bar{\sigma}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \bar{L}\right)
\right) $. For any two languages, the four sets always include the
contradiction, $f\in L^{dp}$ and $\bar{f}\in \bar{L}^{dp}$ and are thus
non-empty.

\addtocounter{example}{-1}

\begin{example}[resumed]
In the example, propositions such as $l_{\left\{ w_{1}...w_{3}\right\} }$, $%
l_{\left\{ w_{4}...w_{6}\right\} }\in L^{dp}$ preserve their meaning under
both inner and outer translation, e.g.:%
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l_{\left\{
w_{1}...w_{3}\right\} }\right) =\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left(
l_{\left\{ w_{1}...w_{3}\right\} }\right) =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}&  & & \text{
and } \\
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left(\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left(\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\right) =l_{\left\{
w_{1}...w_{3}\right\} }&  & &
\end{align*}%
In contrast, $l_{w_{1}}$ does not preserve its meaning, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left(
l_{w_{1}}\right) \right) =f$. Restricted preservation of meaning applies: $%
l_{w_{1}}\wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{t}\right)
=f$, as in (\ref{outer-preservation-meaning}), whereas $f\Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L%
}}^{-}\left( l_{w_{1}}\right) \right) =f$ as in (\ref%
{inner-preservation-meaning}). $\square $
\end{example}

\subsection{Properties of the Translation Operators\label{section:
properties}}

We now collect some properties of the translation operators between
languages based on differential awareness.

\begin{description}
\item[Preservation of Contradictions] A translation operator preserves
contradiction if $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( f\right) =\bar{f}$
\end{description}

It is immediate from Definitions \ref{inner-translation-implication} and \ref%
{outer-translation-implication} that:

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-preservation-contradiction}The inner and the outer translation
operators preserve contradictions: $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}\left( f\right) =\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( f\right) =%
\bar{f}$.
\end{lemma}

In contrast, tautologies need not be preserved, in particular in the case of
restriction.

If two languages have equal expressive power, we would expect that if $l$
translates to $\bar{l}$, then $\bar{l}$ translates to $l$. With differential
awareness, only the following weaker properties, which we call inner / outer
consistency of translation, hold.

\begin{description}
\item[Consistency of Translation] 

\item The translation operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}$ and $%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}$ satisfy \emph{outer consistency of
translation} when 
\begin{equation*}
l\wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}\left( \bar{l}\right) \not=f\text{
iff}
\end{equation*}%
\begin{equation*}
\bar{l}\wedge \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( l\right) \not=\bar{f}
\end{equation*}

\item The translation operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}$ and $%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}$ satisfy \emph{inner consistency of
translation} when for $f\not=l\in L$ and $\bar{f}\not=\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$

\item[$\left( i\right) $] $l\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ and $l\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}\left( \lnot \bar{l}\right) $ imply $\bar{l}\not \Rightarrow _{%
\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( \lnot l\right) $, and

\item[$\left( ii\right) $] $\bar{l}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( t\right) $ and $\bar{l}\not \Rightarrow _{\bar{%
L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( \lnot l\right) $ imply $l\not
\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}\left( \lnot \bar{l}%
\right) $.
\end{description}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-consistency-translation}The outer translation operators $%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+}$ defined in (\ref{outer-translation-implication}) satisfy outer
consistency of translation. The inner translation operators $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$%
defined in (\ref{inner-translation-implication}) satisfy inner consistency
of translation.
\end{lemma}

Outer consistency of translation requires that a proposition $l$ does not
contradict the outer translation of a proposition $\bar{l}$ iff the outer
translation of $l$ does not contradict $l$. In terms of the joint state
space, the hypothesis means that there is some state $q$ which belongs to
the images of both $b_{l}$ and $b_{\bar{l}}$. Thus, the subjective state $%
\bar{w}$ which includes $q$ belongs to the event corresponding to the
translation of $l$, and $\bar{l}$ cannot contradict the translation of $l$.
When $l=l_{w}$ and $\bar{l}=\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$, inner consistency becomes: $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $ iff $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) $. We thus define:

\begin{definition}
\label{definition-relevant-pair}A pair of subjective states $\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \,$for which $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $ (and thus, $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left(
l_{w}\right) $)$\ $is called relevant.
\end{definition}

A pair of states is relevant iff $\sigma \left( w\right) \cap \bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{w}\right) \not=\varnothing $. However, each state may contain
joint states $q$ of which the other language is unaware, i.e., $\sigma
\left( w\right) \not\subseteq \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{W}\right) =\bar{A}$
and $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) \not\subseteq \sigma \left( W\right)
=A$ may hold.

Inner consistency of translation requires that if a proposition $l$ of which
the other language is implicitly aware does not imply the inner translation
of proposition $\lnot \bar{l}$, then neither does $\bar{l}$ imply the inner
translation of $\lnot l$. The hypothesis means that language $\bar{L}$ is
aware of the event corresponding to $l$ and that this event ($\sigma \left(
b_{l}\right) $) is not contained in $\sigma \left( b_{\lnot \bar{l}}\right) $%
. Thus, there is a joint state $q$ to which both $l$ and $\bar{l}$ refer,
whereas $\lnot l$ does not. Since $q$ does not belong to the event
corresponding to the translation of $\lnot l$, $\bar{l}$ does not imply the
translation of $\lnot l$.

For the special case $l=l_{w}$ and $\bar{l}=\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$, we define:

\begin{definition}
\label{definition-dually-relevant-pair}A pair of subjective states $\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) $ for which either $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ and $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $,
or $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{-}\left( t\right) $ and $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\not \Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{%
T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( \lnot l_{w}\right) $ is called "dually
relevant".
\end{definition}

A pair of states is dually relevant iff $\varnothing \not=\sigma \left(
w\right) \cap \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) $ and either $\sigma \left(
w\right) \subseteq \bar{A}$ or $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) \subseteq A
$. Thus, both languages are implicitly aware of all states to which either $w
$ or $\bar{w}$ refer. It is clear that \textquotedblleft dual
relevance\textquotedblright\ implies relevance, but not vice-versa.

While the translation operators do not preserve all logical operations, we
show that the outer translation preserves disjunction, while the inner
translation preserves conjunction.

\begin{description}
\item[Preservation of Disjunction] A translation operator $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}$ preserves disjunction if for any two $l_{1}$, $%
l_{2}\in L$,%
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( l_{1}\vee l_{2}\right) =\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( l_{1}\right) \vee \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}\left( l_{2}\right)
\end{equation*}

\item[Preservation of Conjunction] A translation operator $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}$ preserves conjunction if for any two $l_{1}$, $%
l_{2}\in L$,%
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( l_{1}\wedge l_{2}\right) =\mathcal{T%
}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}\left( l_{1}\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}\left( l_{2}\right)
\end{equation*}
\end{description}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-strong-preservation-meaning-disjunction}The outer translation
operator $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ preserves disjunction. The
inner translation operator $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$
preserves conjunction.
\end{lemma}

\section{The Existence of a joint Language and a joint State Space\label%
{section-existence}}

We now return to a formal demonstration of necessary and sufficient
conditions for the existence of a joint language and a joint state space, as
foreshadowed in Section 3.3

Suppose that two agents use distinct languages $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}%
\right) $, $\left( \bar{\Lambda},\mathcal{\bar{L}}\right) $ and pairs of
translation operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$, $\mathcal{T}%
_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$, $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$, $%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ on the corresponding sets of
equivalence classes $L$ and $\bar{L}$ as in Definition \ref%
{def-translation-operator} (not necessarily those defined by (\ref%
{inner-translation-implication}) and (\ref{outer-translation-implication})).
We ask: What conditions on the translation operators ensure that there
exists a joint language $\left( \Lambda ^{\ast },\mathcal{L}^{\ast }\right) $
with equivalence classes $L^{\ast }$ and an implication relation $%
\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}$ such that the translation operators satisfy
definitions (\ref{inner-translation-implication}) and (\ref%
{outer-translation-implication}) w.r.t. $L^{\ast }$ and $\Rightarrow
_{L^{\ast }}$? Alternatively: What conditions ensure that the agents'
perception of uncertainty can be interpreted as originating from a joint
state space $Q$ but with potentially different combinations of coarsening
and restriction, $\left( A,\sigma \left( w\right) _{w\in W}\right) $ and $%
\left( \bar{A},\sigma \left( \bar{w}\right) _{w\in \bar{W}}\right) $ such
that the translation operators satisfy (\ref{inner-translation}) and (\ref%
{outer-translation}) w.r.t. to this joint state space? We now show that
these two questions are equivalent. Furthermore, a joint state-space and a
joint language exist exactly when the translation operators satisfy the
properties identified in Section \ref{section: properties}, as well as
restricted duality. Finally, the (minimal) joint state space, as well as the
(minimal) joint language can be explicitly constructed.

\begin{proposition}
\label{proposition-full-characterization}Let $\left( \Lambda ,\mathcal{L}%
\right) $, $\left( \bar{\Lambda},\mathcal{\bar{L}}\right) $ be languages and
let $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}$, $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T%
}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ be translation operators between the
corresponding sets of equivalence classes $L$ and $\bar{L}$. The following
statements are equivalent:

\begin{description}
\item[I.] There exists a minimal joint language $\left( \Lambda ^{\ast },%
\mathcal{L}^{\ast }\right) $ with equivalence classes $L^{\ast }$, and an
implication relation $\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}$ for which the translation
operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}$ satisfy (\ref{inner-translation-implication}) and are
thus inner translations, whereas $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$
and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ satisfy (\ref%
{outer-translation-implication}) and are thus outer translations.

\item[II] There exists a minimal joint state space $Q$ for which the
translation operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{%
T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$ satisfy (\ref{inner-translation}) and are
thus inner translations, whereas $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$
and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ satisfy (\ref{outer-translation}%
) and are thus outer translations.

\item[III] The translation operators satisfy the following properties:
\end{description}
\end{proposition}

\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}$ satisfy preservation of contradictions and conjunctions,
as well as outer consistency of translation;

\item $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}$ satisfy preservation of contradictions and disjunctions,
as well as inner consistency of translation; and

\item $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$, as well as $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$ satisfy restricted
duality.
\end{enumerate}

We construct the joint state space in two steps. First, we use the outer
translation operators to construct the minimal joint state space $\tilde{Q}$
for which $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}$ satisfy the definition in (\ref{outer-translation}).
Next, we do the same for the inner translation operators, $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$,
and identify a (potentially different) minimal joint state space $\hat{Q}$.
When duality (rather than restricted duality) holds, and thus, unawareness
takes the form of pure coarsening of the joint state space, these two state
spaces are identical. However, when duality is violated, (at least one
language expresses restriction), the so-identified state spaces differ. We
take their union as the joint state space for the two agents.

To understand the construction, consider the most specific propositions $%
\left( l_{w}\right) _{w\in W}$ and $\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) _{\bar{w}%
\in \bar{W}}$. Use the operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$
and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$, (which satisfy outer
consistency) to identify the relevant (as in Definition \ref%
{definition-relevant-pair}) pairs of states $\tilde{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \in W\times \bar{W}$ and add these to the joint state space $\tilde{Q%
}$. Such a state $\tilde{q}$ represents "the intersection of the elements $%
\sigma \left( w\right) $ and $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) $ of the two
agents' partitions".

If for some $w\in W$ there is no $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ with $\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) $ being relevant, add to $\tilde{Q}$ a state $\tilde{q}=\left(
w,\bigcirc \right) $. In this case, the intersection $\sigma \left( w\right)
\cap \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{W}\right) $ is empty: the language $\bar{L}$ is
``unaware'' of the states in $\sigma \left( w\right) $. Similarly, if for
some $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ there is no $w\in W$ with $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) 
$ being relevant, we add to $\tilde{Q}$ the state $\tilde{q}=\left( \bigcirc
,\bar{w}\right) $. We show that if a pair of translation operators between $%
L $ and $\bar{L}$ satisfy the properties listed in III.1. of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-full-characterization}, then they are outer translations with
respect to $\tilde{Q}$.

The so-constructed $\tilde{Q}$ is the ``minimal'' state space, which is
consistent with the outer translations $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$. It cannot exhibit "more
awareness" than the agents can reveal: i.e., it cannot include contingencies
of which both agents are unaware, nor distinguish between contingencies,
which are indistinguishable for both agents.

While the above properties of $\tilde{Q}$ are a natural consequence of the
limited awareness of the two agents, the so-derived state space suffers from
another drawback. The outer translation operators, cannot identify whether
for a given relevant pair $\tilde{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $, both agents
are aware of all states included in $\sigma \left( w\right) \cup \bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{w}\right) $, or whether there are some states, say in $\sigma
\left( w\right) $, of which only $L$ is aware (or some states in $\bar{\sigma%
}\left( \bar{w}\right) $, of which only $\bar{L}$ is aware). The next
example illustrates this:

\begin{example}
\label{example-inner-but-not outer}Consider an objective state space $%
Q=\left\{ q_{1},q_{2}\right\} $, subjective states spaces $W=\left\{
w\right\} $ and $\bar{W}=\left\{ \bar{w}\right\} $, and two pairs of
mappings, $\left( \sigma ,\bar{\sigma}\right) $ and $\left( \sigma ^{\prime
},\bar{\sigma}^{\prime }\right) $. Let $\sigma \left( w\right) =\left\{
q_{1}\right\} \subset \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) =\left\{
q_{1},q_{2}\right\} $. Thus, $q_{2}\not\in A$: language $L$ is unaware of $%
q_{2}$. We have $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) =%
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}%
_{\bar{w}}\right) =l_{w}$. Let $\sigma ^{\prime }\left( w\right) =\bar{\sigma%
}^{\prime }\left( \bar{w}\right) =\left\{ q_{1},q_{2}\right\} $. Now both
languages are aware of both $\left\{ q_{1},q_{2}\right\} $. Clearly, we have
again $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) =\bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\right) =l_{w}$. The outer translation operator cannot distinguish between
the case $\left( \sigma ,\bar{\sigma}\right) $, where language $L$ is less
aware than $\bar{L}$, and $\left( \sigma ^{\prime },\bar{\sigma}^{\prime
}\right) $, where awareness is identical and identifies the same joint state
space $\tilde{Q}=\left\{ \tilde{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \right\} $. In
contrast, the inner translation operator distinguishes the two scenarios: $%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l_{w}\right) =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$
and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =f
$ holds with $\left( \sigma ,\bar{\sigma}\right) $, whereas $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{\prime -}\left( l_{w}\right) =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$
and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{\prime -}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\right) =l_{w}$ holds for $\left( \sigma ^{\prime },\bar{\sigma}^{\prime
}\right) $. $\square $
\end{example}

We thus construct the minimal joint state space for which the translation
operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}$ satisfy the definition in (\ref{inner-translation}). We
use inner consistency of translation and Definition \ref%
{definition-dually-relevant-pair}, to identify all dually-relevant pairs $%
\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in W\times \bar{W}$ and add the corresponding
states $\hat{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ to the joint state space $\hat{Q}$%
. $\hat{q}$ is interpreted as the intersection of the two subjective events $%
\sigma \left( w\right) $ and $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) $. However,
in this case, both languages are aware of either all states in $\sigma
\left( w\right) $ or of all states in $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) $.
If for some $w\in W$ $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, i.e., language $L$ is at least
partially unaware of $\sigma \left( w\right) $, we add to $\hat{Q}$ a state $%
\hat{q}=\left( w,\bigcirc \right) $. We proceed symmetrically for $\bar{w}%
\in \bar{W}$. We show that a pair of translation operators between $L$ and $%
\bar{L}$ satisfy (\ref{inner-translation}) with respect to the so-defined
state space $\hat{Q}$ if the properties in III.2. of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-full-characterization} hold.

Similarly to $\tilde{Q}$, $\hat{Q}$ only partially identifies the joint
state space by ignoring states of which both agents are fully unaware and
not distinguishing between contingencies which are indistinguishable in any
language. While $\hat{Q}$ allows us to distinguish between states of which
an agent is partially or fully aware, it cannot identify whether a language
is fully or only partially unaware of a subjective state expressed in the
other language. The following example illustrates this:

\begin{example}
\label{example-outer-but-not-inner}Consider an objective state space $%
Q=\left\{ q_{1},q_{2},q_{3}\right\} $, subjective states spaces $W=\left\{
w_{1}\right\} $ and $\bar{W}=\left\{ \bar{w}_{1}\right\} $, and two pairs of
mappings, $\left( \sigma ,\bar{\sigma}\right) $ and $\left( \sigma ^{\prime
},\bar{\sigma}^{\prime }\right) $. Let $\sigma \left( w_{1}\right) =\left\{
q_{1},q_{3}\right\} $, $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}_{1}\right) =\left\{
q_{2},q_{3}\right\} $. Thus, $q_{2}\not\in A$ and $q_{1}\not\in \bar{A}$.
Language $L$ is only partially aware of the event $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}%
_{1}\right) $. We have $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left(
l_{w_{1}}\right) =\bar{f}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =f$. Next, let $\sigma ^{\prime }\left(
w_{1}\right) =\left\{ q_{1}\right\} $ and $\bar{\sigma}^{\prime }\left( \bar{%
w}\right) =\left\{ q_{2},q_{3}\right\} $. Now $L$ is completely unaware of $%
\bar{\sigma}^{\prime }\left( \bar{w}_{1}\right) =\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}%
_{1}\right) $. Yet, again $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left(
l_{w}\right) =\bar{f}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =f$. The inner translation operator cannot
distinguish these two cases and identifies the same joint state space $\hat{Q%
}=\left\{ \hat{q}=\left( w,\bigcirc \right) ,\hat{q}^{\prime }=\left(
\bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \right\} $. The outer translation operator
distinguishes the two scenarios: $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) =\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =l_{w}$ holds with $\left(
\sigma ,\bar{\sigma}\right) $, whereas $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{\prime +}\left( l_{w}\right) =\bar{f}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{\prime +}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =f$ holds for $%
\left( \sigma ^{\prime },\bar{\sigma}^{\prime }\right) $. $\square $
\end{example}

While each type of translation on its own allows for only a partial
identification of the state space, when both the inner and the outer
translation operators are defined and satisfy restricted duality (III.3),
full identification (up to the maximal joint awareness of the agents) is
possible. The joint state space $Q$ is the union of the two state spaces, $%
\tilde{Q}$ and $\hat{Q}$. It distinguishes between awareness and partial
awareness, as well as between partial awareness and full unawareness of a
subjective state.

The corresponding mappings $\sigma $ and $\bar{\sigma}$ are given by: 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sigma \left( w\right)  &=&\left\{ q\in Q\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) 
\text{ for some }\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\text{ or }q=\left( w,\bigcirc \right)
\right\}  \\
\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right)  &=&\left\{ q\in Q\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \text{ for some }w\in W\text{ or }q=\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right)
\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}%
and extended to general sets using the properties specified in Lemma \ref%
{lemma-joint-state-space-map}. The state space thus collects as possible
states exactly those pairs $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ which were identified
as relevant so that both agents are (at least) partially aware of each of
the two states, as well as the states $\left( w,\bigcirc \right) $ of which $%
L$ is (at least) partially unaware, and symmetrically, the states $\left(
\bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) $ of which $\bar{L}$ is (at least partially)
unaware.

Once the state space has been identified, it is possible to construct the
joint language. In particular, each subset of the joint state space
corresponds to an equivalence class of $\left( \Lambda ^{\ast },\mathcal{L}%
^{\ast }\right) $, i.e., 
\begin{equation*}
L^{\ast }=\left \{ l^{\ast }\mid \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L^{\ast }}\left( l^{\ast
}\right) \in 2^{Q}\right \}
\end{equation*}%
and the implication relation is given by 
\begin{equation*}
l_{1}^{\ast }\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}l_{2}^{\ast }\text{ iff }\mathcal{\hat{B%
}}_{L^{\ast }}\left( l_{1}^{\ast }\right) \subseteq \mathcal{\hat{B}}%
_{L^{\ast }}\left( l_{2}^{\ast }\right)
\end{equation*}

Finally, each proposition in the languages $L$ and $\bar{L}$ can be
identified with an event in the joint state space $Q$, and thus, with a
proposition in $L^{\ast }$. The maps $\pi $ and $\bar{\pi}$ can be defined
as 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\pi \left( l\right) &=&\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L^{\ast }}^{-1}\left( \sigma
\left( b_{l}\right) \right) \\
\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}\right) &=&\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}^{\ast
}}^{-1}\left( \bar{\sigma}\left( b_{\bar{l}}\right) \right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
which trivially satisfies the properties in Definition \ref%
{definition-joint-language}.

\section{A Second Look at Comparative Awareness\label%
{section-comparative-awareness}}

In Corollary \ref{corollary-comparative-unawareness-1}, we showed that in
some special cases, languages can be compared relative to the awareness they
express. The construction of the joint state space presented in the proof of
Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-coarsening} allows us to sharpen
this result. Notably, for languages $L$ and $\bar{L}$, and corresponding
state spaces $W$ and $\bar{W}$, we can use the properties of the translation operators, in particular the notion of relevant pairs
(Definition \ref{definition-relevant-pair}), but also duality (Lemma \ref{lemma-restricted-duality}) and exact preservation of meaning (Lemma \ref{lemma-restricted-preservation-of-meaning}) to order the two languages with
regards to their awareness.

For the rest of this section, assume that $L$ and $\bar{L}$ are languages
with sets of most specific propositions given by $\left \{ l_{w}\right \}
_{w\in W}$ and $\left \{ \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right \} _{\bar{w}\in \bar{W}}$.
Let $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}$, $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T%
}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$ be translation operators between $L$ and $\bar{%
L}$ satisfying condition III in Proposition \ref%
{proposition-full-characterization}. The following four corollaries obtain
directly from the construction of the state space $Q$.

\begin{corollary}
\label{corollary-non-nested-partitions}The languages $L$ and $\bar{L}$ are
two non-nested pure coarsenings of a minimal joint state space iff the inner
and outer translations $\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\text{,}%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\right) $ and $\left( \mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-},\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\right) $
are dual, or equivalently, when $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{-}\left( t\right) =\bar{t}$, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) =t$.
\end{corollary}

The corollary says that if the tautologies in the two languages translate
into each other, the sets of states, $\sigma \left( W\right) =A$ and $\bar{%
\sigma}\left( \bar{W}\right) =\bar{A}$ of which the two agents are aware
coincide with the joint state space $Q$.

\begin{corollary}
\label{corollary-pure-coarsening}The language $\bar{L}$ is a (potentially
trivial) pure coarsening of $L$ iff{}

\begin{itemize}
\item  the inner and outer translations $\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\text{,}%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\right) $ and $\left( \mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-},\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\right) $
are dual, and

\item for every $w\in W$, there is a single $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ such that $%
\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant, and

\item for every $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$, there is at least one $w\in W$ such
that $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant.
\end{itemize}
\end{corollary}

The case of one language being a pure coarsening of the other requires both
languages to be pure coarsenings of the same joint state space, and thus,
tautologies have to be translated into tautologies, as in Corollary \ref%
{corollary-non-nested-partitions}. Recall that each relevant pair $\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) $ corresponds to a state $q\in Q$ in the joint state space.
When there is a single element $\bar{w}$ which "intersects" $w$, but
multiple $w$ that \textquotedblleft intersect\textquotedblright\ the same
element $\bar{w}$, $\left( \sigma \left( w\right) \right) _{w\in W}$
generates a partition of each of the states $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}%
\right) $. Then the subjective state space $\bar{W}$ can be interpreted as a
pure coarsening of $W$.

We can relate pure coarsening to the implication defined in the joint
language $L^{\ast }$, $\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}$. Pure coarsening arises
when an agent fails to distinguish between events that are in fact
different. Suppose that the tautologies in each of the languages are mapped
to the joint language tautology, $\pi \left( t\right) =\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{t%
}\right) =t^{\ast }$, and, furthermore, for each $l_{w}\in L$, there is
exactly one $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$ which is not incompatible with it in the
joint language:$\ \pi \left( l_{w}\right) \not \Rightarrow \lnot \bar{\pi}%
\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $. However, for some $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$
there may be more than one $l_{w}$ s.t. $\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\right) \not \Rightarrow \lnot \pi \left( l_{w}\right) $. Thus, $\bar{L}$
is a pure coarsening of $L$.

\begin{corollary}
\label{corollary-pure-restriction}The language $\bar{L}$ is a (potentially
trivial) pure restriction of $L$ iff:

\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right) =\bar{t}$, $%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) \Rightarrow t$,
and

\item  $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$, 
$\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$, and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ satisfy restricted preservation of meaning when translating between the sets $L^{dp}=\left\{ l \in L \mid l  \Rightarrow \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right)\right\}$ and $\bar{L}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{corollary}

%\begin{corollary}
%\label{corollary-pure-restriction}The language $\bar{L}$ is a (potentially
%trivial) pure restriction of $L$ iff:

%\begin{itemize}
%\item $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right) =\bar{t}$, $%
%\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) \Rightarrow t$,
%and

%\item for every $w\in W$, there is either a single $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ s.t. 
%$\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant, and $\left( w,\bigcirc \right)
%\not\in Q$, or there is no $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ s.t. $\left( w,\bar{w}%
%\right) $ is relevant, and

%\item for every $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$, there is a single $w\in W$ s.t. $%
%\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant.
%\end{itemize}
%\end{corollary}

Pure restriction manifests itself in the fact that the tautology in the
"less restricted, more aware" language $L$ is translated into the tautology
of the ``more restricted and less aware'' language $\bar{L}$. However, the
translation of the tautology in $\bar{L}$ only implies, but is not in
general implied, by the tautology in $L$, because the latter considers more
states. The second condition ensures that there is a one-to-one correspondence between 
