\begin{corollary}
\label{corollary-coarsening-and-restriction}The language $\bar{L}$ is a
combination of a (potentially trivial) coarsening and a (potentially trivial) restriction of $L$ iff:

\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right) =\bar{t}$, $%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) \Rightarrow t$,
and

\item for every $w\in W$, either  $l_{w} \Rightarrow \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right)$ and there is a single $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ s.t. $\left( w,\bar{w}\right)$ is relevant, or there is no $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ s.t. $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is
relevant, and

\item for every $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$, there is at least one $w\in W$ s.t. $%
\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant.
\end{itemize}
\end{corollary}

Corollary \ref{corollary-coarsening-and-restriction} combines the conditions
of Corollaries \ref{corollary-pure-coarsening} and \ref{corollary-pure-restriction} by requiring that the language $\bar{L}$ is either implicitly aware of each subjective state $w$, and in this case, has a single most specific proposition that is implied by $l_{w}$, or is fully unaware of it. It characterizes the more general case in which the language $\bar{L}$ is a combination of a coarsening and a restriction of $L$.

\section{Concluding comments}

The interplay between syntactic and semantic representations is of
particular value in considering bounded and differential awareness.

In this paper we studied a syntactic representation of differential
unawareness via different languages. For a given joint language which nests
the languages of the two agents, or, equivalently, for a given joint state
space on which the boundedly aware representations are based, we define and
study translation operators between the two languages. The inner and the
outer translation operators can be seen as ``best approximations'' of the
meaning of a proposition in the target language. This is captured by the
property ``consistency of translation''. While translation is naturally
based on the common meaning (common events) expressed by the propositions,
it does not preserve all logical operations. Yet, the translation operators
preserve contradictions, and whereas the outer translation preserves
disjunctions, the inner translation preserves conjunctions. On the one hand,
these properties demonstrate the limits to meaningful translation between
languages with distinct awareness. On the other, they can be used to
characterize the translation operators and derive the underlying joint
language and the associated joint state space.

The axiomatization of the translation operators provided here has several
advantages. First, it suggests a method for eliciting a joint language, and
in particular, the implication relation between the propositions used by the
agents. Second, and equivalently, it can be used to elicit a minimal joint
state space. Third, the explicit construction of the state space allows for
a comparison between the awareness (with respect to coarsening or
restriction) of the individuals.

The dominant approach in the literature has been to model bounded awareness
in terms of the state space, often with an informal syntactic motivation. By
formalizing the link between syntax and semantics we obtain sharper and
clearer results.

The approach taken here paves the way for further applications: the
translation operator can be used in situations of information acquisition
under differential awareness. A companion paper studies Bayesian updating as
well as increases in awareness as a result of information provided by a
source with a different level of awareness. The framework can also be used
to study epistemic structures in the presence of differential awareness.

The conditions we impose imply an equivalence between the existence of a
joint language and the existence of a joint state space. This is due to two
assumptions: first: translation between languages is always well-defined;
second: the inner and outer translations satisfy restricted. When
these assumptions are relaxed, the equivalence between a joint language and
a joint state space need no longer hold. We study this issue in a companion
paper.

