or 
\begin{equation*}
\bar{l}\wedge \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l\right) \not=%
\bar{f}
\end{equation*}

A symmetric argument shows necessity.

To show that the inner translation operator satisfies inner consistency of
translation, consider case $\left( i\right) $, i.e., $f\not=l\in L$ and $%
\bar{f}\not=\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$ such that $l\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ and $l\not \Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}\right) $.

By the characterization of the inner translation in (\ref{inner-translation}%
), and using (\ref{inner-event}), we have that 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) =\vee _{\left \{
w^{\prime }\in W\mid \sigma \left( w^{\prime }\right) \subseteq \bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{W}\right) \right \} }l_{w^{\prime }}
\end{equation*}%
and thus, the assumption $f\not=l\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
