We prove Proposition \ref{proposition-full-characterization} in several
steps. First, we show that I implies II, which in turn implies III.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-I-II-III}Consider statements I, II and III of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-full-characterization}. I$\Rightarrow $II$\Rightarrow $III.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-I-II-III}}}:

That I implies II follows from Lemma \ref{lemma-joint-state-space-map}. That
II implies III follows from Lemmata \ref{lemma-restricted-duality}, \ref%
{lemma-preservation-contradiction}, \ref{lemma-consistency-translation}, and %
\ref{lemma-strong-preservation-meaning-disjunction}.

We next show that III implies II. To do so, we proceed in three steps. We
first focus on the outer translation operator and show that preservation of
contradictions and disjunctions, as well as outer consistency of translation
are indeed sufficient for the existence of a joint underlying state space,
Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-coarsening}. Next, we consider the
inner translation operator and show that preservation of contradictions and
conjunctions, as well as inner consistency of translation are sufficient for
the existence of a joint underlying state space, Proposition \ref%
{proposition-equivalence-dual}. However, the two underlying state spaces are
in general distinct. Proposition \ref{proposition-common-state-space}
combines the so-constructed state spaces and shows that their union
corresponds to a joint state space, which satisfies condition II of
Proposition \ref{proposition-full-characterization}.

\begin{proposition}
\label{proposition-equivalence-coarsening}Let $L$ and $\bar{L}$ be languages
with sets of most specific propositions given by $\left \{ l_{w}\right \}
_{w\in W}$ and $\left \{ \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right \} _{\bar{w}\in \bar{W}}$.
Let $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}$ be translation operators between $L$ and $\bar{L}$. If
the operators satisfy:

\begin{description}
\item[$\left( i\right) $] preservation of logical contradictions;

\item[$\left( ii\right) $] outer consistency of translation, and

\item[$\left( iii\right) $] preservation of disjunction
\end{description}

then there exists a joint state space $\tilde{Q}$ for which the translation
operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}$ satisfy (\ref{outer-translation}) and are thus outer
translations.
\end{proposition}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-equivalence-coarsening}}}:

The proof is by construction.

Consider an arbitrary tuple $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in W\times \bar{W}$.
If the pair $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant (Definition \ref%
{definition-relevant-pair}), we identify it with a state $\tilde{q}=\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) $.

If for some $w\in W$ there is no $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ with $\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) $ being relevant, we define a state $\tilde{q}=\left( w,\bigcirc
\right) $. Similarly, if for some $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$ there is no $w\in W$
with $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ being relevant, we define a state $\tilde{q}%
=\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) $.

Let the state space $\tilde{Q}$ be defined as: 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{Q} &=&\left \{ \tilde{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \mid \left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \in W\times \bar{W}\text{ s.t. }\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ is
relevant}\right \} \cup \\
&&\left \{ \tilde{q}=\left( w,\bigcirc \right) \mid w\in W\text{ s.t. }%
\nexists \text{ }\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\text{ s.t. }\left( w,\bar{w}\right) 
\text{ is relevant}\right \} \cup \\
&&\left \{ \tilde{q}=\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \mid \bar{w}\in \bar{W}%
\text{ s.t. }\nexists \text{ }w\in W\text{ s.t. }\left( w,\bar{w}\right) 
\text{ is relevant}\right \}
\end{eqnarray*}%
and set the algebra of events to be the power set of $\tilde{Q}$, $2^{\tilde{%
Q}}$

For each $w\in W$, we can define the corresponding set $\sigma _{\tilde{Q}%
}\left( w\right) \subseteq \tilde{Q}$:%
\begin{equation*}
\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) =\left \{ \tilde{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) _{\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} }\mid \tilde{q}%
\in \tilde{Q}\right \} \subseteq \tilde{Q}
\end{equation*}%
and similarly, for $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$, the set $\bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}%
}\left( \bar{w}\right) $ is:%
\begin{equation*}
\bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) =\left \{ \tilde{q}=\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) _{w\in W\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} }\mid \tilde{q}\in 
\tilde{Q}\right \} \subseteq \tilde{Q}
\end{equation*}%
We note that $\bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) \cap \bar{\sigma}%
\left( \bar{w}\right) =\left \{ \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \right \} $ whenever 
$\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant and $\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left(
w\right) \cap \bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) =\varnothing $,
else. We obtain: 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( W\right) &=&\left \{ \tilde{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) _{\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} }\mid w\in W%
\text{, }\tilde{q}\in \tilde{Q}\right \} \\
\bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{W}\right) &=&\left \{ \tilde{q}=\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) _{\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} }\mid 
\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\text{, }\tilde{q}\in \tilde{Q}\right \}
\end{eqnarray*}%
Finally, extend the functions $\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}$ and $\bar{\sigma}_{%
\tilde{Q}}$ to all events in $2^{\tilde{Q}}$ using the properties of
preservation of unions: $\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( E\cup E^{\prime }\right)
=\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( E\right) \cup \sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( E^{\prime
}\right) $, preservation of intersections:$\ \sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( E\cap
E^{\prime }\right) =\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( E\right) \cap \sigma _{\tilde{Q%
}}\left( E^{\prime }\right) $, and preservation of the empty set:$\ \sigma _{%
\tilde{Q}}\left( \varnothing \right) =\varnothing $, and symmetrically for $%
\bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}$, as stated in Lemma \ref%
{lemma-joint-state-space-map}. Denote by $\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$, $\mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$, $%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$ the outer event mappings and the outer
translations defined with respect to $\tilde{Q}$.

We now have to show that the translation operators $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$
satisfy (\ref{outer-translation-implication}) on $\tilde{Q}$, and thus
coincide with $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$, $\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$. Consider the most specific
propositions $l_{w}$ ($\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$) corresponding to states $w$ ($%
\bar{w}$) within a given language $L$ ($\bar{L}$). Using the notation
introduced above for $\tilde{Q}$, and recalling that the outer event can be
written as in (\ref{outer-event}) we have to show that 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) =\vee _{\left \{ 
\bar{w}\mid \bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) \cap \sigma _{%
\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) \not=\varnothing \right \} }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}
\end{equation*}

By our definition of relevant pairs $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $, $\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant iff 
\begin{equation*}
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right)
\end{equation*}%
If $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is not relevant we must therefore have $\bar{l}%
_{\bar{w}}\not \Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) $ and thus, $\bar{w}\not \in \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{%
\bar{L}}\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right)
\right) $. It follows that 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}}\left( \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) \right) &=&\left \{ \bar{w}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \text{ - relevant}\right \} = \\
&=&\left \{ \bar{w}\mid \bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) \cap
\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) \not=\varnothing \right \}
\end{eqnarray*}%
We thus conclude that 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) =\vee _{\left \{ 
\bar{w}\mid \bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) \cap \sigma _{%
\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) \not=\varnothing \right \} }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}=%
\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,%
\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) \right) =\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,%
\tilde{Q}}\left( l_{w}\right)
\end{equation*}%
as required.

Next, consider an arbitrary proposition $l\in L$ and the corresponding set 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l\right) =\left \{ w\mid l_{w\Rightarrow
L}l\right \}
\end{equation*}%
Thus $l=\vee _{w\in \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l\right) }l_{w}$. By
preservation of disjunction, we have that:%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l\right) &=&\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( \vee _{w\in \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L}\left(
l\right) }l_{w}\right) =\vee _{_{w\in \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l\right) }}%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) \\
&=&\vee _{w\in \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l\right) }\vee _{\left \{ \bar{w}%
\mid \bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) \cap \sigma _{\tilde{Q}%
}\left( w\right) \not=\varnothing \right \} }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}} \\
&=&\vee _{\left \{ \bar{w}\mid \bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right)
\cap \sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l\right) \right)
\not=\varnothing \right \} }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}=\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}%
}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}\left( \mathcal{%
\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l\right) \right) \right) =\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L%
}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}\left( l\right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
as per Lemma \ref{lemma-translation-in-states}.

Now consider the case in which for some $w$, there is no $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$
such that $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant. Take the corresponding
state $\left( w,\bigcirc \right) $ and note that by definition, $\sigma _{%
\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) \cap \bar{A}=\sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right)
\cap \sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( W\right) =\varnothing $. By the definition of
relevant pairs, we have that there is no $\bar{w}$ such that:%
\begin{equation*}
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right)
\end{equation*}%
or $\bar{w}\not \in \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}}\left( \mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) \right) $ for any $\bar{w}\in 
\bar{W}$. Since the translation is a proposition in $\bar{L}$, we conclude
that the translation must be the contradiction, 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) =\mathcal{\hat{B}}%
^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}\left( \mathcal{%
\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l_{w}\right) \right) \right) =\mathcal{\hat{B}}%
^{-1}\left( \mathcal{\varnothing }\right) =\bar{f}=\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}\left( l_{w}\right)
\end{equation*}%
as required.

It remains to consider the case of a self-contradiction in $L$, $f$. By
preservation of contradictions, the operator translates it to $\bar{f}$ as
required. We conclude that $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}=\mathcal{T%
}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$ is indeed the outer translation for
the state space $\tilde{Q}$.

As the argument for $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ is symmetric,
this completes the proof. $\square $

\begin{proposition}
\label{proposition-equivalence-dual}Let $L$ and $\bar{L}$ be languages with
sets of most specific propositions given by $\left \{ l_{w}\right \} _{w\in
W}$ and $\left \{ \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right \} _{\bar{w}\in \bar{W}}$. Let $%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}$ be translation operators between $L$ and $\bar{L}$. If the operators
satisfy:

\begin{description}
\item[$\left( i\right) $] preservation of logical contradictions;

\item[$\left( ii\right) $] inner consistency of translation, and

\item[$\left( iii\right) $] preservation of conjunction
\end{description}

then there exists a joint state space $\hat{Q}$ for which the translation
operators satisfy (\ref{inner-translation}) and are thus inner translations.
\end{proposition}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual} }}%
:

Again, the proof is by construction.

Consider an arbitrary tuple $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in W\times \bar{W}$.
If the pair $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is dually relevant (Definition \ref%
{definition-dually-relevant-pair}), we identify it with a state $\hat{q}%
=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $.

If for some $w\in W$, $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, define a state $\hat{q}=\left(
w,\bigcirc \right) $. Similarly, if for some $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$, $\bar{l}%
_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left(
t\right) $, define a state $\hat{q}=\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) $.

Let the state space $\hat{Q}$ be defined as: 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{Q} &=&\left \{ \hat{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \mid \left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \in W\times \bar{W}\text{ s.t. }\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ is
dually-relevant}\right \} \cup \\
&&\left \{ \hat{q}=\left( w,\bigcirc \right) \mid w\in W\text{ s.t. }%
l_{w}\not \Rightarrow \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}%
\right) \right \} \cup \\
&&\left \{ \hat{q}=\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \mid \bar{w}\in \bar{W}%
\text{ s.t. }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\not \Rightarrow \mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right) \right \}
\end{eqnarray*}

For each $w\in W$, we can define the corresponding set $\sigma _{\hat{Q}%
}\left( w\right) \subseteq \hat{Q}$:%
\begin{equation*}
\sigma _{\hat{Q}}\left( w\right) =\left \{ \hat{q}=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) _{%
\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} }\mid \hat{q}\in \hat{Q}%
\right \} \subseteq \hat{Q}
\end{equation*}%
and similarly, for $\bar{w}\in \bar{W}$, the set $\bar{\sigma}_{\hat{Q}%
}\left( \bar{w}\right) $ is:%
\begin{equation*}
\bar{\sigma}_{\hat{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) =\left \{ \hat{q}=\left( w,\bar{w%
}\right) _{w\in W\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} }\mid \hat{q}\in \hat{Q}%
\right \} \subseteq \hat{Q}
\end{equation*}%
Extend the mapping to all events in $2^{\hat{Q}}$ as in the proof of
proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-coarsening}. Denote by $\mathcal{E}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,\hat{Q}}$, $\mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,%
\hat{Q}}$, $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,\hat{Q}}$, $\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,\hat{Q}}$ the inner event mappings and the inner
translations defined with respect to $\hat{Q}$.

We start by proving several simple facts. For Lemmata \ref{lemma-1-duality}
to \ref{lemma-8-duality} below, let $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$
and $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ be translation operators
satisfying the properties of Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual}.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-1-duality}If $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $, then $l_{w}\not
\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-1-duality}}}: By preservation
of conjunction $\left( iii\right) $, 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\wedge \lnot 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l%
}_{\bar{w}}\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right)
\end{equation*}%
By preservation of contradictions, $\left( i \right) $, we obtain: 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\wedge \lnot 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{f%
}\right) =f
\end{equation*}%
Combining the two: 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) \wedge 
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right)
=f
\end{equation*}%
Thus, if $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $, then $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-2-duality}If $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $, then \thinspace $%
l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l%
}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) $ for any $\bar{w}^{\prime }\not=\bar{w}$.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-2-duality}}}:

By preservation of contradictions $\left( i\right) $ and
preservation of conjunction $\left( iii\right) $, 
\begin{equation*}
f=\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\wedge \bar{l%
}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right)
\end{equation*}%
Thus, if $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $, we must have $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{%
T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) $.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-3-duality}Let $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $. If for a given $w$, $\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) $ is the unique dually-relevant state pair, then $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-3-duality}}}:

Since $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is dually-relevant, we have $l_{w}\not
\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $. Since $\left( w,\bar{w}^{\prime }\right) $ is not
dually-relevant for any $\bar{w}^{\prime }\not=\bar{w}$ and since $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}%
\right) $, it must be that $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) $ for all $%
\bar{w}^{\prime }\not=\bar{w}$. By preservation of conjunction, we have:%
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\wedge _{\bar{w}^{\prime }\not=\bar{w}}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) =%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \wedge _{\bar{w}^{\prime }\not=%
\bar{w}}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) \right) =\mathcal{T}%
_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right)
\end{equation*}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-4-duality}%
\begin{equation*}
\vee _{\left \{ _{\substack{ w\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ is the
unique dually-relevant state pair for }w  \\ \text{and }l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) }}\right \}
}l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right)
\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-4-duality}}}:

Follows by combining the results of Lemmata \ref{lemma-2-duality} and \ref%
{lemma-3-duality}.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-5-duality}If $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}\right) $ then for any $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}$
such that $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\bar{l}$, $l_{w}\not
\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\right) $. In particular, $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ implies $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}\right) $ for any $\bar{l%
}\in \bar{L}\backslash \left \{ \bar{f}\right \} $.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-5-duality}}}:

Assume to the contrary that $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}\right) $, $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{%
\bar{L}}\bar{l}$ and $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $. By preservation of conjunction, 
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w%
}}\wedge \bar{l}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}%
\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\right)
\end{equation*}%
a contradiction.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-6-duality}If $\left( w,\bar{w}_{1}\right) $, $\left( w,\bar{w}%
_{2}\right) $ are dually-relevant with $\bar{w}_{1}\not=\bar{w}_{2}$, then $%
l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l%
}_{\bar{w}_{1}}\right) $ and $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\right) $. Furthermore, if $%
\bar{E}\subset \left \{ \bar{w}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}%
\right \} $, then $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{E}}\right) $. Thus,%
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =\vee
_{\left \{ _{\substack{ w\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ is the unique
dually-relevant state pair for }w  \\ \text{and }l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) }}\right \}
}l_{w}\text{.}
\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-6-duality}}}:

Suppose first, in a manner of contradiction, that $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{1}}\right) $.
Then by Lemma \ref{lemma-2-duality}, $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\right) $. But we have
by dual-relevance that $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\right) $. Thus, 
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{1}}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\wedge \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{1}}\right) =%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{1}}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
a contradiction. Thus, $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{1}}\right) $. A symmetric
argument shows that $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{2}}\right) $. It follows that if
a given $w$ corresponds to more than one dually-relevant pair $\left( w,\bar{%
w}\right) $, it will not be contained in the translation of any of these
dually-relevant contingencies.

Now consider $\bar{E}\subset \left \{ \bar{w}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right)
\in \hat{Q}\right \} $ and suppose in a manner of contradiction that $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{E}}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \vee _{\bar{w}%
^{\prime }\in \bar{E}}\bar{l}\right) $. Let $\bar{w}\in \left \{ \bar{w}%
^{\prime }\mid \left( w,\bar{w}^{\prime }\right) \in \hat{Q}\right \}
\backslash \bar{E}$. By the definition of dual-relevance, $l_{w}\not
\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $. However, by preservation of conjunction,%
\begin{eqnarray*}
l_{w} &\Rightarrow &_{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}%
_{\bar{E}}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \vee _{\bar{w%
}^{\prime }\in \bar{E}}\bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{%
L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \left( \vee _{\bar{w}^{\prime }\in \bar{E}}\bar{l}%
_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) \wedge \left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right)
\right) \\
&=&\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \left( \vee _{\bar{w}%
^{\prime }\in \bar{E}}\bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) \right) \wedge 
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\right) \right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
a contradiction.

We now show that if $w$ is such that $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is not
dually-relevant, then $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $. Consider two cases:
first, if $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, the statement is implied by Lemma \ref%
{lemma-5-duality}. Second, suppose $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $. Since $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $
is not dually relevant, we have $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $. Thus, by Lemma %
\ref{lemma-2-duality}, $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $.

Combining this with the result of Lemma \ref{lemma-4-duality}, we obtain:%
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) =\vee
_{\left \{ _{\substack{ w\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ is the unique
dually-relevant state pair for }w  \\ \text{and }l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) }}\right \}
}l_{w}\text{.}
\end{equation*}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-7-duality}Let $w$ satisfy $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ and belong to at least two
distinct dually-relevant pairs. Then $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{%
L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \vee _{\left\{ \bar{w}\in \bar{W}\mid \left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right\} }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-7-duality}}}:

By dual-relevance, $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}_{k}}\right) $ for all $\bar{w}_{k}$ such
that $\left( w,\bar{w}_{k}\right) \in \hat{Q}$. If $\bar{W}=\left\{ \bar{w}%
\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right\} $, then, since $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}%
\right) $, we have that 
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \vee
_{\left\{ \bar{w}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right\} }\bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
Else, there is a $\bar{w}^{\prime }$ s.t. $\left( w,\bar{w}^{\prime }\right)
\not\in \hat{Q}$ with $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) $. By preservation of
conjunctions, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
l_{w} &\Rightarrow &_{L}\wedge _{\left\{ \bar{w}^{\prime }\mid \bar{w}%
^{\prime }\not\in \left\{ \bar{w}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}%
\right\} \right\} }\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}%
_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left(
\wedge _{\left\{ \bar{w}^{\prime }\mid \bar{w}^{\prime }\not\in \left\{ \bar{%
w}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right\} \right\} }\left( \lnot 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}^{\prime }}\right) \right)  \\
&=&\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \vee _{\left\{ \bar{w}\mid
\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right\} }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) 
\end{eqnarray*}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-8-duality}Let $\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$. Then $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}\right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-,\hat{Q}}\left( \bar{l}\right) $ where $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-,\hat{Q}}\left( \bar{l}\right) $ is the inner translation operator
defined in (\ref{inner-translation}) with respect to the joint state-space $%
\hat{Q}$.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-8-duality}}}:

From Lemma \ref{lemma-7-duality}, $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \vee _{\left \{ \bar{w}\in \bar{W}\mid \left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right \} }\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $, whereas
from the proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-6-duality}, $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \vee _{\bar{w}\in \bar{E}}\bar{l%
}_{\bar{w}}\right) $ for every $\bar{E}\subset \left \{ \bar{w}\in \bar{W}%
\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right \} $. It follows that for any 
$\bar{l}\in \bar{L}$ which corresponds to $\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}}\left( 
\bar{l}\right) $, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}\right) &=&\vee _{\left
\{ w\in W\mid \left \{ \bar{w}\in \bar{W}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in 
\hat{Q}\right \} \subseteq \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}}\left( \bar{l}\right)
\right \} }l_{w}=\vee _{\left \{ w\in W\mid \sigma _{\hat{Q}}\left( w\right)
\subseteq \bar{\sigma}_{\hat{Q}}\left( \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}}\left( 
\bar{l}\right) \right) \right \} }l_{w} \\
&=&\mathcal{B}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,\hat{Q}%
}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \bar{l}\right) \right) \right) =\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L%
}\rightarrow L}^{-,\hat{Q}}\left( \bar{l}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
Thus, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{l}\right) $
coincides with the inner translation defined with respect to $\hat{Q}$.


A symmetric argument shows that $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{-}\left( l\right) $ is also the inner translation with respect to $\hat{Q}
$. 
Lemma \ref{lemma-8-duality} completes the proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual}.

We now show that restricted duality in property III. 3. of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-full-characterization} implies that the state spaces $\tilde{Q}$
and $\hat{Q}$ determined in propositions \ref%
{proposition-equivalence-coarsening} and \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual}
are related in a specific way.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-representation-full-1}If the operators $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$,
as well as the operators $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ and $%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$ satisfy properties III. 1., 2. and
3., then they satisfy:
\end{lemma}

\begin{description}
\item[a)] if $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, then $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $ iff $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $ (and symmetrically for $\bar{L}$);

\item[b)] if $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, then $l_{w}\not \Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $;

\item[c)] if $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\not \Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( t\right) $, then $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\not
\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right) $%
.
\end{description}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-representation-full-2}The state space $\tilde{Q}$
determined in the proof of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-equivalence-coarsening} for the outer operators $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$
and the state space $\hat{Q}$ determined in the proof of proposition \ref%
{proposition-equivalence-dual} for the inner operators $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$
satisfy:

\begin{description}
\item[a')] if $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$, then $\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \in \tilde{Q}$; if $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \tilde{Q}$, then
either $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$, or $\left( w,\bigcirc \right)
\in \hat{Q}$ and $\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$;

\item[b')] if $\left( w,\bigcirc \right) \in \tilde{Q}$, then $\left(
w,\bigcirc \right) \in \hat{Q}$;

\item[c')] if $\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \in \tilde{Q}$, then $\left(
\bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$.
\end{description}
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemmata \ref{lemma-representation-full-1} and %
\ref{lemma-representation-full-2}}}:

To prove Lemma\textbf{\ }\ref{lemma-representation-full-1} a), note that by
restricted duality, we have:%
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right)
=\lnot \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right)
\wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
Thus, if, as assumed, $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, then $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $ holds iff 
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\lnot \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( 
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
and thus, (since $l_{w}$ is one of the most specific propositions, and
therefore, for any event $E\in \mathcal{B}\left( L\right) $ either $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}l_{E}$ or $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\lnot l_{E}$), iff%
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
completing the prove of a).

To show Lemma \ref{lemma-representation-full-2} a'), suppose that $\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$. There are two cases: either $l_{w}\Rightarrow
_{L}T_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ and $%
l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}T_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $, which by Lemma \ref{lemma-representation-full-1} a)
implies $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}T_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{%
\bar{w}}\right) $, and thus, $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \tilde{Q}$; or $%
\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}T_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left(
t\right) $ and $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\not\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}T_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}^{-}\left( \lnot l_{w}\right) $, which by a) (after exchanging the
places of $l$ and $\bar{l}$) implies $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}%
}T_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) $, and thus, $\left( w,\bar{%
w}\right) \in \tilde{Q}$. We thus obtain the first part of a'). Conversely,
assume that $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \tilde{Q}$, and thus, $%
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}T_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\right) $ and $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}T_{L\rightarrow \bar{L%
}}^{+}\left( l_{w}\right) $. If $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}T_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, then by a), we have $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow
_{L}T_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\right) $, and
thus, $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$. If $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}T_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( t\right) $, then
by a) (after exchanging $l$ by $\bar{l}$) implies $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}%
}\not\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}T_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( \lnot
l_{w}\right) $, or $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$. Finally, $%
l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}T_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $
and $\bar{l}_{\bar{w}}\not\Rightarrow _{\bar{L}}T_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{-}\left( t\right) $ imply $\left( w,\bigcirc \right) \in \hat{Q}$ and $%
\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$. This proves a').

To prove Lemma \ref{lemma-representation-full-1}\textbf{\ }b), note that by
restricted duality, 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{t}\right) =\lnot 
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{t}\right) \wedge \mathcal{T%
}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
By assumption, $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, and thus, $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\lnot 
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{t}\right) $. Thus, if we
were to assume, in a manner of contradiction that $l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $, we would have
that 
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\lnot \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}\left( \bar{%
t}\right) \wedge \mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) 
\end{equation*}%
and thus, 
\begin{equation*}
l_{w}\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \lnot \bar{%
t}\right) =f
\end{equation*}%
a contradiction. We conclude that $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ and b) obtains. Next note
that the condition in b) is tantamount to there being no $\bar{w}$ such that 
$\left( w,\bar{w}\right) $ is relevant, i.e., $\left( w,\bigcirc \right) \in 
\tilde{Q}$, whereas the conclusion $l_{w}\not\Rightarrow _{L}\mathcal{T}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}\left( \bar{t}\right) $ implies, by the definition
of $\hat{Q}$, $\left( w,\bigcirc \right) \in \hat{Q}$. We thus obtain Lemma %
\ref{lemma-representation-full-2}\textbf{\ }b').

The proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-representation-full-1} c) and Lemma \ref%
{lemma-representation-full-2}\textbf{\ }c') is analogous to that for b) and
b') and thus omitted.

\begin{proposition}
\label{proposition-common-state-space}For the statements II and III of
Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual}, III$\Rightarrow $II.
\end{proposition}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-common-state-space}}%
}:

Again, the proof is by construction. We take the state space $\tilde{Q}$
constructed in the Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-coarsening} and
for which $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+}$ are outer translations, as well as state space $\hat{Q}$
constructed in the proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual},
for which $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-}$ are inner translation operators. The full state space $Q$
is the union of the two: $Q=\tilde{Q}\cup \hat{Q}$.

Define 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sigma \left( w\right) &=&\left \{ q\in Q\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) 
\text{ for some }\bar{w}\in \bar{W}\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} \right \}
\\
\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) &=&\left \{ q\in Q\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \text{ for some }w\in W\cup \left \{ \bigcirc \right \} \right \}
\end{eqnarray*}%
and extend the mappings to all events in $2^{Q}$ as in the proof of
Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-coarsening}. Denote by $\mathcal{E}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}$, $\mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,Q}$, $%
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,Q}$, $\mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+,Q}$ the inner and outer event mappings w.r.t. $Q$, and by $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}$, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,Q}$, $%
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,Q}$, $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+,Q}$ the inner and outer translations w.r.t. $Q$.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-identical-inner-events}The inner events defined with respect to
the joint state space $Q$ exactly coincides with those on $\hat{Q}$ defined
in the proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual}: $\mathcal{E}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}=\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,\hat{Q}}$%
, $\mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,Q}=\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{-,\hat{Q}}$. Thus, $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}=\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,\hat{Q}}=\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$
and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,Q}=\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{-,\hat{Q}}=\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,}$ are
inner translation operators with respect to $Q$.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-identical-inner-events}}}:

The inner event of $E\subseteq W$ in $Q$ given by:%
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}\left( E\right) =\left \{ \bar{w}\in 
\bar{W}\mid \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) \subseteq \sigma \left(
E\right) \right \}
\end{equation*}

Consider the set 
\begin{equation*}
\left \{ \bar{w}\in \bar{W}\mid \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) \subseteq
\sigma \left( E\right) \right \}
\end{equation*}%
Suppose that $\bar{w}$ is such that $\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \in Q$,
and thus, $\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$. Then, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) &=&\left \{ q\in Q\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \text{ for some }w\in W\right \} \cup \left \{ \bigcirc ,\bar{w}%
\right \} \not \subseteq \\
&\not \subseteq &\sigma \left( E\right) =\left \{ q^{\prime }\in Q\mid
q^{\prime }=\left( w^{\prime },\bar{w}^{\prime }\right) \text{ for some }%
w^{\prime }\in E\text{, }\bar{w}^{\prime }\in \bar{W}\cup \bigcirc \right \}
\end{eqnarray*}%
Thus, for all $\bar{w}$ such that $\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q%
}$, $\bar{w}\not \in \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( E\right) $%
. Conversely, suppose that for some $\bar{w}$, $\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}%
\right) \not \in \hat{Q}$, and thus, by property c') in Lemma \ref%
{lemma-representation-full-1}, $\left( \bigcirc ,\bar{w}\right) \not \in Q$.
Then, by property a') in Lemma \ref{lemma-representation-full-1}, $\left( w,%
\bar{w}\right) \in Q$ iff $\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}$. Thus, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) &=&\left \{ q\in Q\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}%
\right) \text{ for some }w\in W\right \} \\
&=&\left \{ q\in \hat{Q}\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ for some }w\in
W\right \}
\end{eqnarray*}

Next note that $q\in \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) $ implies $%
q\not=\left( w,\bigcirc \right) $. Therefore, $\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}%
\right) \subseteq \sigma \left( E\right) $ iff 
\begin{equation*}
\bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right) \subseteq \left \{ q^{\prime }\in Q\mid
q^{\prime }=\left( w^{\prime },\bar{w}^{\prime }\right) \text{ for some }%
w^{\prime }\in E\text{, }\bar{w}^{\prime }\in \bar{W}\right \}
\end{equation*}

Thus, $\bar{w}\in \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}\left( E\right) $
iff 
\begin{gather*}
\left \{ q\in \hat{Q}\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ for some }w\in
W\right \} \subseteq \\
\subseteq \left \{ q^{\prime }\in Q\mid q^{\prime }=\left( w^{\prime },\bar{w%
}^{\prime }\right) \text{ for some }w^{\prime }\in E\text{, }\bar{w}^{\prime
}\in \bar{W}\right \}
\end{gather*}%
iff%
\begin{gather*}
\left \{ q\in \hat{Q}\mid q=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \text{ for some }w\in
W\right \} \subseteq \\
\subseteq \left \{ q^{\prime }\in Q\mid q^{\prime }=\left( w^{\prime },\bar{w%
}\right) \text{ for some }w^{\prime }\in E\right \} \\
=\left \{ q^{\prime }\in \hat{Q}\mid q^{\prime }=\left( w^{\prime },\bar{w}%
\right) \text{ for some }w^{\prime }\in E\right \}
\end{gather*}%
so that $\bar{w}\in \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}\left( E\right) $
iff%
\begin{equation*}
\left \{ w\in W\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}\right \} \subseteq E
\end{equation*}%
Thus, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}\left( E\right) &=&\left \{ \bar{w}%
\in \bar{W}\mid \left \{ w\in W\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \hat{Q}%
\right \} \subseteq E\right \} \\
&=&\left \{ w\in W\mid \sigma _{\hat{Q}}\left( \bar{w}\right) \subseteq \bar{%
\sigma}_{\hat{Q}}\left( E\right) \right \} =\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{-,\hat{Q}}\left( E\right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
and, by Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-dual}, the inner translation operator satisfies 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}\left( l\right) =\mathcal{B}%
^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,\hat{Q}}\left( \mathcal{B}%
\left( \bar{l}\right) \right) \right) =\mathcal{B}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{%
\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-,Q}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \bar{l}\right) \right)
\right) =\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-,Q}\left( l\right)
\end{equation*}%
as desired. A symmetric argument applies to $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{-}$.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma-identical-outer-events}The outer events defined with respect to
the joint state space $Q$ exactly coincide with those on $\tilde{Q}$ defined
in the proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-coarsening}: $%
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,Q}=\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$, $\mathcal{E}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+,Q}=\mathcal{E}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}$. Thus, $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow 
\bar{L}}^{+,Q}=\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}=\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+,Q}=%
\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+,\tilde{Q}}=\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}%
\rightarrow L}^{+,}$ are outer translation operators with respect to $Q$.
\end{lemma}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma-identical-outer-events}}}:

By the definition of an outer event, 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,Q}\left( E\right) =\left \{ \bar{w}\in 
\bar{W}\mid \sigma \left( \bar{w}\right) \cap \bar{\sigma}\left( w\right)
\not=\varnothing \text{ for some }w\in E\right \}
\end{equation*}

By the definition of $Q$, $\sigma $ and $\bar{\sigma}$, we have 
\begin{equation*}
\sigma \left( w\right) \cap \bar{\sigma}\left( \bar{w}\right)
\not=\varnothing \text{ iff}
\end{equation*}%
\begin{equation*}
q=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in Q\text{ iff}
\end{equation*}%
by property a') of Lemma \ref{lemma-representation-full-1}, 
\begin{equation*}
q=\left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \tilde{Q}
\end{equation*}

We then have that the outer event of $E$ in $\bar{L}$ with respect to the
joint state space $Q$ is given by:%
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,Q}\left( E\right) &=&\left \{ \bar{w}%
\in \bar{W}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in Q\text{, }w\in E\right \} \\
&=&\left \{ \bar{w}\in \bar{W}\mid \left( w,\bar{w}\right) \in \tilde{Q}%
\text{, }w\in E\right \} \\
&=&\left \{ \bar{w}\in \bar{W}\mid \bar{\sigma}_{\tilde{Q}}\left( \bar{w}%
\right) \cap \sigma _{\tilde{Q}}\left( w\right) \not=\varnothing \text{ for
some }w\in E\right \} =\mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}%
}\left( E\right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
Thus, by Proposition \ref{proposition-equivalence-coarsening}, the outer translation operator satisfies 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}\left( l\right) =\mathcal{B}_{\bar{L}%
}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,\tilde{Q}}\left( \mathcal{%
\hat{B}}_{L}\left( l\right) \right) \right) =\mathcal{B}_{\bar{L}%
}^{-1}\left( \mathcal{E}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+,Q}\left( \mathcal{\hat{B}}%
_{L}\left( l\right) \right) \right) =\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}%
}^{+,Q}\left( l\right)
\end{equation*}%
as desired. A symmetric argument applies to $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow
L}^{+}$.

Combining the results of Lemmata \ref{lemma-identical-inner-events} and \ref%
{lemma-identical-outer-events} completes the proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-common-state-space}.

\begin{proposition}
\label{proposition-common-language}For the statements I and II of
Proposition \ref{proposition-full-characterization}, II$\Rightarrow $I.
\end{proposition}

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref{proposition-common-language}}}:

By Lemma \ref{lemma-I-II-III} and Proposition \ref%
{proposition-common-state-space}, the existence of a joint state space is
equivalent to the translation operators satisfying statement III of
Proposition \ref{proposition-full-characterization}. Let $Q$ be the joint
state space constructed in the proof of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-common-state-space}. By Lemma \ref{lemma-algebra-to-lang}, for
the algebra $2^{Q}$, we can find an axiomatized language, $\left( \Lambda
^{\ast },\mathcal{L}^{\ast }\right) $ such that its equivalence classes are
given by: 
\begin{equation*}
L^{\ast }=\left \{ l^{\ast }\mid \mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L^{\ast }}\left( l^{\ast
}\right) \in 2^{Q}\right \}
\end{equation*}%
and such that the implication relation is given by 
\begin{equation*}
l_{1}^{\ast }\Rightarrow _{L^{\ast }}l_{2}^{\ast }\text{ iff }\mathcal{\hat{B%
}}_{L^{\ast }}\left( l_{1}^{\ast }\right) \subseteq \mathcal{\hat{B}}%
_{L^{\ast }}\left( l_{2}^{\ast }\right)
\end{equation*}

Each proposition in the languages $L$ and $\bar{L}$ can be identified with
an event in the joint state space $Q$, and thus, with a proposition in $%
L^{\ast }$. The maps $\pi $ and $\bar{\pi}$ can be defined as 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\pi \left( l\right) &=&\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{L^{\ast }}^{-1}\left( \sigma
\left( b_{l}\right) \right) \\
\bar{\pi}\left( \bar{l}\right) &=&\mathcal{\hat{B}}_{\bar{L}^{\ast
}}^{-1}\left( \bar{\sigma}\left( b_{\bar{l}}\right) \right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
which trivially satisfies the properties in Definition \ref%
{definition-joint-language}.

Since we have shown that the translation operators $\mathcal{T}%
_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{-}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{-}$
are inner translations w.r.t. the state space $Q$, by the equivalence of
definitions (\ref{inner-translation-implication}) and (\ref%
{inner-translation}), they are also inner translations with respect to the
joint language $L^{\ast }$. Analogously, since we have shown that the
translation operators $\mathcal{T}_{L\rightarrow \bar{L}}^{+}$ and $\mathcal{%
T}_{\bar{L}\rightarrow L}^{+}$ are outer translations w.r.t. the state space 
$Q$, by the equivalence of definitions (\ref{inner-translation-implication})
and (\ref{inner-translation}), they are also outer translations with respect
to the joint language $L^{\ast }$. We obtain, II$\Rightarrow $I as desired.

\underline{\textbf{Proof of Proposition \ref%
{proposition-full-characterization}}:}

By Lemma \ref{lemma-I-II-III}, we have that I$\Rightarrow $II$\Rightarrow $%
III. By Proposition \ref{proposition-common-state-space}, we have that III$%
\Rightarrow $II. Finally, by Proposition \ref{proposition-common-language},
we know that II$\Rightarrow $I. Combining these, we conclude that I$%
\Leftrightarrow $II$\Leftrightarrow $III as desired.

