To examine normality, we conduct hypothesis testing. The null hypothesis states that the distribution of the capitalization-adjusted Hype Index follows a normal distribution. The specific formulation of the hypothesis depends on the test employed. The following are the p-values and test statistics for various normality tests:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Shapiro-Wilk Test: \( p \approx 1.6 \times 10^{-11} \)
    \item D'Agostino-Pearson Test: \( p \approx 2.8 \times 10^{-22} \)
    \item Jarque-Bera Test: \( p \approx 2.0 \times 10^{-76} \)
    \item Anderson-Darling Test: Test statistic = 4.666, which exceeds the 1\% critical value (1.079)
    \item Kolmogorov-Smirnov Test: \( p = 0.0016 \)
\end{itemize}

All tests indicate a statistically significant deviation from normality. Therefore, the capitalization-adjusted Hype Index is not well-modeled by a normal distribution.


