\shorttitle{Teaching Astronomy with Large Language Models}
\shortauthors{Ting \& O'Briain}

\begin{document}

\title{Teaching Astronomy with Large Language Models}

\author[0000-0001-5082-9536]{Yuan-Sen Ting}
\affiliation{Department of Astronomy, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210, USA}
\affiliation{Center for Cosmology and AstroParticle Physics (CCAPP), The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210, USA}

\author{Teaghan O'Briain}
\affiliation{Department of Astronomy, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH 43210, USA}

\begin{abstract}
We present a study of LLM integration in final-year undergraduate astronomy education, examining how students develop AI literacy through structured guidance and documentation requirements. We developed AstroTutor, a domain-specific astronomy tutoring system enhanced with curated arXiv content, and deployed it alongside general-purpose LLMs in the course. Students documented their AI usage through homework reflections and post-course surveys. We analyzed student evolution in AI interaction strategies and conducted experimental comparisons of LLM-assisted versus traditional grading methods. LLM grading showed strong correlation with human evaluation while providing more detailed and consistent feedback. We also piloted LLM-facilitated interview-based examinations as a scalable alternative to traditional assessments, demonstrating potential for individualized evaluation that addresses common testing limitations. Students experienced decreased rather than increased reliance on LLMs over the semester, developing critical evaluation skills and strategic tool selection. They evolved from basic assistance-seeking to verification workflows, with documentation requirements fostering metacognitive awareness. Students developed effective prompting strategies, contextual enrichment techniques, and cross-verification practices. Our findings suggest that structured LLM integration with transparency requirements and domain-specific tools can enhance astronomy education while building essential AI literacy skills. We provide implementation guidelines for educators and make our AstroTutor repository freely available.
\end{abstract}



\section{Introduction}

Large Language Models are transforming educational landscapes across disciplines, with astronomy education facing both opportunities and challenges. While LLMs demonstrate capabilities in explaining concepts, generating code, and assisting with mathematical derivations \citep{brown2020language, achiam2023gpt}, concerns persist about their potential to undermine critical thinking skills and create dependencies among students \citep{Kasneci2023,Zhai2024,Gerlich2025}.

These concerns are particularly relevant in advanced astronomy courses that are statistics-driven and coding-intensive, where students must develop problem-solving abilities, master computational techniques, and learn to critically evaluate scientific information \citep{Fouesneau2024}. The improvement of LLMs \citep{Wei2022} has rendered classical testing approaches increasingly obsolete for most textbook coding problems, especially at the undergraduate level \citep{Kumar2023,Balalle2025}.

This transformation raises questions about which skills remain valuable to teach and assess, and how educational frameworks should adapt to prepare students for an AI-integrated workforce while maintaining core disciplinary expertise \citep{YukChan2023}. Traditional approaches to AI in education have often been binary—either complete prohibition or unrestricted access. Neither approach adequately prepares students for a future where AI tools are ubiquitous in scientific research and professional practice \citep{Boiko2023, Bran2023, Ramos2024}.

Recent studies in STEM education suggest that the key lies not in whether to integrate AI tools, but in how to implement them strategically to enhance rather than replace human learning processes \citep{Cooper2024,Lehmann2024,Pathak2025}. Several fields have begun exploring structured AI integration \citep{Wu2023, Chen2024, Raihan2024, Wang2024}, with implementations of AI-powered tutoring systems showing that students can access personalized instruction while maintaining learning objectives. Studies have demonstrated that AI-generated personalized hints can reduce student effort in deriving correct solutions \citep{Becker2022,Deng2025}, while meta-learning approaches have achieved feedback precision comparable to human teaching assistants \citep{Kuchemann2023}. However, limited documentation exists for LLM integration specifically in astronomy education, representing a gap in understanding best practices for STEM disciplines that combine theoretical knowledge with computational skills.

Astronomy education presents unique challenges and opportunities in this context. The field requires a blend of theoretical understanding, mathematical proficiency, and computational skills \citep{Borne2009,Feigelson2021}. Students must learn to work with large datasets, implement complex algorithms, and interpret results within the broader context of astrophysical phenomena. These requirements create a testing ground for structured LLM integration, as the discipline naturally demands the critical thinking and verification skills that effective AI usage requires.

We report our findings from integrating LLMs within an advanced astronomical statistics course with 12 students, most preparing for graduate school in astronomy. The course's difficulty level creates naturally self-selecting enrollment, providing a manageable teaching environment while representing a specialized sample of motivated, research-oriented students.

The course structure emphasized problem-solving through four homework assignments and two group projects. The homework assignments applied classical machine learning tools and statistical techniques to simplified versions of real astronomical research problems, with each assignment breaking down complex research questions into manageable, sequential steps that developed critical thinking skills. The two project-based assignments required students to engage in authentic research-like activities, with homework assignments serving as scaffolding for these independent investigations.


\section{AstroTutor}

We developed AstroTutor as a domain-specific tutoring chatbot tailored for astronomy problem-solving. The system was built in December 2024 using Gemini-2.0-Flash as the backbone model\footnote{\url{https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models}}, offering astronomy-specific knowledge at reasonable cost (approximately \$1 per student per month). We upgraded to Gemini-2.5-Flash when it became available near the course's end, though most students had already formed opinions about the tool by then. While stronger models like Gemini-2.5-Pro or Claude-3.7-Sonnet might have provided better responses, the cost difference made Gemini-Flash the practical choice for sustained educational deployment. The source code for AstroTutor is freely available on GitHub\footnote{\url{https://github.com/teaghan/computeastro-tutor}}.

This RAG-based agent  \citep{lewis2020} was constructed using the lecturer's own lecture notes with a ``Socratic'' teaching style, designed to guide students toward solutions through questioning and hints rather than providing direct answers. The pedagogical approach emphasized asking probing questions to help students identify knowledge gaps and develop deeper understanding through guided discovery.

\subsection{Pedagogical Design and Response Framework}

The response style was designed to mirror effective human tutoring practices through several design choices. Rather than providing direct answers, AstroTutor breaks down complex problems into smaller components, asks clarifying questions, and guides students through problem-solving processes step by step. All responses consistently end with questions to encourage continued engagement and self-reflection \citep{Wallin2018}. The system was prompted to avoid being ``overly affirming'' like many commercial LLMs, instead providing constructive feedback and gentle correction when students make errors.

AstroTutor implements active content filtering that prohibits questions outside astrostatistics and machine learning, maintaining focus on course topics and preventing distracting off-topic interactions.

Code assistance follows a tutorial-style presentation approach, alternating between code blocks and explanations rather than providing complete scripts. The system provides code in digestible chunks with accompanying explanations, focusing on concepts and approaches rather than generating entire solutions. This approach emphasizes teaching the ``why'' behind methods, not just the ``how,'' connecting topics to broader themes in astronomy and machine learning.

\subsection{Multi-Agent Architecture}

The system employs a multi-agent architecture comprising several specialized components working in coordination to provide educational support:

\begin{figure*}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{Fig1.pdf}
    \caption{Multi-agent architecture of AstroTutor. The system employs a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) approach with three specialized agents accessing distinct knowledge domains: course materials and lecture notes adapted from the instructor's textbook, trusted reference materials, and a curated database of ArXiv papers from the astro-ph section. All knowledge sources are stored in ChromaDB vector storage for semantic retrieval. The central ReAct (Reasoning + Acting) agent orchestrates responses by exploring available knowledge sources through explicit reasoning steps, following a hierarchical approach that prioritizes course materials, then reference textbooks, and finally inherent LLM knowledge. A dedicated Moderator Agent ensures educational integrity by evaluating all responses against pedagogical guidelines, preventing direct homework solutions, and maintaining pedagogical teaching principles. The system uses Gemini as backbone with real-time monitoring through Papertrail logging, incorporating mechanisms to reduce hallucinations through trusted, domain-specific sources.}
    \label{fig:astrotutor_architecture}
\end{figure*}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RAG Course Material Agent}: The primary retrieval system searches through the lecturer's course materials (lectures, tutorials) adapted from the lecturer's textbook, now released as the online textbook ``Statistical Machine Learning for Astronomy.'' Having trusted, domain-specific sources is important for maintaining accuracy and preventing hallucinations that plague general-purpose models \citep{Alkaissi2023,Huang2023,Towhidul2024}. This agent employs Gemini's embedding-004 model\footnote{\url{https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/embeddings}} to create vector representations of both queries and knowledge base content, enabling semantic matching between student questions and relevant course materials.

\item \textbf{ReAct Agent}: The central orchestrator coordinates between different tools and agents based on student queries. This agent follows a ``Reasoning + Acting'' paradigm, first analyzing the student's question through explicit reasoning steps, determining which tools are needed, executing actions, observing results, and synthesizing responses from multiple sources. The ReAct framework \citep{Yao2022} allows iterative information gathering and ensures thorough responses by systematically exploring available knowledge sources.

\item \textbf{Reference Textbook Agent}: While course materials provide focused coverage of astrostatistics and machine learning techniques, students often require broader theoretical foundations extending beyond immediate class content. Additional RAG systems access Bishop's ``Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning'' \citep{Bishop2006} and other trusted textbooks for advanced theoretical explanations, providing deeper mathematical foundations when students require additional theoretical background.

\item \textbf{Moderator Agent}: A quality control system evaluates all generated responses against predefined pedagogical guidelines using a separate LLM call. The moderator ensures responses maintain appropriate academic standards, use correct mathematical formatting, avoid providing complete homework solutions, and maintain educational integrity by guiding rather than solving.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure*}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Fig2.png}
    \caption{User interface of the AstroTutor system. The interface provides organized access to course materials including lectures, tutorials, and reference textbooks. The main chat interface facilitates pedagogical interactions, offering assistance with course concepts, data analysis and coding support, and paper recommendations for assignments and projects. Students can download chat histories and reset conversations as needed.}
    \label{fig:astrotutor_interface}
\end{figure*}

The system follows a hierarchical approach: first consulting course materials, then auxiliary textbooks, and only using inherent LLM knowledge when relevant information cannot be found in trusted sources. The system utilized a modular configuration approach with YAML files controlling model parameters and separate instruction files defining agent behavior. The ReAct agent was configured with a maximum of 20 iterations and verbose logging for debugging and analysis. Context management was handled through ChromaDB\footnote{\url{https://www.trychroma.com/}} persistent storage, with separate collections for different knowledge domains to prevent cross-contamination and optimize retrieval accuracy. The system included logging through Papertrail\footnote{\url{https://www.papertrail.com/}} for monitoring usage patterns and identifying potential issues in real-time.

\subsection{ArXiv Integration and Knowledge Base Expansion}

Traditional coding assessments might have become obsolete due to LLM capabilities \citep{Finnie-Ansley2022}, leading us to reimagine this upper-level astrostatistics and machine learning course to focus on authentic problem-solving. Previously, such courses typically provided students with predetermined project lists that relied heavily on the lecturer's expertise and limited students to familiar problems. We sought to extend beyond this limitation by enabling students to discover research directions independently.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Paper Recommendation Agent}: A specialized RAG system focused on a curated arXiv database, designed to suggest relevant research papers based on techniques students are studying or wish to apply in their projects. This agent maintains separate vector databases for different technique categories.
\end{itemize}

For supporting group projects, we expanded AstroTutor's knowledge base by leveraging our AstroLLaMA \citep{Pan2024} and AstroSage project \citep{deHaan2025a,deHaan2025b}—a specialized LLM program for astronomical research. We performed Optical Character Recognition (OCR) on approximately 400,000 papers from the astro-ph section of arXiv published through October 2024, extracted summaries, and identified key methodological concepts from each publication. From these concepts, we curated technique-specific subsets containing 500-3,000 papers relevant to individual course techniques (such as linear regression, Principal Component Analysis, or Gaussian Mixture Models), creating vector embeddings using Gemini's text-embedding-004 model for semantic retrieval. 

The system's output format for paper recommendations included paper titles and authors extracted from summary documents, detailed descriptions of relevance to student queries, and direct arXiv links. For example, a PCA applications query would return a curated list of 10-15 papers with explanations of how each paper's methodology related to the student's specific question or project needs, organized by relevance and methodological similarity. This approach provided students with precise summaries and direct links to relevant papers without the hallucinations typically associated with general-purpose LLMs, serving as a research library for exploring potential project ideas through natural language interactions.


\section{LLM Usage}

Throughout the semester, students were informed about AstroTutor and its advantages and limitations, but were not required to use it exclusively. We emphasized the importance of students learning to critically evaluate various LLMs of their choice. Students were encouraged to document their usage of both AstroTutor and general-purpose LLMs for all course assignments through bonus questions at the end of each assignment, with bonus points awarded for thoughtful reflection on their AI interactions. Failed attempts and challenges were particularly welcomed in these reflections. From these self-reports we are able to understand their usage patterns, which are reported here.

Rather than restricting access or providing limited guidance, no specific instructions were provided on how to use these tools, allowing observation of natural patterns of adoption and adaptation. The diversity of tools used illustrates both the rapidly evolving landscape of LLM applications and students' willingness to explore multiple options to find those best suited to their specific needs.

