\textbf{AstroTutor} was used by 80\% of reporting students, making it the second most popular tool after ChatGPT. Students appreciated AstroTutor's domain-specific knowledge but found different use cases for it compared to general-purpose LLMs. Many students developed a pattern of relying on AstroTutor for understanding course material and theoretical concepts while turning to ChatGPT primarily for coding assistance and implementation details. By design, AstroTutor hallucinated much less frequently than general models, but students were still inclined to use general models intermittently because they often provided more direct responses. The pedagogical approach with lengthy, questioning responses from AstroTutor might have discouraged some students, highlighting an ongoing challenge in balancing educational depth with user preference for directness.

\textbf{ChatGPT}\footnote{\url{https://platform.openai.com}} emerged as the dominant tool, used by 90\% of reporting students. This widespread usage demonstrates how critical this study is—turning a blind eye to AI usage is not a viable option. Students referenced various models including standard ChatGPT (GPT-4o) and reasoning models (o1, o3). Many students demonstrated awareness of different capabilities across model versions, with several specifically mentioning using the reasoning-enhanced versions for more complex tasks.

\textbf{Google's Gemini} was used by about 30\% of reporting students, particularly in conjunction with Google Colab. Since students were already familiar with Google Colab from previous courses, integrating Gemini represented a minimal transition. Students appreciated the integrated AI assistant functionality within the Google Colab environment.

\textbf{DeepSeek}\footnote{\url{https://platform.deepseek.com/}} (including v3 \citep{Deepseek2024} and R1 models \citep{Deepseek2025}) was used by 10\% of reporting students, indicating that students were generally aware of cutting-edge tools. Some students encountered access limitations, with several reporting they needed to switch from DeepSeek to alternative LLMs due to server capacity issues, especially during high-demand periods in early 2025.

One notable absence was \textbf{Claude} \footnote{\url{https://console.anthropic.com/}}, which is arguably one of the leading performers in coding assistance \citep{Kevian2024}\footnote{\url{https://www.swebench.com/}}. This highlights that even in a major astronomy program, tool selection often comes from somewhat random information consumption rather than systematic evaluation. While students demonstrated the ability to make reasonable choices, some guided introduction to available tools might have been beneficial, as students may not be aware of all quality options available to them.

\subsection{IDE-Integrated Tools}

Integrated Development Environments (IDEs) are software applications that provide facilities for software development, typically including code editors, debuggers, and build automation tools. We consider integration of LLMs in IDEs to be a critical skill for modern astronomers, as these tools provide more seamless workflows by offering contextual assistance directly within coding environments.

We briefly mentioned IDEs at the beginning of the course, and students observed the lecturer ``live coding'' with Cursor during most tutorials, seeing firsthand how an expert interacts with IDEs to speed up productivity. Most students reported that seeing the lecturer live-coding during lectures was particularly eye-opening. However, students generally showed limited familiarity with modern IDE environments, even within a major astronomy program. This indicates a gap in computational training within the astronomy community that could benefit from more explicit instruction.

\textbf{Google Colab} with its integrated AI assistant (Gemini) was mentioned by about 30\% of students, providing seamless integration with their existing workflow. Students were already familiar with Google Colab from previous courses, making this integration natural.

\textbf{Visual Studio Code}\footnote{\url{https://code.visualstudio.com/}} with GitHub Copilot\footnote{\url{https://copilot.github.com/}} was mentioned by about 30\% of students. Students reported that GitHub Copilot enhanced their coding efficiency by providing contextual suggestions based on existing notebook content, helping them answer questions more rapidly.

\textbf{Cursor AI}\footnote{\url{https://www.cursor.com}} was mentioned by 10\% of students. Cursor has risen considerably in the computer science community. Interestingly, no students knew about this modern tool until it was mentioned in a tutorial session where the lecturer demonstrated it live. Students who adopted it appreciated the in-line generator and chat features, noting how the integrated nature of the tool streamlined their workflow by maintaining awareness of the full coding context.

However, despite seeing it demonstrated live, the subscription costs compared to more readily available options like Google Colab and GitHub Copilot appeared to be a limiting factor, highlighting the importance of resource accessibility. While subscription costs may appear minimal for professional researchers, at the student level they can still constitute a barrier. We thus suggest that institutions or departments invest in such tools, just as most departments now invest in Overleaf and Slack, and recognize their importance. Hopefully this paper will encourage movement in that direction.


