\section{Common Usage Patterns}

Through students' self-reporting in homework assignments and post-course surveys, we observed seven main usage patterns for LLMs (Figure~\ref{fig:usage_patterns}). The frequency reveals that students primarily used LLMs for conceptual understanding and technical problem-solving rather than task completion shortcuts.

\textbf{Understanding Concepts.} Students frequently used LLMs to clarify concepts and problem statements, making this the most common application (70\% usage). This included understanding mathematical formulas, clarifying assignment questions, and connecting theoretical concepts to practical implementations. Since most assignments involved applying techniques to real astronomical research problems, students with different background levels needed varying degrees of context to tackle questions (for example, understanding what Baryon Acoustic Oscillations are). The high usage frequency demonstrates that students valued LLMs primarily as learning tools for comprehension.

\textbf{Debugging.} Debugging was equally dominant (70\% usage), going beyond simple syntax errors to include conceptual issues in algorithm implementation. Matrix operations proved particularly challenging due to their notational subtleties—issues with transposition, dimension matching, broadcasting, and proper matrix multiplication sequence caused difficulties. These linear algebra issues were particularly problematic when working with high-dimensional astronomical data, where matrix shape mismatches could be difficult to diagnose but catastrophic for results. Numerical stability issues presented another challenge where LLMs provided valuable assistance. Common issues included NaN values appearing in loss calculations, overflow errors in exponential functions, and instability in normalization procedures. Students learned important numerical computing principles through these interactions, such as the technique of subtracting the largest logit value when implementing softmax functions to prevent exponential overflow.

\textbf{Translating Math to Code.} Converting mathematical formulas to code was moderately common (60\% usage). These tasks often involve implementation details that can be time-consuming but don't necessarily deepen conceptual understanding. Students sought help translating their understanding of assignment questions into functional code, generating implementations for specific algorithms, or explaining code structure for complex operations. This was particularly evident for algorithms like Principal Component Analysis, Gaussian Mixture Models, and logistic regression. The course encouraged students to build these methods from scratch rather than simply calling functions from libraries like scipy or astropy. Students reported that their experience with manual implementations from tutorials helped them craft better prompts when asking about built-in functions.

\textbf{Data Visualization.} Creating visualizations was another moderate application (60\% usage). Students sought help with advanced techniques like overlaying images on plots, creating 2D density plots, and implementing specialized color maps appropriate for astronomical data. A common visualization challenge involved overlaying images on plots, particularly for tasks like placing thumbnail images on UMAP 2D embeddings to visualize clustering results. Students also requested guidance on creating multi-panel visualizations with subplots, adding custom annotations and labels, and incorporating inset plots to highlight specific features. Students found that LLMs were particularly helpful for these visualization tasks, as they could provide sample code that demonstrated both basic implementation and customization options that improved the clarity and professional appearance of their results.

\textbf{Verifying Completed Work.} A portion of students developed verification practices (40\% usage). After completing problems independently, students presented solutions to LLMs for correctness checks and improvement suggestions. This represented an evolution in AI integration—students leveraged LLMs as validation tools after applying their own understanding rather than for initial solution generation. Students reported this practice helped them gain confidence, identify misunderstandings, and discover alternative approaches.

\textbf{Project Planning.} Moderate usage occurred for project-related tasks (30\%). Open-ended group projects required students to formulate research questions from literature, identify data sources, and develop methodologies. Students employed AstroTutor's curated literature database (500-3,000 arXiv papers per topic) to discover research directions through natural language queries. LLMs helped identify and navigate specialized astronomical databases, with domain knowledge acquisition becoming important for learning concepts like the Tully-Fisher relation or advanced techniques like No U-Turn Sampler for MCMC algorithms.

\textbf{Documentation and Code Organization.} The least common usage involved documentation tasks (10\%). Students requested help generating meaningful comments for complex algorithms and creating structured documentation, improving readability and developing better coding practices. LLMs also assisted with parsing unusual astronomical data formats and technical writing tasks like creating properly formatted references.

