\section{Evolution of LLM Usage}

We were interested in understanding whether students developed useful skills through LLM use. Given that implementing LLMs in daily problem-solving seems inevitable, we chose to encourage their use—with critical thinking—rather than banning them. These findings are based on students' self-reporting throughout the semester and confirmed by post-course survey results (Figures~\ref{fig:poll_results} and \ref{fig:course_evaluation}).

The question was whether actively encouraging LLM use would improve students' skills or worsen educational outcomes. The evidence suggests the former, with \textbf{our key finding being that students actually decreased their dependence on LLMs over time rather than increasing it}—contrary to common assumptions that encouraging LLM use will worsen learning outcomes. Students also developed a community of practice around effective LLM use, with evidence of informal strategy sharing that enhanced their AI proficiency skills. Post-course surveys confirm that students maintained problem-solving independence despite LLM use (median score: 7.0), and that the documentation requirement positively impacted their learning experience (median score: 9.0).

\textbf{Early Assignment Patterns.} In early assignments, students primarily used LLMs for basic clarification and simple coding help. Their initial prompting strategies were underdeveloped, characterized by casual, conversational approaches without attention to prompt engineering \citep{Chen2024,Knoth2024,Schulhoff2024}. Students would write general requests for explanations or ask broad questions without providing context or structure. Students often blamed themselves for miscommunications with AI during this early phase.

As the semester progressed, students developed more sophisticated approaches to AI interaction, moving beyond basic assistance-seeking to strategic engagement patterns.

\textbf{Domain-Specific Role Prompting.} Several students independently discovered the effectiveness of domain-specific role prompting \citep{Xu2023,Zheng2023,ChenJ2024,Kong2024}, instructing models like ChatGPT to assume the persona of an expert astronomer with specialization in machine learning. This approach helped focus the model on relevant domain knowledge and produced more field-appropriate responses. This evolution demonstrates students developing meta-learning skills alongside course content. Students reported notable improvement in their ability to craft effective prompts throughout the semester (median score: 9.0), the highest score among all assessed dimensions. 

\textbf{Targeted Questioning and contextual enrichment.} Instead of asking LLMs for complete solutions, students learned to ask about specific concepts. They discovered that prompts focused on discrete topics were more effective than copying and pasting entire assignment questions. This development was partly by design—anticipating LLM use, some assignment questions were deliberately less structured, avoiding breaking equations into fine-grained steps that would be easy to copy and paste. Students demonstrated growth in learning how to break down questions into manageable pieces that LLMs could handle effectively. This problem structure also helped students develop better decomposition skills. Their prompting strategies evolved to include contextual enrichment: providing code snippets for debugging, offering problem context before asking specific questions, and including previous problem statements to ensure continuity. Students discovered that sharing entire notebooks or code sections produced more accurate and helpful responses compared to sharing isolated fragments, as complete context allowed the AI to better understand dependencies, variable definitions, and overall structure. 

\textbf{Enhanced Learning Focus.} Students valued AI's ability to bridge the gap between theoretical concepts from lectures and their practical implementation in assignments, with this theory-to-practice translation being helpful for connecting abstract mathematical concepts to concrete computational implementations. Students appreciated the rapid implementation assistance and coding efficiency, which allowed them to focus more on understanding concepts rather than getting stuck on implementation details. This time efficiency enabled students to concentrate on conceptual learning rather than minutiae of coding syntax and technical implementation issues. Students with different learning approaches benefited differently—visual learners appreciated assistance with visualization code, while others found conceptual explanations most valuable. Survey results support this observation, with students reporting that LLMs helped them understand course concepts better than traditional methods alone (median score: 8.0).

\textbf{Verification Rather Than Generation.} Students autonomously began asking AI to review their completed work rather than generate solutions from scratch. They would complete solutions independently and then submit their work to the LLM for evaluation, verification, and suggestions for improvement. This approach allowed students to maintain their learning autonomy while benefiting from AI assistance. Students developed iterative refinement strategies: breaking complex problems into smaller steps addressed sequentially, building sequences of related prompts to maintain context, cross-checking outputs against course materials, and trying the same prompt with different tools when facing unclear explanations. Post-course assessments confirm that students developed effective strategies for verifying AI-generated information (median score: 8.0).

\textbf{Critical Tool Comparison.} By experimenting with multiple tools, students developed the ability to evaluate outputs and recognize each tool's strengths and weaknesses. A common pattern emerged where students would use general-purpose models like ChatGPT primarily for coding assistance while relying on AstroTutor for theoretical explanations. Students found the specialized AstroTutor valuable as a complement to general-purpose LLMs (median score: 10.0), though with notable variation in responses (standard deviation: 3.2). In later assignments, students became more sophisticated in recognizing when the AI was at fault rather than blaming themselves. Students described becoming more confident in their AI interactions over time, developing strategies to cope with inconsistent quality in AI responses, such as rewording the same question multiple times or triangulating between different AI tools. By course completion, students reported confidence in recognizing incorrect LLM outputs (median score: 8.0).

\textbf{Decreased Dependence.} This represents our key finding: instead of increasing LLM usage, actively asking students to document and think critically about their usage led many to rely on LLMs progressively less as the course advanced. Students reported developing better debugging skills and greater ability to resolve simpler issues independently. Multiple students reflected on the trade-off between quick AI solutions and deeper learning, showing awareness of the potential to use AI as either a shortcut or a learning enhancer. This pattern suggests that LLMs were serving as effective learning scaffolds rather than creating permanent dependencies.

The growing independence stems from students having developed awareness of LLM limitations—recognizing different error rates between models, understanding the overly affirming nature of LLMs that can impede learning, and developing habits of cross-checking AI outputs against authoritative sources. Documentation requirements contributed to this awareness development (median score: 8.0). This suggests that encouraging active documentation of LLM use, combined with critical reflection, is a key approach to fostering independence rather than dependence in AI-assisted learning. Students strongly believe the skills they developed will be valuable for future academic and professional work (median score: 9.0) and would recommend this approach for future astronomy courses (median score: 9.0).

\section{LLM-Specific Challenges}

The decreased reliance on LLMs observed throughout the semester stemmed partly from students identifying these challenges through active reflection encouraged by bonus points for documenting their AI interactions. Students showed growing understanding of tool limitations.

\textbf{Prompt Engineering Difficulties.} Students struggled with balancing prompt specificity — too general prompts yielded unhelpful generic answers, while overly specific prompts led to irrelevant details or misunderstood questions \citep{Denny2023,Babe2024}. Incorrect prompting could lead to an overcomplicated solution when a simpler approach was needed. When providing only code blocks and asking about function usage, AI would incorrectly assume existing code was correct. LLMs would sometimes generate completely unrelated responses when full assignment context was not provided, making problematic assumptions without complete information. Limited context windows and memory limitations required reestablishing context when switching models, disrupting natural problem-solving flow.

\textbf{Over-reaffirmation.} LLMs occasionally provided incorrect information with high confidence \citep{Peters2025}, particularly problematic for educational applications. Students reported confusion from misleading information that reinforced rather than corrected misconceptions. More fundamentally, LLMs are reluctant to directly contradict user statements or point out errors, instead offering gentle suggestions or building upon flawed premises. Students found this excessive positive reinforcement \citep{Malmqvist2024,Ruwe2024} frustrating compared to human instruction, where clear error correction plays a vital role in deepening understanding. Students noted that specialized tools like AstroTutor were more reliable than general-purpose models, showing awareness that different models have different strengths and limitations.

\textbf{IDE Integration Issues.} GitHub Copilot and similar tools would fixate on previous code patterns, over-training on bootstrapping code and requiring memory resets \citep{Barke2022}. This memory cache behavior compounds over-reaffirmation problems, as AI becomes increasingly confident in potentially incorrect patterns from previous interactions. These issues highlight the need for deeper understanding of IDE features and caching behavior in AI-assisted coding environments.

Many difficulties could be addressed through API usage, which remains largely unknown to students. Even at the graduate level, misconceptions persist that LLMs are only accessible through graphical interfaces, despite APIs offering more powerful interaction possibilities.

