\section{Grading and Assessments with LLM}

Beyond chat-based AI assistance for student learning, homework feedback represents a critical component of undergraduate education. Traditional grading approaches face several challenges at the undergraduate level that LLMs could potentially address.

\textbf{Quality and Consistency Issues.} Teaching assistants are often slightly more senior students who may have taken different versions of the course. In rapidly evolving fields like data analysis and machine learning in astronomy, techniques can change between course iterations, leading teaching assistants to miss important details or apply outdated approaches. Large classes typically employ multiple graders with varying expertise levels, making uniform grading standards difficult to maintain across all submissions \citep{Mutambuki2018,Tong2020}.

\textbf{Timeliness and Feedback Depth.} Student teaching assistants, burdened with their own coursework and research responsibilities, often require time for grading, resulting in delayed feedback when it would be most educationally valuable. Time constraints frequently lead to terse comments that lack the detailed explanations students need for improvement. The situation becomes further complicated by late submission policies—even a single student requesting deadline extensions can delay solution release for the entire class, preventing timely learning reinforcement for students who submitted on schedule \citep{Hattie2007,Hounsell2008}.

LLMs offer potential solutions to these challenges through automated grading with immediate, detailed feedback that maintains consistency across all submissions. We experimentally tested LLM grading alongside traditional human grading throughout this course. However, given ongoing ethical considerations that require broader community consensus \citep{Holmes2021,Akgun2022,KumarR2023}, \textit{all official grades released to students remained based on human evaluation. We conducted LLM grading as a parallel experiment and report our comparative findings here.}

\subsection{Grading Implementation}

The course included four homework assignments, each consisting of approximately 8 coding questions built around simplified research papers. The lecturer deconstructed published research, extracted relevant datasets, and created step-by-step coding exercises that allowed students to reproduce key findings. The four homework topics were: (a) solving binary ellipticity from phase-space observations using Gaia data through Bayesian inference, (b) calibrating telescope responses with linear regression, (c) identifying strong gravitational lenses in James Webb Space Telescope data using logistic regression, and (d) detecting quasar outliers from spectra through Principal Component Analysis and Gaussian Mixture Models.

We developed an automated grading system using two LLM models: Claude-3.7-Sonnet, chosen for its coding reasoning capabilities, and Gemini-2.5-Flash, the more cost-effective option used in AstroTutor. The grading process involved parsing Jupyter notebooks to extract sequential question content, student code, outputs, and generated figures with automatic base64 image decoding and an organized directory structure. Questions were graded sequentially, with the LLM being provided full context of the current problem, the lecturer's solution, and student solutions from earlier questions to ensure consistent evaluation and avoid double-deducting points for recurring conceptual misunderstandings that might propagate through related problems.

The LLM grading system was designed to mirror effective human grading practices through carefully engineered prompts that followed the same grading philosophy communicated to human tutors. Models were instructed to evaluate understanding of core concepts, reasonable code implementation, presence of required visualizations, and appropriate result interpretation. The system generated structured JSON responses with specific fields including earned points, detailed error descriptions with point deductions, and feedback for each question. All scores were rounded to the nearest 0.5 points for consistency with human grading practices. The system generated detailed HTML reports with visual previews of student-generated figures and organized breakdowns by student and question. Figure~\ref{fig:llm_grading_example} shows an example of the detailed feedback provided by the LLM grading system, demonstrating both the identification of specific errors and constructive guidance for improvement.

\subsection{LLM Grading Performance Analysis}

The grading results demonstrate promising performance for LLMs as homework graders, particularly for stronger models like Claude. From a practical standpoint, \textit{Claude-3.7-Sonnet costs approximately USD \$0.10 per student homework grading, while Gemini-2.5-Flash operates at roughly five-fold lower cost.} However, both models exhibit systematic differences from human grading patterns, with regression slopes consistently greater than one, indicating that LLM grading tends to be more stringent than human evaluation.

Despite this systematic bias, the correlation analysis reveals strong consistency in relative student rankings. When analyzing average homework scores for individual students and fitting a linear regression model, Claude achieves $R^2 = 0.83$ with human graders, meaning that student performance rankings are largely preserved—arguably the most critical aspect for educational fairness. Figure~\ref{fig:grading_comparison} illustrates this relationship, showing that while absolute scores differ, the rank ordering remains robust across the grade distribution. The bias-corrected grade boundaries demonstrate that students would generally receive equivalent letter grades after accounting for the systematic scoring difference.

For more economical models like Gemini Flash, performance degradation becomes apparent with $R^2 = 0.63$ and higher variability. While the regression slope is fitted to student averages, the standard deviation is calculated from residuals of all individual homework scores relative to this student-level regression line. This yields standard deviations of 1.51 for Gemini Flash versus 1.17 for Claude, indicating that Gemini shows greater inconsistency at the individual assignment level. While Gemini Flash still serves as a reasonable study companion, providing structured feedback and error identification, the reduced grading consistency limits its utility for formal assessment. The scatter in individual homework scores reveals that Gemini's performance varies across different question types and complexity levels, making it less reliable as a homework grader compared to Claude.

Given the systematic stringency observed in LLM grading, a critical question emerges: are human graders missing legitimate errors, or are LLMs identifying phantom problems? The extensive documentation of errors from both LLM and human graders enables detailed question-by-question comparison, revealing that the former explanation predominates.

\subsubsection{Comparing Claude-3.7-Sonnet with Human Grading}

\textbf{Computational precision.} Claude demonstrates superior performance in identifying technical implementation errors that human graders frequently overlook. These include mistakes in matrix transformations, gradient calculations, and normalization procedures in machine learning contexts. For instance, Claude consistently caught transposition errors in gradient computations where students incorrectly implemented matrix operations, while human graders often missed these errors that fundamentally affect algorithmic correctness. Such technical subtleties prove challenging for human graders to detect consistently across large numbers of submissions.

\textbf{Systematic consistency.} Claude exhibits uniformity in maintaining evaluation standards across students. Analysis of scoring patterns reveals that human graders showed variance in weighting similar types of errors across different students, while Claude applied consistent criteria throughout. Human graders demonstrated inconsistent penalization for equivalent conceptual omissions—such as incomplete Bayesian implementations or missing mathematical derivations—whereas Claude maintained uniform scoring based on specific technical deficiencies.

\subsubsection{Comparing Claude with Gemini-2.5-Flash}

\textbf{Contextual focus and prioritization.} The performance gap between Claude-3.7-Sonnet and Gemini-2.5-Flash primarily manifests in appropriate error identification. Claude correctly identifies complex technical errors while providing specific, actionable feedback, whereas Gemini Flash often over-penalizes superficial issues due to limited context memory. Gemini Flash sometimes makes inappropriate logical judgments, such as heavily penalizing students for acceptable visualization design choices when pedagogical objectives were clearly met, demonstrating a failure to distinguish between stylistic preferences and fundamental errors.

\textbf{Systematic consistency.} Gemini Flash suffers from high variability in grading standards across similar questions and students. Analysis reveals wide scoring ranges for equivalent technical implementations of the same concepts, while Claude maintained consistent evaluation patterns. This inconsistency appears to stem from Gemini Flash's limited context memory, which prevents understanding of multi-part questions and their pedagogical interconnections, leading to fragmented evaluation that misses the broader learning objectives.

\subsection{Mock Interview-Based Assessments}

The strong correlation between LLM and human grading, particularly for performant models like Claude-3.7-Sonnet, opens possibilities beyond traditional grading toward novel assessment methodologies. Given that Claude maintains consistent relative student rankings and demonstrates systematic evaluation standards, we explored whether LLMs could facilitate individualized ``oral'' examinations that address several challenges in undergraduate assessment.

Traditional written examinations suffer from several limitations that become acute in large university settings. Students can share solutions, creating fairness concerns and undermining assessment validity. Static examinations provide single-point failure scenarios where students may become stuck on early questions without opportunity for recovery. Additionally, the archaic nature of traditional testing poorly reflects real-world problem-solving scenarios where students would have access to resources and can seek clarification when needed.

Interview-style assessments, while pedagogically superior in many respects \citep{Graesser2001,Huxham2012}, face scalability challenges. In courses with hundreds of students, conducting individual interview-based examinations requires human resources. When distributed across multiple examiner groups, these assessments often suffer from the same inconsistency issues observed in homework grading above, potentially creating unfair evaluation disparities. The flip-classroom approach, while promoted in educational literature \citep{OFlaherty2015}, remains difficult to implement at scale for assessment purposes.

LLM-based interview systems address these scalability and consistency challenges while preserving the pedagogical benefits of individualized assessment. The system can generate unique question sets from the same course material, eliminating solution sharing concerns. 


\subsubsection{System Architecture and Design}

We developed a Socratic-style interview agent based on similar principles as AstroTutor but designed for assessment rather than tutoring. The system was programmed with course materials and instructed to conduct structured ``oral'' examinations covering conceptual understanding, implementation skills, and debugging capabilities. The agent follows a carefully designed protocol that ensures coverage of key learning objectives while adapting to individual student responses and providing appropriate scaffolding when needed.

\textbf{Assessment Framework.} The interview system implements a structured three-category assessment approach. \textit{Conceptual questions} evaluate theoretical understanding by presenting real-world astronomical scenarios and asking students to select and justify appropriate analytical methods. \textit{Implementation tasks} require students to write focused code segments that demonstrate technical proficiency with course algorithms, emphasizing problem-solving approach over syntactic perfection. \textit{Debugging challenges} present students with flawed code implementations, testing their ability to identify logical errors and propose systematic corrections.

\textbf{Scaffolding and Support Mechanisms.} When students encounter difficulties, the system offers graduated support through a hint mechanism that requires explicit student consent. Hints are posed as guiding questions rather than direct answers, maintaining the assessment integrity while preventing complete failure scenarios. This approach mirrors effective human tutoring practices where struggling students receive guidance without compromising the evaluation of their independent capabilities.

The interview format naturally accommodates diverse learning needs and circumstances \citep{Griful-Freixenet2017}. Students with special accommodations can take examinations at their own pace, pause and resume sessions as needed, and access the assessment environment from familiar locations. 

\subsubsection{Pilot Implementation and Evaluation}

For this pilot implementation, we tested the system with a single student who required make-up assessment due to circumstances beyond their control. The student completed sessions covering the major course topics, submitting chat records for evaluation. No time limits were imposed to accommodate the student's circumstances.

The following shows a paraphrased version (for privacy reasons) of a conversation from the interview-based assessment:

\begin{questionbox}[boxcolor1]
\textbf{LLM Examiner:} Looking at your course topics, I'd like you to choose which topic you'd like the first question to be on. Your options are: Random Variables and Bayesian Inference, Summary Statistics, Linear Regression, Classification and Logistic Regression, Principal Component Analysis, or Clustering with Gaussian Mixture Models.\\

\textbf{Student:} I'll choose Random Variables and Bayesian Inference.\\

\textbf{LLM Examiner:} Great choice. Here's a conceptual question for you: Imagine you're analyzing exoplanet transit data. You have a light curve with some noise, and you want to estimate the planet's radius. You can model the transit depth as a function of the planet's radius, but you also have prior knowledge about the typical sizes of planets in similar systems. How would you approach this problem using Bayesian inference, and why is Bayesian inference a suitable choice here?\\

\textbf{Student:} Bayesian modeling differs from subjective interpretations because probability can influence its belief. With a more standard way of thinking, taking into account the `usual' size of a planet's radius in similar systems would be pointless. However, with Bayesian inference, it updates its probability as more data is revealed. If you already have a good idea about the type of planet it might be observing, this could factor into the statistical interpretation of it.\\

\textbf{LLM Examiner:} That's a good start. You've correctly identified that Bayesian inference allows you to incorporate prior knowledge about the planet's radius. To expand on your answer, can you explain how the prior distribution, likelihood function, and posterior distribution would play a role in this specific exoplanet transit scenario?
\end{questionbox}

The session was recorded and subsequently evaluated by both the human instructor and an offline open-weight LLM (Qwen3-235B-A22B) using identical grading criteria. Here, open-weight means the model weights can be downloaded and run locally on our own GPU hardware, rather than accessed through a cloud API. The offline evaluation approach was chosen to protect student privacy. 

The human instructor assessed performance based on demonstrated conceptual understanding, problem-solving approach, technical implementation skills, and ability to articulate complex ideas clearly. Qwen3-235B-A22B received the complete conversation transcript and evaluated using the same rubric, generating detailed feedback across all assessment dimensions. However, \textit{the official grade used for course records was based solely on the human instructor's evaluation}, with the LLM assessment serving as a comparative validation study.

Both evaluations showed strong consistency in overall performance assessment and specific area identification. The LLM evaluation aligned with the human instructor's judgment regarding the student's conceptual understanding and technical gaps. The interview format revealed learning patterns that traditional written examinations might miss. The real-time feedback mechanism allowed the examiner to probe understanding depths and identify specific misconceptions, providing diagnostic information valuable for both grading and future instruction planning.

This pilot demonstration suggests that LLM-facilitated oral examinations could provide a scalable solution for individualized assessment in large courses while maintaining the pedagogical benefits of adaptive questioning and real-time feedback. The approach addresses limitations of traditional testing while preserving assessment integrity through unique question generation and consistent evaluation standards.

\subsubsection{Automated Video Analysis for Academic Integrity}

Screen recording serves as a deterrent mechanism for academic integrity monitoring. We implemented \textit{offline} LLM-based video analysis with the open-weight Qwen-2.5-VL-72B to detect potential violations in student self-reported Zoom recordings. The analysis identifies suspicious behavior patterns including: (1) extended periods where students appear off-camera, suggesting movement away from the assessment environment, and (2) sustained eye-gaze diversions indicating attention to off-screen resources such as additional browser windows or devices.

The analysis generates timestamped reports identifying intervals warranting closer examination. For example, flagged periods included ``500-510 seconds: Sustained gaze shift---possibly looking off-screen at another browser or document'' and ``850-870 seconds: Sustained gaze shift.''

Following analysis, all flagged segments are extracted and concatenated into a single condensed video for human review, after which original recordings are immediately discarded to protect student privacy. This reduces manual verification time from approximately one hour to one minute of targeted inspection. Human reviewers can efficiently assess whether genuine violations occurred or whether the system detected false positives due to natural movements, lighting conditions, or camera positioning.

While this automated screening approach demonstrates potential scalability for large courses by focusing human review on algorithmically flagged segments rather than requiring comprehensive footage analysis, we deliberately restricted its use to just a single make-up assessment case as described above, using only offline open-weight models run locally on our hardware. This highly limited deployment was intentional, driven by ethical concerns about privacy and surveillance in educational settings \citep{Regan2019,Coghlan2021}. Before considering broader implementation, the academic community needs to engage in thorough discussion about balancing academic integrity verification with student privacy rights and dignity.


\subsection{Ethical Considerations}

While AI as a learning companion represents a less contentious application, AI as a grader and exam setter raises issues that require broader community consensus. We emphasize that all official grading in this course remained entirely under human control, with our LLM analysis serving as a post-hoc comparative study to assess potential capabilities. 

The advantages of LLM-assisted assessment are evident: detailed, personalized feedback can be delivered simultaneously to all students, creating more equitable learning environments. Students receive real-time feedback that identifies knowledge gaps, valuable for formative assessment purposes. The performance stems from LLM's systematic application of evaluation criteria, largely avoiding the potential unconscious bias and inconsistency that research demonstrates affects human graders.

However, human graders retain value through higher-order pedagogical judgment and understanding of individual student circumstances at a more personal level. Beyond technical evaluation capabilities, human accountability — albeit imperfect — remains a more acceptable social norm compared to machine judgment in educational contexts. This parallels ongoing debates around autonomous systems in other domains, where technical capability must be balanced against social acceptance and ethical responsibility.

These broader implications extend beyond the scope of this study. We focus on demonstrating AI as a learning companion while leaving questions of automated grading and assessment implementation to community deliberation. Our findings suggest potential benefits for educational equity and consistency, but widespread implementation requires careful consideration of institutional values, student agency, and pedagogical philosophy \citep{EuropeanCommission2022}. 


\section{Recommendations for Implementation}

Based on our findings, we propose several concrete recommendations for educators in astronomy considering similar LLM integration in astronomy education and related STEM disciplines.

\textbf{Require AI Usage Documentation and Reflection.} Require students to document AI usage through reflection questions that encourage critical evaluation rather than simple reporting. Award credit for thoughtful analysis of AI interactions, including failed attempts and limitations encountered. This documentation requirement proved effective in fostering metacognitive awareness and reducing dependency over time.

\textbf{Develop Domain-Specific AI Tutors.} Invest in specialized AI tutors tailored to course content when resources permit. While more expensive than general-purpose models, domain-specific tools like AstroTutor provide more reliable information and better pedagogical alignment with learning objectives. Students valued the specialized knowledge while still utilizing commercial models for complementary purposes.

\textbf{Guide Multi-Tool Usage Rather Than Restrict Access.} Recognize that students will use multiple AI tools for different purposes. Rather than restricting access, guide students toward understanding when different tools are most appropriate and effective. Introduce surveys of available models at the beginning of courses to help students make informed tool choices.

\textbf{Teach Modern Development Environments and API Usage.} Provide more extensive introductions to Integrated Development Environments (IDEs)—software applications that combine text editors, debugging tools, and project management features in a single interface for writing and testing code—particularly for modern AI-integrated development environments like Cursor AI or GitHub Copilot, to better prepare students for professional practice. Teaching students to use Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)—direct computer-to-computer communication methods that allow automated interactions with AI systems—eliminates limitations stemming from students only knowing Graphical User Interfaces (GUIs), the familiar point-and-click web interfaces like ChatGPT's website. API access enables more sophisticated AI interactions through automated workflows, batch processing, and integration with coding environments.

\textbf{Consider LLM-Assisted Assessment Methods.} Consider implementing LLM-assisted grading for consistent, detailed feedback while maintaining human oversight for official evaluation. Explore interview-based assessments facilitated by AI for personalized evaluation at scale, particularly for make-up examinations or alternative assessment needs.

Several improvements could enhance future iterations. Expanding agentic AI tutors with more extensive astronomy-focused code bases would improve domain-specific assistance quality. Introducing AI literacy concepts early, including prompt engineering and output verification strategies, would accelerate student proficiency development. Creating structured opportunities for peer sharing of effective AI strategies could enhance community learning around these tools.


\section{Conclusion}

This study represents an examination of LLM integration in astronomy education through an advanced undergraduate course in astro-statistics targeting final-year students preparing for graduate school in astronomy. The course emphasized problem-solving through machine learning and statistical techniques applied to real astronomical research problems, providing an ideal context for exploring AI integration in computationally intensive STEM education. While conducted with a small class size, the outcomes provide insight to warrant broader consideration.

Our study yielded several key findings that challenge common assumptions about AI in education. The development and deployment of AstroTutor demonstrated that domain-specific AI tutors can provide reliable, contextually appropriate assistance while reducing hallucinations common in general-purpose models. Students valued this specialized tool as a complement to commercial LLMs, developing strategies for using different tools for different purposes.

The documentation requirement and encouragement of critical thinking proved effective in fostering reflective AI usage. Rather than creating dependency, structured LLM integration led to decreased reliance over time as students developed independent problem-solving capabilities. This trajectory suggests that LLMs can serve as effective learning scaffolds when properly implemented with appropriate reflection mechanisms.

Students demonstrated evolution in their approach to AI tools, progressing from basic assistance-seeking to strategic, critical engagement. Students approached LLMs as learning tools rather than shortcuts, attempting problems independently first and seeking targeted assistance for specific obstacles. This approach contradicts common concerns about AI undermining educational objectives.

Students developed AI literacy skills through their LLM interactions, learning to evaluate different tools' strengths and limitations, formulate effective prompts, verify outputs against authoritative sources, choose appropriate tools for specific tasks, and balance efficiency with learning goals. These AI proficiency skills will likely remain valuable throughout their careers as AI continues to evolve in scientific and technical fields.

Our comparative grading analysis revealed strong correlation between LLM and human evaluation. The systematic consistency of LLM grading offers potential solutions to scalability and fairness challenges in large courses, while the pilot interview-based assessment demonstrated novel possibilities for individualized evaluation. However, ethical considerations require community consensus before widespread implementation of automated assessment.

This case study suggests that thoughtfully integrated LLMs can enhance astronomy education when students are encouraged to use them as learning tools rather than answer providers, and when instructors create structures that promote reflection on effective AI integration strategies. The key finding is that structured encouragement of AI use, combined with transparency requirements and domain-specific tools, can enhance astronomy education without compromising learning objectives, preparing students for an AI-integrated future while maintaining the rigor and independence that characterize effective STEM education.

