\section{Averaging procedure}
\label{sec:averaging}
We recall the governing equations
\begin{align}
    \div \vec u &= 0, \label{eq:supp_mass_cons} \\
    \div [ 2 \mu (T) \gv \varepsilon ] &= \grad p,  \label{eq:supp_momentum_cons} \\
    \pd T t + \vec u \bdot \grad T - \kappa \laplacian{T} &= 0. \label{eq:supp_advdiff}
\end{align}
We define the pressure, velocity and temperature field averaged along the $z$-direction
\begin{equation}
    p_{\parallel} = \frac{1}{2 h} \int_{-h}^{h}\! p(x,y,z)\, dz, \quad
    \vec u_{\parallel} = \frac{1}{2 h} \int_{-h}^{h}\! \vec u(x,y,z)\, dz, \quad
    T_{\parallel} = \frac{1}{2 h} \int_{-h}^{h}\! T(x,y,z)\, dz
    \label{eq:supp_avg_quantities}
\end{equation}
We recall that we assume laminar flow through all the domain, so $p = p_\parallel$ and the velocity field $\vec u$ can be written as
\begin{equation}
    \vec u  = \frac{3}{2} \vec u_\parallel \left(1 - \left(\frac{z}{h}\right)^2\right)
    \label{eq:supp_poiseuille}
\end{equation}
where $h$ is half the cell thickness.

For the incompressibility condition \eqref{eq:supp_mass_cons}, we have $\div \vec u = \grad_\parallel \bdot \vec u_\parallel = 0$, where we defined $\grad_\parallel = \evec_x \partial_x + \evec_y \partial_y$.

For the Darcy law \eqref{eq:supp_momentum_cons}, using \eqref{eq:supp_mass_cons} we can rewrite the left-hand side:
\begin{equation}
    \grad \mu(T) \bdot (\grad \vec u + \grad \vec u^\top) + \mu(T) \laplacian\vec u = \grad p.
\end{equation}
From the laminar flow profile \eqref{eq:supp_poiseuille}, we then have
\begin{equation}
    \frac{3}{2}\left(1-\left(\frac{z}{h}\right)^2\right) \left( \grad \mu(T) \bdot \left(\grad \vec u_\parallel + \grad \vec u_\parallel^\top \right) + \mu(T) \laplacian\vec u_\parallel \right) - \frac{3}{h^2}\mu(T)\vec u_\parallel = \grad p .
\end{equation}
We now average both sides of the equation along $z$. Assuming laminar flow in the cell implies $p = p(x,y)$, so the average of the pressure gradient gives $\grad_\parallel p_\parallel$. On the other hand, we are assuming that the temperature changes in the direction perpendicular to the plates are negligible with respect to the ones in the perpendicular direction, so that the $z$-averaged viscosity is
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2h} \int_{-h}^{h}\!\mu(T(z))\, dz \simeq \frac{1}{2h} \int_{-h}^{h}\!\mu(T_\parallel)\, dz = \mu(T_\parallel).
\end{equation}
These considerations lead to the following flow equation
\begin{equation}
     \left( \grad \mu(T_\parallel) \bdot \left(\grad \vec u_\parallel + \grad \vec u_\parallel^\top \right) + \mu(T) \laplacian\vec u_\parallel \right) - \frac{3}{h^2}\mu(T)\vec u_\parallel = \grad p.
\end{equation}
If we now adopt the lubrication approximation, for which $|\grad \vec u_\parallel| \ll |\vec u_\parallel| / h$, and we assume viscosity gradients to be small such that $|\grad \mu| \ll \mu / h$, we obtain a linear relationship between the 2D pressure gradient and the corresponding velocity field:
\begin{equation}
    -\frac{3}{h^2} \mu(T_\parallel) \vec u_\parallel = \grad_\parallel p_\parallel.
    \label{eq:supp_momentum_cons_avg}
\end{equation}

We now discuss the averaging of the advection-diffusion equation \eqref{eq:supp_advdiff}. We will follow a derivation based on large-time asymptotics \citep{Liñán2020} applied to our planar geometry. For simplicity, we adopt a reference system for which the local velocity is directed toward $x$, namely $\vec u_\parallel = (u, 0)$ where $u$ is the mean velocity modulus. Suppose the fluid has a temperature distribution $T$ introduced into the pipe at $t=0$ with an arbitrary initial profile. If this initial temperature distribution is compact (i.e., the temperature has a localized variation and is not uniform throughout the pipe), the temperature field will be convected along the pipe at the mean velocity $u$. We adopt a frame moving with the mean velocity and scaled with following non-dimensional scales
\begin{equation}
    t' = \frac{t}{h^2/\kappa}, \quad x' = \frac{x-u t}{h}, \quad z' = \frac{z}{h}, \quad \Peclet = \frac{u h}{\kappa}
    \label{eq:supp_resc}
\end{equation}
where $\kappa$ is the thermal diffusivity of the fluid, $h^2/\kappa$ is the characteristic time for the temperature to diffuse across the channel and $\Peclet$ is the thermal Péclet number. In this way,  equation \eqref{eq:supp_advdiff} becomes
\begin{equation}
    \pd{T}{t'} + \Peclet (1 - 2 z'^2)\pd{T}{x'} - \pdd{T}{x'} - \pdd{T}{z'} = 0 
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_resc}
\end{equation}
In this moving frame, at times $t' \sim 1$ (in dimensional variables, $t \sim h^2/\kappa$), the temperature will diffuse vertically within the cell. For $t' \gg 1$ (in dimensional variables,  $t \gg h^2/\kappa$), thermal diffusion will cause the temperature to become uniform along the vertical direction, although temperature gradients along the $x$-direction may still persist, for which the related diffusion process is described by Taylor dispersion. If $t' \sim 1/\epsilon \gg 1$, where $\epsilon \ll 1$ (namely, times much greater than the out-of-plane diffusion time $h^2/\kappa$), the temperature field will have spread along $x$ over a distance $x \sim \sqrt{t} \sim \sqrt{1/\epsilon} \gg 1$. To quantify large-time behavior, the following rescaling can be introduced:
\begin{equation}
    \tau = \epsilon t', \quad  \zeta = \sqrt{\epsilon} x',
    \label{eq:supp_resc_eps}
\end{equation}
Equation \eqref{eq:supp_advdiff_resc} is then rewritten as
\begin{equation}
    \epsilon \pd{T}{\tau} + \sqrt{\epsilon}\, \Peclet (1 - 2 z^2)\pd{T}{\zeta} - \epsilon \pdd{T}{\zeta} - \pdd{T}{z'} = 0
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_resc2}
\end{equation}
Since $\epsilon \ll 1$, the solution can be expanded in an asymptotic series, $T = T_0 + \sqrt{\epsilon}T_1 + \epsilon T_2 + \dots$ 

To leading order,  equation \eqref{eq:supp_advdiff_resc2} becomes
\begin{equation}
    \pdd{T_0}{z'} = 0 
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_resc2_order0}
\end{equation}
which, together with the symmetry condition $\pd{T}{z'}|_{z'=0}=0$, gives $T_0=T_0(\xi,\tau)$, namely $T_0$ is independent of $z'$. This is a direct consequence of the thermal diffusion that, at large times, makes the concentration uniform across the cell. Note that $T_0 = T_\parallel$ as defined in \eqref{eq:supp_avg_quantities}.
To order $\sqrt{\epsilon}$ we have
\begin{equation}
    \Peclet (1 - 2 z'^2)\pd{T_\parallel}{\zeta} - \pdd{T_1}{z'} = 0
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_resc2_ordersqrtepsilon}
\end{equation}
which, together with the condition $\pd{T}{z'}|_{z'=0}=0$ gives
\begin{equation}
    T_1(\xi, z', \tau) =  \Peclet \frac{z'^2}{2} \left(1 - \frac{z'^2}{3}\right)\pd{T_\parallel}{\zeta} + T_{1,z'=0}
    \label{eq:supp_T1_sol}
\end{equation}
where $T_{1,z'=0}$ is the unknown value of $T_{1}$ at $z'=0$. The following equation is obtained at order $\epsilon$:
\begin{equation}
    \pd{T_\parallel}{\tau} - \pdd{T_\parallel}{\zeta} + \Peclet (1 - 2 z'^2)\pd{T_1}{\zeta} - \pdd{T_2}{z'} = 0.
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_resc2_orderepsilon}
\end{equation}
This can be solved by averaging it over the cell thickness, i.e.\ integrating it between $z'=0$ and $1$. Applying \eqref{eq:supp_T1_sol} and the boundary conditions $\pd{T}{z'}|_{z'=0}=0$ and $\pd{T}{z'}|_{z'=1}=(H h/\alpha)(T_c - T_\parallel)$ (the latter coming from \eqref{eq:bc_walls_3d}), we obtain
\begin{equation}
    \pd{T_\parallel}{\tau} - \pdd{T_\parallel}{\zeta}\left(1 + \frac{2 \Peclet^2}{105}\right) + \frac{H h}{\alpha \epsilon}(T_\parallel - T_c)  = 0
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_resc2_orderepsilon_avg}
\end{equation}
Returning to the original coordinates, the equation becomes
\begin{equation}
    \pd{T_\parallel}{t} + u \pd{T_\parallel}{x} - \kappa \pdd{T_\parallel}{x}\left(1 + \frac{2 U^2h^2}{105 \kappa^2}\right) + \frac{H}{h\rho c}(T_\parallel - T_c)  = 0
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_2d_x}
\end{equation}
where $T_\parallel$ is as defined in \eqref{eq:supp_avg_quantities}. Generalizing for any direction parallel to the cell plates:
\begin{equation}
    \pd{T_\parallel}{t} + \vec u_\parallel \bdot \grad_\parallel T_\parallel - \grad_\parallel \bdot (\mat{D} \grad_\parallel T_\parallel) + \frac{H}{h\rho c}(T_\parallel - T_c) = 0
    \label{eq:supp_advdiff_2d}
\end{equation}
where the anisotropic dispersion is represented by a $2\times 2$ tensor:
\begin{equation}
   \mat{D} = \kappa \left( \mat{I} + \frac{2}{105}\left(\frac{h}{\kappa}\right)^2\vec u_\parallel \otimes \vec u_\parallel\right).
    \label{eq:supp_keff}
\end{equation}

\section{Numerical method}

\subsection{Fully coupled model}
\label{sec:num_meth_full}
We solve the fully coupled model \ref{eq:full_model_2d} using a finite element method. We use a semi-implicit first-order time-splitting scheme formulated in the following.

We define $\Omega$ as the domain $\vec x \in [0, L_x] \times [0, L_y]$, as sketched in figure \ref{fig:model}(b), discretized into a quadrilateral grid. We further define the scalar function space $S$ as the space of $Q_1$ functions on this grid.

The time-stepping scheme is given by:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item Initialize $T^0 \in S$ according to the base state \eqref{eq:base_state}. Set the iteration index $n=1$.
    \item Find $p^n \in S$ such that for all $b \in S$,
    \begin{align}
 \int_\Omega \beta^{-T^{n-1}} \grad p^n \bdot \grad b \, \diff^2 x = \int_{0}^{L_y} u_{x}(0, y, t) b (0, y) \, \diff y,
    \label{eq:fem_full_p}
    \end{align}
    where $u_x(0, y, t)$ is given by equations \eqref{eq:sinusoidal_pert} or \eqref{eq:random_pert},
    subject to the Dirichlet boundary condition
    $p^n (L_x, y, t) = 0$.
    \item Find $T^n \in S$ such that for all $b \in S$,
    \begin{multline}
        \frac{1}{\Delta t} \int_\Omega T^n b \, \diff^2 \vec x 
   +  \int \vec u^n \bdot \grad T^n b \, \diff^2 \vec x 
    + \frac{1}{\Peclet} \int \grad T^n \bdot \grad b \, \diff^2 \vec x \\
    + \frac{2 \Peclet}{105} \int ( \vec u^n \bdot \grad T^n ) ( \vec u^n \bdot \grad b ) \, \diff^2 \vec x 
    + \Gamma \int T^n b \, \diff^2 \vec x 
    = \frac{1}{\Delta t} \int_\Omega T^{n-1} b \, \diff^2 \vec x,
    \label{eq:fem_full_T}
    \end{multline}
    where $\Delta t$ is the time step, $T^n(0, y, t) = 1$, and
    \begin{align}
        \vec u^n = - \beta^{-T^{n-1}} \grad p^n
    \end{align}
    is the estimated velocity based on previously computed values.
    \item Stop the simulation if $n \Delta t \geq t_{\rm tot}$, where $t_{\rm tot}$ is the predefined total simulation time. Otherwise, increase $n \leftarrow n+1$ and repeat from step (ii).
\end{enumerate}

We implement the scheme above in FEniCSx 0.9.0 \citep{baratta2023dolfinx} using periodic boundary conditions at $y=0$ and $y=L_y$. 
We solve both equations numerically using iterative solvers: equation \eqref{eq:fem_full_p} using the conjugate gradient method with the Hypre BoomerAMG preconditioner \citep{yang2002boomeramg}, equation \eqref{eq:fem_full_T} using the stabilized bi-conjugate gradient method with a Jacobi preconditioner.

The implementation can be found in the Git repository \citep{CoolingGit2025} in the file \texttt{cooling.py}.

\subsection{Linearized model}
\label{sec:num_meth_lin}
To solve the linearized model \eqref{eq:lsa_k} we use a first-order implicit time-stepping scheme with mixed finite elements.
The scheme can be written as the following:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item Initialize $T^0(x)$ according to \eqref{eq:base_state}. Set $n=1$.
    \item Find $(T^n, u^n) \in W$ such that for all $(Q, v) \in W$:
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{multline}
    %\mathcal F_T (T^k, u^k; T^{k-1}) = 
    \frac{1}{\Delta t} \int_0^{L_x} (T^n - T^{n-1}) Q \, \diff x 
        + \int_0^{L_x} \d {T^n} x Q \, \diff x 
        + \kappa_{\rm eff} \int_0^{L_x} \d {T^n} x \d Q x \, \diff x \\
        + (\kappa k^2 + \Gamma) \int_0^{L_x} {T^n} Q \, \diff x
        + \xi \int_0^{L_x} T_0(x) \left[ -(1 + 2 \kappa_{\parallel} \xi) u^n + \kappa_\parallel \d {u^n} x \right] Q \, \diff x = 0
\end{multline}
and
\begin{align}
    - \int_0^{L_x} \d {u^n} x \d v x \, \diff x 
        + \psi \xi \int_0^{L_x} T_0(x) \d {u^n} x v \, \diff x
        - k^2 \int_0^{L_x} u^n v \, \diff x 
        + k^2 \psi \int_0^{L_x} T^n v \, \diff x = 0
\end{align}\label{eq:fem_lin}\end{subequations}
under the Dirichlet boundary conditions $T^n (0) = 0$ and $u^n(0) = \epsilon \Theta(t_{\rm pert} - t)$ where $\Theta$ is the Heaviside step function.
\item If $n \Delta t \geq t_{\rm tot}$, where $t_{\rm tot}$ is the total simulation time, stop the simulation. Otherwise, increase $n \rightarrow n + 1$ and go to step (ii).
\end{enumerate}

The equation system \eqref{eq:fem_lin} is solved using FEniCS 2019.2.0 \citep{logg2012automated} by means of a direct solver (LU factorization).
The implementation can be found in the Git repository \citep{CoolingGit2025} in the file \texttt{linear\char`_model.py}.

\subsection{Mesh length and resolution}

To perform simulations of the linearized model, we need to specify the length of the one-dimensional domain $L_x$, the number of tiles along it $N_x$, the time step $\delta t$ and the total simulation time $t_{\rm tot}$.

We can derive a rough estimate of the solution profile length from the inverse of the characteristic length of the base state $\xi$, defined in equation \eqref{eq:def_xi}. Given a value for the cooling rate $\Gamma$ and the Péclet number $\Peclet$, we can compute $\xi$ from \eqref{eq:def_xi}. Hence, we choose to set the domain length to $L_x = 10/\xi$. Since the maximum of $T_x$ located at the beginning of the domain, as visible in figure \ref{fig:T_and_u_vs_x}, the ratio between its peak value and its value at the end of the domain, $x=L_x$, can be estimated as $\max_x \hat T_k(x)/T_k(x = L_x) \simeq e^{10}$. This indicates that the tail of the temperature perturbation decays by approximately four orders of magnitude across the chosen domain length, ensuring that boundary effects at $x=L_x$ remain negligible.

The number of tiles $N_x$ was specified such as the mesh resolution was constant for all simulations and equal to $N_x/L_x=10$. Moreover, the time step was set to $\delta t=0.005$. Both choices are motivated by observing that, respectively, higher values for $N_x/L_x$, or lower values of $\delta t$, would alter the measured value of $\gamma$ by a relative change $\varepsilon_{\rm rel} \lesssim 10^{-4}$, which we consider acceptable for our purposes.

We see that, by setting the perturbation time to $t_{\rm pert} = 0.1$, the linear growth regime is reached at $t \sim 5$ at the latest (see figure \ref{fig:T_and_u_vs_t}). Hence, for all the simulations performed, the total time was set to $t_{\rm tot} = 20$; this choice produce a time interval sufficiently large for calculating $\gamma$ as the slope of the best fit’s curve in an interval corresponding to the linear growth regime.

With respect to the linearized model, for the full non-linear simulations we also need to specify the length of the domain along the $y$-axis $L_y$ and the number of rectangular tiles $N_y$ along it. Being the imposed perturbation variation along the $y$-axis, a mesh resolution along $y$ higher than along $x$ is in general needed. For the simulations with random perturbation at the inlet we performed in this work, we set $L_y =60$, $N_x/L_x = 2$, $N_y/L_y = 50$, $\delta t = 0.005$. $L_x$ and $t_{\rm tot}$ varied depending on $\Peclet$. This choice allowed to obtain a good agreement between the measured values of $\gamma_*$ ($k_*$) and the corresponding values of $\gamma_{\max}$ ($k_{\max}$) from the linearized model, as we can see from figure \ref{fig:max_values_vs_Pe_compare}. For a  proper study of the non-linear terms of the solution, a more refined choice is needed. However, this goes beyond the scope of the our present work.

The linearized model was run on a single node with 4× Intel® Xeon® Gold 6254 CPUs (3.10 GHz, 72 physical cores / 144 threads total) and typically completed in 2–3 minutes. However, to determine ($k_{\max}$, $\gamma_{\max}$) for a given set of global parameters, we perform multiple simulations at different $k$-values followed by a quadratic fit, as explained in \S\,\ref{sec:lsa_gammamax_kmax}. As a result, exploring each global parameter combination requires a few hours of computation time. In contrast, full non-linear simulations were executed using 64 MPI processes and took several hours each on average. Depending on the scheduler configuration, these parallel simulations may have utilized one or more nodes.

\section{Large $\Peclet$ limit of the linearized model}
\label{sec:app:largePe}
We consider the linearized equations \eqref{eq:lsa_hatk}, inserting $T_0 = e^{-\xi x}$:
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    \left( \dd{}{x} + \psi \xi e^{-\xi x} \d{}{x} - k^2\right) \hat u_k  &=  - k^2 \psi \hat T_k, \label{eq:darcy_laplace_simp} \\
    \left(\gamma + \d{}{x} - \kappa_{\rm eff} \dd{}{x} + \kappa k^2 + \Gamma \right) \hat T_k &= \xi e^{-\xi x} \left( 1 + \kappa_{\parallel}  \left(2 \xi - \d{}{x} \right) \right) \hat u_k \label{eq:advdiff_laplace_simp}.
\end{align}
\label{eq:linmod_simp}
\end{subequations}
We use the length scale $\xi^{-1}$ to rescale $x$ and $k$:
\begin{align}
    x = \xi^{-1} \tilde x, \quad k = \xi \tilde k.
    \label{eq:xk_scaling}
\end{align}
Equation \eqref{eq:linmod_simp} then becomes:
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    \left( \dd{}{\tilde x} + \psi e^{-\tilde x} \d{}{\tilde x} - \tilde k^2\right) \hat u_k  &=  -\tilde k^2 \hat \tau_k, \label{eq:darcy_laplace_simp2} \\
    \left(\gamma +\xi \d{}{\tilde x} - \kappa_{\rm eff} \xi^2 \dd{}{\tilde x} + \kappa \xi^2 \tilde k^2 + \Gamma \right) \hat \tau_k &= \xi \psi e^{-\tilde x} \left( 1 + \kappa_{\parallel} \xi \left(2 - \d{}{\tilde x} \right) \right) \hat u_k \label{eq:advdiff_laplace_simp2},
\end{align}\label{eq:linmod_simp2}\end{subequations}
where we defined $\hat\tau_k = \psi \hat T_k$.

We now assume that $\kappa_{\rm eff} \gg \Gamma^{-1}$, which implies, recalling from \eqref{eq:def_xi}
\begin{align}
    \xi = \frac{- 1 + \sqrt{1 + 4\Gamma \kappa_{\rm eff}}}{2 \kappa_{\rm eff}}
    \simeq \sqrt{\frac{\Gamma}{\kappa_{\rm eff}}}.
    \label{eq:xi_simp}
\end{align}
We further consider the high $\Peclet$ limit, which means $\kappa_\parallel \gg \kappa$. Combining both assumptions with \eqref{eq:linmod_simp2} and \eqref{eq:xi_simp}, equation \eqref{eq:darcy_laplace_simp2} remains unchanged while \eqref{eq:advdiff_laplace_simp2} simplifies to
\begin{align}
    \left(\frac{\gamma}{\Gamma} + 1 - \dd{}{\tilde x} \right) \hat \tau_k &= \psi e^{-\tilde x} \left(2 - \d{}{\tilde x} \right) \hat u_k \label{eq:advdiff_laplace_highPe}.
\end{align}
We see from \eqref{eq:darcy_laplace_simp2} and \eqref{eq:advdiff_laplace_highPe} that the only dimensionless parameters are $\tilde\gamma \equiv \gamma/\Gamma$, $\tilde k$ and $\psi$.
Hence, the growth rate $\gamma$ must in the high $\Peclet$ limit be given by
\begin{align}
    \gamma ( k; \Gamma, \kappa_{\parallel}, \psi) = \Gamma \tilde \gamma (\tilde k; \psi).
\end{align}
Since the rescaled dispersion relationship $\tilde\gamma(\tilde k)$ depends only on the parameter $\psi$, its global maximum and the corresponding wavelength, which we call $\tilde \gamma_\textrm{max}$ and $\tilde k_\textrm{max}$ respectively, are solely functions of $\psi$ as well. Hence,
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    k_{\rm max} ( \kappa_\parallel,\Gamma, \psi ) &= \sqrt{\frac{\Gamma}{\kappa_\parallel}} \tilde k_{\rm max} (\psi), \\
    \gamma_\textrm{max} ( \kappa_\parallel, \Gamma, \psi) &= \gamma(k_\textrm{max}; \kappa_\parallel, \Gamma, \psi) = \Gamma \tilde \gamma (\tilde k_\textrm{max} (\psi); \psi) = \Gamma \tilde \gamma_{\rm max} (\psi),
\end{align}\label{eq:supp_kmax_and_gammamax_largePe}\end{subequations}
In summary, as $\Peclet \to \infty$, the asymptotic scalings for the fastest mode and its growth rate are $k_{\rm max} \sim \Peclet^{-1/2} \Gamma^{1/2}$ and $\gamma_{\rm max} \sim \Gamma$, respectively.
We further note that as $\psi_c$ is the value of $\psi$ where $\gamma_\textrm{max} (\psi_c) = 0$, it follows that for high Péclet numbers $\beta_c=e^{-\psi_c}$ is a universal numerical value independent of any system parameters.

\subsection{Large viscosity ratio}
Taking the analysis a step further, we may assume that $\psi$ is \emph{large}.
We introduce the scaled quantities $\tilde{\tilde k} = \psi^{-1} \tilde k$ and $\tilde{\tilde x} = \psi \tilde x$.
Equations \eqref{eq:darcy_laplace_simp2} and \eqref{eq:advdiff_laplace_highPe} become
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    \left( \dd{}{\tilde{\tilde x}} + e^{-\tilde{\tilde x}/\psi} \d{}{\tilde{\tilde x}} - \tilde{\tilde k}^2\right) \hat u_k  &=  -\tilde{\tilde k}^2 \hat \tau_k, \label{eq:darcy_laplace_psilarge} \\
    \left( \frac{\frac{\gamma}{\Gamma} + 1}{\psi^2} - \dd{}{\tilde{\tilde x}} \right) \hat \tau_k &= e^{-\tilde{\tilde x}/\psi} \left( \frac{2}{\psi} - \d{}{\tilde{\tilde x}} \right) \hat u_k \label{eq:advdiff_laplace_highPe_psilarge}.
\end{align}\label{eq:model_highPe_psilarge}\end{subequations}
$\hat u_k$ will typically decay with $x$ as $\hat u_k \sim e^{-\tilde \eta \tilde x}$ where $\tilde \eta = \min \{ \tilde k, \tilde \gamma \}$.
Hence, for large $\psi$, $e^{-\tilde{\tilde x}/\psi} \simeq 1$ wherever $\hat u_k$ is significant.
To the lowest order in the small parameter $1/\psi$, equations \eqref{eq:model_highPe_psilarge} become
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    \left( \dd{}{\tilde{\tilde x}} + \d{}{\tilde{\tilde x}} - \tilde{\tilde k}^2\right) \hat u_k  &=  -\tilde{\tilde k}^2 \hat \tau_k, \label{eq:darcy_laplace_psilarge2} \\
    \left( \frac{\frac{\gamma}{\Gamma} + 1}{\psi^2} - \dd{}{\tilde{\tilde x}} \right) \hat \tau_k &= - \d{}{\tilde{\tilde x}}\hat u_k \label{eq:advdiff_laplace_highPe_psilarge2}.
\end{align}\label{eq:model_highPe_psilarge2}\end{subequations}
Here, all the system parameters are grouped together in the first term of \eqref{eq:advdiff_laplace_highPe_psilarge2}, which should be solely given by $\tilde{\tilde k}$.
This scaling thus explains why $k_\textrm{max} \sim \psi$ and $\sqrt{ \gamma_{\max}/\Gamma + 1 } \sim \psi$ for large $\psi$, up to numerical prefactor.

It should be noted that expanding \eqref{eq:model_highPe_psilarge} to the first order in $1/\psi$ would give a more accurate estimate of $k_\textrm{max} \sim \psi ( 1 + \textrm{const.}/\psi ) \sim \textrm{const.} + \psi$ (and similar for $\gamma_\textrm{max}$). This linear functional form is in excellent agreement with our data for $\psi > \psi_c$.

\section{Relation to the dispersion-free model}
As mentioned in \S\,\ref{sec:model_description}, a model where in-plane temperature transport is purely advective and thermal dispersion is entirely neglected cannot be obtained as a $\Peclet\to\infty$ limit of \eqref{eq:advdiff_laplace_simp}. Instead, we can take the artificial dispersion-free limit $\kappa, \kappa_\parallel \to 0$, which through \eqref{eq:def_xi} becomes
\begin{align}
    \xi = \frac{- 1 + \sqrt{1 + 4\Gamma \kappa_{\rm eff}}}{2 \kappa_{\rm eff}}
    \simeq \Gamma.
    \label{eq:xi_simp_helfrich}
\end{align}
With the scaling \eqref{eq:xk_scaling}, we obtain \eqref{eq:darcy_laplace_simp2} and \eqref{eq:advdiff_laplace_simp2}. In this case, the latter simplifies to
\begin{align}
    \left(\frac{\gamma}{\Gamma} + 1 + \d{}{\tilde x} \right) \hat \tau_k &= \psi e^{-\tilde x} \hat u_k \label{eq:advdiff_laplace_helfrich}.
\end{align}
With the same reasoning as in Appendix \ref{sec:app:largePe}, we can derive a scaling for the fastest mode wavenumber and its growth rate
\begin{align}
    k_\textrm{max} (\Gamma, \psi ) = \Gamma \tilde k^\textrm{H}_\textrm{max} (\psi), \\
    \gamma_\textrm{max} (\Gamma, \psi ) = \Gamma \tilde \gamma^\textrm{H}_\textrm{max} (\psi).
\end{align}
with $\tilde k^\textrm{H}_\textrm{max} (\psi)$ and $\tilde \gamma^\textrm{H}_\textrm{max} (\psi)$ are functions of $\psi=-\log \beta$.
\end{appen}

