\begin{abstract}
%% Text of abstract
In this work, we propose a variant of non-uniform cellular automata, named as {\em Temporally Non-Uniform Cellular Automata (t-NUCAs)}, which temporally use two rules, $f$ and $g$ in a sequence $\mathcal{R}$.  To observe {\em reversibility} in {\em t-NUCAs}, we study their {\em injectivity} and {\em surjectivity} properties. Unlike classical CAs, some {\em irreversible} {\em t-NUCAs} show the behavior similar to {\em reversible} {\em t-NUCAs}. To study this behavior, we define {\em restricted  surjectivity} of {\em t-NUCA} and introduce {\em restricted reversibility} which shows {\em reversibility}  of {\em t-NUCA} for a set of initial configurations. By further investigating the remaining irreversible {\em t-NUCAs}, some {\em t-NUCAs} are found which have many-to-one mapping in their configuration space, but do not have {\em non-reachable} (Garden-of-Eden) configurations. We name these {\em t-NUCAs} as {\em weakly reversible} {\em t-NUCAs}. Under finite lattice size, a {\em t-NUCA}, like any classical CA, shows cyclic behavior. We explore this cyclic behavior and discuss its relation with rule sequence. Finally, we note down the possible longest cycle length of a {\em t-NUCA}, based on the lattice size and rule sequence.
\end{abstract}


