\section{Introduction}
Cellular Automaton (CA) is a discrete dynamical system that consists of a regular network of cells and the cells update their states depending on the current states of the cell itself and the neighboring cells. Classically, cellular automata (CAs) use a single local rule to update the states of all cells synchronously during evolution~\citep{BhattacharjeeNR16}.  In this work, we study a class of non-classical cellular automata, where different rules at different time steps can be used to update their cells. We name these automata as {\em temporally Non-Uniform Cellular Automata (t-NUCAs)}.

Non-uniformity in cellular automata was first introduced in 1986~\citep{Pries86}, which allows the cells to use different rule. These non-uniform CAs were characterized by a number of researchers with a target to use them in VLSI design \& test and other related application domains~\citep{Horte89c,das1992characterization,chowdhury1995,cattell1996synthesis,ppc1,tcad/DasS10}. Later, this class of non-uniform CAs have been generalized and defined in~\citep{CattaneoDFP09,dennunzio2012non}. Dynamical behavior of these CAs, such as  {\em injectivity, surjectivity, permutivity, equicontinuity, decidability, structural stability} etc. have also been investigated in~\citep{CattaneoDFP09,dennunzio2012non,dennunzio2014three}. Another class of non-uniform CAs, called asynchronous cellular automata, has been studied, which do not force all the cells to be updated simultaneously~\citep{naka}. A number of variations of ACAs have been proposed over the time~\citep{Fates20061,Alberto12,Formenti2013,Fates14}, and their dynamical behavior have also been studied~\citep{Alberto12,Manzoni2012,Sarkar12,fates2018}.

However, in all the above cases the local rule are not changed during evolution of the CA. The present works deals with a different class of non-uniformity where local rules are changed during evolution. In this work, we assume only two rules , say $f$ and $g$, which are used to update the states of the cells in a temporal sequence during evolution. The behavior of a {\em t-NUCA} depends on both the rules ($f$ and $g$) and the sequence in which the rules are applied. In literature, we find that such a non-uniform CA ({\em t-NUCA}) was first used to solve density classification problem~\citep{Fuk05}. Recently, some dynamical behavior of a special class of {\em t-NUCA} have been studied in~\citep{ghosh2023}. We have also studied a sub-class of {\em t-NUCAs}, named {\em Temporally Stochastic Cellular Automata}, where the system uses a default rule ($f$) for evolution, but is stochastically affected sometime by a noise ($g$)~\citep{roy2022atsca}. The present work generalizes this model.% and studies dynamical behavior of the {\em t-NUCAs}.


We start our investigation by exploring the set theoretic properties of {\em t-NUCAs}, keeping an eye on the reversibility of them. We first investigate {\em injectivity}, {\em surjectivity} and {\em bijectivity} of {\em t-NUCAs} in classical sense. As usual, bijective {\em t-NUCAs} are reversible and we find that when individual CAs with local rules $f$ and $g$ are reversible then, a {\em t-NUCA} consists of $f$ and $g$ with a rule sequence is {\em reversible}.

We investigate the behavior of irreversible {\em t-NUCAs}, and observe that some of them exhibit reversible CA-like behavior. We find that, some {\em irreversible t-NUCAs} shows {\em injectivity} (reps. {\em surjectivity}) for a set of initial configurations. We name such {\em t-NUCAs} as {\em restricted injective} (resp. {\em restricted surjective}) t-NUCAs (see Section~\ref{reversibility-on-restriction}).  

A {\em t-NUCA} behaves in a way that is similar to being {\em reversible} for a set of initial configurations if it is both {\em restricted injective} and {\em restricted surjective}. This sort of {\em t-NUCAs} was given the name {\em restricted reversible t-NUCAs}. In addition to this, we find more by looking at the remaining irreversible {\em t-NUCAs}. We observe that certain {\em t-NUCAs} do not have {\em non-reachable} configurations despite having many-to-one mapping in their configuration space. These {\em t-NUCAs} have weak resemblance with the reversibility and name them as {\em weakly reversible t-NUCA}.

A {\em t-NUCAs}, like any classical CA, exhibits cyclic behavior when the lattice size is finite. In Section~\ref{Cyclic behavior}, we investigate the cyclic behavior of {\em t-NUCAs} with finite lattice size. We analyze the relationship between cycles and rule sequences. Finally we discuss the Hamiltonian cycle related to the transition diagram of t-NUCA and we report the Eular cycle length of t-NUCA. 

%Finally, we discuss the t-NUCA cycle problem and propose an approximation algorithm along with different case studies. Based on this algorithm, we find a larger cycle length than a traditional cycle length of a CA from the transition diagram of {\em t-NUCA}. In addition to this, we report the existence of Hamiltonian cycles and Eulerian cycles in the transition diagram of a t-NUCA.%Eulerian cycle length of {\em t-NUCAs}. 

\section{The Proposed Model}\label{model}

Like classical cellular automata, the proposed {\em temporally Non-Uniform Cellular Automata (t-NUCAs)} are also defined as a quadruple $(\mathcal{L}, \mathcal{N}, \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{R})$ where,
\begin{itemize}
	\item $\mathcal{L}\subseteq\mathbb{Z}^D$ is the $D$-dimensional lattice. Elements of $\mathcal{L}$ are called cells.
	\item $\mathcal{N}=(\vec{v}_1,\vec{v}_2,\cdots,\vec{v}_m)$ is the neighborhood vector of each cell $\vec{v}$ of the lattice and $(\vec{v}+\vec{v}_i)\in\mathcal{L}$, where $m$ is the number of neighbors.

	\item  $\mathcal{S}$ is the finite set of states.

	\item  $\mathcal{R}=(\mathcal{R}_t)_{t\geq 1}$ is the temporal rule sequence of local rules, where the rule $\mathcal{R}_t :\mathcal{S}^m\rightarrow \mathcal{S}$ is applied to all cells at the time step $t$. If $s_{\vec{v}}$ is the present state of cell $\vec{v}\in \mathcal{L}$, then the next state of the cell at $t$ is $\mathcal{R}_t(s_{\vec{v} + \vec{v}_1},s_{\vec{v} + \vec{v}_2},\cdots,s_{\vec{v} + \vec{v}_m})$. %calculates

\end{itemize}

The primary difference of {\em t-NUCA} and classical CA is, a classical CA uses a single rule during evolution, whereas a {\em t-NUCA} uses a set of rules temporally in some sequence. In this sense, classical CAs are a special case of {\em t-NUCAs}. Although a {\em t-NUCA} can use any set of local rules, we use only two local rules in this work, $f$ and $g$, which act in some sequence.

A configuration, denoted by $c:\mathcal{L}\rightarrow \mathcal{S}$, is a snapshot of the states of all cells at a specific time instance, which can be represented as $c=(s_{\vec{v}})_{\vec{v}\in\mathcal{L}}$, where $s_{\vec{v}}$ is the state of cell $\vec{v}\in\mathcal{L}$. We denote $\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{S}^{\mathcal{L}}$ as the set of all configurations of the CA.

The local rule (say, $f$) of a classical CA induces a global transition function $G_f:\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\mathcal{C}$ of the CA, where,
\begin{align}
	G_f(\vec{v})\:|_{\vec{v}\in\mathcal{L}}=f(s_{\vec{v} + \vec{v}_1},s_{\vec{v} + \vec{v}_2},\cdots,s_{\vec{v} + \vec{v}_m})
\end{align}


Although the results presented here are applicable to any $D,\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{N}$, the examples given in this article mostly adhere to ECA (Elementary Cellular Automata) settings and employs $f$ and $g$ as two ECA rules. ECAs are one-dimensional, two-state, three-neighborhood cellular automata, in which a cell changes its state  based on its left neighbor, self, and right neighbor. Updates are made synchronously at each time step in accordance with a local rule, such as  $f:\mathcal{S}^3\rightarrow\mathcal{S}$.  A global function $G_f:\mathcal{S}^\mathcal{L}\rightarrow\mathcal{S}^\mathcal{L}$ is defined such that, for a given local rule $f$ and a set of cells $\mathcal{L}$, the image $y=(y_i)_{i\in\mathcal{L}}=G_f(c)$, where $c$ is a configuration, $c=(c_i)_{i\in\mathcal{L}}\in\mathcal{S}^\mathcal{L}$. Rule $f$ can be defined as,

\begin{align}
	\forall i\in\mathcal{L},y_i=f(c_{i-1},c_i,c_{i+1})
\end{align}

Each rule $f$ is associated with a `decimal code' $w$, where $w$ = $f$($0,0,0$) $\cdot$ $2^0$ + $f$($0,0,1$) $\cdot$ $2^1$ + $\cdots$ + $f$($1,1,1$) $\cdot$ $2^7$, for the naming purpose. Following examples use ECA rules to show working of {\em t-NUCAs}. 

\begin{example}	\label{ex2}	
	Let us consider a finite t-NUCA with ECA rules $14$ and $243$. The number of cells (lattice size) is assumed as $4$ and the boundary condition is periodic. Suppose the rules are used in the following sequence: $14,14,243,14,14,243,\cdots$. That is, rule $14$ is applied twice, and then the rule $243$ is used once, and this repeats. In Figure~\ref{fig:14X243_1},  we can find the transition diagram of $4$-cell ECA rule $14$ and rule $243$ in Figure~\ref{fig:14X243_1}(a) and Figure~\ref{fig:14X243_1}(b) respectively. The transition diagram of {\em t-NUCA} is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:14X243_1}(c). In this diagram, if a transition is due to rule $14$, it is marked by {\em black} edge, if by rule $243$ then marked by {\em blue} edge. Figure~\ref{fig:14X243_1}(d) is a derived transition diagram, named as partial transition diagram of the t-NUCA where only the configurations, marked by white circle are considered as initial configurations, from where the execution starts and the intermediate configurations\footnote{In a partial transition diagram, an intermediate configurations is not considered as initial configuration. However, the configuration is reached during evolution of a t-NUCA from an initial configuration.} (marked by gray circle) are reached during evolution. 

\end{example} 

