The sequence of rules ($\mathcal{R}$) affects the dynamics of a {\em t-NUCA}. Any sequence of $f$ and $g$ can be used in a {\em t-NUCA}. Sometime some well-defined condition on time step may be used to get a sequence. Let us consider that $\Theta(t)$ is such a condition, which if holds the rule $f$ is applied, otherwise rule $g$ is applied. That is,

\begin{align}\label{rt}
	\mathcal{R}_t &=
	\begin{cases}
		f  &  \Theta(t) \text{ holds }\\
		g  & \text{ otherwise }\
	\end{cases}
\end{align}

We generally identify a {\em t-NUCA} by the notation $(f,g)[\mathcal{R}]$. If we note down the time $t$ when $\Theta(t)$ holds, then we can get a sequence of integers. These integers mean the time steps when $f$ is applied. For example, let us assume that $\Theta(t)$ holds if $t$ is odd. That is, the series for $t$ is: $1,3,5,7,9,\cdots$. The series of $t$ when $\Theta(t)$ holds can represent the rule sequence. So, in our further reference, we shall call this series as rule sequence. Some time, it is difficult to represent a rule sequence in the notation of t-NUCA when the sequence is long enough. Hence, we need to consider a rule sequence identifier to represent a rule sequence. 

A rule sequence can be identified in various way, e.g., using binary bits: $101010\cdots$ ($\Theta(t)$ holds if $t$ is odd), where $1$ (resp. $0$) represents the application of $f$ (reps. $g$). Since, in the sequence $101010\cdots$, the term $10$ repeats over and over, one can consider the sequence as  $(10)^+$ or $\textbf{10}$, hence, the notation of the t-NUCA is $(f,g)[(10)^+]$. Alternatively, in the database of OEIS~\citep{oeis}, the integer sequences are designated by some lexicographical ordering number. The rule sequence of a {\em t-NUCA} can be identified by such an ordering number, say $\chi$, then a {\em t-NUCA} with two ECAs $f$ and $g$ can be identified as t-NUCA $(f,g)[\chi]$. 

For example, a {\em t-NUCA} with two ECAs $f$ and $g$ and the series of $t$, when $f$ is applied: $1,3,5,7,9,\cdots$, i.e., $A005408$~\citep{oeis} can be identified as $(f,g)[A005408]$. This paper considers such notations to represents a t-NUCA, where the lexicographical ordering numbers are considered as the identifiers of the rule sequences.

The rule sequences can be classified as, {\em periodic} and {\em aperiodic},

\textbf{Periodic :}
A periodic sequence (cyclic sequence) is a sequence for which the same terms are repeated over and over: For example, 
$\mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2, ..., \mathcal{R}_l,\\  \mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2, ..., \mathcal{R}_l,  \mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2, ..., \mathcal{R}_l, ...$ is a {\em periodic} rule sequence where $(\mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2, ..., \mathcal{R}_l)$ is repeated in the sequence. The $l$ number of repeated terms is called the {\em period}. That is,  $\mathcal{R}=(R_t)_{t \geq 1} = (\mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2, ..., \mathcal{R}_l)^+$. %, where 

For example, let us assume a {\em t-NUCA}, where $\mathcal{R}_t\in\{f,g\}$. 
\begin{align}\label{eq5}
	\Theta(t) &=
	\begin{cases}
		True \; \text{ $t$ is not divisible by $3$,}\\
		False \; \text{ otherwise,}\\
	\end{cases}
\end{align} 
Hence the time steps of applying $f$ is as follow : $1,2,4,5,7,8,\cdots$ (OEIS $A001651$). That is, rule $f$ is applied twice and the rule $g$ is used once, and this repeats (see Figure~\ref{fig:14X243_1}). This represents a periodic rule sequence with period length $3$. Alternatively, to examine the detailed transitions of a t-NUCA with periodic rule sequence, we present the space-time diagram of a t-NUCA $(90,73)[A001651]$ in Figure~\ref{Fig:Type1}. Here, the cells with states $1$, updated by $f$ or $g$, are marked by {\em blue} or {\em red}, respectively. The cells with state $0$ are represented by {\em white} cells. 

