\section{Injectivity and Surjectivity}\label{Set theoretic properties}

A classical CA with rule $f$ is injective (resp. surjective) if its global transition function $G_f$ is injective (resp. surjective). In classical finite CAs, injectivity implies surjectivity, hence bijectivity. A surjective CA has no {\em non-reachable} (Garden-of-Eden) configurations. In this section, we investigate the existence of {\em non-reachable} configurations in a {\em t-NUCA} through the injectivity and surjectivity of CA $f$ and CA $g$.

\begin{defnn}
	A configuration of a CA is non-reachable if the CA can not reach to the configuration during its evolution from any initial configuration.
\end{defnn}

Figure~\ref{fig:14X243_1}(a) shows the transition diagram of a non surjective CA, where the configurations $7,~11,~13,~14$, and $15$ are {\em non-reachable} configurations. This is not a surjective CA. For a non-surjective CA, we always get a set of {\em non-reachable} configurations. In case of t-NUCA, non-surjectivity follows the same. 


\begin{theoremm}\label{th1}
	Let $X_f$ and $X_g$ be the sets of non-reachable configurations of $G_f$ and $G_g$ respectively. Then, there exist non-reachable configurations in a {\em t-NUCA} for any rule sequence if $X_f \cap X_g \neq \emptyset$.
\end{theoremm}

\begin{proof}
	Let $X_f$ and $X_g$ be the sets of non-reachable configurations of $G_f$ and $G_g$ respectively, and $X_f \cap X_g \neq \emptyset$. 
	Now if $x\in X_f \cap X_g$ and $\mathcal{C}$ is the set of all configurations of a t-NUCA, then both of the following statements are true:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $\forall y \in \mathcal{C}, \forall k \in \mathbb{N},\: x \neq G^k_f(y)$
		\item $\forall z \in \mathcal{C}, \forall l \in \mathbb{N},\: x \neq G^l_g(z)$
	\end{enumerate}
	From the above statements, we get the following -\\
	Let us consider $(G_t)_{t\geq 1}$ be the sequence of rules that are used in a t-NUCA. Here, $G_t$ is either $G_f$ or $G_g$. Hence, $$\forall t\in\mathbb{N}, x\notin G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}(\mathcal{C}_t)$$
%	$$x \neq G^l_g(G^k_f(z)) \text{ and } x \neq G^k_f(G^l_g(y))$$
	This implies, the configuration $x$ can not be reached from any configuration. Hence, for a t-NUCA consists of $f$ and $g$, $x$ is a non-reachable configuration.
\end{proof}


Theorem~\ref{th1}, however, does not state the sufficient condition for existence of {\em non-reachable} configuration in a {\em t-NUCA}. There exists {\em non-reachable configuration} even if CAs $f$ and $g$ do not have any {\em non-reachable} configurations in common (that is, $X_f\cap X_g=\emptyset$). Following example illustrates the case.

