\begin{theoremm}\label{th2}
	A {\em t-NUCA} with any rule sequence has no non-reachable configuration sequence iff $G_f$ and $G_g$ both are bijective.
\end{theoremm}
\begin{proof}
	Let us consider $(G_t)_{t\geq 1}$ be the sequence of CAs used in a t-NUCA. Here, $G_t$ is either $G_f$ or $G_g$. If $G_f$ and $G_g$ both are bijective, then each $(G_t)_{t\geq 1}$ remains bijective. For a rule sequence of a t-NUCA, the compositions of $G_t$ and $G_{t+1}$ for all $t\geq 1$  are bijective. Hence, all the configurations are reachable.% from some other initial configurations. 

	Let us now consider that the {\em t-NUCA} has no non-reachable configuration. By contradiction, let us assume that $G_f$ is not surjective and it has non-reachable configurations. That is, $G_f(\mathcal{C})\subseteq \mathcal{C}$.

	Now consider another t-NUCA with the same couple of CAs $f$ and $g$, consists of a trivial rule sequence where only rule $f$ is used in each step. Then, obviously, the {\em t-NUCA} has {\em non-reachable} configuration, i.e., not surjective. This contradicts our consideration that the {\em t-NUCA} has no {\em non-reachable} configuration. Hence $G_f$ is to be bijective. With the similar argument, $G_g$ is also to be bijective. Hence the proof.

\end{proof}



\begin{example}	
	Let us consider an  infinite {\em t-NUCA} with ECA rules $51$ and $204$. Suppose the rule $51$ is used in the following sequence $1,3,5,7,\cdots$, (OEIS $A005408$). The t-NUCA $(51,204)[A005408]$ shows bijectivity. Here, the ECAs $51$ and $204$ both are bijective. Figure~\ref{fig:inf} illustrates some transitions of the t-NUCA. All other transitions behave similarly.
\end{example}

