Although the proof of Theorem~\ref{th2} considers trivial rule sequence (only one rule is used in each time step), there are a number of rule sequences for which a {\em t-NUCA} can have {\em non-reachable} configurations when $G_f$ and $G_g$ are not bijective.

\begin{corollary}
	Let, a t-NUCA with a rule sequence consists of two rules $f$ and $g$, then each configuration of the t-NUCA is reached by both $f$ and $g$ during evolution iff both the rules are bijective. 
\end{corollary}

Let us now extend the definition of injectivity of classical CAs to the {\em t-NUCA}. From the above discussion, we get that a {\em t-NUCA} behaves like an injective (resp. surjective) CA if $G_f$ and $G_g$ both are injective (resp. surjective). This observation leads us to the following definition.

\begin{defnn}
	A t-NUCA is injective (resp. surjective) iff $G_f$ and $G_g$ both are injective (resp. surjective).
\end{defnn}

Similar to injectivity, if $G_f$ and $G_g$ both are surjective then a t-NUCA consists of $f$ and $g$ remains surjective. Surjectivity of a t-NUCA implies that, each configuration has $k$ predecessor for $k$ number of different rules using in it. In this work, two rules are utilized, that is, each configuration $c\in\mathcal{C}$ of a t-NUCA is reached by both the rules during evolution. 

%Similar to injectivity, if $G_f$ and $G_g$ are surjective then $G^k_f\circ G^l_g(\text{ or } G^k_g\circ G^l_f)$ for any $k,l\geq 1$ remains surjective. That is, no {\em non-reachable} configuration exists for $G^k_f\circ G^l_g(\text{ or } G^k_g\circ G^l_f)$. Hence, the {\em t-NUCA} is surjective.

