Reversibility is a property of some mathematical and physical systems for which time-reversed dynamics are well-defined. That is, no information loss is there during the evolution. Hence, a {\em t-NUCA} can be considered as reversible if the evolution of the t-NUCA can be inverted with respect to time. In other word, a t-NUCA is said to be reversible if it is invertible. This is only possible if the {\em t-NUCA} is bijective.


\begin{proposition}
	A t-NUCA is said to be reversible iff the CA is bijective (injective and surjective).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}

In case of two bijective rules $f$ and $g$, there may exist $z\in \mathcal{C}$, for which,  $G_f(x)=G_g(y)=z$, for some $x,y \in \mathcal{C}$, $x\neq y$. That is, the configuration $z$ has two predecessors for two different rules. However, since a specific rule sequence can be found in a {\em t-NUCA}, any predecessor can be reached from one configuration by a given rule (as per rule sequence) during inversion. That implies, for a {\em t-NUCA}, a configuration has only one predecessor for a specific rule. Hence, no information loss is there. So, the {\em t-NUCAs}, consist of two bijective rules, are reversible {\em t-NUCAs}.
\end{proof}

