\section{Restricted and weak Reversibility}\label{reversibility-on-restriction}

Theorem~\ref{th1} has proved that there exist {\em non-reachable} configurations for a t-NUCA consists of $f$ and $g$ if $X_f\cap X_g\neq \emptyset$. Example~\ref{ex5} has next shown that there exist non-reachable configurations of a t-NUCA with a rule sequence even if $X_f\cap X_g=\emptyset$. However, if one rule is bijective (then,  $X_f\cap X_g=\emptyset$), we can find a rule sequence for which the t-NUCA has no {\em non-reachable} configuration. This provides the evidence that, $X_f\cap X_g=\emptyset$ is a necessary condition for a t-NUCA which has no non-reachable configuration.

\begin{lemmaa}\label{prop1}
	There exists a t-NUCA consists of $f$ and $g$ with a rule sequence, for which the t-NUCA has no non-reachable configuration if $G_f$ or $G_g$ is bijective. 
\end{lemmaa}

\begin{proof}
	Without loss of generality, let us consider that $G_f$ is bijective. Construct a rule sequence of a t-NUCA where first rule $f$ is used and then rule $g$ is used. If $G_g$ is non-bijective, then there exist some {\em non-reachable} configurations in the CA $g$. Suppose $X_g$ is the set of {\em non-reachable} configuration of $G_g$. However, all members of $X_g$ are reachable by $G_f$. Since $G_f$ is used first in the sequence, all the configurations, including $X_g$, are reachable during the t-NUCA evolution. Hence the proof. 
\end{proof}

From the proof of Lemma~\ref{prop1}, a number of rule sequences, where one rule is bijective, are identified for which a {\em t-NUCA} has no non-reachable configuration. 

A CA with no non-reachable configurations implies that the CA is {\em surjective}. For a {\em t-NUCA}, if both the rules are not {\em surjective} then the {\em t-NUCA} is not {\em surjective}. However, if there exists a rule sequence for which, all configurations can be reached, then one can say that the {\em t-NUCA} has no non-reachable configuration for that sequence. Hence, the {\em t-NUCA} can behave like a {\em surjective} CA, though the rules are not {\em surjective}. In this direction, the {\em t-NUCAs}, consist of non-surjective ECAs, behaves like surjective CA for some rule sequences. We named this type of {\em t-NUCA} as {\em restricted surjective t-NUCA}. 

\begin{defnn}
	A t-NUCA is restricted surjective if there exists a set of configuration to which all the other configuration can be reached during evolution and the t-NUCA has no non-reachable configuration.
\end{defnn}

In other word, for a {\em t-NUCA}, if all the configurations are covered during evolution, then the t-NUCA is called {\em restricted surjective} t-NUCA. Obviously, all {\em surjective } t-NUCAs are {\em restricted surjective} but the reverse is not true.

Let us consider that $(G_t)_{t\geq1}$ is the sequence of CAs used in a {\em t-NUCA}. Here, $G_t$ is either $G_f$ or $G_g$. We can now define a restriction on $G_t$ for some set $\mathcal{C}_t\subseteq \mathcal{C}$, i.e., $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}(x)=G_t(x)$ for $x\in\mathcal{C}_t$. This implies, the restriction of $G_t$ to $\mathcal{C}_t$ is the same function as $G_f$ or $G_g$. Then we get a sequence of restrictions $(G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t})_{t\geq 1}$. The following propositions are noted based on this considerations,

\begin{proposition}\label{prop3}
	A t-NUCA is {\em restricted surjective} for $t\geq 1$, if it satisfies the following conditions,
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}=G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}(\mathcal{C}_t)$
		\item Each $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}: \mathcal{C}_t\rightarrow\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ is surjective
		\item There exists an $n\in\mathbb{N}$, for which, $\bigcup_{t=1}^{n-1} \mathcal{C}_{t+1}=\mathcal{C}$
	\end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
	Let us assume $\mathcal{C}$ be a set of all configuration of t-NUCA. By condition $1$, $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}=G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}(\mathcal{C}_t)$ where each $\mathcal{C}_t\subseteq\mathcal{C}$ and $G_t\in\{G_f,G_g\}$. Now, we suppose that without loss of generality, each restriction $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}~:~\mathcal{C}_t\rightarrow\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$, $\forall t\geq1$ have onto map, that is surjective (Condition~$2$).

	In a non-surjective $G_t$, there exists non-reachable configuration. However, by condition $2$, in each restrictions, each reachable configuration in $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ has a predecessor. Now, let all configurations are reachable in a t-NUCA if each non reachable configuration of $G_{t_1}|_{\mathcal{C}_{t_1}}$ is reachable by $G_{t_2}|_{\mathcal{C}_{t_2}}$ where ${t_1}\neq{t_2}$. That is, $\bigcup_{t=1}^{n-1} \mathcal{C}_{t+1}=\mathcal{C}$ which is exactly equivalent to the condition $3$.

	Hence the proof. 
%	Let us assume $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ be a set of next configurations of set $\mathcal{C}_t$ for a function $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}: \mathcal{C}_t\rightarrow\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ where, $t\geq 1$. The second condition depicts that, the function $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}$ is surjective. Hence, the function shows onto map.
%	
%	Next, let us focus on the third condition, which considers the union of each   $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}, t\geq 1$. Let us assume the union of all the $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}, t\geq 1$ is equal to $\mathcal{C}$, this implies, all the configurations are reached during evolution of the t-NUCA. Hence, the t-NUCA has no non-reachable configuration.
%	
%	These are the evidence that, a t-NUCA is restricted surjective if it satisfies all the above conditions.
%	
\end{proof}


\begin{example}\label{ps-ex}
	Let us consider a {\em t-NUCA} with ECAs $15$ and $25$. Suppose the sequence is OEIS $A001651$, i.e., $15,15,25,15,15,25,\cdots$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:15X25}). In this t-NUCA, no non-reachable configuration is there. Hence the CA is restricted surjective. However, ECA $25$ is not surjective, hence the t-NUCA is not a surjective t-NUCA.
\end{example}
