\begin{proposition}\label{prop-restricted-surjective}

A t-NUCA is {\em restricted injective} if the t-NUCA satisfies the following conditions,
\begin{enumerate}
	\item $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}=G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}(\mathcal{C}_t)$
	\item Each $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}: \mathcal{C}_t\rightarrow\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ is injective
	\item For two functions $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_i}$ and $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_j}$, $x_1=x_2$ iff $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_i}(x_1)=G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_j}(x_2)$, where $x_1\in\mathcal{C}_i$ and $x_2\in\mathcal{C}_j$ (the same clause applies to $G_g$).
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}

	Let us first assume that each $\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ contains all reachable configurations (Condition $1$). Now we suppose that each $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}: \mathcal{C}_t\rightarrow\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ has one-to-one map, that is injective (Condition $2$). This ensures that each configuration say $x \in \mathcal{C}_{t+1}$ has unique predecessor for each restriction $G_t|_{\mathcal{C}_t}$%: \mathcal{C}_t\rightarrow\mathcal{C}_{t+1}$. 

%	Let us assume a configuration, say $x$ is in $\mathcal{C}_{i+1}$ and $\mathcal{C}_{j+1}$ is reachable by $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_i}$ and $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_j}$ respectively. Now 


	Let us assume the configuration $x$ are in both $\mathcal{C}_{i+1}$ and $\mathcal{C}_{j+1}$, and the restrictions are $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_i}$ and $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_j}$. According to condition $2$, $x$ has one unique predecessor say $x_1\in\mathcal{C}_i$ for restriction $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_i}$ and another unique predecessor $x_2\in\mathcal{C}_j$ for restriction $G_f|_{\mathcal{C}_j}$. Now, according to the definition of restricted injectivity, since same $G_f$ is considered in the restrictions, $x_1$ and $x_2$ should be same, i.e., $x_1=x_2$ which is exactly equivalent to the condition $3$.

	Hence the proof.
