\begin{remark}
	Let us consider a t-NUCA, consist of $f$ and $g$, and  $\mathcal{C}_x$ and $\mathcal{C}_y$ are the set of configurations, for which the CAs $G_f$ and $G_g$ have many-to-one map respectively.  Then the t-NUCA is restricted injective if  $\mathcal{C}_x \cap \mathcal{C}_y=\emptyset$. 
\end{remark}

It is obvious that, if $G_f$ and  $G_g$ shows many-to-on map for the same set of configurations, then there exists no restrictions for which the t-NUCA shows restricted injectivity.

Whenever a {\em t-NUCA} shows restricted bijectivity, i.e., restricted surjective and restricted injective for a set of initial configurations, say $\mathcal{C}_{in}$, the time-reversed dynamics can be observed in the {\em t-NUCA} if it starts journey from any configuration of $\mathcal{C}_{in}$. Although the {\em t-NUCA} is not bijective, there is no information loss throughout evolution. Therefore, the {\em t-NUCA} can be inverted with respect to time, similar to the behavior of reversible {\em t-NUCA}.

\begin{defnn}
	A t-NUCA is said to be restricted reversible iff the t-NUCA is restricted surjective and restricted injective, that is restricted bijective.
\end{defnn}

For example, let us consider a $4$-cell {\em t-NUCA} $(7,40)[A001651]$. The {\em t-NUCA} has no non-reachable configuration (see Figure~\ref{fig:7X40}). So, it is a restricted surjective {\em t-NUCA}. The {\em t-NUCA} is also {\em restricted injective} (see Example~\ref{ri-ex}), hence the t-NUCA is {\em restricted reversible}.

It is described that there exists a sequence for which a {\em t-NUCA} is {\em restricted reversible}, if any one rule is bijective. However there exists some {\em t-NUCA} where both the rules are not bijective. But, if all the configurations of a set of no-reachable configurations, say $X_f$ (resp. $X_g$) can be reachable by $G_g$ (resp. $G_f$) then, one can find that, $X_f\cap X_g=\emptyset$.

The above argument is generalized in the following proposition.

\begin{proposition}
	Let us assume a t-NUCA consists of two irreversible ECAs $f$ and $g$ where the first rule of the rule sequence is $f$ and $\mathcal{C}$ is the set of all configurations. Let,
	\begin{align*}
		S_f&=\{y~|~y=G_f(\mathcal{C})\}\\
		S_g&=\{z~|~z=G_g(S_f)\}
	\end{align*}
	The t-NUCA is not a restricted / week reversible t-NUCA if it satisfy any of the following condition --
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $S_g \subseteq S_f$
		\item $\exists y, y\in  \{\mathcal{C}-S_g\}$, such that, $y\notin S_f$% 
	\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
	This proposition first assumes that $S_f$ and $S_g$ are the sets of reachable configurations of a t-NUCA by $f$ and $g$ respectively where $f$ is the first rule in the rule sequence. Let $\mathcal{C}$ be the set of all configurations of the t-NUCA.

	\textbf{Case 1:} Since, $f$ is the first rule in the rule sequence, if $f$ is not bijective then, $S_f\subset \mathcal{C}$. The best case scenario of getting maximum number of reachable configurations by $g$ is that, $g$ is applied after the first $f$ (applied second) in the rule sequence, because $G_f^k(\mathcal{C})\subseteq G_f(\mathcal{C}), k>1$. Now, $S_g=\{y~|~y=G_g(S_f)\}$, if $S_g \subseteq S_f$ then the non-reachable configurations of $f$, i.e., $\{\mathcal{C}-S_f\}$ are also non-reachable by $g$. There exists non-reachable configuration in the t-NUCA, i.e., the t-NUCA is not restricted surjective. Hence, the t-NUCA is not a restricted / week reversible t-NUCA.

	\textbf{Case 2:} Let us assume, $\exists y \in \mathcal{C}, y\notin S_f$, where, $y$ is the non reachable configuration of $f$. Now, if $y\notin G_g(S_f)$ then $y$ become the non-reachable configuration of the t-NUCA. There exists non-reachable configuration in the t-NUCA, i.e.,  $\mathcal{C} - \{S_f\cup S_g\} \neq \emptyset$ and the t-NUCA is not a restricted surjective. Hence, the t-NUCA is not restricted/week reversible.     

	Hence the proof. 

\end{proof}


In this direction, one can observe that the `{\em reversibility}' of the {\em t-NUCA} depends on the rules but does not depend on the rule sequence. However, the {\em restricted reversibility} of {\em t-NUCA} depends on both the rules as well as the rule sequence. Hence, the {\em restricted reversibility} is rule sequence sensitive. Further, the {\em restricted reversibility} of a {\em t-NUCA} is initial configurations sensitive. 

\vspace{2 em}

Let us further explore the remaining irreversible {\em t-NUCAs} (the irreversible {\em t-NUCAs} excluding {\em restricted reversible} {\em t-NUCAs}). We find that some of them are {\em restricted surjective} but not {\em restricted injective}. Whenever a {\em t-NUCA}  shows {\em restricted surjectivity} then there exist no {\em non-reachable} configuration. If many-to-one map may exist in the {\em t-NUCA}, the {\em t-NUCA} does not show {\em restricted bijectivity}. Although all configurations are reachable, there exist information-loss during evolution. Hence, the {\em t-NUCAs} can not be reversed with respect to time. If we consider the lattice is finite, then the configurations of such t-NUCAs form cycles (since, no non-reachable configuration exists). For classical CAs under finite lattice, if all configurations are reachable, then the CA is reversible, and the configurations form cycles. From that resemblance with the reversibility behavior of finite CA, let us call such {\em t-NUCAs} as {\em weakly reversible t-NUCAs}. 


\begin{defnn}
	A t-NUCA is called as weakly reversible if the CA is restricted surjective.
\end{defnn}

Proposition~\ref{prop1} has proved that there exists a rule sequence in a {\em t-NUCA} for which the t-NUCA has no non-reachable configurations if $G_f$ or $G_g$ is bijective. Hence, one can find that there exists at least one rule sequence for  which a {\em t-NUCA} is weakly reversible if any rule is bijective.

