There exists no {\em non-reachable} configuration in a t-NUCA if $X_g\subseteq \mathcal{C}_f$ and $X_f\subseteq \mathcal{C}_g$, where $\mathcal{C}_f$ and $\mathcal{C}_g$ are the sets of reachable configurations by $f$ and $g$ in the t-NUCA respectively. Such a {\em t-NUCA} is {\em restricted surjective}, that is, all the configurations are reached during evolution. Although there exist no non-reachable configuration, the {\em t-NUCA} is not {\em restricted injective} (many-to-one map exists). As a result, the t-NUCAs are able to develop cycles when the lattice is considered as finite.  %Such t-NUCAs are referred to as {\em weakly reversible} t-NUCAs. 


\section{Cyclic behavior}\label{Cyclic behavior}

Let us now turn our attention to the finite lattice size only. Under this setting, we explore here cyclic behavior of a {\em t-NUCA}. To facilitate our study, we introduce following terminologies by taking vocabulary from {\em Markov Chain} theory.

\begin{defnn}
	For an initial configuration $c\in\mathcal{S}^\mathcal{L}$, a configuration $x\in \mathcal{S}^\mathcal{L}$ is transient if the configuration can not be reachable after leaving it. In contrast, a configuration is recurrent for $c$ if it is not transitory and the configuration can be reachable after leaving it.
\end{defnn}


In the transition diagram of a {\em t-NUCA} in Figure~\ref{fig:3X15}(c), configuration $5$ is transient for the initial configuration $5$, whereas configurations $0$ and $15$ are recurrent for the same initial configuration. If the CA evolves further, the configurations $0$ and $15$ are visited again, but the configuration $5$ can never be visited. Here, configuration $5$ is a non-reachable configuration. Similarly, if the {\em t-NUCA} starts journey from $9$, configurations $2$ and $12$ are recurrent. However, if the {\em t-NUCA} starts journey from $4$, then configuration $2$, which was recurrent, becomes transient here. Note that, configuration $2$ is a reachable configuration in both the scenarios.

There are three parameters to decide if a configuration of a {\em t-NUCA} is recurrent: the rules ($f$ and $g$), a rule sequence, and a set of initial configurations ($\mathcal{C}_{in}$). We next explore the recurrent configurations in a {\em t-NUCA}.

