\subsection{The recurrent configurations and cycles}

The recurrent configurations are visited many times during evolution of a {\em t-NUCA}. Let us call the time required to recur a configuration as {\em recurrent length}. Then, for a recurrent configuration, we can get a sequence of {\em recurrent lengths}. We shall denote this sequence by $\Lambda^c_x$, where $x$ is the recurrent configuration for the initial configuration $c$.

Let us consider a $4$-cell {\em t-NUCA} with ECA rules $7$ and $40$ where rule $7$ is applied in the following sequence: $1,2,4,5,7,8,\cdots$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:7X40}). Here, $10$ is a recurrent configuration for the initial configuration $5$. One can check that $\Lambda^5_{10}=(1,1,4,1,1,4,1,\cdots)$. 

In some cases, it is observed that a sub-sequence of recurrent lengths repeats in the sequence. In the above example, the lengths $(1,1,4)$ repeats in $\Lambda^5_{10}$. Hence, this sequence is periodic. Following is an interesting result.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop4}
	The sequence of recurrent lengths in $\Lambda^c_x$ of a recurrent configuration $c$ is periodic iff the rule sequence is periodic.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
	Let $\Lambda^c_x=(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\cdots)$. Suppose the recurrent length $\lambda_1$ is obtained by applying the rule sequence $s_1$, $\lambda_2$ by $s_2$ and so on. So, the rule sequence for this case is $S=(s_1,s_2,\cdots)$. Suppose, $S$ is periodic. Then $s_1$ will periodically be observed. Since $x$ is a recurrent configuration, at same time the CA is to be at configuration $x$ before following the rule sub-sequence $s_1$. Then the recurrent length $\lambda_1$ is to be observed. Subsequently, the recurrent lengths $\lambda_2,\lambda_3,\cdots$ will be observed. This makes $\Lambda^c_x$ as periodic.

	Now consider $\Lambda^c_x$ as periodic. That is, $\Lambda^c_x=(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\cdots,\lambda_l)^+$ for some $l\geq 1$ corresponding rule sequence become $(s_1,s_2,\cdots,s_l)^+$. This is obviously a periodic rule sequence. Hence the proof.

\end{proof}

We now define cycle and cycle lengths for recurrent configuration.
\begin{defnn}
	A configuration is cyclic if it repeats after a fixed (say, $\mathcal{K}$) number of time steps. All the configurations, visited in the $\mathcal{K}$ time steps form a cycle, and $\mathcal{K}$ is called the cycle length.
\end{defnn}

Hence, according to Proposition~\ref{prop4}, recurrent configurations of a {\em t-NUCA} with periodic rule sequence can form cycles. Unlike the cycle of classical CAs, a cycle of {\em t-NUCA} may contain a configuration multiple times. If $\Lambda^c_x=(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\cdots,\lambda_l)^+$, then the corresponding cycle length $\mathcal{K}=\lambda_1+\lambda_2+\cdots+\lambda_l$. A {\em t-NUCA} with aperiodic rule sequence has no such $\mathcal{K}$. Hence, all the configurations of such {\em t-NUCAs} may cyclic in their configuration space, but the exact cycle length can not be found.
\begin{proposition}
	All configurations of a reversible t-NUCA with finite lattice size and periodic rule sequence are recurrent.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}	
	Let us consider the rule sequence is periodic and the {\em t-NUCA} is reversible. That implies, the rules $f$ and $g$ are bijective. Also assume that the t-NUCA is finite. It is obvious that, if the rule sequence consists of only one rule (say $f$), then for a finite t-NUCA all configurations are recurrent (since both the rules are bijective).

	Let us consider such rule sequences, where both the rules have been utilized. Since the sequence is periodic, $f$ and $g$ are utilized repeatedly after fixed time interval. That implies, a configuration can be reached multiple times (since, the t-NUCA is finite). An initial configuration can also be reached multiple times during evolution since, there exists no non-reachable configuration of $f$ and $g$, if we consider all configurations as initial configurations, then each configuration is reached multiple times during evolution.  Hence the proof.
\end{proof}

Let us now study the graph theoretic properties of a combined transition diagram to explore more about cycles of a {\em t-NUCA}. Each transition diagram consists of several connected components (connected sub-graphs). A connected component of a graph is a sub-graph.% in which a path exists between every pair of nodes of the sub-graph. 

\subsection{Cycles of t-NUCA from cycles of f and g}

For a lattice size $n$, we can get transition diagram for CAs $f$ and $g$. These two diagrams can be combined into a single one, and the cyclic behavior of the {\em t-NUCA} can be examined from this combined transition diagram. Let us assume $G_1=(V,E_1)$ and $G_2=(V,E_2)$ be the transition diagrams (labelled graph) of CAs $f$ and $g$ respectively. This combined transition diagram is a {\em multigraph}, which is constructed by combining $G_1$ and $G_2$, i.e., the combined transition diagram is defined by $G=(V,E)$, where $E=E_1\cup E_2$. All the edges of $E_1$ and $E_2$ are labelled by different indicators in the {\em multigraph} $G$. The labelled {\em multigraph} $G$ contains edges with own identity (in Figure~\ref{fig:15X180_4}(c), the edges are labelled as black or blue for $f$ and $g$ respectively). A pair of nodes may be connected by more than one labelled edge.

