\section{Introduction}
\label{SE Introduction}

\noindent
Our objective is to suggest an explicit analogue of the Higman Embedding Theorem proven in the fundamental article \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}, i.e., an algorithm that for a given recursive group $G$ constructively outputs its explicit embedding into a finitely presented group $\mathcal G$  given by its generators and defining relations directly, see the definitions and references in Section~\ref{SU Recursive enumeration and recursive groups}. 
Algorithm~\ref{AL Algorithm for explicit embedding of a recursive group} is outlined in Section~\ref{SU The explicit embedding algorithm} below, and it is based on some auxiliary facts and constructions from our recent work \cite{Embeddings using universal words,
The Higman operations and  embeddings,
On explicit embeddings of Q}.

Higman shows \textit{possibility} of such an embedding by proving that a group with finitely many generators (or a group with effectively enumerable countably infinite generators) is embeddable into a finitely presented group, as soon as it is recursive, see Theorem 1 and Corollary on p.\,456 in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}. 
%
Since it is a simple combinatorial observation that inside a finitely presented group any subgroup generated by a set of effectively enumerated generators is recursive, the main part of arguments in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} is to prove sufficiency in Theorem~1, i.e., \textit{existence} of embedding; and that is the part we plan to make explicit. 



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{On explicitness of the embedding in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}}
\label{SU On explicitness of the embedding in Higmans work}  

Higman has never alluded  the embedding of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} is explicit. 
Moreover, for some groups with very uncomplicated recursive presentations it is (or was until recently) an open problem to explicitly embed them into finitely pre\-sen\-ted groups, see sections~\ref{SU An application of the algorithm for Q}, \ref{SU The problem of explicit  embedding for GL(n Q)} below. 
%
However, in \cite{Valiev example} Valiev remarks:
\textit{``G. Higman's proof is constructive in the sense that from  constructive description of the group $G$ one can, in principle, extract a description for [the finitely presented group], but this construction is so vast that it is practically impossible to do this.''}
%Under $K_G$ Valiev means the finitely presented group into which the recursive groups is embedded $G$. Below we prefer to use the notation $\mathcal G$ for other purpose.
%
