\subsection{Other embedding methods}
\label{SU Other embedding methods}

After Higman's paper \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} a few further articles also appeared to prove the same theorem by other methods, see 
\cite{Valiev, Aanderaa, Adyan Durnev}, etc. The textbooks  
\cite{Lyndon Schupp, Rotman} reflect and modify their techniques, see also the Appendix in \cite{Shoenfield}.  

The reason why we for Algorithm~\ref{AL Algorithm for explicit embedding of a recursive group} stick with the  methods of Higman is not only the fact that \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} is the oldest and most celebrated approach, but also that it is based on the \textit{classic} definition of recursion via  composition, primitive recursion and minimization applied to the zero, successor and projections functions, see \cite{Davis, Rogers, Boolos Burgess Jeffrey}.
%
The above cited other methods, on the contrary, are deducing recursion to ``third party'' results, such as, 
the solution of Hilbert's Tenth Problem \cite{Matiyasevich Diophantine} showing that a subset of $\Z^n$ is recursively enumerable if and only if it is Diophantine; 
the interpretation of Turing machines via semigroups \cite{Aanderaa, Rotman};
the Boone-Britton group  \cite{Boone Certain simple unsolvable problems, Britton The word problem}; the group diagrams \cite{Rotman}, etc.








