\subsection{Is Higman's embedding constructive?}
\label{SU Is Higman's construction explicit} 


Suspicion, that not all steps of Higman's original construction \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} may be constructive, arise already from the fact that for some well-known recursive groups the problem of their explicit embedding  into finitely presented groups has been (or still is) open for decades. 
See, for example, the question on explicit embedding of the recursive group $\Q$ into a finitely presented group asked in \cite{De La Harpe 2000, kourovka, Johnson on Higman's interest} and outlined in Section~\ref{SU An application of the algorithm for Q} above, including the cited remark of Johnson about embedding of $\Q$ that \textit{``continues to elude us''}.
%
Yet another example of this type is the matrix group $GL(n, \Q)$ mentioned in Section~\ref{SU The problem of explicit  embedding for GL(n Q)}.

As far as we know, neither Higman himself nor his descendants had ever stated explicitness of the embedding of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} in the literature.

\smallskip
Besides such indirect arguments of ``historical'' nature, it is reasonable to analyze the steps of Higman embedding \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} to directly indicate its parts which suggest \textit{no explicit algorithm} to accomplish them, i.e., to state what makes some parts of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} non-constructive.

\smallskip
With this objective in mind, suppose a recursive group is explicitly given as $G = \langle\, X \mathrel{|} R \,\rangle$, and try to follow Higman's construction \textit{literally} to see which obstacles we face.


\begin{Remark}
\label{RE we are not using the below steps}
Notice that in our modified constructions in the proofs of
Theorem~\ref{TH Theorem A}
and
Theorem~\ref{TH Theorem B}
below we are \textit{not} going to use those steps causing obstacles. We mention those parts just to study explicitness of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}.
\end{Remark}



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsubsection{Construction of $\mathcal X$ and $A_{\!\mathcal X}$ from $R$}
\label{SU Construction of X and A_X from R}

To build the finitely presented group $K$ used in Step 5 for the given recursive group $G = \langle\, X \mathrel{|} R \,\rangle$, see \ref{SU Step 5. Final embedding by Higman Rope Trick} above, \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} starts from the recursively enumerable relations set $R$ in the free group $F$ of rank $|\,X|$. As the  enumeration of $R$ is very easy to continue on its normal closure, $\langle R \rangle^F$\! also is recursively enumerable (\cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} denotes this closure by $R$).

Then ``moving backwards'' via Higman's
Step~4\,--\,Step~2 for $\langle R \rangle^F$\!, one should arrive to the respective  subset $\mathcal X$ inside $\E$, and to the benign subgroup $A_{\!\mathcal X}$ inside the free group $\langle a,b,c\rangle$ of rank $3$.

To do this Higman first embeds the group $F$ into a free group $F_2=\langle x,y \rangle$ of rank $2$, see p.\,474 in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}, using any of the well known textbook methods, check the chapters concerning the subgroups of free groups in any of 
\cite{Robinson, 
Rotman, 
Kargapolov Merzljakov,
Bogopolski}. 
%
By the remark preceding Lemma~3.8 in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}, the normal subgroup $\langle R \rangle^F$ is benign in $F$ if and only if it is benign in $F_2$. 
%
Hence, we can imagine all the relations from $\langle R \rangle^F$ are rewritten in just \textit{two} letters $x,y$.
%
The purpose of this passage is that it allows to trivially output the integer sequences $f\in \mathcal X$ from words on $x,y$. For example, from the relation $x^2 y^5 x^3 \in F_2$ one can extract the sequence $f\!=\!(2, 5, 3)\in \mathcal X$. This $f$ later is being used in the free group $\langle a,b,c\rangle$ to produce the product 
$b_f=
b_{0}^{2} 
b_{1}^{5}
b_{2}^{3}$ and the conjugate $a_{f}\!=a^{b_f}$ inside $A_{\mathcal X}$, 
see Section~\ref{SU Defining subgroups by integer sequences} above.

In the next steps of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} Higman is going to use this benign subgroup $A_{\mathcal X}$ of $\langle a,b,c\rangle$ 
to build, for the benign subgroup $\langle R \rangle^F$ of $F_2$, the respective finitely presented overgroup $K_{\mathcal X}$ with a finitely generated subgroup $L_{\mathcal X} \le K_{\mathcal X}$, such that $F_2 \cap L_{\mathcal X} = \langle R \rangle^F$ holds.

\smallskip
This passage, regretfully, creates two hurdles for the coming steps in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}:

\smallskip
\textit{Firstly}, Higman's process outputs the groups $K_{\!\mathcal X}$ and  $L_{\!\mathcal X}$ \textit{not} directly for the initial $F$ from the original presentation $G=F/\langle R \rangle^F$\!,\, but for the auxiliary free group $F_2$.  Thus, following \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}, we have to ``return back'' from $F_2$ to $F$ in order to modify the obtained $K_{\!\mathcal X}$ and  $L_{\!\mathcal X}$ for the free group $F$ of rank $|\,X|$. 
%
That part is not hard to do by a few routine steps because by the remark before Lemma~3.8 in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}, a group is benign in $F_2$ if and only if it is benign in $F$.
%
In order \textit{not} to introduce new notation, suppose the above $K_{\mathcal X}$ and $L_{\mathcal X}$ are those found for $F$, that is, $F \cap L_{\mathcal X} = \langle R \rangle^F$ holds inside a certain finitely presented $K_{\mathcal X}$ for the recursive subgroup $\langle R \rangle^F$\!.

\smallskip
\textit{Secondly}, the passage from $R$ to $\langle R \rangle^F$ very much amplifies the set of relations we have to work with. For, even if the group $G = \langle\, X \mathrel{|} R \,\rangle$ is defined with some plainly simple set $R$, the subgroup $\langle R \rangle$ and the normal closure $\langle R \rangle^F$ may contain a by far \textit{wider} stockpile of relations, which generates a by far \textit{wider} set $\mathcal X$, and makes it even harder to write that set via the operations 
\eqref{EQ Higman operations} 
in the coming steps.
The reader could take, say, the $3$-generator direct power $G=\Z^3$\! of the cycle $\Z$, and starting from its \textit{three} defining relations, create an infinite set of new relations in the closure $\langle R \rangle^F$. Then each of these new relations provides a new sequence $f$ to be included in the ``exploding'' set $\mathcal X$, which then has to be built via \eqref{EQ Higman operations}. 


\begin{Remark}
\label{RE we do not use <R>^F}
These two obstacles could already be sufficient for us to refrain from naming the construction in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} an explicit one. Yes, some additional proofs can be added to it to overcome these barriers at the cost of complicating the whole process. 
%
