\subsubsection{Explicit construction of $\mathcal X$ via the Higman operations}
\label{SU Explicit construction of X via Higman operations}

According to Step 2, see \ref{SU Step 2. Characterization of the recursively enumerable subsets via benign subgroups} above, $A_{\!\mathcal X}$ is benign if and only if the set $\mathcal X$ is recursively enumerable in $\E$; and by Step 1, see \ref{SU Step 1. Characterization of recursively enumerable subsets via Higman operations}, that only happens when the set of functions $\mathcal X$ belongs to $\mathscr S$\!. Hence, the next objective is to \textit{explicitly} construct $\mathcal X$ via the Higman operations
\eqref{EQ Higman operations} from $\Zz$ and $\S$.

Lemma 2.8 in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} provides a long but \textit{yet explicit} algorithm to construct certain partial recursive functions $f(n,r)$, $a(r)$, $b(r)$, with $r=0,1,2,\ldots$, which one-by-one ``record'' all the sequences $g\in\mathcal X$, and ``mark'' their start- and end points, in the sense that for any such $g$ there is some $r$ for which $g(n)=f(n,r)$ for all $n\in \Z$, while $f(n,r)=0$ for all the remaining coordinates $n<a(r)$ and $n>b(r)$. 
%
Such functions 
$f_{\E}(n,r)$, 
$a_{\E}(r)$, 
$b_{\E}(r)$  
are explicitly built first for the case of the whole set $\mathcal X = \E$, and then for any other generic recursively enumerable subset $\mathcal X \subseteq \E$ there exists a partial recursive function $h(s)$ such that the $r$'th function $f_{\E}(n,r)$ corresponds to a function in $\mathcal X$ if and only if $r=h(s)$ for some $s$. Existence of such $h(s)$ is clear, as $\mathcal X$ is recursively enumerable, and it consists of functions with finite supports only. 

Then for every multi-variable integer-based function $f(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ its \textit{graph} is being defined before Lemma 2.2, and $\mathscr F$ is  denoted to be the set of all functions whose graphs are in $\mathscr S$. Lemmas 2.1\,--\,2.7 show that $\mathscr F$ contains all the partial recursive functions. 
%
Hence, the graphs of the above functions $f,a,b$ also are in $\mathscr F$, i.e., their graphs can be constructed via the operations \eqref{EQ Higman operations}.


Using the above steps, Higman finishes the proof of Theorem 3 on \textit{pp}.\,463\,--\,464 by showing how the earlier picked sequence $g\in \mathcal X$ can constructively be obtained  via \eqref{EQ Higman operations} using the graphs of these functions $f,a,b$.  

Although each of the listed steps is doable, and is explicit, the \textit{only} way to construct $\mathcal X$ via \eqref{EQ Higman operations} suggested in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} is to present the functions $f,a,b$ by  Kleene's characterization, that is, to construct $f,a,b$ from the basic functions \,
$n(x)=0$,\, 
$s(x)=x\!+\!1$, \,
$u_m^i(x_1,\ldots,x_m)$\,
by means of composition $\circ$, primitive recursion $\rho$, and minimization $\mu$, and then to apply a series of operations \eqref{EQ Higman operations} for each of the steps of that characterization to construct their graphs via \eqref{EQ Higman operations}, then to load these into the explicit proof of Theorem 3.

These proofs certainly are constructive, but they require such a vast routine of steps that their application to non-trivial groups is a hard task. In construction of our explicit embeddings for recursive groups, in particular for the group $\Q$ in \cite{On explicit embeddings of Q}, we never follow it, see Remark~\ref{RE we are not using the below steps}. We use the much shorter constructions from \cite{The Higman operations and  embeddings} where applicable.




%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsubsection{Missing steps in construction of $K_{\!\mathcal X}$ and  $L_{\!\mathcal X}$}
\label{SU Missing steps in 
construction of K_X and L_X}

Now assume the set $\mathcal X$ has already been constructed from $\Zz$ and
$\S$ via some known sequence of operations \eqref{EQ Higman operations}. Try to directly follow the steps of proof for Theorem~4, see lemmas 4.4\,--\,4.10 in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}, in order to explicitly construct the finitely presented overgroup $K_{\!\mathcal X}$ and its finitely generated subgroup $L_{\!\mathcal X}$ for the benign subgroup $A_{\!\mathcal X}$ in $\langle a,b,c\rangle$. There are two reasons which do \textit{not} allow us to use these proofs from \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} \textit{directly}:

\textit{Firstly}, Higman notices on p.\,468 in  \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} that if $A$ is a subgroup of two finitely generated groups $G$ and $H$ which are embeddable into some finitely presented group, then $A$ is benign in both $G$ and $H$, or in neither. For the theoretical purposes of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} this makes it very convenient to sometimes ``switch'' the group in which we show $A$ is benign. This shortens the proofs of lemmas 4.4\,--\,4.10 very much, but this is \textit{not} sufficient for our practical purposes, because after each Higman operation we wish to always keep the current $A_{\!\mathcal X}$ benign in the \textit{same} group $\langle a,b,c \rangle$, necessarily, so that we are in position to apply the next operation as many times as needed, to get the terminal group $A_{\!\mathcal X}$ again benign in $\langle a,b,c \rangle$. 

\textit{Secondly}, \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} often uses Lemma 3.7 stating that if $H$ is the image of $G$ under a homomorphism $\phi$, then under some conditions $A$ is benign in $G$ if and only if $\phi(A)$ is be\-nign in $H$. Again, this shortens the proofs in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} very much, as it sometimes is enough to show that the given $A_{\!\mathcal X}$ is an image (or a pre-image) of a known benign subgroup. But this is \textit{not} sufficient for purposes of this work because one has to make sure if the function $\phi$ itself is \textit{computable}, and because extra steps must be added to go from the finitely presented overgroup of $\phi(A)$ (or of $\phi^{-1}(A)$) to that of $A$.

\smallskip
At the end of the construction, as we warned in Point~\ref{SU Construction of X and A_X from R}, when the groups $K_{\!\mathcal X}$ and  $L_{\!\mathcal X}$ have been constructed, \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} has to ``come back'' from $F_2$ to modify them for the case of group $F$ from the original representation $G=F/\langle R \rangle^F$ of our initial group $G = \langle\, X \mathrel{|} R \,\rangle$. Our construction below does not need this step, see Remark~\ref{RE we are not using the below steps}.

\smallskip
At the very end \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} proceeds to Step 5 with the ``Higman Rope Trick'' for some HNN-extension of the direct product $K\!\times \!G$ mentioned in  \ref{SU Step 5. Final embedding by Higman Rope Trick} above. The proofs of this step are constructive, we can use them with minimal adaptation. 


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsubsection{Conclusion}
\label{SU Conclusion}

The above points in 
\ref{SU Construction of X and A_X from R}\,--\,\ref{SU Missing steps in 
construction of K_X and L_X} show that 
the original embedding in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} involves steps that are either non-constructive (at least, in their current form), or steps that require extra amendments to become constructive. Even for some uncomplicated groups, such as the readily finitely presented $3$-generator direct power 
$\Z^3$\!, mentioned right before Remark~\ref{RE we do not use <R>^F}, we were unable to build the needed embedding by just following Higman's steps \textit{literally}.  

Yes, it could be possible to append  some new chapters to Higman's original proofs to try to make the explicit analog of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups}.
%
However, that would produce a by far longer proof cluttered with details, perhaps concealing the main purpose of Higman's work.
%
The central objective of the fundamental research 
in \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} is to reveal  deep connections between mathematical logic and group theory. Hence, overloading it with many more details could be counterproductive to achieving this major goal, we think. 

Higman's construction \textit{can} be called constructive only in the sense that it contains \textit{no theoretical obstacles} which could render it unadaptable by some (serious) changes that would make it constructive.
%
As mentioned in \nameref{SE Introduction}, constructiveness of \cite{Higman Subgroups of fP groups} and the quoted remark of Valiev from \cite{Valiev example} have been a subject of conference disputations, and we found it appropriate to consider this issue here before proposing the modified construction in the chapters below.






