\subsection{The proof for the operations $\iota$ and $\upsilon$}
\label{SU The proof for iota and upsilon} 

Suppose $\mathcal Y = \iota(\mathcal X_1, \mathcal X_2)=\mathcal X_1 \cap \mathcal X_2$, and 
the finitely presented overgroups
$K_{\mathcal X_1}$ and $K_{\mathcal X_2}$ of $F$ together with finitely generated subgroups
$L_{\mathcal X_1} \le K_{\mathcal X_1}$
and
$L_{\mathcal X_2} \le K_{\mathcal X_2}$
are explicitly given: $K_{\mathcal X_1}=\langle
\,Z_{1}
\;|\;
S_{1}
\rangle$ and $K_{\mathcal X_2}=\langle
\,Z_{2}
\;|\;
S_{2}
\rangle$, while the (finitely many) generators of $F$, $L_{\mathcal X_1}$, $L_{\mathcal X_2}$ can effectively be computed via the generators from $Z_{1}$, $Z_{2}$, respectively.

Since $K_{\mathcal X_1}$ and $K_{\mathcal X_2}$ both are overgroups of $F$, their intersection contains $F$.
Without loss of generality we may assume $K_{\mathcal X_1} \!\cap K_{\mathcal X_2}$ is strictly \textit{equal} to $F$ because these two overgroups are built independently, and the only requirement they share is to contain $F$. I.e., we may assume none of the generators of $K_{\mathcal X_1}$, except $a,b,c$, has been used in construction of $K_{\mathcal X_2}$.

Then the $\bigast$-construction $K_{\mathcal Y}=\textstyle{\bigast}_{i=1}^{2}(K_{\mathcal X_i}, L_{\mathcal X_i}, v_i)_F$ 
build for $G=M=F$
is finitely presented, and  
by Lemma~\ref{LE intersection in bigger group multi-dimensional} and 
Lemma~\ref{LE intersection in HNN extension multi-dimensional}
we have: 
$$
F \cap F^{v_1 v_2} = A_1 \cap\, A_2 = \big(F \cap L_{\mathcal X_1}\big)\cap 
\big(F \cap L_{\mathcal X_2}\big) 
\,=\,
A_{\mathcal X_1} \cap \, A_{\mathcal X_2}
= A_{\mathcal X_1 \cap\, \mathcal X_2}
=\, A_{\mathcal Y}.
$$
As a finitely generated subgroup of $K_{\mathcal Y}$ choose $L_{\mathcal Y} = F^{v_1 v_2}$ with just \textit{three} generators $a^{v_1 v_2}\!,$ $b^{v_1 v_2}\!,$ $ c^{v_1 v_2}$. As to explicit presentation of $K_{\mathcal Y}$, we may use \eqref{EQ star construction short form} and \eqref{EQ initial form of star construction} to write:
\begin{equation}
\label{EQ relations K_Y}
\begin{split}
K_{\mathcal Y}
& \!=\! 
\big\langle
\,Z_{1}, Z_{2}, v_1, v_2  
\mathrel{\,|\,}  
a(Z_{1})\!=\! a(Z_{2}),\, 
b(Z_{1})\!=\! b(Z_{2}),\,
c(Z_{1})\!=\! c(Z_{2});\\
& \hskip35mm 
\text{$v_1$ fixes the generators of $L_{\mathcal X_1}$}; \\
& \hskip35mm 
\text{$v_2$ fixes the generators of $L_{\mathcal X_2}$} 
\big\rangle
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $a(Z_{1})$ is the copy of $a$ written as a word on $Z_{1}$, $a(Z_{2})$ is the copy of $a$ written as a word on $Z_{2}$, etc.; 
we made them equal to guarantee $K_{\mathcal X_1} \!\cap K_{\mathcal X_2} = F$.

Notice that in \eqref{EQ relations K_Y} we did not include any relations identifying two copies of $M$ 
(compare to \eqref{EQ initial form of star construction}) because here $M=F$, and we had already identified the copies of $F$ in both groups in the first row of \eqref{EQ relations K_Y}.

