\end{figure}

The case  $\mathcal Y = \upsilon(\mathcal X_1, \mathcal X_2)=\mathcal X_1 \cup \mathcal X_2$ is analogous to the proof above, with one difference: we have $A_{\mathcal Y} =
A_{\mathcal X_1 \cup\, \mathcal X_2}
=\langle\,  A_{\mathcal X_1} ,\; A_{\mathcal X_2}\rangle
$. Using Lemma~\ref{LE intersection in bigger group multi-dimensional} and 
Lemma~\ref{LE join in HNN extension multi-dimensional}
we have: 
$$
F \cap 
\langle\,  F^{v_1} \!,\; F^{v_2}\rangle
=\langle\,  A_1,\; A_2\rangle
=  \big\langle F \cap L_{\mathcal X_1} ,\;
F \cap L_{\mathcal X_2}\big\rangle 
\,=\,
\langle\,  A_{\mathcal X_1} ,\; A_{\mathcal X_2}\rangle
= A_{\mathcal X_1 \cup\, \mathcal X_2}
= \,A_{\mathcal Y}.
$$
This time we choose $L_{\mathcal Y} = \langle\,  F^{v_1} \!,\; F^{v_2}\rangle$ with just \textit{six} generators 
$a^{v_1}\!,$ $b^{v_1}\!,$ $ c^{v_1}\!,$
$a^{v_2}\!,$ $b^{v_2}\!,$ $ c^{v_2}$. 
As $K_{\mathcal Y}$ we take the same group $\mathscr{A}$ used above admitting explicit presentation \eqref{EQ relations K_Y}.



