\end{equation}
where $g(j)=f(j)+n$, and $g(i)=f(i)$ for all $i\neq j$. Here we used a positive $n$, while the negative case could be covered by $f_j^-$.
Choosing yet another \textit{odd} $j$ we could apply the analog of \eqref{EQ next apply odd d_j on a_f} on $\bar a_g$. After finitely many such steps we construct all the elements $\bar a_g$ from 
$\bar A_{\vartheta \mathcal X}$. Since these $\bar a_g$ also are in $\bar F$,  we arrive to the inclusion
$
\bar A_{\vartheta \mathcal X} \subseteq
\bar F \cap \langle\, \bar A_{\mathcal X}, O\rangle$.

For the reverse inclusion apply \eqref{EQ elements <X,Y>} 
for the sets $\X=\big\{\bar a_f \;|\; f\in \mathcal X\big\}$
and $\Y=\big\{
\bar d_{j} \;|\; j=2k\!+\!1,\; k \in \Z
\big\}$.
Write every word $w\in \langle\, \bar A_{\mathcal X}, O\rangle= \langle \X,\Y \rangle$ as:
\begin{equation}
\label{EQ write w as a product u.v for pi}
