\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

We analyse a high-resolution cluster simulation obtained with the Eulerian code ENZO \citep[][]{enzo14}, already used by our group in previous work \citep[e.g.][]{va18mhd,dom19,bonafede21}. 
This ideal MHD simulation used up to eight levels of Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) to reach a peak spatial resolution of  $\Delta x = 3.95 \rm ~ kpc/cell$ in most of the cluster core, and a resolution of $\Delta x= 15.8 \rm ~ kpc/cell$ in the entire virial volume \citep[][]{va18mhd}. 
 We choose this cluster in particular  because our previous work showed that it has: i) a total mass similar to the real Coma cluster ($M_{200} \approx 1.1 \cdot 10^{15} M_{\odot}$ at $z=0.02$); ii) a turbulent kinetic pressure (within a $\sim 300 \rm ~kpc$ scale) estimated to be $\sim 5 \%$ of the total pressure he innermost $\rm Mpc^3$; iii) a profile of Faraday Rotation compatible with the real one observed in Coma \citep[][]{bo13}, arising from the small-scale dynamo turbulent amplification of an initial $0.1 \rm nG$ seed field  \citep{va18mhd}; iv) a simulated synchrotron radio power, assuming reacceleration of electrons by the turbulence in the cluster, based  on a Fermi II adiabatic stochastic acceleration model \citep[e.g.][]{2016MNRAS.458.2584B}, compatible with the real emission from Coma \citep[][]{bonafede21}.   %In summary, we want to test whether our best 3-dimensional model of gas turbulence in the Coma cluster found in a cosmological simulation can be reconciled with the apparently much lower turbulent budget estimated by recent XRISM observations, or whether there is any significant tension between simulations and reality. 
To compare with XRISM observations, we computed the continuum and line X-ray emission from all cells in the simulation within the  $[5,7] \rm ~keV$ energy rang, assuming  a constant composition and metallicity ($30 \%$ of the solar one) for all cells, with the B-APEC emission model (\url{https://heasarc.gsfc.nasa.gov/xanadu/xspec/manual/Models.html}). For our analysis, we also had to identify the turbulent part of the 3-dimensional gas velocity field in the ICM, with a 
%Gas motions in clusters of galaxies simulated in cosmology are made by two main components: i) large-scale motions, associated with ongoing matter accretions, which are anisotropic and biased in the radial direction towards the cluster centre and mostly compressive and ii) an almost isotropic turbulent component, which can be robustly identified with a high-pass filtering strategy to excise large-scale infall motions, which typically has a dominating solenoidal component  \citep[e.g.][]{do05,va09turbo,va17turb,va18turb,2019ApJ...874...42V,2020MNRAS.495..864A,2022A&A...658A.149S}.
%We identify turbulent gas motions in the 3-dimensional velocity field of the simulation with 
a small-scale filtering approach, based on previous work \citep{2006MNRAS.369L..14V,va09turbo,va11turbo,va12filter,va17turb,2020MNRAS.495..864A,2022A&A...658A.149S}.  While more complex approaches using a variable filtering length, combined with the masking of shocks can be used for a more accurate removal \citep[][]{va12filter,2020MNRAS.495..864A,2019ApJ...874...42V}, our previous work showed that setting $\Lambda_t=300 \rm ~kpc$ gives a robust enough separation between large-scale bulk motions and smaller scales isotropic turbulent motions in $\sim 10^{15} M_{\odot}$ clusters. Therefore in the remainder of this paper we estimate of the 3-dimensional turbulent velocity field is given by  $\delta \vec{v} = \vec{v} - \langle \vec{v} \rangle_{\Lambda_{t}}$,  where $\vec{v}$ is  the local velocity and density and $\langle \vec{v} \rangle_{\Lambda_{t}}$ is the velocity averaged within $\Lambda_t$.




