We consider for further illustration interactions across a plasma generated by the Heisenberg uncertainty via Casimir-induced high temperatures. In these estimates aiming to predict the magnitude of the Casimir-Yukawa interaction, we consider a $\sim0.1-2$\,femtometer separation between two perfectly conducting surfaces with a surface area for illustration chosen to correspond to that of the surface of a nuclear particle Eq.\,(\ref{BWNapproxFreeEnergy}). These estimates were chosen with either an electron-positron plasma or a quark-gluon plasma in mind.
The free energy is (for high enough temperatures at any separations or any finite temperature at large enough separations)\,\cite{PhysRevA.67.030701,EPJDNinham2014},
\begin{equation}
G(d,T)=-\frac{kT\kappa}{4\pi} \frac{e^{-2\kappa d}}{d} \Big[1+\frac{1}{2d\kappa}\Big]-\frac{(kT)^2 e^{-2 \eta d}}{\hbar c}\frac{e^{-\rho^*\eta d}}{d}+ O(e^{-4 \eta d}),
\end{equation}
where $\kappa=\omega_p/c$ where $\omega_p$ is the plasma frequency, $k$ is the Boltzmann constant, $\rho^\ast=\rho\ e^2 \hbar^2/(\pi m_e k^2\ T^2)$, $\eta=2kT/(\hbar c)$ and $\kappa$ is defined above. Both terms behave as Yukawa potentials~\cite{Ninham_Brevik_Bostrom_2022}. 
Hence, the Heisenberg relation proposed in this work leads to a high virtual temperature for perfect metal surfaces at femtometer separations. This leads to an electron-positron plasma via Eq.\,(\ref{eqn:7}) and a screened Casimir interaction. The terms both contribute to the Casimir-Yukawa binding energy, depending on the separation $\sim$0.9\,MeV from the n=0 term and $\sim$3.6\,MeV from the n$>$0 term.  Notably, this leads to a binding energy of the same magnitude as the nuclear binding energy. One can show that one gets out about the right meson mass, meson lifetime, and the correct Yukawa screening length\,\cite{Ninham_Brevik_Bostrom_2022}. Notably, if these assumptions have any relevance then distances of 1-2\,femtometer corresponds to an extremely high temperature ranging from $\sim10^{11}$\,K to $10^{12}$\,K.  This same temperature range has been predicted for Bose-Einstein critical temperatures if they could exist in stellar interiors\,\cite{Ninham_Brevik_Malyi_Bostrom_2023}.

