\begin{abstract}
Cell-free integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) systems have emerged as a promising paradigm for sixth-generation (6G) networks, enabling simultaneous high-rate data transmission and high-precision radar sensing through cooperative distributed access points (APs). Fully exploiting these capabilities requires a unified design that bridges system-level optimization with multi-target parameter estimation. This paper proposes an end-to-end graph learning approach to close this gap, modeling the entire cell-free ISAC network as a heterogeneous graph to jointly design the AP mode selection, user association, precoding, and echo signal processing for multi-target position and velocity estimation. In particular, we propose two novel heterogeneous graph learning frameworks: a dynamic graph learning framework and a lightweight mirror-based graph attention network (mirror-GAT) framework. The dynamic graph learning framework employs structural and temporal attention mechanisms integrated with a three-dimensional convolutional neural network (3D-CNN), enabling superior performance and robustness in cell-free ISAC environments. Conversely, the mirror-GAT framework significantly reduces computational complexity and signaling overhead through a bi-level iterative structure with share adjacency. Simulation results validate that both proposed graph-learning-based frameworks achieve significant improvements in multi-target position and velocity estimation accuracy compared to conventional heuristic and optimization-based designs. Particularly, the mirror-GAT framework demonstrates substantial reductions in computational time and signaling overhead, underscoring its suitability for practical deployments. 
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), dynamic graph learning, cell-free, position and velocity estimation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\maketitle
 \pagestyle{empty}
%\vspace{-0.4 cm}
\section{Introduction}

Integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) has emerged as a pivotal technology for sixth-generation (6G) wireless systems. It enables the simultaneous operation of radar sensing and data communication on a unified platform \cite{ITU}. In contrast to conventional single-node ISAC designs, a networked multi-node cooperative ISAC approach leverages geographically dispersed nodes to jointly perform both communication and sensing. This collaborative strategy significantly enhances spatial coverage, improves robustness, and increases target sensing accuracy by enabling multi-perspective observations.


Among various architectures, cell-free massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) has recently attracted considerable attention as a promising solution for cooperative ISAC. In cell-free systems, numerous geographically distributed access points (APs) collaboratively serve multiple users without predefined cell boundaries. Such dense and flexible topologies inherently support cooperative ISAC by providing high spatial diversity, effective interference management, and uniform user-centric coverage \cite{ISAC_ZD}. These attributes make cell-free architectures particularly well-suited for delivering high-quality communication services and precise radar sensing \cite{CF_ISAC}.

From the communication perspective, cell-free MIMO systems effectively mitigate cell-edge performance degradation and shadowing effects by allowing users to be simultaneously served by multiple nearby APs. This approach significantly enhances spectral efficiency and ensures uniformly high data rates across the entire service area \cite{ZhangVTM2021}. However, in practical large-scale deployments, establishing full connectivity between each user and all APs is infeasible due to limited transmit power, finite bandwidth, and network complexity. Thus, appropriate user-centric AP association strategies with optimized precoding are essential to fully capitalize on the spatial advantages of cell-free systems \cite{EmSP2021}.


From the sensing perspective, the cell-free architecture inherently supports multi-static radar sensing by enabling the simultaneous illumination and observation of targets from multiple spatial angles. Such multi-perspective radar configurations effectively overcome line-of-sight blockage issues, significantly improving target detection reliability and the accuracy of estimating parameters \cite{WCNC2025}, \cite{XJSTSP2025}. To maximize these sensing benefits, it is crucial to dynamically select each AP’s operating mode (transmit or receive) to create favorable observation geometries; to optimize precoding at transmit-mode APs in order to enhance probe signal power toward targets while suppressing illumination of clutter; and to develop  robust parameter estimation and information fusion algorithms at receive-mode APs.


Therefore, fully exploiting the advantages of cooperative cell-free ISAC systems in both communication and sensing requires a unified design framework that seamlessly integrates system-level network optimizations (such as AP mode selection, user association, and precoding) with multi-target parameter estimation. Although some prior studies \cite{cellfreeisac}-\cite{Glob2023WS} have partially explored these aspects (as summarized in Table \ref{summary}), truly comprehensive designs that jointly address both network optimization and target parameter estimation remain largely unexplored. For instance, recent studies \cite{cellfreeisac}-\cite{HYMTWC2024} investigate joint AP mode selection and precoding designs but neglect cooperative information fusion. Similarly, approaches focusing on user-centric association and precoding optimization often disregard adaptive AP role selection and effective information fusion \cite{TWC2022, ZJ2023}. Moreover, earlier works addressing cooperative information fusion and precoding strategies in cell-free ISAC \cite{WZHICASSP, ACTWC2024} lack flexible AP mode selection or user association strategies. In summary, these fragmented approaches do not fully leverage the cooperative capabilities of cell-free architectures, substantially limiting overall system performance. This highlights a critical research gap: the absence of integrated methodologies capable of jointly addressing network optimization and target parameter estimation.


Moreover, existing studies commonly assume prior knowledge of target-specific parameters (such as radar cross-section (RCS), angle-of-arrival (AoA), and channel state information (CSI)) to simplify system design \cite{HJTAES2024}. However, such assumptions are impractical in real-world deployments, since target information is often unavailable or unreliable prior to actual sensing operations. Therefore, there is an urgent need to develop adaptive ISAC systems that can dynamically configure AP operating modes and precoding strategies based on sensing feedback rather than relying on prior target information.


