To address these challenges, advanced methodologies beyond conventional optimization are required. Recently, deep learning techniques, particularly graph neural networks (GNNs), have demonstrated exceptional potential in tackling the complexities of cell-free ISAC system design \cite{Glob2023AA}-\cite{WZH1}. GNNs are naturally adept at capturing the irregular and distributed topology of cell-free deployments by representing interactions among multiple APs and users in a graph model. Building on this capability, recent studies have proposed frameworks such as sensing and communication GNN, which employs transformer-based heterogeneous GNNs for joint sensing and communication channel modeling and significantly outperforms conventional null-space projection and deep neural network (DNN) baselines \cite{WZH1}. Moreover, a link-heterogeneous GNN has been developed for cooperative positioning and communication, optimally weighting communication and sensing links through attention-based message passing \cite{LHGNN}. However, while these studies underscore the effectiveness of graph learning in addressing specific ISAC problems, a unified framework for complex joint ISAC system design, spanning from network optimization to parameter estimation, remains largely unexplored. Further investigation is required, particularly in practical scenarios where prior information about the target is unavailable.
%Despite demonstrating the effectiveness of graph learning techniques in addressing specific ISAC issues, existing studies have not yet provided a unified framework capable of comprehensively managing complex joint designs — from network optimization to parameter estimation — particularly under practical conditions where prior target information is unavailable. 


Motivated by the potential of GNNs, this paper introduces two novel heterogeneous graph-learning frameworks to address the joint design challenges in cooperative cell-free ISAC systems. These frameworks jointly optimize AP mode selection, user association, transmit precoding, and parameter estimation, with the aim of improving multi-target position and velocity estimation accuracy while maintaining high multi-user communication performance. The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{Comprehensive Cell-Free ISAC Architecture:} We present the first practical cooperative cell-free ISAC architecture with a complete system model, comprehensively addressing key design challenges such as dynamic AP mode selection, user association, cooperative transmit precoding, and distributed receiver-side parameter estimation and information fusion, all under a realistic scenario where prior target information is unavailable.

\item \textbf{Dynamic Graph Learning for Joint ISAC Design:} We develop a dynamic graph learning framework based on heterogeneous graph attention networks (GATs) to enable a multi-step ISAC design without requiring any prior target information. This framework integrates structural and temporal attention mechanisms for decision making (e.g., AP mode selection and user association). In addition, it employs graph-based message passing combined with a 3D convolutional module for transmit precoding optimization and target parameter estimation.

\item \textbf{Mirror-GAT with Reduced Complexity:} We introduce a mirror-GAT framework that achieves the same joint design objectives with significantly reduced complexity and overhead. It employs a bi-level mirror-iteration architecture with shared-edge connections and a distributed lightweight design to minimize network communication requirements and computational cost.

\item \textbf{Performance Gains and Efficiency:} Simulation results demonstrate that both proposed graph-learning frameworks significantly outperform heuristic and optimization-based designs in terms of targe estimation accuracy while maintaining high achievable rates. Notably, the mirror-GAT framework achieves substantial reductions in computational time and signaling overhead compared to the dynamic graph learning framework, underscoring its suitability for practical, large-scale deployments.
\end{itemize}



