\section{System Model and Problem Formulation}
\label{SystemModel}
\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=3.5 in]{Sys.eps}
\caption{Cell-free ISAC system.}
\label{system}
\vspace{-0.2 cm}
\end{figure}
We consider a cell-free ISAC system, as depicted in Fig.~\ref{system}. The network consists of $J$ distributed dual-function APs, each equipped with a uniform linear array (ULA) of $M$ antennas spaced at half-wavelength intervals. These APs cooperatively serve $K$ single-antenna communication users (CUs) while simultaneously sensing $Q$ potential targets.  
In this cell-free ISAC system, each AP can dynamically switch its operation mode between transmission (for communication transmissions and target illumination) and dedicated sensing reception (for target detection and estimation). This mode-switching flexibility allows the system to fully exploit available spatial degrees of freedom (DoFs), leverage multi-perspective observations, and avoid self-interference, thereby substantially enhancing overall system performance. 
Let $T$ denote the number of APs operating in transmit mode, and $R$ the number operating in receive mode, such that $T + R = J$. The corresponding sets of AP indices are defined as $\mathcal{T}$ and $\mathcal{R}$, respectively. We introduce a binary mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa} = [\kappa_1,\kappa_2,\ldots,\kappa_J]^T \in \{0,1\}^J$, where $\kappa_j = 1$ if AP $j$ is in transmit mode and $\kappa_j = 0$ otherwise. To ensure proper system configuration, the mode selection must satisfy $\sum_{j=1}^J \kappa_j = T$, thereby guaranteeing that exactly $T$ APs operate in transmit mode.


In addition to AP operating mode selection, an effective AP-CU association is also important to fully harness the spatial diversity offered by both APs and CUs. This association, which assigns each transmit-mode AP (Tx-AP) specific CUs to serve, is crucial for efficient AP resource utilization, balanced load distribution across APs, and high-quality service for all users. To explicitly characterize this association, we define the binary association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}\in \{0,1\}^{J \times K}$, where $\Lambda_{j,k} = 1$ if and only if the $j$-th AP operates in transmit mode and serves the $k$-th CU; otherwise, $\Lambda_{j,k} = 0$. Specifically, we set $\Lambda_{j,k} = 0$ for all CUs whenever $j \in \mathcal{R}$, reflecting the fact that receive-mode APs (Rx-APs) do not serve any CUs. In addition, if each Tx-AP simultaneously serves at most $K_{\mathrm{u}}$ designated CUs, the constraint $\sum_{k=1}^K \Lambda_{j,k} \leq K_{\mathrm{u}}$ must be enforced.




The considered cell-free ISAC system utilizes orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) waveforms for both communication and sensing functions. Specifically, each Tx-AP constructs OFDM frames consisting of $N_{\mathrm{s}}$ orthogonal subcarriers spanning $L$ OFDM symbols. The subcarrier spacing is denoted by $\Delta f = B/N_{\mathrm{s}}$, with $B$ being the total available bandwidth. Each OFDM symbol includes a cyclic prefix (CP) of duration $T_{\mathrm{p}}$, resulting in a total OFDM symbol duration $T_{\mathrm{sym}} = 1/\Delta f + T_{\mathrm{p}}$. 


For the $i$-th subcarrier during the $l$-th OFDM symbol, let $\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{c},i}[l]\triangleq[s_{\mathrm{c},i,1}, s_{\mathrm{c}, i,2},\ldots,s_{\mathrm{c}, i, K}]^T\in\mathbb{C}^{K\times1}$ denote the vector of modulated communication symbols for the $K$ CUs. Additionally,  for the $j$-th Tx-AP, let $\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{r},j,i}[l] \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times 1}$ represent the sensing (radar) symbol vector generated from a Zadoff-Chu (ZC) sequence. It is worth emphasizing that different Tx-APs employ distinct and quasi-orthogonal ZC sequences with coprime root indices. This strategy enables Rx-APs to reliably distinguish echo signals originating from different TX-APs, thereby facilitating multi-perspective observation and preventing ghost targets.
To simultaneously multiplex communication data and sensing sequences onto the same  subcarrier-$i$, each Tx-AP $j \in \mathcal{T}$ utilize beamforming matrices $\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{c},j,i}\triangleq[\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{c},j,i,1}, \mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{c},j,i,2},\ldots,\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{c},j,i,K}] \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times K}$ for communication and $\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{r},j,i} \triangleq [\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{r},j,i,1}, \mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{r},j,i,2},\ldots,\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{r},j,i,M}]\in \mathbb{C}^{M \times M}$ for sensing. Consequently, the signal emitted from AP-$j$ on subcarrier-$i$ during the $l$-th OFDM symbol is formulated as
\begin{align}
\mathbf{x}_{j,i}[l] = \kappa_{j}\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{r},j,i}\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{r},j,i}[l] + \kappa_{j}\sum_{k=1}^K\Lambda_{j,k}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{c},j,i,k}  s_{\mathrm{c},i,k}[l].
\end{align}

% \textcolor{blue}{For brief expression, we define the combined transmit beamforming matrix at AP~$j$ as $\mathbf{W}_{j,i} \triangleq [\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{r},j,i}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{c},j,i}] \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times (M+K)}$. Accordingly, the precoding matrix set in the designed cell-free system can be defined as $\mathcal{W}\triangleq\big\{\{\mathbf{W}_{j,i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{\rm s}}\big\}_{j=1}^{J}$.}


\subsection{Multi-User Communication Metric}
For the communication function, the received signal at the $k$-th CU on the $i$-th subcarrier during the $l$-th time slot is represented as
\be
y_{\rm c, \it i,k}[l] = \sum_{j=1}^{J}\mathbf{h}_{j,i,k}^{H}\mathbf{x}_{j,i}[l] + n_{\rm c, \it i, k}[l],
\ee
where $\mathbf{h}_{j,i,k}\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times1}$ denotes the baseband channel from the $j$-th AP to the $k$-th CU on the $i$-th subcarrier, and $n_{\rm c, \it i, k}[l]\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_{\rm c}^2)$ denotes the additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN). The corresponding SINR for the $k$-th CU on the $i$-th subcarrier is given by 
\be
\textrm{SINR}_{\rm c, \it i, k}=\frac{\big|\sum\limits_{j=1}\limits^{J}\kappa_{j}\Lambda_{j,k}\mathbf{h}_{j,i,k}^{H}\mathbf{w}_{{\rm c}, j,i,k}\big|^2}{\mu_{i,k}+\iota_{i,k}+\sigma_{\rm c}^2},
\ee
where $\mu_{i,k}$ and $\iota_{i,k}$ denote the multi-user interference and radar interference, respectively, and are defined as follows:
\begin{subequations}\begin{align}
\mu_{i,k}&=\sum\limits_{n=1,n\neq k}\limits^{K}\big|\sum\limits_{j=1}\limits^{J}\kappa_{j}\Lambda_{j,k}\mathbf{h}_{j,i,k}^{H}\mathbf{w}_{\rm c,\it j,i, n}\big|^2,   \\     \iota_{i,k}&=\sum\limits_{m=1}\limits^{M}\big|\sum\limits_{j=1}\limits^{J}\kappa_{j}\mathbf{h}_{j,i,k}^{H}\mathbf{w}_{\rm r, \it j,i, m}\big|^2.
\end{align}\end{subequations}
We employ the achievable sum-rate as the communication performance metric for the cell-free ISAC system, which can be calculated as
\begin{align}
\label{commun_metric}
R_\text{c} =\sum\limits_{k=1}^{K}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N_s}\textrm{log}_2(1+\textrm{SINR}_{\rm c, \it i, k}).
\end{align}


\subsection{Radar Sensing Metric}
For the radar sensing function, the received signal at an Rx-AP comprises target echoes, clutter interference, direct interference from TX-APs, and AWGN. Specifically, the frequency-domain baseband echo received at the $r$-th Rx-AP is represented as in \eqref{eq:yr} at the top of this page. The first term of \eqref{eq:yr} denotes the target echoes, where $\beta_{j,r,q}$ signifies the RCS of target $q$, illuminated by Tx-AP-$j$ and observed by Rx-AP-$r$, with $\mathbb{E}{|\beta_{j,r,q}|^2} = \zeta_q^2$. The distances from target $q$ to Rx-AP-$r$ and Tx-AP-$j$ are denoted as $d_{r,q}$ and $d_{j,q}$, respectively, associated with angles $\theta_{r,q}$ and $\theta_{j,q}$. The Doppler shift caused by the radial velocity component $\mathbf{v}_q$ of the target is denoted as $f_{\mathrm{d},j,r,q}$. The speed of light is denoted by $c$, and the distance-dependent path loss is expressed as $\mathrm{PL}(d)$, assuming line-of-sight (LoS) propagation. The steering vector at angle $\theta$ is defined as $\mathbf{a}(\theta) \triangleq [1, e^{\jmath\pi\sin\theta}, \ldots, e^{\jmath(M-1)\pi\sin\theta}]^T \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times 1}$. The second term in \eqref{eq:yr} captures clutter interference, where $\eta_{j,r,u}$ denotes the RCS of clutter-$u$, with $\mathbb{E}\{|\gamma_{j,r,u}|^2\} = \zeta_u^2$. Distances $d_{r,u}$, $d_{j,u}$, angles $\theta_{r,u}$, $\theta_{j,u}$, and Doppler shifts $f_{\mathrm{d},j,r,u}$ associated with clutter scatterers are defined analogously to target parameters. The third term describes direct AP-to-AP interference, characterized by the LoS channel matrix $\mathbf{G}_{j,r}$ from Tx-AP-$j$ to Rx-AP-$r$. Finally, the term $\mathbf{z}_{r,i}[l]$ represents AWGN, following the complex Gaussian distribution $\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_{\mathrm{r}}^2 \mathbf{I}_M)$.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\begin{align}\label{eq:yr}
\mathbf{y}_{r,i}[l] & =\sum_{j=1}^{J} \sum_{q=1}^{Q} \beta_{j,r,q} \sqrt{\text{PL}(d_{r,q})\text{PL}(d_{j,q})}  \mathbf{a}_{\rm r} (\theta_{r,q})\mathbf{a}_{\rm t}^T (\theta_{j,q}) \mathbf{x}_{j,i}[l]e^{-\jmath 2 \pi i \Delta f \left(d_{r,q} + d_{j,q}\right)/c} e^{\jmath 2 \pi l T_\text{sym} f_{\rm d, \it j,r,q}} \nonumber \\
&\quad + \sum_{j=1}^{J} \sum_{u=1}^{U} \eta_{j,r,u} \sqrt{\text{PL}(d_{r,u})\text{PL}(d_{j,u})} \mathbf{a}_{\rm r} (\theta_{r,u})  \mathbf{a}_{\rm t}^T (\theta_{j,u}) \mathbf{x}_{j,i}[l]e^{-\jmath 2 \pi i \Delta f \left( d_{r,u} + d_{j,u} \right)/c} e^{\jmath 2 \pi l T_\text{sym}  f_{\rm d, \it j,r,u}} \nonumber \\
&\quad + \sum_{j=1}^{J} \sqrt{\text{PL}(d_{j,r})}\mathbf{G}_{j,r}\mathbf{x}_{j,i}[l]e^{-\jmath 2 \pi i \Delta f d_{j,r}/c}+\mathbf{z}_{r,i}[l],
\end{align}  
\rule[-0pt]{18 cm}{0.05em}
\end{figure*}


All received echo signals $\mathbf{y}_{r,i}[l],~\forall r, i, l$, are aggregated into a data tensor $\mathbf{Y}_{\mathrm{r}} \in \mathbb{C}^{R \times N_{\rm s} \times L \times M}$ for subsequent processing. This tensor encapsulates the range-velocity-angle information of the targets. With the observed echo tenor $\mathbf{Y}_{\mathrm{r}}$ at Rx-APs along with the known sensing sequence tensor $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{r}}\in\mathbb{C}^{T\times M \times N_{\rm{s}} \times L}$, our task is to jointly estimate the positions and velocities of $Q$ targets. Specifically, let $\{{\mathbf{p}}_q\}_{q=1}^{Q}$ and $\{{\mathbf{v}}_q\}_{q=1}^{Q}$ represent the ground-truth target positions and velocities, respectively. Then, the joint estimation task can be expressed as the following mapping function:
\begin{equation}
\label{mapping}
\{\hat{\mathbf{p}}_q, \hat{\mathbf{v}}_q \}_{q=1}^{Q} \;=\; \mathcal{M}( \mathbf{Y}_{\mathrm{r}}, \mathbf{S}_{\mathrm r})\,. 
\end{equation} 
The sensing performance is evaluated using the root-mean-square error (RMSE) metrics, which quantify the accuracy by measuring the deviations between the true and estimated target positions/velocities. These sensing metrics are defined as: 
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{sensing_metric1}
\mathrm{RMSE_p}  &=& \sqrt{\,\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\frac{1}{Q}\sum_{q=1}^{Q} \big\|\,\mathbf{p}_{q} - \hat{\mathbf{p}}_{q}\,\big\|^2\Bigg]}, \\
   \label{sensing_metric2}
\mathrm{RMSE_v}  &=& \sqrt{\,\mathbb{E}\Bigg[\frac{1}{Q}\sum_{q=1}^{Q} \big\|\,\mathbf{v}_{q} - \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{q}\,\big\|^2\Bigg]}.    
\end{eqnarray}
% where $\{\mathbf{p}_{q}\}_{q=1}^{Q}$ and  $\{\mathbf{v}_{q}\}_{q=1}^{Q}$ denote the ground-truth positions and velocities of the targets, respectively.



\subsection{Problem Formulation}
We aim to jointly design the AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}$, user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$, transmit precoding matrices $\mathbf{W}_{j,i} \triangleq [\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{r},j,i}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{c},j,i}] \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times (M+K)},~\forall j, i$, and estimation algorithm $\mathcal{M}$ to minimize sensing error while maintaining satisfactory communication throughput. In particular, we seek to minimize the sensing RMSEs in \eqref{sensing_metric1} and \eqref{sensing_metric2},  subject to  the multi-user sum-rate $R_{\mathrm{c}}$ (defined in \eqref{commun_metric}) requirement, transmit power and association constraints. The optimization problem is formulated as  
\begin{subequations}\label{initial}
\begin{align}
&\underset{ \bm{\kappa},\,\bm{\Lambda}, \mathbf{W}_{j,i}, \mathcal{M} }{\min}~  \quad  \omega\mathrm{RMSE_p} + (1-\omega)\mathrm{RMSE_v} \label{obj_initial}\\
&\text{s.t.}~  R_{\mathrm{c}}  \geq\gamma,\label{commun_const}\\
&~~~~ \sum_{i=1}^{N_s} \big\| \mathbf{W}_{j,i} \big\|_{F}^{2} \;\le\; P_j,   \forall\, j \in \mathcal{T}, \label{power_const}\\
& ~~~~ \sum_{j=1}^J\kappa_j =  T,   \kappa_{j} \in \{0,1\}, \forall\, j \in \mathcal{J}, \label{kappa_binary} \\
& ~~~~ \sum_{k=1}^K \Lambda_{j,k} \leq K_{\mathrm{u}},  \Lambda_{j,k} \in \{0,1\},  \forall\, j \in \mathcal{J}, \forall\, k \in \mathcal{K}, \label{assoc_count}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where $\gamma$ represents the required minimum achievable rate, $P_j$ is the transmit power budget at Tx-AP-$j$, and $0 \leq \omega \leq 1$ denotes the task weight factor. 



In this cooperative cell-free ISAC system, the joint optimization of discrete parameters (such as AP mode selection and user association), precoding matrices, and estimation algorithms, as formulated in \eqref{initial}, presents inherent difficulties. First of all, due to its non-convex and mixed-integer nature, this problem cannot be effectively addressed by conventional convex optimization methods. Another critical obstacle arises from the absence of prior information about potential targets, which precludes deriving explicit performance metrics, such as the Cram\'{e}r-Rao bound (CRB), that directly relate estimation accuracy to optimization parameters. Consequently, conventional optimization-driven approaches fail to determine the optimal precoding strategies and AP scheduling. Moreover, the target estimation algorithm described in the mapping procedure \eqref{mapping} requires integrating signal-level data from multiple distributed APs. This multi-point data fusion introduces substantial computational and communication complexity, posing significant challenges for traditional centralized algorithms. Consequently, these algorithms struggle to efficiently handle and accurately process the high-dimensional, distributed data streams, thereby impairing precise estimation of target positions and velocities.

To overcome these challenges, this paper introduces two novel methodologies based on advanced graph-learning techniques. These proposed approaches effectively address the aforementioned limitations, thereby fully exploiting the inherent cooperative advantages of cell-free ISAC systems.


\section{Proposed Dynamic Graph Learning Framework} \label{A} 

In this section, we first define the heterogeneous dynamic graph structure and its corresponding cell-free ISAC system scenario, highlighting its deployment advantages. Subsequently, we propose a performance-priortized dynamic graph learning framework based on graph neural networks. Finally, we present the training strategy adopted for the proposed framework.


\subsection{Graph Definition}
\label{Graph_define}


\begin{figure*}[!t] 
\centering
\includegraphics[width=7.0 in]{define.eps}
\caption{Cell-free ISAC scenario and its corresponding heterogeneous graph structure.}
\label{scenario}
\end{figure*}

As shown in Fig. \ref{scenario}, a practical cell-free ISAC scenario can be modeled as a heterogeneous graph with multiple types of nodes and edges. Specifically, each Tx-AP, Rx-AP, and CU is represented as a node of the corresponding type (colored blue, purple, and red, respectively). The communication links in the network correspond to edges connecting Tx-AP nodes to CU nodes (colored green). Similarly, the sensing links correspond to edges connecting Tx-AP nodes to Rx-AP nodes (colored yellow). It is important to note that sensing edges capture not only the direct interference signals between Tx-APs and Rx-APs, but also the echo signals reflected from potential targets. Since no prior information about potential targets is available initially, these echo signals are not included as edges in the initial heterogeneous graph; instead, they are treated as asynchronous compensation features.


The characterizations of the ISAC network topology constitute a heterogeneous graph representation. For illustration, Fig.~\ref{scenario} presents three heterogeneous graphs corresponding to distinct network topologies. By explicitly representing the network structure through various node and edge types, this heterogeneous graph captures intricate connectivity relationships, such as AP operating modes and user association strategies. Furthermore, this graphical framework intuitively models multi-user and radar interference effects as propagation characteristics along the graph edges. Motivated by these advantages, we adopt heterogeneous graph neural networks to effectively leverage the inherent topological information within cell-free ISAC networks, thereby facilitating optimized system design.



\begin{figure*}[!t] 
\centering
\includegraphics[width=5.8 in]{net.eps}
\caption{Proposed dynamic graph learning framework.}\vspace{-0.2 cm}
\label{network}
\end{figure*}


However, due to the lack of prior information about potential targets, employing a static topology graph significantly restricts the capability to achieve globally optimal designs. Specifically, a fixed AP mode configuration, corresponding to a fixed graph topology,  fails to fully exploit the spatial DoFs inherent in cooperative ISAC deployments. Moreover, precoding matrices derived directly from a static inference methodologies inherently lack adaptive power control, thus limiting the capability to accurately estimate the positions and velocities of potential targets.


To overcome these limitations and enhance the capability for exploratory sensing of unknown target parameters, this paper introduces a novel \textit{dynamic} heterogeneous graph-based framework characterized by multiple temporal snapshots with evolving graph topologies \cite{graph}. In this proposed framework, each temporal snapshot represents an instantaneous graph featuring a specific topology configuration. The graph dynamically alters its edge connectivity from one snapshot to another. Specifically, the cell-free ISAC network at the $n$-th snapshot is represented as $\mathcal{G}^{(n)} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}^{(n)})$, where $\mathcal{V}$ is the invariant node set, and $\mathcal{E}^{(n)}$ denotes the set of edges defined specifically for snapshot $n$. The node set $\mathcal{V}$ comprises three distinct node types: Tx-AP, Rx-AP, and CUs, explicitly expressed as $\mathcal{V} = \{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}},\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{Rx-AP}}, \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{CU}}\}$. Within each snapshot, dynamic edges connect Tx-AP nodes to CU nodes and Tx-AP nodes to Rx-AP nodes, representing instantaneous communication and sensing interactions, respectively.



\subsection{Graph Initialization}
\label{Dynamic_label_initial}

After defining the dynamic heterogeneous graph, we further embed the prior information of the considered cell-free ISAC system into the graph initialization state to better address the challenges brought by problem \eqref{initial}. 
Specifically, by adopting the embedding CSI of the communication link, the feature associated with an edge between the $t$-th Tx-AP and the $k$-th CU is defined as 
\begin{align}\label{eq:htk}
\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,k}=\bigl[\mathrm{vec}^T(\Re\{\mathbf{h}_{t,:,k}\}), \mathrm{vec}^T(\Im\{\mathbf{h}_{t,:,k}\})\bigr]\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 2N_{\mathrm{s}}M}.
\end{align}
Similarly, using embedding AP-to-AP interference, the feature associated with an edge between the $t$-th Tx-AP and the $r$-th Rx-AP is given by 
\begin{align}\label{eq:Gtr}
\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,r}=\bigl[\mathrm{vec}^T(\Re\{\mathbf{G}_{t,r}\}), \mathrm{vec}^T(\Im\{\mathbf{G}_{t,r}\})\bigr]\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times2M^2}.
\end{align}

Then, the dynamic graph in each snapshot is processed by a graph neural network with $\tau_{\mathrm{tot}}$ hidden layers. The initial hidden state of each node is formed by concatenating the feature vectors of all edges incident on that node. Accordingly, the initial hidden states for the Tx-AP, Rx-AP, and CU nodes are given by:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{s}^{(0)}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}(t) &= \big[\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,1},\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,2},\, \ldots,\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,K},\, \tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,1},\, \ldots,\, \tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,R} \,\big], \label{Tx-AP_feature}\\
\mathbf{s}^{(0)}_{\mathrm{Rx-AP}}(r) &= \big[\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,1},\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,2},\, \ldots,\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,K},\, \tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{1,r},\, \ldots,\, \tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{T,r} \,\big], \label{Rx-AP_feature}\\
\mathbf{s}^{(0)}_{\mathrm{CU}}(k) &= \big[\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{1,k},\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{2,k},\, \ldots,\, \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{T,k} \,\big]. \label{user_feature}
\end{align}



\subsection{Graph Module}
\label{Graph_module}
After graph initialization, we partition the optimization problem formulated in \eqref{initial} into three interconnected modules: User association, AP mode selection, and signal  processing for target position and velocity estimation, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{network}. Specifically, a structural attention module learns the user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$. A temporal attention module exploits the received echo signals to dynamically optimize the AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}$ for the next snapshot. Meanwhile, a three-dimensional convolutional neural network (3D-CNN) module processes the received echo signals to extract essential features across the antenna, subcarrier, and symbol dimensions. Finally, using the Tx-AP node features obtained at the final snapshot, we derive the optimal precoding matrix $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$.


\subsubsection{Structural Attention Module}
In each snapshot, hidden states of the Tx-AP nodes are iteratively updated using a masked structural attention mechanism that integrates attention scoring, message passing, and aggregation:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau+1,n)}(t)=f_{\mathrm{agg}}\Big(f_{\mathrm{att}}\big(\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau,n)}(t)\big)\odot\underset{\xi\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}{f_{\mathrm{mp}}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau,n)}(\xi)\big)\Big),
\end{equation}
where $f_{\mathrm{agg}}(\cdot)$ aggregates node features, $f_{\mathrm{att}}(\cdot)$ computes attention scores, and $f_{\mathrm{mp}}(\cdot)$ conducts message passing from neighboring nodes. Here, $\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)$ denotes the neighbor set connected to the $t$-th Tx-AP node. For clarity, the snapshot index $n$ is omitted in subsequent equations of structural attention module, as the graph topology remains unchanged within a snapshot.


For efficient utilization of power and bandwidth resources, each Tx-AP must selectively associate with appropriate CUs. Thus, masked attention priortizes CU nodes while excluding irrelevant Rx-AP nodes. Specifically, the masked attention function is defined as:
\begin{align}
&f_{\mathrm{att}}(\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau)}(t))=   \nonumber \\  & \underset{K_{\mathrm{u}}}{\mathrm{Top}}\Bigl\{\mathrm{Softmax}\bigl(\underset{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}{\|}(\mathbf{k}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}^{(\tau)})^T\mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}^{\mathrm{att}}\mathbf{q}^{(\tau)}(t)+\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{structural}}\bigr)\Bigr\},
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}^{\mathrm{att}}$ is the attention weight matrix, $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{structural}}$ is the structural mask matrix, and $\|$ denotes concatenation operation. The query and key vectors are generated through linear projections:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{q}^{(\tau)}(t)&=\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{query}}\left(\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau)}(t);\mathbf{W}_{t}^{\mathrm{query}}\right),\\
\mathbf{k}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}^{(\tau)}&=\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{key}}\left(\mathbf{k}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}^{(\tau)};\mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}^{\mathrm{key}}\right),
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{query}}(\cdot)$ and $\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{key}}(\cdot)$ represent linear transformations with corresponding projection matrices $\mathbf{W}_{t}^{\mathrm{query}}$ and $\mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}^{\mathrm{key}}$, respectively. The node information from neighbors is aggregated similarly to produce value vectors:
\begin{equation}
\underset{\xi\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}{f_{\mathrm{mp}}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau)}(\xi))=\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{value}}\left(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau)}(\xi);\mathbf{W}_{\xi}^{\mathrm{value}}\right).
\end{equation} 

Ultimately, the aggregation of structural attention outputs incorporates residual connections to enhance stability and performance:
\begin{align}
f_{\mathrm{agg}}(\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau)}(t))&= \mathbf{W}_{t}^{\mathrm{agg}}\mathrm{LeakyReLU}\Bigl\{f_{\mathrm{att}}(\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau)}(t)) \\
& \odot\underset{\xi\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)}{f_{\mathrm{mp}}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau)}(\xi))\Bigr\}\nonumber+\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau)}(t).
\end{align}


After processing each snapshot with structural attention, we obtain the optimal user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$ via unsupervised training. However, structural attention alone inadequately determines the AP mode vector $\bm{\kappa}$, as this decision significantly influences both communication sum-rate and target estimation accuracy. Furthermore, using only direct-path interference as prior features limits global radar waveform optimization. To address these limitations, we propose the following scheduling-aware temporal attention module.


\subsubsection{Scheduling-Aware Temporal Attention Module}
Following extraction of the node and edge features from snapshot $\mathcal{G}^{(n)}$, we reconstruct echo signals at each Rx-AP, denoted as $\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n)}(r)\in\mathbb{R}^{2N_{\mathrm{s}}LM}$. These signals enhance hidden states for snapshot $(n+1)$:
\begin{align}
\label{enhance1}
\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau_{\mathrm{tot}},n+1)}(t)=\bigl[&\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,K},\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,1},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n)}(1))^T,\ldots,\nonumber\\ 
&\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,R},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n)}(R))^T\bigr],\\[4pt]
\label{enhance2}
\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{Rx-AP}}^{(\tau_{\mathrm{tot}},n+1)}(r)=\bigl[&\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,K},\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{1,r},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n)}(r))^T,\ldots,\nonumber\\
&\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{T,r},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n)}(r))^T\bigr].
\end{align}
To maintain dimensional consistency, these hidden states are embedded as $\bar{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}^{(\tau_{\mathrm{tot}},n+1)}(t)$ and $\bar{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{Rx-AP}}^{(\tau_{\mathrm{tot}},n+1)}(r)$ between snapshots. Employing these embedding operations, the scheduling-aware temporal attention function to optimally select AP modes across snapshots is formulated as:
\begin{align}
f_{\mathrm{att}}\bigl(\{\bar{\mathbf{s}}^{(\tau_{\mathrm{tot}},n)}\}_{n=1}^{N}\bigr)=\underset{J-R}{\mathrm{Top}}\bigl\{&\underset{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}}{\mathrm{Softmax}}\bigl(\|(\bar{\mathbf{k}}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}}^{(\tau_{\mathrm{tot}})})^T\bar{\mathbf{W}}_{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}}^{\mathrm{att}}\bar{\mathbf{q}}^{(\tau_{\mathrm{tot}})}\nonumber\\
&+\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{temporal}}\bigr)\bigr\},
\end{align}
where $\bar{\mathbf{k}}^{(\tau_{\rm tot})}_{\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}}$, $\bar{\mathbf{W}}^{\rm att}_{\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}}$, and $\bar{\mathbf{q}}^{(\tau_{\rm tot})}$ denote the key vector, attention matrix, and the query vector, respectively, and $\mathbf{M}_{\rm temporal}$ is the mask matrix. Due to the similarity with the structural attention module and space constraints, detailed derivations are omitted here.

\subsubsection{3D-Convolution Module}
After scheduling-aware temporal attention-based processing,  the reconstructed echo signals, denoted as $\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n)}(r)$, are obtained by concatenating their real and imaginary components along the channel dimension, yielding the tensor $\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}^{(n)} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times RM \times N_{\mathrm{s}} \times L}$. Meanwhile, the Rx-APs leverage knowledge of the radar sequence $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{r}}$ and the Tx-APs' positions at the $n$-th snapshot to construct another real-valued tensor, denoted by $\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{r}}^{(n)} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times TM \times N_{\mathrm{s}} \times L}$. Subsequently, both the echo signal tensor and the radar sequence tensor are input to a 3D-CNN module.


In particular, each Rx-AP  first performs feature extraction on the combined raw inputs $\{\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}^{(n)}\|\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{r}}^{(n)}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times JM\times N_{\mathrm{s}}\times L}$ via the 3D-CNN \cite{CNN}. The extracted features are then compressed into a lower-dimensional representation $\mathbf{R}^{(n)} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times R \times \varrho}$ through multiple linear layers, with $\varrho$ representing the compression dimensionality scale. Afterwards, the central processing unit (CPU) of the cell-free system aggregates the multi-path compressed features $\mathbf{R}^{(n)}$ for the $n$-th snapshot using a multilayer perceptron (MLP). This distributed feature-compression and centralized fusion framework significantly reduces backhaul signaling overhead associated with data-level information fusion. Finally, the CPU outputs the estimated target positions and velocities $\{\{\hat{\mathbf{p}}_q, \hat{\mathbf{v}}_q\}_{q=1}^{Q}\}_{n=1}^{N}$ for all temporal snapshots.



\subsection{Training Strategy}
\label{strategy}
We first construct a training dataset comprising ground-truth position samples $\{\mathbf{P}_{\chi}\}_{\chi=1}^{X}$, velocity samples $\{\mathbf{V}_{\chi}\}_{\chi=1}^{X}$, and their corresponding CSI $\{\mathbf{H}_{\chi}\}_{\chi=1}^{X}$, where $X$ denotes the dataset size. Initially, the AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}$ is randomly initialized, and the communication objective is temporarily disregarded to independently pre-train the 3D-CNN module designed for position and velocity estimation. The pre-training objective function is expressed as:\vspace{-0.3 cm}

\begin{small}\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}^{(\mathrm{pre})}(\mathbf{P}, \mathbf{V}, \widehat{\mathbf{Y}})& = \frac{1}{XQ}\sum\limits_{\chi=1}^{X}\sum\limits_{q=1}^{Q}\Bigl\{\omega\|\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_{\chi},(\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{\rm r})_{\chi})(q)-\mathbf{P}_{\chi}(q)\|_2^2\nonumber\\
& \hspace{-1.5 cm}  +(1-\omega)\|\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_{\chi},(\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{\rm r})_{\chi})(q)-\mathbf{V}_{\chi}(q)\|_2^2\Bigr\},    
\end{align}\end{small}
\vspace{-0.3 cm}

\noindent where $Q$ is the total number of sensing targets.

Following this pre-training phase, the parameters of the 3D-CNN module are transferred to the dynamic graph learning framework. At this stage, by transforming constraint \eqref{commun_const} into a Lagrangian form, we jointly optimize the AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}$, the user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$, and the precoding matrix $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$ to concurrently maximize the communication sum-rate and minimize the target position and velocity estimation errors. The joint training objective is therefore formulated as:\vspace{-0.3 cm}


\begin{small}\begin{align}
\label{loss}
&\mathcal{L}^{(\mathrm{train})}(\mathbf{P},\mathbf{V},\mathbf{H}) =  \frac{1}{X}\sum\limits_{\chi=1}^{X}\Biggl\{\frac{\omega}{Q}\sum\limits_{q=1}^{Q}\|\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_{\chi},(\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{\rm r})_{\chi})(q)\!-\!\mathbf{P}_{\chi}(q)\|_2^2\nonumber\\
&+\frac{1-\omega
}{Q}\sum\limits_{q=1}^{Q}\|\mathcal{M}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_{\chi},(\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{\rm r})_{\chi})(q)-\mathbf{V}_{\chi}(q)\|_2^2+\rho \big(\gamma-R_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathbf{H}_{\chi})\big)\Biggr\},
\end{align}\end{small}
\vspace{-0.3 cm}

\noindent where $\rho\in[0,1]$ is the weighting factor balancing the communication and sensing objectives, and $R_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathbf{H}_{\chi})$ denotes the achievable communication rate given the CSI $\mathbf{H}_{\chi}$.

Leveraging this two-stage training strategy, the dynamic graph learning framework integrates structural and temporal attention modules along with the 3D-CNN during multiple snapshots to systematically optimize AP mode selection, user associations, precoding design, and echo signal processing and fusion, thereby enhancing the overall performance of the cell-free ISAC system.


\section{Proposed Mirror-GAT Framework}
While the previously introduced dynamic graph learning framework demonstrates promising performance for small-scale systems, its scalability is hindered by substantial computational overhead and the large number of model parameters required due to the multiple snapshot structure. These factors render it less suitable for practical deployment in large-scale scenarios. To overcome this limitation, we propose a lightweight \emph{mirror-GAT} framework, specifically designed for large-scale cell-free ISAC networks.

The mirror-GAT framework adopts a mirror-based alternating iterative inference strategy, in which a single shared pair of heterogeneous GATs is employed, rather than training multiple attention modules independently across different temporal snapshots. This design significantly reduces the number of trainable parameters and computational complexity, enabling efficient large-scale deployment. Nevertheless, the inherent multivariate optimization complexity in
the original formulation remains a key challenge. To fully leverage the benefits of the mirror-based alternating iterative structure, we reformulate the original problem into two interdependent subproblems: one for precoding and another for AP mode selection and user association. These subproblems are then addressed by two adjacency-shared heterogeneous GATs in an alternating manner. The remainder of this section details the proposed methodology.



\subsection{Problem Transformation}
\label{task}

To enable independent optimization within the mirror-GAT framework, we first incorporate the pre-trained 3D-CNN module (as described in Section~\ref{strategy}) as a fixed signal-level fusion mapping function. This allows us to exclude the CNN module from the optimization variables in the original problem formulation. With the target estimation algorithm fixed, we focus on jointly optimizing the AP mode selection, user association, and precoding strategies.

To improve tractability, we decompose the problem into two interconnected bilevel subproblems. Given fixed AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}^\star$ and user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}^\star$, the lower-level subproblem focuses on precoding matrix optimization and is formulated as follows:
\begin{subequations}
\label{first_stage}
\begin{align}
&\underset{\mathbf{W}_{j,i}}{\min}\quad
\omega\mathrm{RMSE}_{\text{p}}+(1-\omega)\mathrm{RMSE}_{\text{v}}\\
&\mathrm{s.t.}\quad R_\mathrm{c} \geq\gamma,\\
&\quad\quad~\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{s}}}\|\mathbf{W}_{j,i}\|_{F}^{2}\leq P_j, ~\forall j\in\mathcal{T}.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Using the solution from the lower-level problem \eqref{first_stage}, the upper-level problem aims to optimize the AP mode selection and user association, and is expressed as:
\begin{subequations}
\label{second_stage}
\begin{align}
&\underset{\bm{\Lambda},\bm{\kappa}}{\min}\quad \omega\mathrm{RMSE}_{\text{p}}+(1-\omega)\mathrm{RMSE}_{\text{v}}\\
&\mathrm{s.t.}\quad R_\mathrm{c} \geq \gamma.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
This bilevel reformulation decouples the original joint optimization task into two more manageable subproblems, allowing for efficient and structured optimization. In the following sections, we describe how the proposed mirror-GAT framework iteratively solves subproblems \eqref{first_stage} and \eqref{second_stage} to obtain locally optimal solutions for $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}^\star$, $\bm{\kappa}^\star$, and $\bm{\Lambda}^\star$.

%After iteratively solving the subproblems \eqref{first_stage} and \eqref{second_stage} to obtain locally optimal $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}^{*}$, $\bm{\kappa}^{*}$, and $\bm{\Lambda}^{*}$, we can continue updating the signal fusion mapping function \textcolor{red}{by XXX}. In this section, our focus is the optimization of the precoding matrix $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$, the AP mode selection vector $\kappa$, and the user association matrix $\Lambda$, using mirror-GAT framework.  the corresponding description of the signal fusion module is omitted. 



\subsection{Graph Definition}
Following problem transformation, we establish two heterogeneous mirror graphs, denoted by $\mathcal{G}_{1}=(\mathcal{V}_1,\mathcal{E})$ and $\mathcal{G}_2=(\mathcal{V}_2,\mathcal{E})$, to iteratively optimize the precoding matrix  $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$, the AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}$, and the user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$. 
The two graphs share identical adjacency structures but differ fundamentally in message-passing directions and objectives. Specifically, the association-oriented graph $\mathcal{G}_2$ utilizes node-to-edge message passing for AP mode selection and user association, whereas the precoding-oriented graph $\mathcal{G}_1$ employs edge-to-node message passing to design optimal precoding solutions.


\subsubsection{Precoding-Oriented Graph}
The node set $\mathcal{V}_1$ of the precoding-oriented graph comprises three distinct node types: Tx-AP, Rx-AP, and CU. Accordingly, we express the node set as $\mathcal{V}_1=\{\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}},\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{Rx-AP}},\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{CU}}\}$. Edges between nodes represent communication and sensing interactions, defined through reshaped channel features shown in \eqref{eq:htk} and \eqref{eq:Gtr}.
% \begin{align}
% \tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,k}&=\bigl[\mathrm{vec}^T(\Re\{\mathbf{h}_{t,:,k}\}), \mathrm{vec}^T(\Im\{\mathbf{h}_{t,:,k}\})\bigr]\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times 2N_{\mathrm{s}}M},\\
% \tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,r}&=\bigl[\mathrm{vec}^T(\Re\{\mathbf{G}_{t,r}\}), \mathrm{vec}^T(\Im\{\mathbf{G}_{t,r}\})\bigr]\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times2M^2}.
% \end{align}

Each Tx-AP node propagates its features with $\tau_{1}^{\mathrm{tot}}$ layers:
\begin{equation}
\label{forward}
\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{1}+1)}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}(t)\!=\!h_{\mathrm{agg}}\bigl(h_{\mathrm{att}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{1})}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}(t))\odot h_{\mathrm{mp}}(\{\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{1})}(\xi)\}_{\xi\in\mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{n}}(t)})\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $h_{\mathrm{agg}}(\cdot)$, $h_{\mathrm{att}}(\cdot)$, and $h_{\mathrm{mp}}(\cdot)$ denote aggregation, attention scoring, and message passing functions, respectively.



In the precoding design task, we assume that the framework has prior knowledge of the AP modes and user association strategies. Unlike the proposed dynamic graph learning framework (which extracts masked attention scores from edge information), the precoding-oriented graph employs a weighted scoring criterion for attention-enabled aggregation. Accordingly, the attention function $h_{\textrm{att}}(\cdot)$ is defined as:
\begin{align}
h_{\textrm{att}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_1)}_{\textrm{Tx-AP}}(t))&= \alpha \textrm{Softmax}\big(\underset{\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}(t)}{\|}(\mathbf{k}^{(\tau_1)}_{\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}(t)})^T\mathbf{W}^{\rm att}_{\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}(t)}\mathbf{q}^{(\tau_1)}(t)\big)\nonumber \\
&+(1-\alpha)\{(\mathbf{1}-\bm{\kappa})^T\|\bm{\Lambda}(t,:)\},
\end{align}
where $\alpha$ is the weighting factor for the attention score, $\mathbf{W}^{\rm att}_{\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}(t)}$, $\mathbf{k}_{\mathcal{V}_\text{n}(t)}^{(\tau_1)}$, and $\mathbf{q}^{(\tau_1)}$ denote the attention weight matrix, query vector, and key vector, respectively.

Then, the global information from each neighboring node is aggregated via a message-passing operation to form value vectors in a similar manner:
\be
\underset{\xi\in\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}(t)}{h_{\textrm{mp}}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_1)}(\xi))=\mathcal{M}^{\rm value}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_1)}(\xi);\mathbf{W}^{\rm value}_{\xi}).
\ee


Finally, the feature aggregation stage incorporates the attention outputs and residual connections to ensure stability and enhance performance:
\begin{align}
h_{\textrm{agg}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_1)}_{\textrm{Tx-AP}}(t))&=\mathbf{W}^{\rm agg}_t\textrm{LeakyReLU}\big\{h_{\textrm{att}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_1)}_{\textrm{Tx-AP}}(t)) \nonumber
\\
&\odot\underset{\xi\in\mathcal{V}_{\rm n}(t)}{h_{\textrm{mp}}}(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_1)}(\xi))\big\}\!+\!\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_1)}_{\textrm{Tx-AP}}(t). 
\end{align}
By integrating the attention mechanism (with prior edge weights) and using residual connections, each Tx-AP node ultimately outputs the locally optimal precoding matrix $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$, thereby fulfilling the precoding design sub-task defined in (\ref{first_stage}).



\subsubsection{Association-Oriented Graph}
It is worth noting that, due to the inherent ambiguity in AP mode selection, the association-oriented graph employs only two types of nodes: AP nodes and CU nodes. Within this framework, the network dynamically redistributes connections among APs and establishes weighted communication links based on the channel gains and interference characteristics of AP-CU pairs. This approach effectively addresses the AP mode selection and user association tasks simultaneously. Accordingly, the edge set $\mathcal{E}$ consists of two categories: communication edges ($\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{com}}$), representing direct AP-CU links, and sensing edges ($\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sen}}$), representing interconnections among AP nodes. Formally, the edge
set is expressed as $\mathcal{E} = \{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{com}}, \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sen}}\}$.


Assuming the association-oriented graph has prior knowledge of the multi-user CSI at each AP and the corresponding locally optimal precoding matrix $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$, we define the initial edge hidden states in terms of normalized precoded channel gains as:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{s}^{(0)}_{\mathrm{com}}(j,k)&=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{s}}}\mathbf{h}_{j,i,k}^H\mathbf{w}_{\text{c},j,i,k},\\
\mathbf{s}^{(0)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell)&=\frac{1}{M^2}\sum_{n=1}^M\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{s}}}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\frac{1}{\mathbf{G}_{j,\ell}\mathbf{w}_{\text{r},j,i,m}(n)},
\end{align}
where $\ell\in\mathcal{J}\setminus\{j\}$.
\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=3.4 in]{mirror.eps}
\caption{Proposed mirror-GAT framework.}\vspace{-0.2 cm}
\label{mirror}
\end{figure}

Next, each edge state is processed by a GAT with $\tau_{2}^{\mathrm{tot}}$ hidden layers. The forward propagation update in the association-oriented graph is given by:
\begin{align}
\label{fp_2}
\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{2}+1)}_{\mathrm{com}}(j,k)&=g_{\mathrm{agg}}\Bigl(g_{\mathrm{att}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{2})}_{\mathrm{com}}(j,k)\big)\odot \underset{\epsilon \in\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,k)}{g_{\mathrm{mp}}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{2})}(\epsilon)\big)\Bigr),\\
\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{2}+1)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell)&=g_{\mathrm{agg}}\Bigl(g_{\mathrm{att}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{2})}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell)\big)\odot \underset{\epsilon \in\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,\ell)}{g_{\mathrm{mp}}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_{2})}(\epsilon)\big)\Bigr),
\end{align}
where the functions $g_{\mathrm{agg}}(\cdot)$, $g_{\mathrm{att}}(\cdot)$, and $g_{\mathrm{mp}}(\cdot)$ denote feature aggregation, edge-based attention scoring, and message passing operations, respectively. Here, $\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,\ell)$ represents the set of neighboring edges involved in message passing for edge $(j,\ell)$. To incorporate global node-weight information and guide the association decisions, the attention scoring functions are explicitly defined as: 
\begin{align}
g_{\mathrm{att}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{com}}(j,k)\big)&=\underset{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,k)}{\mathrm{Softmax}}\bigl(\|(\mathbf{k}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,k)})^T\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{att}}_{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,k)}\mathbf{q}^{(\tau_2)}(j,k)\nonumber\\
&\qquad\qquad\quad~+\mathbf{u}_{j,k}^T\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{com}}^{(\tau_2)}\bigr),\\
g_{\mathrm{att}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell)\big)&=\underset{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,\ell)}{\mathrm{Softmax}}\bigl(\|(\mathbf{k}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,\ell)})^T\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{att}}_{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,\ell)}\mathbf{q}^{(\tau_2)}(j,\ell)\nonumber\\
&\qquad\qquad\quad~+\mathbf{u}_{j,\ell}^T\mathbf{s}_{\mathrm{sen}}^{(\tau_2)}\bigr),
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{att}}_{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}}$, $\mathbf{k}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}}$, and $\mathbf{q}^{(\tau_2)}$ respectively denote the attention weight matrix, key vectors, and query vectors employed in edge-based attention computations. Moreover, $\mathbf{u}$ comprises trainable parameters utilized to enhance edge features during the attention scoring phase. 


Next, the weight information from neighboring edges is aggregated via the message-passing operation to produce the value vectors:
\begin{align}
\underset{\epsilon\in\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}}{g_{\mathrm{mp}}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}(\epsilon)\big)=\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{value}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}(\epsilon);\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{value}}_{\epsilon}\big).
\end{align}

Finally, a local edge-feature aggregation stage combines the attention outputs with residual connections to improve network stability and enrich feature representations. In particular, the aggregated edge states are updated as:
\begin{align}
&g_{\mathrm{agg}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{com}}(j,k)\big)=\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{com}}(j,k)\nonumber\\
&+\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{agg}}_{j,k}\mathrm{Softmax}\Bigl\{g_{\mathrm{att}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{com}}(j,k)\big)\odot\underset{\epsilon\in\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,k)}{g_{\mathrm{mp}}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}(\epsilon)\big)\Bigr\},\\
&g_{\mathrm{agg}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell)\big)=\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell)\nonumber\\
&+\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{agg}}_{j,\ell}\mathrm{Softmax}\Bigl\{g_{\mathrm{att}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell)\big)\odot\underset{\epsilon\in\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{n}}(j,\ell)}{g_{\mathrm{mp}}}\big(\mathbf{s}^{(\tau_2)}(\epsilon)\big)\Bigr\}.
\end{align}
By performing this edge-based information integration, the association-oriented graph ultimately yields the locally optimal AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}$ and the user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$ from the computed global edge weights.


\subsection{Mirror-based Iteration}
Although the precoding-oriented and association-oriented graph frameworks independently yield locally optimal solutions, their inherently mirrored structure tends to converge to suboptimal local minima when executed in a single iteration. Furthermore, the proposed mirror-GAT framework operates without prior knowledge of potential target positions and velocities, rendering one-pass inference insufficient for high-precision estimation. To address these limitations, we introduce a mirror-based iterative strategy that cyclically refines the original optimization problem \eqref{initial} by incorporating reconstructed echo signal feedback, as depicted in Fig.~\ref{mirror}.

Let the total number of mirror iterations be denoted by $N_{\mathrm{m}}$. During the $n_{\mathrm{m}}$-th mirror iteration, both the precoding-oriented graph $\mathcal{G}_1^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}$ and the association-oriented graph $\mathcal{G}_2^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}$ perform forward inference. As a result, the mirror-GAT pair jointly outputs the locally optimal precoding matrices $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}$, AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}$, and user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}$ for the current iteration. 


Following extraction of node and edge features and determination of local solutions, the echo signal tensor $\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}\in\mathbb{C}^{R\times M\times N_{\mathrm{s}}\times L}$ is reconstructed at each Rx-AP. This tensor is then reshaped into feature vectors $\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}(r)\in\mathbb{R}^{2N_{\mathrm{s}}LM}$ for each Rx-AP node $r\in\mathcal{R}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}$, where $\mathcal{R}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}$ denotes the set of Rx-AP nodes in the $n_{\mathrm{m}}$-th iteration. For all $n_{\mathrm{m}}>1$, these echo features are utilized to update the initial hidden states for the subsequent iteration as follows:
\begin{align}
\label{update_1}
\mathbf{s}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}}+1, 0)}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}(t)=&\bigl[\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,K},\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,1},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}(r))^T,\ldots,\nonumber\\ 
&\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{t,R},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}(R))^T\bigr],
\end{align}
\begin{align}
\label{update_2}
\mathbf{s}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}}+1, 0)}_{\mathrm{Rx-AP}}(r)=&\bigl[\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbf{h}}_{t,K},\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{1,r},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}(r))^T,\ldots,\nonumber\\
&\tilde{\mathbf{G}}_{T,r},(\hat{\mathbf{y}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}(r))^T\bigr],
\end{align}
\begin{align}
\label{update_3}
\mathbf{s}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}}+1, 0)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell )\!=\!\frac{1}{M^2}\!\sum_{n,i,m}\!\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{l}\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}})}(\ell,m,i,l)}{\mathbf{G}_{j,\ell}\mathbf{w}_{\text{r},j,i,m}(n)}\!+\!\mathbf{s}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}}, 0)}_{\mathrm{sen}}(j,\ell ).
\end{align}
Following principles similar to those presented in \eqref{enhance1} and \eqref{enhance2}, an embedding operation is performed between iterations to ensure consistent dimensionality for subsequent node processing. 
%The embedded node states after this operation are represented as $\bar{\mathbf{s}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}}+1, 0)}_{\mathrm{Tx-AP}}(t)$ and $\bar{\mathbf{s}}^{(n_{\mathrm{m}}+1, 0)}_{\mathrm{Rx-AP}}(r)$.



\subsection{Training Strategy}
\label{TS:2}
The training and testing phases of the mirror-GAT framework employ the same ground-truth datasets and pre-trained 3D-CNN modules as described in Section~\ref{strategy}. To accelerate convergence of the iterative process, we initialize the precoding-oriented graph $\mathcal{G}_1$ using a hierarchical proximity-based strategy.


In the absence of target position information, the initial AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}^{(0)}$ is formed by selecting the $R$ AP nodes with the largest aggregate distances from all other APs:
\begin{align}
    \bm{\kappa}^{(0)}=\underset{R}{\mathrm{Top}}\left\{\sum_{s=1,s\neq j}^{J}d_{:,s}\right\}.
\end{align}
After determining $\bm{\kappa}^{(0)}$, each AP node selects the  $K_{\mathrm{u}}$ user nodes with the strongest channel gains as its initial association vector:
\begin{align}    \bm{\Lambda}_{j,:}^{(0)}=\underset{K_{\mathrm{u}}}{\mathrm{Top}}\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{s}}}\left|\mathbf{h}_{j,i,:}\right|^2\right\}.
\end{align}
Using these initializations, the mirror-GAT framework iteratively computes the optimal precoding matrices $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$, along with the optimal AP mode selection vector 
 $\bm{\kappa}$ and user association matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$. The complete procedure of the proposed mirror-GAT framework is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:mirror_GAT}.
\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Proposed mirror-GAT framework}
\label{alg:mirror_GAT}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \REQUIRE $\mathbf{H}, \mathbf{P}, \mathbf{V}, \{\mathbf{p}_j\}_{j=1}^{J},\bm{\kappa}^{(0)}, \bm{\Lambda}^{(0)}$.
    \ENSURE $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}, \bm{\kappa}, \bm{\Lambda}, \mathcal{M}$.
    \FOR {$\textrm{epoch}=1$ : $\textrm{Epoch}$}
        \FOR {$n_{\rm m}=1$ : $N_{\rm m}$}
            \STATE {Construct the topological state of the mirror graph pair $\{\mathcal{G}_1,\mathcal{G}_2\}$ using $\bm{\kappa}^{(n_{\rm m}-1)}$ and $\bm{\Lambda}^{(n_{\rm m}-1)}$.}
            \STATE {Obtain locally optimal precoding matrix $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}^{(n_{\rm m})}$ using the forward propagation (\ref{forward}).}
            \STATE {Obtain locally optimal AP mode selection vector $\bm{\kappa}^{(n_{\rm m})}$ and $\bm{\Lambda}^{(n_{\rm m})}$ using the forward propagation (\ref{fp_2}).}
            \STATE {Process echo signal $\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}^{(n_{\rm m})}$ through 3D-CNN module to obtain estimated $\{\hat{\mathbf{p}_q}\}_{q=1}^{Q}$ and $\{\hat{\mathbf{v}_q}\}_{q=1}^Q$.}
            \STATE {Compute the training loss by (\ref{loss}) and execute backward propagation.}
            \IF {$n_{\rm m}>1$}
                \STATE {Enhance the initial hidden states of next iteration based on (\ref{update_1}), (\ref{update_2}), and (\ref{update_3}).}
            \ENDIF
        \ENDFOR
    \STATE {Repeat mirror iterations and validate the mirror GAT framework.}
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE {Obtain $\mathbf{W}_{j,i}$, $\bm{\kappa}$, $\bm{\Lambda}$, and $\mathcal{M}$.}
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
