In this section, we present simulation results to validate the performance of the proposed dynamic graph learning and mirror-GAT frameworks in a cooperative cell-free ISAC system. Unless otherwise specified, all simulation parameters and experimental configurations are as listed in Table~\ref{table1}.

\vspace{-0.2 cm}
\subsection{Illustration of AP Selection and CU Association} 
Fig.~\ref{scatter} provides a visual illustration of the AP mode selection and CU association obtained by the proposed dynamic graph learning framework. In this figure, various network entities are indicated by distinct markers: Tx-APs (blue circles), Rx-APs (red squares), CUs (green triangles), sensing targets (purple pentagrams), and clutters (yellow diamonds). Blue lines indicate the communication links established between each Tx-AP and its associated CU. We observe that the proposed algorithm effectively selects Tx-APs and associates them with appropriate CUs. Furthermore, APs located near sensing targets yet sufficiently isolated from clutter interference are preferentially designated as Rx-APs. This spatially-aware AP mode selection strategy enhances target echo reception quality, mitigates clutter-induced interference, and thereby improves the precision of target position and velocity estimation.


\vspace{-0.2 cm}
\subsection{Position and Velocity Estimation Performance}
\label{Performance}
After illustrating the optimal association strategy above, we evaluate the target position and velocity estimation performance of the proposed frameworks. To highlight the advantages of the proposed dynamic graph learning (\textbf{Prop. dynamic graph}) and mirror-GAT (\textbf{Prop. mirror-GAT}) frameworks, we compare them with the following schemes for comparison:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Heuristic+GAT}: This scheme uses the hierarchical proximity principle (see Sec.~\ref{TS:2}) instead of the association-oriented graph in the mirror-GAT framework, yielding a heuristic AP mode selection and user association.
\item \textbf{Random+GAT}: This scheme randomly selects AP modes and user associations within the mirror-GAT framework.
\item \textbf{MMSE+CNN}: This scheme \cite{WZHICASSP} uses the well-known minimum mean squared error (MMSE) precoder and the heuristic association strategy. In addition, a centralized 3D-CNN model is employed for signal-level fusion and parameter estimation.
\item \textbf{ZF+FFT}: This scheme employs a zero-forcing (ZF) precoder with the heuristic association strategy, and uses the classic 3D-FFT method \cite{XZCTSP2024} for least-squares information fusion.
\end{itemize}


