Fig.~\ref{Sim:RMSE_P}(a) and Fig.~\ref{Sim:RMSE_P}(b) show the target position RMSE and velocity RMSE, respectively, as functions of the per-AP transmit power $P_j$. As anticipated, increasing $P_j$ leads to consistent improvements in both position and velocity estimation accuracy across all considered schemes. Notably, the proposed graph-learning frameworks substantially outperform heuristic, random selection/association, and conventional precoding schemes. These results clearly demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed graph-learning-based approach, achieving position RMSE below 1 m and velocity RMSE below 1 m/s under moderate transmit power through efficient resource utilization and multi-perspective observations. Additionally, the dynamic graph learning framework exhibits superior performance compared to the mirror-GAT framework, albeit at the expense of increased computational complexity and overhead.




Fig.~\ref{Sim:RMSE_ML} shows the impact of the number of transmit antennas $M$ and the number of OFDM symbols $L$ on position and velocity estimation accuracy. As shown in Fig.~\ref{Sim:RMSE_ML}(a), increasing $M$ significantly improves positioning accuracy for both the dynamic graph learning and mirror-GAT frameworks, primarily due to enhanced spatial resolution and greater antenna array gain. Similarly, as shown in Fig.~\ref{Sim:RMSE_ML}(b), increasing $L$ improves velocity estimation performance, thanks to longer coherent processing intervals and finer Doppler resolution. Notably, even when evaluated beyond the training scenario ($M=8$, $L=16$), the proposed schemes continue to provide excellent position and velocity estimation performance in testing, demonstrating strong generalization ability. More importantly, the proposed frameworks consistently outperform the heuristic and random selection schemes across all scenarios, highlighting the benefits of the graph-learning-based joint optimization and estimation approaches.
