\subsection{Overhead and Complexity Analysis}
To assess the practical feasibility of the proposed graph learning-based frameworks in cell-free networks, we analyze their backhaul signaling overhead (in terms of double-precision numbers) and then examine their model size (number of parameters) and computational complexity (average execution time).

\subsubsection{Backhaul Signaling Overhead}
The backhaul signaling overhead in the cell-free ISAC system includes the exchange of CSI data streams, AP mode selection and user association information, and compressed echo signal information. Specifically, the mirror subgraph pairs exploit the AP mode selection and user association strategy from the previous iteration. As a result, the information required for association and scheduling per mirror iteration is only $J + T K$ double-precision numbers, instead of $J + J K$ in the dynamic graph learning framework. Meanwhile, in the mirror-GAT framework, the CPU collects only the valid local CSI from Tx-APs and their associated CUs, incurring $N_{\rm m}(T M N_{\rm s} K_{\rm u} + T R M^2)$ double-precision numbers. In contrast, the dynamic graph learning framework requires collecting the global CSI, incurring $N(J M N_{\rm s} K + J(J-1) M^2)$ double-precision numbers. Note also that both frameworks employ a 3D-CNN module for signal-level fusion. Thus, the feature maps uploaded from Rx-APs to the CPU require only $2R\varrho$ double-precision numbers (instead of $2R M N_{\rm s} L$), significantly reducing the backhaul transmission overhead. In summary, the total overhead of the dynamic graph learning framework is on the order of $\mathcal{O}(N(J M N_{\rm s} K + J^2 M^2 + 2R\varrho))$, whereas for the mirror-GAT framework it is on the order of $\mathcal{O}(N_{\rm m}(T M N_{\rm s} K_{\rm u} + T R M^2 + 2R\varrho))$.

\subsubsection{Model Parameter and Computational Complexity}
Furthermore, we evaluate the model parameter count and average execution time for deployments with different numbers of antennas $M$ (see Fig.~\ref{Sim:complexity}), since these metrics reflect the practical computational complexity at the AP side. In this analysis, the backhaul signaling is modeled as an additional delay proportional to a subframe’s transmission time for the overhead. Accordingly, the reported average execution time includes both the model inference time and the simulated backhaul transmission delay. As shown in Fig.~\ref{Sim:complexity}, the mirror-GAT framework requires fewer model parameters and has a shorter execution time than the dynamic graph learning framework. These results suggest that the mirror-GAT framework is more amenable to practical deployment in cell-free networks, while still achieving multi-user communication and sensing performance comparable to that of the dynamic graph learning framework.






\section{Conclusions}
\label{Conclusions}
In this paper, we considered the joint optimization of network configuration and multi-target position and velocity estimation in cooperative cell-free ISAC systems. We proposed a novel dynamic graph learning framework leveraging temporal and structural dual-attention mechanisms to cooperatively optimize AP operation modes, user association, precoding strategies, and echo signal processing. Additionally, a lightweight mirror-GAT framework was developed to significantly reduce computational complexity and signaling overhead, enhancing practical deployment feasibility, particularly in large-scale networks. Simulation results validated that both proposed frameworks significantly outperform conventional heuristic schemes and recent deep-learning-based methods, achieving superior communication performance alongside highly precise target parameter estimation. Notably, the mirror-GAT framework exhibited substantial computational efficiency improvements, underscoring its suitability for realistic, scalable deployments. Overall, this work underscores the significant potential of graph-learning-based methodologies to realize efficient and robust joint optimization and estimation in future cooperative cell-free ISAC systems.

