\section{Introduction}

Two-sided matching markets often rely on centralized mechanisms to make assignment decisions among participating agents. Prominent examples include school choice and college admissions, where a central clearinghouse, e.g., the Ministry of Education, matches students to schools or universities. Most research in this area focuses on designing mechanisms that ensure \emph{stability}, a desirable property that discourages agents from circumventing the matching, fosters trust in the system, enhances fairness by eliminating \emph{justified envy}, and contributes to the long-term success and viability of the system~\citep{roth1990twosided}.

A substantial body of work investigates \emph{strategy-proof} mechanisms, where agents do not benefit by misrepresenting their preferences, regardless of the actions of other agents. These mechanisms are intended to incentivize truthful reporting. For instance, the student-proposing version of the well-studied Deferred Acceptance algorithm ({\DAA})~\citep{gale1962college} is known to be strategy-proof for students in school choice and college admission applications.

However, empirical evidence shows that agents often misreport their preferences or act strategically even when the mechanism is strategy-proof~\citep{chen2006school,hassidim2017mechanism}. This occurs for several reasons. First, agents may have limited information about the mechanism, or mistrust its functionality and the truthfulness of other participants, prompting them to strategize~\citep{rees2018suboptimal}. \citet{rees2018experimental} showed that a considerable number of medical students misreported their preferences to the National Resident Matching in the United States, despite the process being designed to discourage such behavior. In school choice, students might not list selective schools despite preferring them, due to uncertainty about admission chances~\citep{chen2019self,fack2019beyond}. Thus, students' subjective beliefs about admission chances influence their reported preferences, which may diverge from their true preferences even in a strategy-proof environment~\citep{fabre2021application,arteaga2022smart}. \citet{shorrer2023dominated} found that, in the Hungarian college admission system, many students declined the opportunity to receive a tuition waiver. Similarly, \citet{hassidim2021limits} analyzed the Israeli Psychology Master's admission process, where students frequently ranked unfunded programs higher than identical funded ones, despite the funding having no strings attached. These behaviors highlight a clear discrepancy between students' reported and true preferences. Second, in many applications, the length of agents' preference lists is limited, compelling them to behave strategically when selecting their reported preferences~\citep{haeringer2009constrained}. Third, in many cases, non-strategy-proof mechanisms are employed, leading agents to choose their preferences strategically~\citep{abdulkadirouglu2003school,ergin2006games}. 

Agents' strategic behavior is shaped by both their true preferences and their beliefs about admission chances~\citep[see, e.g.,][]{agarwal2018demand,agarwal2020revealed}. However, as shown by~\citet{kapor2020heterogeneous}, these beliefs often differ from agents' actual admission probabilities, undermining the effectiveness of the mechanism. In two-sided many-to-one matching markets, the capacity of the many-sided agents affects both the real and perceived admission chances of the one-sided agents. These chances are shaped by the cutoff score of the many-sided group---the minimum score required for a one-sided agent to be accepted~\citep{kapor2020heterogeneous}---which, in turn, depends on the many-sided agents' capacities. For example, misinformation regarding changes in the many-sided agent capacity---whether increases or decreases---can distort the one-sided agents' perceptions of their chances, leading to choices that differ from reality~\citep{agarwal2018demand}. 

Previous research on two-sided many-to-one matching markets has primarily addressed settings in which the many-sided agents have fixed capacity. More recently, studies have begun to examine flexible capacity models in which the clearinghouse can adjust capacities~\citep[see][]{abe2022anytime,kumano2022quota,bobbio2022capacity,bobbio2023capacity,chen2023optimal,afacan2024capacity}. These works, often in the context of school choice, assume that students’ preferences are known in advance, and thus that the clearinghouse jointly determines both school capacity allocations and student-stable assignments. As such, capacity decisions are made in response to deterministic preferences, which are also assumed to be truthful.

Nevertheless, in many applications, capacity decisions must be made before preferences are revealed, regardless of whether they are ultimately reported truthfully or strategically. For instance, in vaccine distribution, governments must establish inoculation venues and allocate vaccine doses before knowing citizen preferences over venues~\citep{kumano2022quota}. In school systems, long-term investments in infrastructure must be planned under uncertainty about future student enrollment and preferences. For example, school districts in the U.S. often enroll more students than their physical capacity allows, leading to overcrowding. The survey of the~\citet{survey} indicates that 22\% of schools experience overcrowding, with 8\% exceeding their capacity by 25\%. While temporary measures---such as portable classrooms and staggered schedules---offer short-term relief, persistent overcrowding requires permanent capacity expansion. Similar timing constraints arise in other public sector decisions: education ministries may allocate funding or scholarships before knowing applicants’ preferences, healthcare authorities may increase residency positions without knowing where candidates wish to apply, and refugee resettlement agencies often distribute regional support resources prior to assessing individual or family preferences. In all such cases, agents' preferences are inherently uncertain at the time of resource or capacity allocation and must be modeled accordingly.

In what follows, for consistency, we adopt the terminology of the school choice application introduced by~\citet{abdulkadirouglu2003school}, referring to the two groups of agents as students and schools. The previous discussion illustrates three key points:
(i) In many applications, the capacity of schools is expanded to enhance future matchings when students' preferences are still unknown and, therefore, uncertain;
(ii) Students often behave strategically, even in strategy-proof environments, with their reported preferences influenced by their perceived admission chances; and 
(iii) Students' perceived admission chances are affected by the schools' capacity.
Nonetheless, no study has investigated matching markets considering the first point, nor has any study examined them while considering all three points simultaneously. %//

%\subsection{Contributions}
\paragraph{\textbf{Contributions.}}
This work introduces a canonical model of a centralized two-stage stochastic two-sided many-to-one matching market and proposes a mechanism to optimize, in expectation, student outcomes under preference uncertainty. Specifically:
(i) In the first stage, the clearinghouse decides on school capacity expansions before students' preferences are revealed;
(ii) Students' uncertain preferences are either exogenous (decision independent), i.e., they report their true preferences, or endogenous (decision-dependent), i.e., they strategically adjust their preferences based on their perceived admission chances---which are influenced by capacity decisions.
In the second stage, the clearinghouse implements a student-optimal stable matching based on the decided school capacities and students' reported preferences. This gives rise to a \emph{two-stage stochastic program with either exogenous} ({\SP}) \emph{or endogenous} ({\SPEU}) \emph{uncertainty}, where capacity planning and matching represent first- and second-stage decisions, respectively. We refer to this problem as the \emph{two-stage stochastic two-sided many-to-one matching problem with capacity expansion and uncertain preferences} ({\STMMP}), with school choice as our illustrative example. Our main contributions are as follows: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item We introduce the {\STMMP} and formally define it. For the endogenous case ({\SPEU}), we adopt the methodological framework proposed by~\citet{bazotte2024solving}.
%
\item We explore three behavioral models for students’ preference reporting: (i) Utility Maximization model ({\UM}), where students report their true preferences, resulting in exogenous uncertainty; (ii) Conjoint Expected Utility Maximization ({\CEUM}); and Individual Expected Utility Maximization ({\IEUM}). In both {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}, students strategically report preferences by solving a utility maximization problem that accounts for their true preferences and perceived admission chances, which depend on capacity decisions. Reported preferences are then endogenous in these cases, with utility maximization serving as a random variable transformation from true to reported preferences. We introduce mathematical formulations for all three behaviors.
%
\item Building on the sample average approximation ({\SAA}) method, we design solution approaches for the {\STMMP} under each behavioral model and analyze the resulting statistical estimators. We develop mathematical formulations for the corresponding {\SAA} programs of the {\STMMP}: under {\UM}, we extend the $L$-constraints formulation of~\citet{bobbio2023capacity}; under {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}, we adapt the cutoff score formulation of~\citet{agarwal2020revealed}. 
%
Solving the {\SAA} program of the {\STMMP} under {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} by feeding our mathematical formulations into a solver is significantly more computationally demanding than under {\UM}, due to the added complexity of endogenous uncertainty. Even under {\UM}, solving the {\SAA} program of the {\STMMP} becomes computationally difficult for large instances. Hence, we introduce a suite of behavior-specific heuristics to efficiently compute near-optimal solutions:
%
\begin{enumerate}
\item For the {\SAA} program under {\UM} behavior, we generalize a heuristic from~\citet{bobbio2023capacity} to propose the Assignment-based ({\ASG}) heuristic. Additionally, we develop a Lagrangian relaxation scheme based on the modified $L$-constraints formulation with relaxed stability constraints, which provides stronger lower bounds than its linear relaxation. We combine this scheme with the {\DAA} to propose a Lagrangian-based heuristic that yields tight lower and upper bounds for the problem. Notably, when the Lagrangian multipliers are set to zero, the subproblem reduces to the {\ASG} heuristic.
\item For the {\SAA} program under all three behavioral models, we introduce a greedy Local Search ({\LS}) heuristic and a Simulated Annealing ({\SA}) heuristic. These heuristics explore the feasible space of capacity expansion decisions, apply behavior-specific algorithms to define students' reported preferences, and use the {\DAA} to evaluate resulting matchings.
\end{enumerate}
\item  We conduct extensive computational experiments to assess the impact of uncertainty and behavioral modeling on capacity decisions. Our results show that the clearinghouse selects significantly different allocations depending on student behavior. In particular, capacity expansion decisions made under the nonstrategic {\UM} behavior result in lower-quality assignments when students behave strategically, compared to decisions that account for their actual strategic behavior. This decline in matching quality is even more pronounced when decisions are based on an incorrect strategic behavior, i.e., assuming {\IEUM} when students behave according to {\CEUM}, underscoring the importance of accurately modeling student strategic reporting. Across all behaviors, {\SAA}-based approaches consistently outperform the average scenario (Expected Value {\EV}) approach across multiple performance metrics, demonstrating the critical role of stochastic preference modeling in capacity planning. 
\end{enumerate}

%\paragraph{\textbf{Organization.}}
The remainder of this work is structured as follows. Section~\ref{sec:literature} reviews related work. Section~\ref{sec:prob-stat} introduces the {\STMMP} through a timeline, and Section~\ref{sec:formulation} formalizes it under the three student behaviors. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} presents the proposed {\SAA}-based solution approaches. Section~\ref{sec:results} examines the impact of stochastic and behavioral preference modeling and compares the computational performance of the proposed methods. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:conclusions} concludes our analyses.

\section{Related Works} \label{sec:literature}

This section reviews the literature most relevant to our research. We divide the discussion into two parts: studies on matching markets, and studies on two-stage programs with exogenous or endogenous uncertainty.

\subsection{Matching Markets}

We focus on \emph{two-sided many-to-one matching problems} ({\TMMP}'s), a topic with extensive prior work. We underline studies most closely related to our setting, and refer to~\citet{manlove2013algorithmics} for a detailed background.

\paragraph{Mechanisms and Mathematical Formulations.} 
The well-known {\DAA} was introduced by~\citet{gale1962college}. Its student-proposing version yields a student-optimal stable matching in polynomial time that is strategy-proof for the students. This matching can also be obtained efficiently with linear programming~\citep{baiou2000stable}. However, many special features make the problem of finding and optimizing over the set of stable-matchings NP-hard~\citep{ronn1990np,manlove2002hard,biro2010college}. In these cases, integer programming techniques have proven effective. Formulations to optimize over stable matchings in problems with ties, lower and common quotas, couples, paired applications, and other extensions~\citep{Kwanashie2014,delorme2019mathematical,biro2014hospitals,agoston2016integer,agoston2022college,rios2024stable}. Unlike our case, these studies assume agents' capacities and preferences are fixed and deterministic.

\paragraph{Capacity expansion.} A small but growing body of work has examined stable matching problems with capacity expansion, primarily in the context of school choice. In these models, the clearinghouse simultaneously allocates additional seats to schools and identifies a student-optimal stable matching. These studies assume that student preferences are known before capacity planning, leading to capacity allocations that enhance the existing matching. Most papers consider that schools start with initial capacities, which can be increased within the limits of the maximum available extra seats. Unlike the classical student-optimal stable matching problem, the capacity expansion variant is NP-hard~\citep{bobbio2022capacity}. To address it,~\citet{bobbio2023capacity} offers a toolkit composed of mixed-integer programming-based methods.~\citet{afacan2024capacity} introduced a myopic algorithm in which each iteration obtains a student-optimal stable matching under a fixed capacity using the {\DAA}. Both studies showed that their mechanisms are not strategy-proof in the general case, though~\citet{bobbio2023capacity} also showed that their approach is \emph{strategy-proof in the large}.

\citet{abe2022anytime} introduced a heuristic based on upper confidence Monte Carlo search trees, which explores the space of capacity expansion decisions and also uses the {\DAA} to compute a student-optimal stable matching for each fixed capacity.~\citet{kumano2022quota} studied a similar problem in which schools (or programs) are divided into departments, each with a limit on the number of students it can accommodate. Capacity decisions involve defining the distribution of available seats between schools within each department. Similar to~\citet{afacan2024capacity}, their method iteratively applies the {\DAA} for each fixed capacity expansion solution, but differs in its initialization and capacity update rules. To our knowledge, no prior work considers capacity expansion decisions made \emph{before} student preferences are revealed, nor models strategic preference reporting in response to those decisions.

\paragraph{Stochastic setting.} Most research on {\TMMP} has focused on deterministic settings. A few studies consider cases where a stable matching is defined first, and uncertainty arises from agents leaving, declining assignments, or new agents entering the system~\citep[e.g.,][]{bampis2023online}. %,gajulapalli2019stability}. 
Others examine robust stable matchings against pair breakups~\citep{genc2017finding} or changes in preferences~\citep{bredereck2020adapting}, while some model preference uncertainty due to limited information~\citep{aziz2020stable}. In contrast, our setting has uncertainty arising from the timing of students’ preference submissions, which occur after capacity decisions are made. 

\paragraph{Agents' behavior.}
Agents' behavior in {\TMMP} has been extensively studied; see~\citet{rees2023behavioral} for a comprehensive review. That work categorizes three main groups of reasons for misreporting true preferences: (i) incorrect incentive processing; (ii) nonstandard preferences; and (iii) biased beliefs about admission chances. Biased estimation of admission chances has received the most attention~\citep{benjamin2019errors}. A foundational model in this line is the portfolio choice problem ({\PCP} -- \citealt{chade2006simultaneous,olszewski2016simultaneous}), a simultaneous selection problem where agents select a subset of ranked options, respecting a selection limit, and maximize expected utility based on their admission chances and true preferences. The {\PCP} serves as a model for the strategic behavior of agents and is often used to estimate their preferences~\citep[see, e.g.,][]{agarwal2018demand}. In addition to strategic behavior, agents' preferences have been estimated in cases of truthful reporting or mechanism stability~\citep[see, e.g.,][]{fack2019beyond}. We refer to~\citet{agarwal2020revealed} for a review of behavior models estimating students' preferences in the school choice application. Traditionally, agents' behavior and the {\TMMP} are treated separately: agents' preferences are determined first, for example by solving the {\PCP}, and then used as input for the {\TMMP}. In our setting, school capacities affect perceived admission chances, which in turn influence reported preferences. This motivates a joint optimization framework that integrates behavioral modeling with capacity planning.

\subsection{Exogenous and Endogenous Uncertainty}

Uncertainty is inherent in most real-world decision-making problems and requires appropriate modeling frameworks~\citep{kuccukyavuz2017introduction}. The majority of existing work focuses on \emph{exogenous uncertainty}, where the random vector is independent of the decisions~\citep{li2021review}. We refer to \citet{shapiro2021lectures} for an overview of theoretical foundations and popular algorithms. Our {\SP} belongs to this case. In contrast, \emph{endogenous uncertainty} arises when the random vector depends on the decisions.~\citet{goel2006class} classify endogenous uncertainty into Type 1, where the probability distribution of the random vector is decision-dependent, and Type 2, where the timing of random vector realization depends on the decisions. We consider {\SPEU} to have Type~1 endogenous uncertainty and refer to it simply as endogenous uncertainty. The mathematical properties of a stochastic program depend on whether uncertainty is exogenous or endogenous, with the latter being significantly more challenging to handle due to the common presence of nonlinearity and nonconvexity~\citep{dupacova2006optimization}. In the endogenous case, most research focuses on solving the mixed-integer nonlinear (or linear in specific cases) deterministic equivalent~\citep[e.g.,][]{hellemo2018decision,bhuiyan2020stochastic}, which is challenging due to its nonlinearity and the need to enumerate all scenarios. To address this,~\citet{bazotte2024solving} model endogenous random variables as transformations of first-stage decisions and exogenous random variables, converting the {\SPEU} into a transformed {\SP}. The transformed {\SP}, for which efficient solution methods exist~\citep[see][]{shapiro2021lectures}, has a convex linear relaxation in many cases~\citep{bazotte2024solving}. We adopt this approach to solve the {\STMMP} under endogenous uncertainty (see Section~\ref{sec:formulation}).
Nonetheless, the transformation function is problem-specific and can be difficult to define in certain cases. In our setting, it introduces significant complexity into the formulation, highlighting the need for further methodological advances---both in defining such functions and in solving the transformed {\SP}.
To our knowledge, we are the first to study endogenous uncertainty in the {\TMMP}.

\section{Problem Statement} \label{sec:prob-stat}

The timeline of the {\STMMP} is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:timeline-problem}, comprising three main time steps. At Time Step~1 (first stage of the stochastic program), the clearinghouse determines a capacity expansion plan: it allocates additional capacity (seats) to schools subject to a predefined budget. At this point, schools also rank students based on their priorities, which are treated as deterministic. At Time Step~2, a scenario unfolds: the realization of students' random \emph{true} preferences over schools is revealed. Students then submit their \emph{reported} preferences to the clearinghouse. We assume that students are aware of the capacity and their individual priority at each school, but lack complete information about the priorities assigned to other students. This assumption reflects a realistic scenario where students have partial information about their standing but remain uncertain about their relative position in the broader applicant pool. They are also unaware of other students' true and reported preferences, as submissions occur simultaneously. This setting captures a common form of information asymmetry in large-scale market design~\citep{azevedo2019strategy}. We assume that students follow distinct behavioral models: (i) truthful behavior, in which they report their true preferences directly, leading to exogenous uncertainty; and (ii) strategic behavior, in which their reported preferences depend on both their true preferences and their perceived admission chances---defined by their priority at each school and the schools’ capacities---resulting in endogenous uncertainty. In the latter case, students' reported preferences are a function of capacity decisions and their true preferences, as formalized in Section~\ref{subsec:students-behavior}.

At Time Step~3 (second stage of the stochastic program), the clearinghouse computes a student-optimal stable matching based on reported preferences, priorities, and schools' expanded capacities. Overall, the {\STMMP} seeks capacity allocation decisions that respect the budget while minimizing the value of the second stage---the expected sum of the ranks that students assign to their matched schools in the stable matching---accounting for all possible realizations of students' preferences and their probabilities.

