\section{Problem Formulation} \label{sec:formulation}

Let $\mathcal{C}=\{c_1, \ldots, c_m\}$ denote the set of $m$ schools, and $\mathcal{S}=\{s_1, \ldots, s_n\}$ be the set of $n$ students, all assumed---without loss of generality---to belong to the same education level. \endnote{Assuming a fixed number of students $n$ is justified by the reliable availability of annual statistics tracking student progression across education levels.} We use the notation $\varnothing$ to represent an unassigned student. Each school $c \in \mathcal{C}$ has a capacity of $q_c \in \mathbb{Z}_+$ seats, while $\varnothing$ has a sufficiently large capacity such that all students may remain unassigned (i.e., $q_{\varnothing} = n$). Let $B$ be the maximum number of extra seats allocated to schools, with $\mathcal{B}=\{1, \ldots, B\}$ and $\mathcal{B}^0 = \mathcal{B} \cup \{0\}$. Each school $c \in \mathcal{C}$ ranks all the students in $\mathcal{S}$ according to a deterministic strict order $\succ_c$, with $\succ_{\mathcal{C}} = \{\succ_c\}_{c \in \mathcal{C}}$. These preferences can be represented by scores $e_{s,c}$, where school $c$ prefers student $s$ over student $s'$ (i.e., $s \succ_c s'$) iff $e_{s,c} > e_{s',c}$. We assume $e_{s,c}$ to be a reverse rank of student $s$ in school $c$ and thus $e_{s,c} \leq n$. Students can list at most $ K $ schools in their reported preferences, where $K < m$, and we define the sets $\mathcal{K} = \{1, \dots, K\}$ and $\mathcal{K}^{0} = \mathcal{K}\cup\{0\}$.

In the first stage of the {\STMMP}, the clearinghouse allocates up to $B$ additional seats to schools, considering that:
(i) Students submit \emph{reported} preferences based solely on their \emph{true} preferences (nonstrategic case), or on both their perceived admission chances and true preferences (strategic case), while respecting the list size limit~$K$.
(ii) Perceived admission chances depend on schools' capacities and thus on first-stage decisions; students expect higher chances at schools with more seats. Students' random \emph{reported} preferences are then endogenous in the strategic case.
(iii) Students' random \emph{true} preferences are exogenous, being independent of school capacities while capturing variability in student-school affinities due to factors such as educational quality, commute distance, or sibling enrollment.
(iv) Once students and schools reveal their preferences, a student-optimal stable matching is computed.
We now model uncertainty in students' preferences.

The continuous exogenous random variables $\boldsymbol{u}_{s,c}$ denote the utility of student $s \in \mathcal{S}$ upon admission to school $c \in \mathcal{C}$. Let $\mathcal{W}$ represent the set of scenarios corresponding to the possible realizations of these random variables. For each scenario $w \in \mathcal{W}$, we define ${u_s^w = \{u_{s,c}^w\}_{c \in \mathcal{C} \cup \{\varnothing\}}}$ as the realization of the utility vector ${\boldsymbol{u}_s = \{\boldsymbol{u}_{s,c}\}_{c \in \mathcal{C} \cup \{\varnothing\}}}$. These utilities determine the random \emph{true} strict preference order of students, ${\boldsymbol{\succ}_{\mathcal{S}} = \{\boldsymbol{\succ}_{s}\}_{s \in \mathcal{S}}}$, over $\mathcal{C} \cup \{\varnothing\}$. In each scenario $w \in \mathcal{W}$, this preference order is realized as $\succ_{\mathcal{S},w} = \{\succ_{s, w}\}_{s \in \mathcal{S}}$, where student $s$ \emph{prefers} school $c$ over school $c'$ in scenario $w$ (i.e., $c \succ_{s,w} c'$) iff $u_{s,c}^w > u_{s,c'}^w$. We use $c' \succeq_{s,w} c$ to indicate that either $c' \succ_{s,w} c$ or $c' = c$. We also set $u_{s,\varnothing}^w = 0$ for every student $s \in \mathcal{S}$ and scenario $w \in \mathcal{W}$, and allow for $u_{s,c}^w < 0$ for some $c \in \mathcal{C}$ such that $\varnothing \succ_{s, w} c$, meaning students may truly prefer being unassigned over being matched with certain schools. Thus, in certain scenarios, fewer than $n$ students may apply to the matching, as some may prefer $\varnothing$ over all available schools---reflecting settings in which some students opt out of centralized assignments (e.g., due to private school alternatives). We let $\mathcal{C}_{s,w} \subseteq \mathcal{C}$ be the set of schools in the true preference $\succ_{s,w}$, i.e., the set of schools that student $s$ \emph{truly} prefers over being unassigned in scenario $w$ ($c \in \mathcal{C}_{s,w}$ iff $c \succ_{s,w} \varnothing$), and $r_{s,c,w}$ be the rank of school $c \in \mathcal{C}_{s,w}$ in the true preference $\succ_{s,w}$. %, and $r_{s,\varnothing,w} = \min(K,|\mathcal{C}_{s,w}|)+1$ be the penalty for having student $s$ unassigned in scenario $w$.

Nonetheless, we assume that students may have different behaviors, and their random \emph{reported} preference order $\boldsymbol{\succ}^{*,a}_{\mathcal{S}} = \{\boldsymbol{\succ}_{s}^{*,a}\}_{s \in \mathcal{S}}$, based on behavioral model $a \in \mathcal{A}$, may differ from their \emph{true} preference order $\boldsymbol{\succ}_{\mathcal{S}}$ and should respect the size limit of $K$ schools. We let $\mathcal{A}=\{{\CEUM},{\IEUM},{\UM}\}$ represent the considered behaviors (see Section~\ref{subsec:students-behavior}). As noted, reported preferences are determined by students' exogenous true preferences in all cases, and, in strategic cases, also by their perceived admission chances---and thus by capacity expansion decisions. Hence, $\boldsymbol{\succ}^{*,a}_{\mathcal{S}}$ is exogenous in the nonstrategic case ($a = {\UM}$) and endogenous in the strategic cases ($a \in \{{\CEUM},{\IEUM}\}$). For each scenario $w \in \mathcal{W}$ (for each realization of $\boldsymbol{u}_{s,c}$), we obtain a realization ${\succ_{\mathcal{S},w}^{*,a} = \{\succ_{s,w}^{*,a}\}_{s \in \mathcal{S}}}$ of the reported preference order ${\boldsymbol{\succ}_{\mathcal{S}}^{*,a} = \{\boldsymbol{\succ}_{s}^{*,a}\}_{s \in \mathcal{S}}}$. We also denote $\mathcal{C}^{*,a}_{s,w}$ as the set of schools in the reported preference $\succ_{s,w}^{*,a}$, satisfying $|\mathcal{C}_{s,w}^{*,a}| \leq K$, and use the indicator $\xi^{*,a}_{s,c,w}$, which equals one if $c \in \mathcal{C}_{s,w}^{*,a}$ and zero otherwise. In this context, we assume $\mathcal{C}_{s,w}^{*,a} \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{s,w}$. We let $r^{*,a}_{s,c,w}$ be the rank of school $c \in \mathcal{C}_{s,w}^{*,a}$ in the reported preference list $\succ_{s,w}^{*,a}$ of student $s$ in scenario $w$, and $r_{s,\varnothing,w}^{*,a} = |\mathcal{C}_{s,w}^{*,a}|+1$ be the penalty for having student $s$ unassigned in scenario $w$, i.e., $r^{*,a}_{s,c,w}=1$ implies that $c$ is student $s$’s top preference in scenario $w$ ($c \succ_{s,w} c'$ for all $c' \in \mathcal{C} \cup \{\varnothing\} \backslash \{c\}$). Table~\ref{tab:notation} illustrates our conventions for notation.

