\section{Experimental Results}\label{sec:results}

The goal of our experimental analysis is threefold. First, we estimate the value of explicitly modeling stochastic preferences, as opposed to relying on an average scenario ({\EV} problem). Second, we examine how capacity allocation decisions are affected by different models of student behavior. Third, we assess the performance of our {\SAA}-based methods for solving the {\STMMP}.

The {\SAA} program, the {\ASG} {\SAA} program, the {\SAA} {\LR} subproblems, and the {\EV} problem were implemented in C++ and solved using Gurobi 12.0.0. The {\DAA}, {\MIA}, and {\IOA} were also coded in C++. These algorithms are used to compute the estimators $\hat{g}_N^a(x)$ for any feasible solution $x \in \mathcal{X}$, and thus to evaluate the value of feasible first-stage decisions in multiple contexts: Step~2 of the {\SAA} method, Step~2 of the {\ASG} heuristic, the upper-bound procedure in the {\LR} heuristic, and the neighborhood evaluation step of both the {\LS} and {\SA} heuristics. They are also used to compute the expected result of using the {\EV} solution ({\EEV}). All experiments were run single-threaded on an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake CPU @ 2.4 GHz. All instances, results, and code are publicly available.\endnote{\url{https://github.com/mariabazotte/endog-school-choice}\label{fn:github}}

