\subsubsection{Preprocessing.}

Under the {\UM} behavior, both $\mathcal{C}_{s,w}^w$ and $\succ_{s,w}^K$ are exogenous and thus fully precomputable. For the {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} behaviors, as previously discussed, the reported preference set $\mathcal{C}_{s,w}^{*,a}$ and order $\succ^{*,a}_{s,w}$ can be precomputed \emph{a priori} when $|\mathcal{C}_{s,w}| \leq K$, in which case they coincide with the true preferences $\mathcal{C}_{s,w}$ and $\succ_{s,w}$ regardless of the first-stage decision $x$. Additional conditions enabling partial precomputation under {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:preprocess}.

\subsection{Value of the Stochastic Solution}\label{subsec:vss}

This section analyzes the value of the stochastic solution ({\VSS}) of the {\STMMP} under all student behaviors; in simple terms, whether accounting for the diversity of scenario realizations adds value. The relative ${\textrm{{\VSS}}^a}$ under behavior $a \in \mathcal{A}$ is defined as ${(\textrm{{\EEV}}^a - v^{a}_{opt} )/v^{a}_{opt}}$, where $v^{a}_{opt}$ is the optimal value of Program~\eqref{prog:stmmp-1st}, and $\textrm{{\EEV}}^a$ represents the objective value of solution $x^{a}_{ev}$ in Program~\eqref{prog:stmmp-1st}, with $x^{a}_{ev}$ being the optimal solution of the {\EV} problem, all with respect to behavior $a$. We refer to the deterministic version of Program~\eqref{prog:stmmp-1st} as the {\EV} problem, which considers the expected value of utilities $\boldsymbol{u}$. Hence, our {\EV} problem is not the conventional version used in stochastic programming but rather an approximation; it uses the expected values of $\boldsymbol{u}$ instead of those of~$\boldsymbol{\succ}^{*,a}_{\mathcal{S}}$ (see Appendix~\ref{app:vss} for details). Finally, we focus our analysis on large instances.

We compute the estimator ${\overline{\textrm{{\VSS}}}^a= 100\% \cdot [\overline{\textrm{{\EEV}}}^a - \hat{g}^a_{N'}(\overline{x}_N^a)]}/{\hat{g}^a_{N'}(\overline{x}_N^a)}$ for $a \in \mathcal{A}$, where ${\overline{\textrm{{\EEV}}}^a = \hat{g}^a_{N'}(x^a_{ev})}$. We also evaluate the effect of capacity expansion decisions on the average number of students who 
(i) enter the matching, i.e., previously unassigned students with \(B = 0\) who are assigned to one of their preferences under expanded capacities, and;
(ii) improve their assignments, i.e., previously assigned students with $B = 0$ who are reassigned to more preferred schools under expanded capacities.
Let \(\hat{e}^a_{N'}(x)\) and \(\hat{i}^a_{N'}(x)\) denote the estimators of the average number of entering and improving students considering $N'$ scenarios, respectively, under behavior \(a \in \mathcal{A}\) for a given allocation \(x\). We compute the estimators ${\overline{\textrm{{\VSSENTER}}}^a= 100\% \cdot [\hat{e}^a_{N'}(\overline{x}_N^a) - \hat{e}^a_{N'}(x_{ev}^a)]}/{\hat{e}^a_{N'}(\overline{x}_N^a)}$ and ${\overline{\textrm{{\VSSIMPROV}}}^a= 100\% \cdot [ \hat{i}^a_{N'}(\overline{x}_N^a) - \hat{i}^a_{N'}(x_{ev}^a) ]}/{\hat{i}^a_{N'}(\overline{x}_N^a)}$ for all behaviors. Unlike the original objective of minimizing students' matching preferences, larger values for the number of students entering or improving are preferable. Hence, the sign of the numerator is reversed in both $\overline{{\VSSENTER}}^a$ and $\overline{{\VSSIMPROV}}^a$ compared to $\overline{{\VSS}}^a$. We use the feasible solution $\overline{x}_N^a$ from the {\SAA} method with $N = 100$ to compute the estimators for all behaviors. We set $N' = 5 \times 10^3$, as the standard deviation of $\hat{g}^a_{N'}(\overline{x}_N^a)$ and $\hat{g}^a_{N'}(x_{ev}^a)$ was below 0.125\% of their mean across all large instances, indicating low variance.

Figure~\ref{fig:vss} shows the mean and quantiles of $\overline{{\VSS}}^a$, $\overline{{\VSSENTER}}^a$, and $\overline{{\VSSIMPROV}}^a$, averaged over large instances with identical budgets, for each behavior $a \in \mathcal{A}$. Detailed results by budget $B$ and list size limit $K$ are presented in Appendix~\ref{app:vss}. Based on the computational analysis shown later (Section~\ref{subsec:comparison-performance}), the {\SAA} program was solved to termination using the {\LR} heuristic under {\UM}, and the {\SA} heuristic under {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}. Their hyperparameters are specified in Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}. For the {\EV} problem, all instances under {\UM} were solved to optimality with Gurobi using the modified $L$-constraints formulation within 500 seconds. For {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}, exact methods struggled to solve the {\EV} efficiently, and the {\SA} heuristic was used with the parameters in Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}, within 250 seconds. The best feasible solution found was used in place of the optimum $x_{ev}^a$.

