\subsection{Comparison of Students' Behavior} \label{subsec:comparison-strategic}

In this section, we evaluate how capacity expansion decisions derived under one assumed student behavior perform when applied to other behavioral models. Our goal is to assess the robustness of capacity decisions to behavioral misspecification. Given a feasible solution $\overline{x}_N^{a'}$ obtained from the {\SAA} method under behavior $a' \in \mathcal{A}$, we evaluate its performance under a different behavior $a \in \mathcal{A}\backslash\{a'\}$ by using $\hat{g}_{N'}^{a}(\overline{x}_N^{a'})$, which computes the expected students' matching preferences assuming behavior $a$, but with capacity expansion decisions planned for $a'$. To quantify the performance loss from misassumed behavior, we define the stochastic ($a'$--$a$) behavior gap as ${\overline{\texttt{gap}}^{a'-a}_{N'} = 100\% \cdot [\hat{g}_{N'}^a(\overline{x}_N^{a'}) - \hat{g}_{N'}^a(\overline{x}_N^{a}) ] / \hat{g}_{N'}^a(\overline{x}_N^{a})}$. This measures the relative loss in expected students' matching preferences under behavior $a$ when implementing capacity expansion decisions optimized for $a'$. A similar analysis can be conducted for deterministic approximations. We evaluate the optimal solution $x^{a'}_{ev}$ obtained from the {\EV} problem under behavior $a'$ by computing the estimator $\hat{g}_{N'}^{a}(x^{a'}_{ev})$ for a different behavior $a$. In this case, we define the deterministic ($a'$--$a$) behavior gap estimator as ${\overline{\texttt{gap}}_{ev,N'}^{a'-a} = 100\% \cdot [\hat{g}_{N'}^a(x^{a'}_{ev}) - \hat{g}_{N'}^a(x^{a}_{N}) ] / \hat{g}_{N'}^a(x^{a}_{N})}$, which measures the relative loss in expected student matching preference under behavior $a$ when using a capacity expansion decision obtained for the average scenario under behavior $a'$, instead of the full stochastic problem under $a$. 

As previously discussed, the relatively low penalty for unassigned students in the objective function leads the clearinghouse to prioritize improving the quality of assignments over minimizing the number of unmatched students. Based on this, we evaluate the average number of students who benefit from expanded capacities compared to the baseline with no additional capacities ($B=0$). This includes both students who newly enter the matching and those who improve their assigned school. We define the benefit estimator as: $\hat{b}_{N'}^a(x) = \hat{i}_{N'}^a(x) + \hat{e}_{N'}^a(x)$, where $\hat{i}_{N'}^a(x)$ and $\hat{e}_{N'}^a(x)$ respectively denote the estimated number of improving and entering students under behavior $a$ for a given decision $x$. Using this metric, we compute the ($a'$--$a$) behavior stochastic and deterministic gaps: ${\overline{\texttt{gap-b}}_{N'}^{a'-a} = 100\% \cdot [ \hat{b}_{N'}^a(\overline{x}_N^{a}) -\hat{b}_{N'}^a(\overline{x}_N^{a'}) ] / \hat{b}_{N'}^a(\overline{x}_N^{a})}$ and ${\overline{\texttt{gap-b}}_{ev,N'}^{a'-a} = 100\% \cdot [ \hat{b}_{N'}^a(x^{a}_{N}) - \hat{b}_{N'}^a(x^{a'}_{ev})] / \hat{b}_{N'}^a(x^{a}_{N})}$. Note that the sign of the numerator is reversed relative to the gap definitions based on the original objective function. This reflects the fact that while the original objective is minimized (lower is better), the benefit metric is maximized (higher is better).

We examine the impact of assuming truthful behavior when students act strategically, and of misrepresenting their strategic behavior, on capacity expansion decisions. Thus, we compute the stochastic and deterministic ({\UM}--{\CEUM}), ({\UM}--{\IEUM}), and ({\IEUM}--{\CEUM}) behavior gaps for both metrics. Figures~\ref{fig:stoch_behavior_gaps} and \ref{fig:det_behavior_gaps} show the stochastic and deterministic behavior gaps, respectively, averaged over instances with the same size limit $K$ and budget $B$. As in the previous section, we set $N' = 5 \times 10^3$, solve the {\SAA} program with $N = 100$ to obtain $\overline{x}_N^a$, and apply the solution procedures outlined earlier (see Appendix~\ref{app:comparison-strategic} for detailed results).

