Figure~\ref{fig:perf-um} shows that the {\SAA} Program~\eqref{prog:saa} under {\UM} behavior can be effectively solved using the exact method based on the modified $L$-constraints formulation within a reasonable time. This approach solved 18\% of large instances to optimality, achieved optimality gaps below 5\% in 90\% of cases, and maintained upper bound gaps under 0.5\% across all instances (average of 0.082\%) within a two-hour limit, with an average runtime of 5980.1 seconds. This approach is particularly suitable when capacity expansion decisions are made well in advance of the matching process---for example, during annual strategic planning---where computational time is less restrictive.

Nonetheless, improving the quality of the {\SAA} method may require either solving multiple {\SAA} Programs~\eqref{prog:saa} with different scenario samples $\mathcal{W}_\mathcal{N}$, or increasing the number of scenarios $N$ to better capture uncertainty. In such cases, solving the {\SAA} program exactly becomes considerably more challenging. This is particularly relevant in time-sensitive contexts---for example, when public funding, such as government grants and scholarships, must be allocated and implemented rapidly. In these situations, relying solely on exact methods may be impractical, and efficient heuristics become necessary.

As mentioned previously, the {\ASG} heuristic is a restricted version of the {\LR} heuristic, limited to a single iteration of the subgradient method with Lagrangian multipliers set to zero. Figure~\ref{fig:perf-um} shows that {\ASG} heuristic already yields high-quality solutions in many cases; it produced feasible solutions with an average upper bound gap of 0.106\% under 27 seconds across all instances. The {\LR} heuristic performs even better, achieving the lowest average upper bound gap of all methods at just 0.031\%. The {\LR} heuristic also consistently produces significantly better optimality gaps than {\ASG} heuristic, with averages of 4.834\% and 9.323\%, respectively (see Figure~\ref{subfig:optgap-um}). Importantly, while heuristics do not typically provide formal guarantees on solution quality, the inclusion of lower bounds in the {\LR} framework distinguishes them from standard heuristic approaches and provides performance guarantees. The computational time of both the {\ASG} and {\LR} heuristics is stable over instances (see Figure~\ref{subfig:time-um}), indicating their efficacy in handling instances with a large first-stage feasibility set. Hence, in cases where speed is critical for the matching process, these methods are highly effective. 

Figure~\ref{subfig:ubgap-um} shows that the {\LS} heuristic consistently yields higher upper bound gaps than the other approaches, averaging 1.475\%. Its performance is budget-sensitive and, thus, influenced by the size of the first-stage feasibility set. Figure~\ref{subfig:time-um} evidences that the {\LS} heuristic reached the time limit in 26.5\% of the instances---primarily those with larger budgets ($B \in \{30, 45, 60\}$)--—resulting in lower solution quality. In these cases, the average upper bound gap reaches 2.94\%, for a runtime of 5380 seconds. By contrast, for smaller budgets ($B \in \{1, 5, 15\}$), it consistently found high-quality solutions within the time limit, with an average gap of 0.011\% and a runtime of 1054 seconds (see Appendix~\ref{app:perf-nonstrat} for details). 

These trends stem from the neighborhood structure in the {\LS} heuristic, which directly impacts the computational time. For large-budget instances, the approach requires a greater number of iterations to converge, leading to longer runtimes. The {\LS} heuristic is thus more effective for instances with lower budget values, where convergence is achieved in fewer iterations. In contrast, the {\SA} heuristic maintained low upper bound gaps across all budgets, averaging 0.105\%, with a lower and more stable runtime over instances than the {\LS} heuristic (see Figure~\ref{subfig:time-um}). The {\SA} heuristic performs a randomized search over the neighborhood rather than exhaustively exploring it. As a result, its computational time is more closely tied to the hyperparameters (iteration limits and temperature decay) defining its termination than to the size of the feasibility set. This makes the {\SA} heuristic more scalable to large-budget instances. We also observe that the runtime and upper bound gap of the {\LR} heuristic are slightly lower than those of the {\SA} heuristic, suggesting that {\LR} remains the most effective among the three in terms of both solution quality and efficiency.

\subsubsection{Strategic Behaviors.}\label{subsubsec:perf-strat}

To solve the {\SAA} Program~\eqref{prog:saa} of the {\STMMP}-{\IEUM} and {\STMMP}-{\CEUM} problems, we compare the computational performance of the exact cutoff score formulations solved with the Gurobi solver against the {\LS} and {\SA} heuristics. Initial experiments revealed that, even when relaxing the stability constraints (as in the {\ASG} and {\LR} heuristics), the resulting problems remain computationally challenging due to the endogenous preferences. In particular, solving exactly the {\SAA} program under {\CEUM} or {\IEUM} is significantly harder than under {\UM}, with large instances often exhibiting optimality gaps near 100\% even after eight hours of computation. We also explored integer and alternating L-shaped decomposition techniques~\citep{angulo2016improving}, but found them ineffective due to weak cuts---an issue typical in two-stage models with integer second-stage decisions---resulting in slow convergence and loose bounds. This limits our ability to assess the quality of feasible solutions produced by the heuristics. 

Given these challenges, we structure our analysis in two parts. First, we evaluate the computational performance of the proposed methods on small instances, where optimality can be more reliably assessed. Then, we compare the performance of the {\LS} and {\SA} heuristics on large instances, where exact methods become impractical. Appendix~\ref{app:perf-strat} details the hyperparameter values used for the {\SA} heuristic, while Appendix~\ref{app:warm-start} describes the warm-start strategy used in the exact approach.

Figures~\ref{fig:perf-ceum-small} and~\ref{fig:perf-ieum-small} present the results for small instances with $N = 50$ scenarios under the {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} behaviors, respectively. All experiments were run with a time limit of eight hours. Specifically, we report the computational time (Figures~\ref{subfig:time-ceum-small} and~\ref{subfig:time-ieum-small}), the optimality gap [$\text{gap}_{opt}$] (Figures~\ref{subfig:optgap-ceum-small} and~\ref{subfig:optgap-ieum-small}), and the upper bound gap [$\text{gap}_{ub^*}$] (Figures~\ref{subfig:ubgap-ceum-small} and~\ref{subfig:ubgap-ieum-small}), as defined in the previous section. Each metric is presented as a function of the percentage of instances. Figures~\ref{fig:perf-ceum-large} and~\ref{fig:perf-ieum-large} report the corresponding results for large instances, using $N = 100$ scenarios for {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}, respectively, also under the eight-hour time limit. For these larger cases, we restrict our analyses to the local search–based heuristics, and thus report the runtime (Figures~\ref{subfig:time-ceum-large} and~\ref{subfig:time-ieum-large}) and the upper bound gap (Figures~\ref{subfig:ubgap-ceum-large} and~\ref{subfig:ubgap-ieum-large}). We refer to Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results} for details.

