Figures~\ref{fig:perf-ceum-small} and~\ref{fig:perf-ieum-small} show that even small instances of the {\SAA} Program~\eqref{prog:saa} under the strategic {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} behaviors are challenging to solve exactly to optimality, even within a generous eight-hours time limit. The exact approach based on the cutoff score formulation solved approximately 48.7\% and 56\% of the small instances to optimality under the {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} behaviors, respectively; it achieved optimality gaps below 5\% for about 78\% and 85\% of the instances, and below 10\% for 99\% of instances in both cases. The corresponding upper bound gaps were below 2.6\% and 2.3\%, respectively. These results highlight the increased complexity due to endogenous preferences, as the same problem is significantly easier under the {\UM} behavior. Among the programs under the strategic settings, {\IEUM} is slightly easier to solve than {\CEUM}.

This behavior is expected: in the endogenous case, students' reported preferences must be explicitly defined for each scenario, via functions that depend on first-stage decisions and true preferences. These involve embedded optimization problems---Program~\eqref{eq:max-exp-utility} for {\CEUM} and Program~\eqref{eq:max-exp-utility-simple} for {\IEUM}---making the overall program bilevel. While single-level problem reformulations are possible, they require numerous binary variables and loose constraints, leading to weaker linear relaxations and poor computational performance. Hence, even in contexts where capacity expansion is determined well in advance of the matching process, heuristic methods may be necessary to obtain high-quality feasible solutions---particularly when a large scenario set $\mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{N}}$ is required, or when multiple {\SAA} Programs~\eqref{prog:saa} must be solved with distinct scenario sets. 

Both the {\SA} and {\LS} heuristics require extremely low runtimes for small instances (under 109 and 8 seconds, respectively). While the {\SA} heuristic is slightly slower, it consistently delivers higher-quality feasible solutions. The average upper bound gap of the {\SA} heuristic is 0.000\% under {\CEUM} and 0.003\% under {\IEUM}, compared to 0.189\% and 0.223\% of the {\LS} heuristic, respectively. In contrast, the exact approach takes significantly longer time to reach comparable or even slightly inferior solutions, notably when compared to the {\SA} heuristic. Overall, the {\SA} heuristic offers the best trade-off between runtime and solution quality, outperforming both the exact method and the {\LS} heuristic in most cases.

Figures~\ref{subfig:ubgap-ceum-large} and~\ref{subfig:ubgap-ieum-large} show that the {\LS} heuristic consistently yields higher upper bound gaps than the {\SA} heuristic under both behaviors for large instances, averaging 1.478\% for {\CEUM} and 0.494\% for {\IEUM}. As with the {\UM} behavior, the performance of the {\LS} heuristic is budget-dependent and strongly influenced by the size of the first-stage feasible region under {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}. Figures~\ref{subfig:time-ceum-large} and~\ref{subfig:time-ieum-large} reveal that the {\LS} heuristic reached the time limit in 22\% and 13.5\% of the instances for {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}, respectively---primarily those with larger budgets ($\mathcal{B} \in \{30, 45, 60\}$). For these budgets, the average runtime and upper bound gap were 19,128 seconds and 2.915\% for {\CEUM}, and 15,340.3 seconds and 0.976\% for {\IEUM}. For smaller budgets ($\mathcal{B} \in \{1, 5, 15\}$), average runtimes and gaps dropped significantly to 2,761 seconds and 0.400\% for {\CEUM}, and 1,515 seconds and 0.011\% for {\IEUM}. In contrast, the {\SA} heuristic maintained low upper bound gaps across all budgets, averaging 0.109\% for {\CEUM} and 0.266\% for {\IEUM}, and outperformed the {\LS} heuristic in terms of solution quality. It also consistently exhibited lower and more stable runtimes across budget levels. These trends stem from the same analysis as in the {\UM} case: the impact of the {\LS} neighborhood on convergence under large budgets for the {\LS} heuristic, and the role of hyperparameters in terminating the {\SA} heuristic.

Both the {\LS} and {\SA} heuristics exhibit shorter runtimes under the {\UM} behavior than under the strategic {\IEUM} and {\CEUM} behaviors. On average, the {\SA} heuristic is 2.6 and 2 times slower for {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}, respectively, compared to {\UM}; similarly, the {\LS} heuristic is 3.4 and 2.6 times slower. This difference stems from the neighborhood evaluation step: under {\UM}, this step uses the {\DAA} algorithm directly, whereas under {\IEUM} and {\CEUM}, it requires computing reported preferences via the {\IOA} and {\MIA}, increasing computational cost.


\section{Conclusions} \label{sec:conclusions}

This work introduced and formalized the {\STMMP}, where the clearinghouse must decide on capacity expansions before students' reported preferences are revealed. These preferences may be exogenous (truthful) or endogenous (strategically adjusted based on admission chances influenced by capacities). Then, after these preferences are revealed, a student-optimal stable matching is implemented based on the expanded capacities and students' reported preferences.

We considered three behavioral models: truthful reporting ({\UM}) and two forms of strategic behavior ({\CEUM} and {\IEUM}). For each, we formulated the corresponding {\STMMP} and solved it using the {\SAA} method. We developed a toolkit with both exact and heuristic approaches for solving the {\SAA} program of the {\STMMP}. Our results showed that the {\SAA} programs under strategic behaviors are substantially more difficult to solve to optimality than under nonstrategic, due to the endogenous nature of students’ reported preferences. Nonetheless, our proposed heuristics consistently yield feasible, high-quality solutions across all behaviors.

We also found that explicitly modeling stochasticity via the {\SAA} method consistently outperforms solving the deterministic {\EV} problem, across all student behaviors and evaluation metrics---including students' matching preferences, the number of entering students, and those improving their assignments. Moreover, assuming incorrect behavioral models when making capacity expansion decisions consistently leads to worse outcomes. Overall, our findings highlight the importance of both accounting for uncertainty and accurately modeling student behavior when planning capacity expansion decisions in centralized matching markets.

The research perspectives connected to our work are numerous. These include extensions to alternative settings---such as capacity reductions, tuition waiver allocations, secured enrollment mechanisms, lower and common quotas, couples matching, and paired applications---as well as further exploration of alternative models of agent behavior, such as reputation-based behavioral biases~\citep{kleinberg2024modeling}. Moreover, investigating scenario clustering procedures offers a promising direction to better represent uncertainty in the {\SAA} framework by preserving key stochastic features while reducing the number of scenarios through aggregation. Beyond the school choice context, the {\STMMP} framework is also adaptable to a wide range of applications where capacity must be allocated before preferences are known. Examples include expanding refugee settlements, distributing vaccines across venues, allocating medical resources, and assigning tuition waivers in college admissions. In all these cases,  uncertainty about preferences at the time of capacity planning presents a central challenge that our framework is well positioned to address. 

