\begin{result}\label{res:um-exact}
    The exact approach using the modified $L$-constraints solved approximately 18\% of large instances to optimality within the two-hour limit (average time: 5980.1 seconds), yielding high-quality solutions for {\SAA} Program~\eqref{prog:saa} under {\UM}. It achieved optimality gaps below 5\% in 90\% of cases (maximum: 7.627\%, average: 2.075\%) and maintained upper bound gaps below 0.5\% across all instances (average: 0.082\%).
\end{result}
\begin{result}\label{res:um-lag}
    The {\ASG} heuristic produced high-quality feasible solutions with an average upper bound gap of 0.106\% and very low computational times (under 27 seconds for all large instances; average: 3.406 seconds). However, it showed a relatively high average optimality gap of 9.323\%, and its upper bound gap was slightly higher---by 0.024 percentage points on average---than that of the exact method. The {\LR} heuristic improved upon both gaps, achieving average optimality and upper bound gaps of 4.834\% and 0.031\%, respectively. These improvements are primarily due to a stronger lower bound, though the upper bound also improved relative to {\ASG}. While {\LR} incurs higher computational costs due to the need to solve multiple Lagrangian subproblems, it remains considerably faster than the exact method—on average, 4.86 times faster (average time: 1230.707 seconds). Notably, its optimality gap is only 2.759 percentage points higher than that of the exact method, while its upper bound gap is 0.051 percentage points lower, with a maximum of 0.328\%.
\end{result}
\begin{result}\label{res:um-ls}
    The {\LS} and {\SA} heuristics achieved average upper bound gaps of 1.475\% and 0.105\%, respectively. The {\LS} heuristic hit the time limit in 26.5\% of instances---mainly those with large budgets ($\mathcal{B} \in \{30, 45, 60\}$)---leading to reduced solution quality and a total average runtime of 3216.665 seconds. In large-budget cases, it reported an average upper bound gap of 2.94\% and runtime of 5380 seconds. For smaller budgets ($\mathcal{B} \in \{1, 5, 15\}$), it consistently produced high-quality solutions within the time limit, with an average gap of 0.011\% and runtime of 1054 seconds. In contrast, the {\SA} heuristic maintained low upper bound gaps across all budgets, with a stable average runtime of 1872 seconds---lower than {\LS} for large budgets and slightly higher for small ones. Compared to {\LR}, {\SA} incurred an average runtime increase of 641.3 seconds and a 0.074 percentage point increase in the upper bound gap.
\end{result}

