\begin{result}\label{res:strat-small-exact}
    The exact approach, based on the cutoff score formulation, solved approximately 48.7\% and 56\% of the small instances to optimality within the eight-hour time limit under the {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} behaviors, respectively. The optimality gap was below 5\% for about 78\% of the instances under {\CEUM} and 85\% under {\IEUM}, and below 10\% for 99\% of the instances for both behaviors, with average gaps of 2.306\% and 1.934\%, respectively. The method produced high-quality feasible solutions, with upper bound gaps below 2.6\% for {\CEUM} and 2.3\% for {\IEUM}, and average gaps of 0.166\% and 0.157\%, respectively. The average computational time was 17,732.073 seconds for {\CEUM} and 14,628.893 seconds for {\IEUM}.
\end{result}
\begin{result}\label{res:strat-small-ls}
    For both strategic behaviors, the {\LS} and {\SA} heuristics exhibit significantly low computational times on small instances---under 8 seconds for {\LS} and 109 seconds for {\SA}. Moreover, both approaches yield notably small upper bound gaps. Specifically, {\LS} achieved gaps of 0.189\% and 0.223\% for the {\CEUM} and {\IEUM} behaviors, respectively---just 0.023 and 0.066 percentage points higher than those obtained by the exact approach. In contrast, {\SA} reached upper bound gaps of 0.0\% and 0.003\%, outperforming the exact method by 0.166 and 0.154 percentage points.  
\end{result}
\begin{result}\label{res:strat-large}
    For large instances, the {\SA} heuristic achieved an average computational time and upper bound gap of 4810.572 seconds and 0.109\% for the {\CEUM} behavior, and 3687.202 seconds and 0.266\% for the {\IEUM} behavior. In contrast, the {\LS} heuristic had higher computational times and upper bound gaps, with averages of 10944.776 seconds and 1.478\% for {\CEUM}, and 8427.935 seconds and 0.494\% for {\IEUM}. The {\LS} heuristic reached the eight-hour time limit for approximately 22\% and 13.5\% of the instances for {\CEUM} and {\IEUM}, respectively---mostly for instances with large budgets, as seen in the results for the {\UM} behavior---thereby limiting solution quality. For instances with budgets $B \in \{30,45,60\}$, the average computational time and upper bound gap were 19128.3 seconds and 2.915\% for {\CEUM}, and 15340.3 seconds and 0.976\% for {\IEUM}. In contrast, for smaller budgets $\mathcal{B} \in \{1,5,15\}$, these values were significantly lower, averaging 2761.3 seconds and 0.4\% for {\CEUM}, and 1515.58 seconds and 0.011\% for {\IEUM}. Notably, the {\SA} heuristic demonstrated more consistent runtime across budget levels, outperforming the {\LS} heuristic for large-budget instances, while requiring more time for small-budget ones.
\end{result}


\section{Detailed Results for Value of the Stochastic Solution}\label{app:vss}

This section presents the detailed results for the estimators of the {\VSS} defined in Section~\ref{subsec:vss}. To compute these estimators, the {\EV} problem under behavior $a \in \mathcal{A}$ is formulated as $\min\{ Q^a(x, \overline{u}) : x \in \mathcal{X}\}$, where $ \overline{u} = \{\overline{u}\}_{s \in \mathcal{S}, c \in \mathcal{C}} $ represents the vector of average utilities. Therefore, the procedures introduced in Section~\ref{sec:methodology} for the {\SAA} program can be applied to solve the {\EV} problem, but with the distinction that only the average-case scenario is considered, rather than the set $\mathcal{W_N}$ of sampled scenarios. As highlighted in Section~\ref{subsec:vss}, our {\EV} problem is an approximation rather than the standard formulation in stochastic programming. The typical {\EV} problem would consider the expected values of $\boldsymbol{\succ}^{*,a}_{\mathcal{S}}$, which includes the choice $\boldsymbol{\xi}^{*,a}$ and ranking $\boldsymbol{r}^{*,a}$. In contrast, we apply the function that determines $\boldsymbol{\succ}^{*,a}_{\mathcal{S}}$ directly to the average $\overline{u}$ of $\boldsymbol{u}$ to obtain $\overline{\succ}^a_{\mathcal{S}}$. As a result, $\overline{\succ}^a_{\mathcal{S}}$ is not the average of $\boldsymbol{\succ}^{*,a}_{\mathcal{S}}$. Nonetheless, this approach simplifies the formulation and avoids fractional values for $\boldsymbol{\xi}^{*,a}$, which would cause infeasibility.


Table~\ref{tab:vss} reports the average values of the estimators $\overline{{\VSS}}^a$, $\overline{{\VSSENTER}}^a$, and $\overline{{\VSSIMPROV}}^a$ for each student behavior $a \in \mathcal{A}$, categorized by budget $B$ and maximum rank limit $K$ of the instances. In addition, Figure~\ref{fig:diff-rank-small} displays the values of the estimator $\overline{{\texttt{DIFF-RANK}}}^k$ across different values of $K$ and $B$, averaged over all instances with the same parameters, for budgets $B \in {1, 5, 15, 30}$. The values of the estimator $\overline{{\texttt{DIFF-RANK}}}^k$ for these budgets follow the same pattern observed for budgets $B = 45$ and $B = 60$.
