\begin{abstract}
We prove that every 5-qubit absolutely maximally entangled (AME) state is equivalent by a local unitary transformation to a point in the unique $((5,2,3))$ quantum error correcting code $\mathcal{C}$. Furthermore, two points in $\mathcal{C}$ are equivalent if and only if they are related by a group of order 24 acting on $\mathcal{C}$. There exists a set of 3 invariant polynomials that separates equivalence classes of 5-qubit AME states. We also show that every 4-qubit pure code is equivalent to a subspace of the unique $((4,4,2))$ and construct an infinite family of 3-uniform $n$-qubit states for even $n\geq 6$. The proofs rely heavily on results from Vinberg and classical invariant theory.
\end{abstract}
\maketitle



\section{Introduction}
This paper studies connections between quantum coding theory, uniform states, Vinberg theory, and invariant theory. To see how these topics are related, we showcase a family of quantum error correcting codes generated by the 6-qubit absolutely maximally entangled state. This will also establish our main setting. First, let us introduce some elementary notions.

For us, the word ``state'' simply means a vector in $(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$. We do not consider mixed states. A state $\ket{\varphi}\in (\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ is \textit{$r$-uniform} if every reduction of $\ket{\varphi}\bra{\varphi}$ to $r$ qubits is proportional to the identity. A subspace $\mathcal{C}\subset(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ is \textit{$r$-uniform} if every $\ket{\varphi}\in\mathcal{C}$ is $r$-uniform. Note that $\ket{\varphi}$ being $r$-uniform implies that $\ket{\varphi}$ is $r'$ uniform for all $1\leq r'\leq r$ and $r\leq \lfloor n/2\rfloor.$ If $\ket{\varphi}$ is maximally uniform, that is, $r$-uniform with $r=\lfloor n/2\rfloor$, then $\ket{\varphi}$ is called \textit{absolutely maximally entangled} (AME). These AME states have applications in quantum secret sharing \cite{PhysRevA.86.052335}, open-destination teleportation \cite{Helwig:2013qoq}, and (as we shall see) quantum error correction. There has been much interest in the classification \cite{Burchardt}, construction, and existence of AME states \cite{Huber_2018,AMEtable}.

A quantum error correcting code is a subspace $\mathcal{C}\subset(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ designed to be able to detect and correct local errors. If $\{\ket{u_i}:1\leq i\leq K\}$ is an orthonormal basis of a code $\mathcal{C}$, the injection $\CC^K\to\mathcal{C}$ where $\ket{i}\mapsto \ket{u_i}$ encodes qudits from $\CC^K$. Uniform states and quantum error correcting codes are related by the following fact: $\mathcal{C}$ is a pure quantum error correcting code with distance $d$ if and only if $\mathcal{C}$ is $(d-1)$-uniform. We give more information about quantum error correcting codes in \Cref{sec:QECC}, including precise definitions of some of the terms that appear above.

Let $\ket{\varphi}$ be an element of the tensor product $V_1\otimes\dots\otimes V_n$ of complex vector spaces $V_i$. For each $1\leq i\leq n$, there is a linear map $V_i^*\to V_1\otimes\dots\otimes V_{i-1}\otimes V_{i+1}\otimes\dots\otimes V_n$ which corresponds to $\ket{\varphi}$. We write $\bra{u}_i\ket{\varphi}$ for the image of $\bra{u}\in V_i^*$ under this map. Similarly, $\bra{u}_{ij}\ket{\varphi}$ is the image of $\bra{u}\in V^*_i\otimes V^*_j$ under the linear map associated to $\ket{\varphi}$. Throughout this paper, $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ are the Pauli matrices. 

\subsection{The main MDS family}\label{sec:main} The 6-qubit AME state is known to be unique up to local unitary equivalence. There are many known ways to construct versions of this AME state \cite{Borras_2007,PhysRevA.97.062326,Helwig:2013qoq,782103,MHein,4qubit}. In this paper, we use the 6-qubit AME state
\begin{equation*}\label{eq:AME}
\ket{\Psi}= e^{-\im \pi/4}(T^\dagger)^{\otimes 3}(\ket{000000}+\ket{010101}+\ket{101010}+\ket{111111}),
\end{equation*}
where $T$ is the $4\times 4$ unitary matrix
\begin{equation}\label{eq:T}
    T=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{pmatrix}
        1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\
        0 & \im & \im & 0\\
        0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\
        \im & 0 & 0 & -\im
    \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
We shall see in \Cref{sec:3uniform} that $\ket{\Psi}$ is a member of an infinite family of $3$-uniform states.

Another important character is the 4-dimensional $1$-uniform subspace $\mathcal{C}_2\subset(\CC^2)^{\otimes 4}$, which is also unique up to local unitary equivalence. This subspace is a linear quantum error correcting code: it can be realized as the simultaneous eigenspace of the operators $X\otimes X\otimes X\otimes X$ and $Z\otimes Z\otimes Z\otimes Z$ with eigenvalue 1. The code $\mathcal{C}_2$ is spanned by the vectors
\begin{align*}
\ket{u_1} &= \ket{0000}+\ket{1111}, \\
\ket{u_2} &= \ket{0011}+\ket{1100}, \\
\ket{u_3} &= \ket{0101}+\ket{1010}, \\
\ket{u_4} &= \ket{0110}+\ket{1001}.
\end{align*}
The connection to Vinberg theory comes from the fact that, as an $\SL_2^{\times 4}$-module, the four-qubit state space $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 4}$ is the grade-1 piece of the $\mathbb{Z}_2$-graded complex Lie algebra
$
\mathfrak{so}_8\cong \mathfrak{so}_4^{\times 2}\oplus (\CC^2)^{\otimes 4}.
$
The code $\mathcal{C}_2$ embedded in $\mathfrak{so}_8$ is a Cartan subalgebra. In past work, this connection to Vinberg theory was used to classify pure 4-qubit states \cite{ChtDjo:NormalFormsTensRanksPureStatesPureQubits,dietrich2022classification} and compute 4-qubit stationary points of entanglement measures \cite{4qubit}.

Taking the AME state $\ket{\Psi}$ and contracting with the first tensor factor, we obtain a 2-dimensional 2-uniform subspace $\mathcal{C}_1$ spanned by
\begin{align*}
\bra{0}_1\ket{\Psi} &= \ket{0}(\ket{u_1}-\im\ket{u_2})+\ket{1}(\im\ket{u_3}+\ket{u_4}),\text{ and} \\
\bra{1}_1\ket{\Psi} &= \ket{0}(\ket{u_3}+\im\ket{u_4})+\ket{1}(-\im\ket{u_1}+\ket{u_2}).
\end{align*}
The subspace $\mathcal{C}_1$ is also a linear code. Contracting again, we obtain a 4-dimensional 1-uniform subspace spanned by the vectors
\begin{align*}
\bra{00}_{12} \ket{\Psi} &= \ket{u_1}-\im\ket{u_2}, \\
\bra{01}_{12} \ket{\Psi} &= \im\ket{u_3}+\ket{u_4}, \\
\bra{10}_{12} \ket{\Psi} &= \ket{u_3}+\im\ket{u_4}, \\
\bra{11}_{12} \ket{\Psi} &= -\im\ket{u_1}+\ket{u_2}.
\end{align*}
Of course, these four vectors form a different basis of $\mathcal{C}_2$.

Rains proved that $\mathcal{C}_0=\text{span}\{\ket{\Psi}\}$, $\mathcal{C}_1$, and $\mathcal{C}_2$ are all unique up to local unitary equivalence \cite{746807}. (Note that, by our choice of notation, $\mathcal{C}_i$ is $2^i$-dimensional.) Here, we mean that they are unique as $r$-uniform subspaces of maximal dimension; however, we shall see that this can be strengthened. Wallach showed that every $1$-uniform state in $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 4}$ is equivalent to a point in $\mathcal{C}_2$, hence every $1$-uniform state arises from $\ket{\Psi}$. Due to the connection with Vinberg theory, it is also known that two points in $\mathcal{C}_2$ are equivalent if and only if they are related by a group of order 144, the Weyl group of type $D_4$, acting on $\mathcal{C}_2$.

\begin{theorem}[Wallach]\label{thm:wallach1u}
    The set of 1-uniform states in $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 4}$ is equal to the $\SU_2^{\times 4}$-orbit of $\mathcal{C}_2$. Two points in $\mathcal{C}_2$ are equivalent if and only if they are $W$-equivalent, where $W$ is the Weyl group of the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{so}_8$ and acts on $\mathcal{C}_1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    This follows from \Cref{cor:wal} and \Cref{thm:wallachsecond} later in the text. More general versions of these results can also be found in Wallach's book \cite{WallachGIT}.
\end{proof}

There are two main results of this paper, both included in \Cref{thm:mainresult} below. First, we prove that every AME (i.e., $2$-uniform) state in $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 5}$ is equivalent to a point in $\mathcal{C}_1$. Second, two points in $\mathcal{C}_1$ are equivalent if and only if they are related by a group of order 24 acting on $\mathcal{C}_1$. Since 4-qubit AME states do not exist \cite{HIGUCHI2000213}, this completes the classification of maximally uniform states in $(\CC^2)^{\otimes n}$ for $n$ equal to $4,5,$ and $6$. The classification of $r$-uniform states is generally a hard problem; classifying all 2-uniform qubit states would solve the Hadamard conjecture \cite{PhysRevA.81.062347}.

To prove the first main result, we first show that every 4-qubit pure code (which is necessarily 1-uniform) is equivalent to a subspace of $\mathcal{C}_2$ (\Cref{cor:1uniform}). This simplifies the situation, since every $5$-qubit AME state produces a 2-dimensional 1-uniform subspace of $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 4}$. The proof of the second main result uses tools from classical invariant theory. This approach also gives a separating set of invariant polynomials on the real $\SU_2^{\times 5}$-variety consisting of all 5-qubit AME states (\Cref{thm:separating}). That is, we find three polynomials $f_1,f_2,f_3$, of degrees 6, 8, and 12, such that two AME states $\ket{\varphi},\ket{\psi}\in(\CC^2)^{\otimes 5}$ are $\SU_2^{\times 5}$-equivalent if and only if $f_i(\ket{\varphi})=f_i(\ket{\psi})$ for all $i$. This gives an easy way to determine whether two 5-qubit AME states are equivalent. It is known that the set of all $\SL_D^{\times n}$-invariant polynomials separates states in $(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ with closed orbits \cite{PhysRevLett.111.060502}, which include all $r$-uniform states. However, there are many $\SL_D^{\times n}$-invariant polynomials and they are difficult to compute. Even for 5 qubits, finding the complete set of secondary $\SL_2^{\times 5}$-invariants seems beyond reach \cite{Luque_2006}.

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]\label{thm:mainresult}
    The set of 2-uniform states in $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 5}$ is equal to the $\SU_2^{\times 5}$-orbit of $\mathcal{C}_1$. Two points in $\mathcal{C}_1$ are $\SU_2$-equivalent if and only if they are $W$-equivalent, where $W$ is a group of order 24 acting on $\mathcal{C}_1$. Furthermore, $W\cong\langle\im X,\im Z,Q\rangle$, where
    \[
Q=\frac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix}
    1+\im & 1+\im \\
    -1+\im & 1-\im
\end{pmatrix}.
    \]
\end{theorem}

The group $W$ of \Cref{thm:wallach1u} (resp. \Cref{thm:mainresult}) arises from the group of local unitary operators $U\in \SU_2^{\otimes 4}$ (resp. $U\in \SU_2^{\otimes 5}$) such that $U\mathcal{C}_2=\mathcal{C}_2$ (resp. $U\mathcal{C}_1=\mathcal{C}_1$). These are examples of what are known as fault-tolerant operations in the field of quantum error correction \cite{Gottesman}.

\subsection{Another MDS family}\label{sec:another}
Although not the main topic of this paper, we wish to mention another family of pure codes that have a connection to Vinberg theory.

There is a way to construct an AME state in $(\CC^D)^{\otimes 4}$ from a pair of orthogonal Latin squares \cite{PhysRevA.92.032316,PhysRevLett.128.080507}. Define a \textit{Latin square} to be a $D\times D$ matrix $L$ with entries $L_{ij}\in \{0,1,\dots,D-1\}$ such that every row and every column of $L$ contains $D$ distinct entries. Two Latin squares $L$ and $L'$ are \textit{orthogonal} if $(L_{ij},L'_{ij})\neq (L_{kl},L'_{kl})$ whenever $(i,j)\neq (k,l)$.
If two $D\times D$ Latin squares $L,L'$ are orthogonal, the pair determines the $4$-qudit AME state
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{D-1}\sum_{j=0}^{D-1} \ket{i}\ket{j}\ket{L_{ij}}\ket{L'_{ij}} \in (\CC^D)^{\otimes 4}.
\]
If $D$ is prime, there is a simple construction of orthogonal Latin squares that produces the AME state
\[
\ket{\Omega_D} = \sum_{i=0}^{D-1}\sum_{j=0}^{D-1} \ket{i}\ket{j}\ket{i+j}\ket{j-i},
\]
where addition and subtraction of indices is performed modulo $D$. For example, $\ket{\Omega_3}\in(\CC^3)^{\otimes 4}$ is the sum of these 9 basis vectors:
\begin{align*}
\ket{0000},\quad \ket{0111},\quad \ket{0222}, \\
\ket{1012},\quad \ket{1120},\quad \ket{1201}, \\
\ket{2021},\quad \ket{2102},\quad \ket{2210}.
\end{align*}
Similarly to $\ket{\Psi}$, it is known that every 3-qutrit AME state is local unitarily equivalent to $\ket{\Omega_3}$; in contrast, there exist infinitely many equivalence classes of AME states in $(\CC^D)^{\otimes 3}$ when $D>3$ \cite{PhysRevA.108.032412}. By the uniqueness of $\ket{\Omega_3}$, it follows (see \Cref{prop:toporbit}) that every 3-dimensional 1-uniform subspace of $(\CC^3)^{\otimes 3}$ is equivalent to the $1$-uniform subspace $\mathcal{D}$ spanned by the following:
\begin{align*}
\bra{0}_1 \ket{\Omega_3}&=\ket{000}+\ket{111}+\ket{222}, \\
\bra{1}_1 \ket{\Omega_3}&=\ket{012}+\ket{120}+\ket{201}, \\
\bra{2}_1 \ket{\Omega_3}&=\ket{021}+\ket{102}+\ket{210}.
\end{align*}
As an $\SL_3^{\times 3}$-module, the three-qutrit state space $(\CC^3)^{\otimes 3}$ is the grade-1 piece of the $\mathbb{Z}_3$-graded complex Lie algebra
$\mathfrak{e}_6\cong\mathfrak{sl}_3^{\times 3}\oplus (\CC^3)^{\otimes 3}\oplus ((\CC^3)^{\otimes 3})^*$ \cite{di2023classification}. As a subspace of $\mathfrak{e}_6$, $\mathcal{D}$ is not a Cartan subalgebra---its dimension is too small for this to be possible. However, there exists a Cartan subalgebra $\mathfrak{a}\subset\mathfrak{e}_6$ such that $\mathfrak{a}\cap (\CC^3)^{\otimes 3}=\mathcal{D}$ and furthermore the dimension of the intersection is maximal over all Cartan subalgebras of $\mathfrak{e}_6$. In Vinberg theory, $\mathcal{D}$ is known as a Cartan subspace of $(\CC^3)^{\otimes 3}$. Like in the 4-qubit case, this connection to Vinberg was used to classify $\SL_3^{\times 3}$-orbits in $(\CC^3)^{\otimes 3}$ \cite{Nurmiev,Vinberg-Elasvili}. To the best of the author's knowledge, it is not known if every 1-uniform state in $(\CC^3)^{\otimes 3}$ is equivalent to a point in $\mathcal{D}$.

\subsection{Overview}
We cover preliminaries in \Cref{sec:prelims}, including content about quantum error correcting codes, $r$-uniform states, and critical states. We also explain a certain construction of new quantum error correcting codes from old ones due to Rains (\Cref{prop:rains}); this is the process that generates the families of codes in \Cref{sec:main,sec:another}.

In \Cref{sec:three}, we cover results that do not rely on Vinberg theory or invariant theory. In \Cref{sec:3uniform}, we show that the construction of the 6-qubit AME state $\ket{\Psi}$ generalizes, giving a 3-uniform state in $(\CC^2)^{\otimes n}$ for each even $n\geq 6$. In \Cref{sec:stab}, we explain the relationship between the local symmetries of an $r$-uniform state and the fault-tolerant operations on the codes the state generates.

In \Cref{sec:vinberg}, we cover the necessary Lie theory and Vinberg theory. Much comes directly from Vinberg's paper \cite{Vinberg_1976}. We explain how $\mathfrak{so}_8$ is a $\ZZ_2$-graded Lie algebra with the 4-qubit state space $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 4}$ for its grade 1 piece.

\Cref{sec:classification} includes our major results. In \Cref{sec:1uniform}, we show that every 4-qubit pure code is equivalent to a subspace of $\mathcal{C}_2$. In \Cref{sec:first}, we prove the first part of \Cref{thm:mainresult}. In \Cref{sec:finite}, we cover results from the invariant theory of finite groups. In \Cref{sec:2pr}, we prove the second part of \Cref{thm:mainresult} and provide a set of separating invariants for $\SU_2^{\times 5}$-orbits of 5-qubit AME states.

\subsection{Notation and conventions}
In this paper, we assume that a Lie group or Lie algebra is complex unless otherwise indicated. For example, $\SL_D=\SL_D(\CC)$ is a complex Lie group while $\SL_D(\RR)$ is a real Lie group. The group $\GL_D^{\times n}$ has a natural action on $(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ defined by
$
g.\ket{\varphi}=(g_1\otimes\dots\otimes g_n)\ket{\varphi}
$
for all $g=(g_1,\dots,g_n)\in \GL_D^{\times n}$ and $\ket{\varphi}\in (\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$. This induces by restriction an action of any subgroup of $\GL_D^{\times n}$ on $(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$. Let $G\subset\GL(V)$ and $H\subset\GL(V')$ be matrix groups acting on complex vector spaces $V$ and $V'$. We write $G\otimes G$ to denote the image of the natural representation $G\otimes G\to \GL(V\otimes V)$, then define $G^{\otimes n}=G\otimes\dots\otimes G$ by iteration. Similarly, we write $G\oplus H$ to denote the image of the natural representation $\xi :G\times H\to \GL(V\oplus V')$. If $A\in G$ and $B\in H$, then the block diagonal matrix $A\oplus B$ is the image of $(A,B)$ under $\xi$. The Lie algebra of $G$ is $\text{Lie}(G)=\{A\in \CC^{D\times D}:\exp(tA)\in G\text{ for all $t\in\RR$}\}$. The symmetric group on $m$ elements is $\mathfrak{S}_m$. If $v$ is a vector, $\bar{v}$ is its complex conjugate.

\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelims}

\subsection{Quantum error correcting codes}\label{sec:QECC} In order to state the definition of a quantum error correcting code, we need to introduce some preliminary notions. Let $\mathcal{B}$ be an orthonormal basis of $\CC^{D\times D}$ such that $I\in\mathcal{B}$. Note that $\mathcal{B}\setminus\{I\}$ is a basis of the space $\mathfrak{sl}_D=(\text{span}\{I\})^\perp$ of traceless $D\times D$ matrices. From $\mathcal{B}$, we obtain the basis
$
\mathcal{E}:=\{E_1\otimes\dots\otimes E_n:E_i\in\mathcal{B},\: 1\leq i\leq n\}
$
of $\text{End}((\CC^D)^{\otimes n})$. The elements of $\mathcal{E}$ represent errors that a code tries to correct. The \textit{weight} $\text{wt}(E)$ of an error $E=E_1\otimes\dots\otimes E_n$, where $E_i\in\mathcal{B}$, is equal to the number $|\{i:E_i\neq I\}|$ of single-qubit subsystems on which $E$ acts nontrivially.

\begin{definition}
    Let $\mathcal{C}\subset (\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ be a $K$-dimensional subspace with orthonormal basis $\{\ket{u_i}:1\leq i\leq K\}$. The subspace $\mathcal{C}$ is a \textit{quantum error correcting code} (or simply \textit{code}) with parameters $((n,K,d))_D$ if $\bra{u_i}E\ket{u_j}=c(E)\delta_{ij}$ whenever $E\in\mathcal{E}$ such that $\text{wt}(E)<d$, where $c(E)$ is a complex constant depending on $E$. A code $\mathcal{C}$ is \textit{pure} if $c(E)=0$ whenever $0<\text{wt}(E)<d$.
\end{definition}

The parameter $d$ is the \textit{distance} of the code and represents the amount of error the code can correct: a code with distance $d\geq 2t+1$ can correct errors that affect up to $t$ subsystems. For a more details, see \cite{Bacon_2013}. The following, known as the quantum Singleton bound, is due to Rains.

\begin{theorem}[Quantum Singleton bound]\label{thm:singleton}
    If $\mathcal{C}$ is a quantum error correcting code with parameters $((n,K,d))_D$, then $\log_D K\leq n-2(d-1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    See \cite[Theorem 2]{782103} and \cite[Theorem 13]{Huber2020}.
\end{proof}
Codes that reach equality in the Singleton bound are called \textit{maximum distance separable} (MDS). It is true that every MDS code is pure; in the pure case, we have an alternate definition of a code (\Cref{prop:obs}) which gives a connection between $r$-uniform states and quantum error correction. We also state (\Cref{thm:rainsuniqueness}) the uniqueness of the codes in the main MDS family of \Cref{sec:main}.
\begin{theorem}[Rains]\label{thm:QMDS-pure}
    Every MDS code is pure.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    See \cite[Theorem 2]{782103} and \cite[Theorem 5]{Huber2020}.
\end{proof}
\begin{theorem}\label{prop:obs}
A subspace $\mathcal{C}\subset (\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ is a pure $((n,K,d))_D$ code if and only if it is $(d-1)$-uniform.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    See \cite[Observation 1]{Huber2020}.
\end{proof}
\begin{theorem}[Rains]\label{thm:rainsuniqueness}
    The MDS code with parameters $((4,4,2))_2$ is unique up to local unitary equivalence. The same is true for the MDS code with parameters $((5,2,3))_2$ or $((6,1,4))_2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    See \cite{746807}. Note that it is equivalent to say that these codes are unique as $r$-uniform subspaces of maximal dimension, by \Cref{thm:QMDS-pure,thm:singleton}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Critical states} In the introduction, we mentioned that $r\leq \lfloor n/2\rfloor$ whenever a state $\ket{\varphi}\in(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ is $r$-uniform; those uniform states that achieve this upper bound are called AME states. On the other extreme, 1-uniform states are called \textit{critical} states. Every $r$-uniform state is critical. Let us discuss some important properties of such states.

\Cref{prop:equiv} is taken to be the definition of a critical state in other texts (e.g. \cite{Gour_2011}). We choose instead to define a critical state by its 1-uniformity and to prove the equivalence of definitions using \Cref{prop:obs}.

\begin{theorem}\label{prop:equiv}
    A state $\ket{\varphi}\in(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ is critical if and only if $\bra{\varphi}E\ket{\varphi}=0$ for every $E$ in the Lie algebra of $\SL_D^{\otimes n}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose $\ket{\varphi}$ is critical. By \Cref{prop:obs}, the span of $\ket{\varphi}$ is a pure $((n,1,2))_D$ code. Therefore $\bra{\varphi} E' \ket{\varphi}=0$ for every $E'\in\mathcal{E}$ with weight $1$. The elements $E\in\text{Lie}(\SL_D^{\otimes n})$ are precisely those with the form
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:der}
    E = (E_1\otimes I\otimes\dots\otimes I)+(I\otimes E_2\otimes\dots\otimes I) + \dots +(I\otimes I\otimes\dots\otimes E_n),
    \end{equation}
    where each $E_i\in\mathfrak{sl}_D$. Since $\mathcal{B}\setminus\{I\}$ is a basis of $\mathfrak{sl}_D$, it follows that $\bra{\varphi}E\ket{\varphi}=0$ for any $E\in\text{Lie}(\SL_D^{\otimes n})$. 

    Conversely, suppose $\bra{\varphi}E\ket{\varphi}=0$ for all $E\in\text{Lie}(\SL_D^{\otimes n})$. Any error with weight $1$ is in $\text{Lie}(\SL_D^{\otimes n})$ since it has the form \eqref{eq:der} with $E_i=0$ for all but one index. Therefore the span of $\ket{\varphi}$ is a pure $((n,1,2))_D$ code. By \Cref{prop:obs}, $\ket{\varphi}$ is critical.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The Kempf-Ness theorem]\label{prop:K-N}
Let $\ket{\varphi}\in(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$. The following are true:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[1.] $\ket{\varphi}$ is critical if and only if $\bra{\varphi}A^\dagger A \ket{\varphi}\geq \braket{\varphi|\varphi}$ for all $A\in \SL_D^{\otimes n}$.
    \item[2.] If $\ket{\varphi}$ is critical and $A\in \SL_D^{\otimes n}$ such that $\bra{\varphi}A^\dagger A \ket{\varphi}=\braket{\varphi|\varphi}$, then there exists $U\in \SU_D^{\otimes n}$ such that $A\ket{\varphi}=U\ket{\varphi}$.
    \item[3.] The $\SL_D^{\times n}$-orbit of $\ket{\varphi}$ is closed if and only if it contains a critical state.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    See \cite[Theorem~2]{Gour_2011}.
\end{proof}
In more intuitive language, the first item of Proposition~\ref{prop:K-N} states that $\ket{\varphi}$ is critical if and only if it minimizes the norm on its $\SL_D^{\times n}$-orbit. The second item states that if $\ket{\psi}$ is another minimizer in the $\SL_D^{\times n}$-orbit of a critical state $\ket{\varphi}$, then $\ket{\psi}$ is in fact in the $\SU_D^{\times n}$-orbit of $\ket{\varphi}$. The third item characterizes orbits of critical states. A consequence of the Kempf-Ness theorem is that two critical states are $\SL_D^{\times n}$-equivalent if and only if they are $\SU_D^{\times n}$-equivalent.

\subsection{Rains's construction} We now present a method for generating new pure codes from old ones due to Rains. \Cref{prop:rains2} restates the result without the language of density matrices. This gives rise to the families of MDS codes in \Cref{sec:main,sec:another}.

\begin{theorem}[Rains]\label{prop:rains}
    Let $\{\ket{\varphi_i}\}$ be an orthonormal basis of a pure $((n,K,d))_D$ code. The image of the operator
    \[
    \text{Tr}_{\{1\}}(\ket{\varphi_1}\bra{\varphi_1}+\dots + \ket{\varphi_K}\bra{\varphi_K})
    \]
    is a pure $((n-1,DK,d-1))_D$ code.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    See \cite[Theorem 19]{Rains1996QuantumWE}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:uniformorth}
    Let $V_1$ and $V_2$ be finite-dimensional complex vector spaces, $\ket{\varphi}\in V_1\otimes V_2$, and $\ket{\varphi_j}=\bra{j}_1 \ket{\varphi}\in V_2$ for $1\leq j\leq K=\dim V_1$. The following are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[1.] $\text{Tr}_{\{2\}}(\ket{\varphi}\bra{\varphi})$ is proportional to the identity,
        \item[2.] $\{\ket{\varphi_j}\}$ is orthonormal up to global rescaling.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} We have $\ket{\varphi}=\sum_j \ket{j}\ket{\varphi_j}$. The claim follows from the equation
        \begin{equation*}
    \text{Tr}_{\{2\}}(\ket{\varphi}\bra{\varphi})=\text{Tr}_{\{2\}}(\sum_{i,j}\ket{i}\bra{j}\otimes \ket{\varphi_i}\bra{\varphi_j})=\sum_{i,j}\braket{\varphi_j | \varphi_i} e_{ij},
    \end{equation*}
    where each $e_{ij}=\ket{i}\bra{j}$ is a standard basis vector of $\CC^{K\times K}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{prop:rains2}
Let $\{\ket{\varphi^i}:1\leq i\leq K\}$ be an orthonormal basis of a pure $((n,K,d))_D$ code. For each $\ket{\varphi^i}$ and $j\in\{0,\dots,D-1\}$ define $\ket{\varphi^i_j}=\bra{j}_1\ket{\varphi^i}$. Then, up to global rescaling,
\[
\mathcal{S}=\{\ket{\varphi^i_j}:1\leq i\leq K, 0\leq j\leq D-1\}
\]
is an orthonormal basis of a pure $((n-1,DK,d-1))_D$ code.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Note that $\ket{\varphi^i}=\sum_j \ket{j}\ket{\varphi^i_j}$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:rains}, the image of the operator
    \begin{align}\label{eq:trace}
    \begin{split}
\sum_{i=1}^K  \text{Tr}_{\{1\}}(\ket{\varphi^i}\bra{\varphi^i})
&=\sum_{i=1}^K \text{Tr}_{\{1\}}(\sum_{j_1=0}^{D-1}\sum_{j_2=0}^{D-1}\ket{j_1}\bra{j_2}\otimes\ket{\varphi^i_{j_1}}\bra{\varphi^i_{j_2}}) \\
&=\sum_{i=1}^{K}\sum_{j=0}^{D-1}\ket{\varphi^i_j}\bra{\varphi_j^i}
    \end{split}
    \end{align}
    is a pure $((n-1,DK,d))_D$ code. It remains to show that the basis $\mathcal{S}$ is orthonormal (up to scale). For each $i$, since $\ket{\varphi^i}$ is $1$-uniform, $\{\ket{\varphi^i_j}:j=0,\dots,D-1\}$ is orthonormal, by \Cref{lem:uniformorth}. Furthermore, since every element in the span of $\{\ket{\varphi^i}\}$ is $1$-uniform, for every pair of indices $i_1,i_2$ and $t\in\RR$ the vectors
    \[
\ket{\Phi_j}:=\bra{j}_1(\ket{\varphi^{i_1}}+e^{\im t}\ket{\varphi^{i_2}})=\ket{\varphi^{i_1}_j}+e^{\im t}\ket{\varphi^{i_2}_j}
    \]
    form a pairwise orthogonal set $\{\ket{\Phi_j}\}$. Then
    \begin{align*}
\braket{\Phi_j|\Phi_k} &=\braket{\varphi_j^{i_1}|\varphi^{i_1}_k}+e^{\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_1}_j|\varphi^{i_2}_k}+e^{-\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_2}_j|\varphi^{i_1}_k}+\braket{\varphi_j^{i_2}|\varphi^{i_2}_k}\\
&=e^{\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_1}_j|\varphi^{i_2}_k}+e^{-\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_2}_j|\varphi^{i_1}_k}=0.
    \end{align*}
    Applying the operator $-\im\frac{d}{dt}$, we obtain the system of linear equations
    \begin{equation*}
    \begin{cases}
    e^{\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_1}_j|\varphi^{i_2}_k}+e^{-\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_2}_j|\varphi^{i_1}_k}=0\\
    e^{\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_1}_j|\varphi^{i_2}_k}-e^{-\im t}\braket{\varphi^{i_2}_j|\varphi^{i_1}_k}=0
    \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    from which we conclude we conclude that $\braket{\varphi_j^{i_1}|\varphi_k^{i_2}}=0$ whenever $i_1\neq i_2$. Finally, we note that $\braket{\varphi_j^{i_1}|\varphi_j^{i_1}}=\braket{\varphi_j^{i_2}|\varphi_j^{i_2}}$ since $\sum_j \braket{\varphi_j^{i_1}|\varphi_j^{i_1}}=\braket{\varphi^{i_1}|\varphi^{i_1}}=\braket{\varphi^{i_2}|\varphi^{i_2}}=\sum_j \braket{\varphi_j^{i_2}|\varphi_j^{i_2}}$.
\end{proof}

Suppose $\ket{\varphi}\in (\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ is an AME state where $n$ is even. We may iterate \Cref{prop:rains2} to obtain a family of MDS codes with the following parameters.
\[
((n,1,\frac{n}{2}+1))_D,\quad ((n-1,D,\frac{n}{2}))_D, \quad ((n-2,D^2,\frac{n}{2}-1))_D,\quad \dots
\]
This process cannot always be reversed: it could be the case that a $((n-i,D^i,\frac{n}{2}-i+1))_D$ MDS code exists but no $((n-i+1,D^{i-1},\frac{n}{2}-i+2))_D$ MDS code exists (see also \cite[Section 7]{Huber2020}). However, Huber and Grassl show that the construction can always be reversed at the top two levels of a MDS family arising from an AME state. We note in \Cref{prop:toporbit} that Huber and Grassl's result holds at the level of $\SU_D^{\times n}$-orbits.


\begin{theorem}[Huber-Grassl]\label{prop:top}
    Let $\ket{\varphi}\in(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ with $n$ even and let $\ket{\varphi_i}=\bra{i}_1 \ket{\varphi}$ for $i\in\{0,\dots,D-1\}$. The following are equivalent:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[1.] $\ket{\varphi}$ is AME.
        \item[2.] $\ket{\varphi}$ spans a $((n,1,\frac{n}{2}+1))_D$ MDS code.
        \item[3.] $\{\ket{\varphi_i}\}$ is proportional to an orthonormal basis of a $((n-1,D,\frac{n}{2}))_D$ MDS code.
    \end{itemize}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    See \cite[Proposition 7]{Huber2020}. The equivalence of the first two items should be clear from \Cref{thm:QMDS-pure}.
\end{proof}
\begin{theorem}\label{prop:toporbit}
    There is a one-to-one correspondence between the set of local unitary orbits of $((n,1,\frac{n}{2}+1))_D$ MDS codes and the set of local unitary orbits of $((n-1,D,\frac{n}{2}))_D$ MDS codes.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Each $((n,1,\frac{n}{2}+1))_D$ MDS code corresponds to a unique unit AME state $\ket{\varphi}\in (\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ spanning the code. By \Cref{prop:top}, the set of vectors $\ket{\varphi_i}=\bra{i}_1\ket{\varphi}$ spans a $((n-1,D,\frac{n}{2}))_D$ MDS code. Conversely, any $((n-1,D,\frac{n}{2}))_D$ MDS code has an orthonormal basis $\{\ket{\varphi_i}\}$ which by purification gives the AME state $\ket{\varphi}=\sum_i\ket{i}\ket{\varphi_i}$ which spans a $((n,1,\frac{n}{2}+1))_D$ MDS code.

    Let $\ket{\varphi},\ket{\psi}\in(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ be unit AME states, $\mathcal{C}=\text{span}\{\ket{\varphi_i}:i=0,\dots,D-1\}$ where $\ket{\varphi_i}=\bra{i}_1\ket{\varphi}$, and $\mathcal{C}'=\text{span}\{\ket{\psi_i}:i=0,\dots,D-1\}$ where $\ket{\psi_i}=\bra{i}_1\ket{\psi}$. We show that $\ket{\varphi}$ and $\ket{\psi}$ are $\U_D^{\times n}$-equivalent if and only if $\mathcal{C}$ and $\mathcal{C}'$ are $\SU_D^{\times (n-1)}$-equivalent. The result follows.

    Suppose that $\ket{\varphi}=e^{\im t}U\ket{\psi}$ for some $t\in\RR$ and $U=U_1\otimes U_2\otimes\dots\otimes U_n\in\SU_D^{\otimes n}$ and let $\tilde{U}=U_2\otimes\dots\otimes U_{n}$. Specializing Equation \eqref{eq:trace} to $K=1$, we calculate
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=0}^{D-1}\ket{\varphi_i}\bra{\varphi_i}&=\text{Tr}_{\{1\}}(\ket{\varphi}\bra{\varphi})
    =\text{Tr}_{\{1\}}(U\ket{\psi}\bra{\psi}U^\dagger)
    = \sum_{i=0}^{D-1} \tilde{U}\ket{\psi_i}\bra{\psi_i}\tilde{U}^\dagger.
\end{align*}
    Therefore, the image $\mathcal{C}$ of the operator $\sum_i\ket{\varphi_i}\bra{\varphi_i}$ is related to the image $\mathcal{C}'$ of the operator $\sum_i\ket{\psi_i}\bra{\psi_i}$ by the equation $\mathcal{C}=\tilde{U}\mathcal{C}'$. Conversely, suppose $\mathcal{C}=\tilde{U}\mathcal{C}'$ for some $\tilde{U}\in\SU_D^{\otimes (n-1)}$. Then the vectors $\ket{\tilde{\psi}_i}=\tilde{U}\ket{\psi_i}$ form a basis of $\mathcal{C}$. Since both bases $\{\ket{\varphi_i}\}$ and $\{\ket{\tilde{\psi_i}}\}$ of $\mathcal{C}$ are orthonormal after global rescaling, we have $\ket{\varphi_i}=\sum_j U_{ij}\ket{\tilde{\psi_j}}$ where the constants $U_{ij}$ are the entries of a unitary matrix $U\in\U_D$. Then 
    \begin{align*}
        \ket{\varphi} = \sum_i\ket{i}\otimes\ket{\varphi_i}
        &= \sum_i \ket{i}\otimes(\sum_j U_{ij}\ket{\tilde{\psi}_j})\\
        &= \sum_{i,j}U_{ij}\ket{i}\otimes\ket{\tilde{\psi}_j}\\
        &= \sum_j (\sum_i U_{ij} \ket{i})\otimes\ket{\tilde{\psi}_j}\\
        &= \sum_j U\ket{j}\otimes\tilde{U}\ket{\psi_j}
    \end{align*}
    so that $\ket{\varphi}=(U\otimes\tilde{U})\ket{\psi}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Local symmetries of the 6-qubit AME state}\label{sec:three}

\subsection{A unitary change of coordinates}\label{sec:unitarychange} We now discuss an important property of the $4\times 4$ unitary matrix $T$ defined in \eqref{eq:T}. The matrix gives us the double cover
\[
p:\SL_2\times\SL_2\to\SO_4,\quad p(A,B)= T(A\otimes B)T^\dagger.
\]
The space $\CC^2\otimes \CC^2$ is naturally isomorphic to $\CC^4$ by the correspondence of basis vectors $\ket{i}\ket{j}\mapsto \ket{2i+j}$ for $i,j\in\{ 0,1\}$. Consider the unitary map $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 2m}\to (\CC^4)^{\otimes m}$ where $\ket{u}\mapsto T^{\otimes m}\ket{u}$. Under this change of coordinates, the action of $(A_1,B_1,\dots,A_m,B_m)\in \SL_2^{\times 2m}$ on $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 2m}$ corresponds to the action of $(T(A_1\otimes B_1)T^\dagger,\dots,T(A_m\otimes B_m)T^\dagger)\in\SO_4^{\times m}$ on $(\CC^4)^{\otimes m}$. Therefore, the $\SL_2^{\times 2m}$-module $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 2m}$ is equivalent to the $\SO_4^{\times m}$-module $(\CC^4)^{\otimes m}$. Furthermore, since $p(\SU_2\times \SU_2)=\SO_4(\CC)\cap\U_4=\SO_4(\RR)$, the $\SU_2^{\times 2m}$-module $(\CC^2)^{\otimes 2m}$ is equivalent to the $\SO_4(\RR)^{\times m}$-module $(\CC^4)^{\otimes m}$.

Let us be clear about what this means in terms of quantum information. Systems of $m$ ququads (local dimension 4) are like systems of $2m$ qubits. Any local unitary operation on qubits corresponds to a local unitary operation from $\SO_4(\RR)^{\times m}
\subset \U_4^{\times m}$ on ququads (plus some global phase change). Any local invertible operation on qubits corresponds to a local invertible operation from $\SO_4(\CC)^{\times m}\subset \SL_4^{\times m}$ on ququads. Any entanglement property of ququads that qubits do not have can only be exhibited with the help of local unitary operations outside of $\SO_4(\RR)^{\times m}$ or local invertible operations outside of $\SO_4(\CC)^{\times m}$. (It is known that two states are related by a local invertible operation if and only if one can be converted to the other via stochastic local operations and classical communication \cite{PhysRevA.62.062314}.)

This change of coordinates has been used to develop an algorithm for finding unique normal forms of $n$-qubit states under the action of $\SL_2^{\times n}$ \cite{tensordecompositions} and, in the 4-qubit case, gives us the connection to Vinberg theory (see \Cref{sec:embed}).

\subsection{Construction of 3-uniform states}\label{sec:3uniform} The 6-qubit AME state $\ket{\Psi}$ introduced in \Cref{sec:main} is a member of an infinite family of $3$-uniform states. The general construction gives a $3$-uniform state $\Ket{\Psi_m}\in (\CC^2)^{\otimes 2m}$ for each $m\geq 3$. We take the $m$-ququad generalized GHZ state \[\ket{GHZ(m)}:=\sum_{i=0}^3 \ket{i}^{\otimes m}\in (\CC^4)^{\otimes m},\]
then apply the change of coordinates $\ket{u}\mapsto (T^\dagger)^{\otimes m} \ket{u}$. The result is a 3-uniform state in the $2m$-qubit state space.
\begin{theorem}
    The state $\ket{\Psi_m}=(T^\dagger)^{\otimes m}\ket{GHZ(m)}\in (\CC^2)^{\otimes 2m}$ is 3-uniform for all $m\geq 3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    A direct check verifies the claim for $m=3$. Suppose $m>3$. Let $V_i=\CC^4$ for $1\leq i\leq m$ and let $V'_j=\CC^2$ for $1\leq j\leq 2m$. Any partial trace of a GHZ state is still a GHZ state. Thus, for any $V=V_{i_1}\otimes\dots\otimes V_{i_r}$ with $r=m-3$ we have
    \[
    \text{Tr}_V(\ket{GHZ(m)}\bra{GHZ(m)})=\ket{GHZ(3)}\bra{GHZ(3)}.
    \]
    Given any $S=\{j_1,j_2,j_3\},$ we can choose $i_1,i_2,i_3$ such that $V'_{j_1}\otimes V'_{j_2}\otimes V'_{j_3}\subset V_{i_1}\otimes V_{i_2}\otimes V_{i_3}$. Choosing $V$ to be the complement of $V_{i_1}\otimes V_{i_2}\otimes V_{i_3}$ we have
    \begin{align*}
    \text{Tr}_{S}(\ket{\Psi_m}\bra{\Psi_m})&=\text{Tr}_{S}((T^\dagger)^{\otimes m}\ket{GHZ(m)}\bra{GHZ(m)}T^{\otimes m}) \\
    &=\text{Tr}_{S}\circ\text{Tr}_V((T^\dagger)^{\otimes m}\ket{GHZ(m)}\bra{GHZ(m)}T^{\otimes m}) \\
    &= \text{Tr}_{S}((T^\dagger)^{\otimes m}\ket{GHZ(3)}\bra{GHZ(3)}T^{\otimes m}) \\
    &=\text{Tr}_{S}(\ket{\Psi_3}\bra{\Psi_3})\propto I,
    \end{align*}
    as desired.
\end{proof}

Zang et al. \cite{ZangTianFeiZuo} construct an infinite class of $n$-qubit 3-uniform states for all $n\geq 6$ and $n\neq 7,8,9,11$. In comparison, our construction gives a 3-uniform state for all even $n\geq 6$. Huber et al. show that no 7-qubit 3-uniform state exists \cite{PhysRevLett.118.200502}.


\subsection{Stabilizers and fault-tolerant operations}\label{sec:stab} Let $\mathcal{C}\subset(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ be a quantum error correcting code. We denote the stabilizer of $\mathcal{C}$ in $\SL_D^{\times n}$ with
\[
N(\mathcal{C})=\{g\in \SL_D^{\times n}:g.\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{C}\}.
\]
The rationale for our choice of notation should become clear in \Cref{sec:vinberg}. There exists a natural representation $\mu:N(\mathcal{C})\to \GL(\mathcal{C})$ defined by $\mu(g)\ket{\varphi}=g.\ket{\varphi}$ for all $g\in\SL_D^{\times n}$ and $\ket{\varphi}\in\mathcal{C}$. A choice of basis for $\mathcal{C}$ gives coordinates to the image of $\mu$. That is, if $\{\ket{u_i}:1\leq i\leq K\}$ is a basis of $\mathcal{C}$, then $\mu(g)$ is a $K\times K$ matrix with entries $\mu(g)_{ji}$ given by
\[
g.\ket{u_i}=\sum_{j} \mu(g)_{ji}\ket{u_j},\quad\text{$\forall i$.}
\]
We define the group $W(\mathfrak{\mathcal{C}})$ to be the image $\mu(N(\mathcal{C}))$. The action of $N(\mathcal{C})$ on $\mathcal{C}$ naturally induces an action of $W(\mathcal{C})$ on $\mathcal{C}$. The group $W(\mathcal{C})$ represents all fault-tolerant operations on encoded states that can be achieved with local operations.

In \Cref{thm:GHZ3}, we compute (up to isomorphism) the stabilizer of $\ket{\Psi}$ in $\SL_2^{\times 6}$. Using the change of coordinates $\ket{u}\mapsto T^{\otimes 3}\ket{u}$, this is equivalent to computing the stabilizer of $\ket{GHZ(3)}$ in $\SO_4^{\times 3}$. Due to the relationship between the stabilizer of $\ket{\Psi}$ and $W(\mathcal{C}_1)$ (\Cref{prop:stab}), we can then compute $W(\mathcal{C}_1)$ (\Cref{thm:grp}).

By a result of Spee et al., the stabilizer of $\ket{\Psi}$ tells us about its reachability: it gives a characterization of the pure states that can be converted to $\ket{\Psi}$ via finitely many rounds of local operations assisted with classical communication \cite{Spee_2017}. Englbrecht and Kraus have a general algorithm for finding the stabilizer of a graph state\cite{PhysRevA.101.062302}, of which the 6-qubit AME state is an example \cite{helwig2013absolutelymaximallyentangledqudit}.

\begin{theorem}\label{prop:stab}
    Let $\ket{\Phi}\in \CC^K\otimes(\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ such that $\rho=\text{Tr}_{\{1\}}(\ket{\Phi}\bra{\Phi})$ is proportional to the identity. Fix the orthogonal basis \[\{\ket{\Phi_i}=\bra{i}_1\ket{\Phi}:0\leq i\leq K-1\}\] of the image $\mathcal{C}\subset (\CC^D)^{\otimes n}$ of $\rho$. This choice of basis gives coordinates to the image of the induced representation $\mu:N(\mathfrak{\mathcal{C}})\to\GL(\mathcal{C})$. For $g=(g_0,g_1,\dots,g_n)\in \SL_K\times\SL_D^{\times n}$, the following are equivalent: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[1.] $g.\ket{\Phi}=\ket{\Phi}$,
        \item[2.] $(g_1,\dots,g_{n})\in N(\mathcal{C})$ such that $\mu(g_1,\dots,g_{n})=(g_0^{-1})^\top$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    First notice that $g.\ket{\Phi}=\ket{\Phi}$ if and only if \[(I,g_1,\dots,g_{n}).\ket{\Phi}=(g_0^{-1},I,\dots,I).\ket{\Phi}.\] Since $\ket{\Phi}=\sum_{i=0}^{K-1} \ket{i}\ket{\Phi_i}$, this expands to
    \begin{align*}
         \sum_{i=0}^{K-1}\ket{i}\otimes (g_1,\dots,g_{n}).\ket{\Phi_i}&=\sum_{i=0}^{K-1}g_0^{-1}.\ket{i}\otimes\ket{\Phi_i} \\
         &= \sum_{i=0}^{K-1}\left(\sum_{j=0}^{K-1}(g_0^{-1})_{ji}\ket{j}\right)\otimes\ket{\Phi_i} \\
         %&=\sum_{j=0}^{K-1}\sum_{i=0}^{K-1} \ket{j}\otimes(g_0^{-1})_{ji}\ket{\Phi_i} \\
         &=\sum_{j=0}^{K-1}\ket{j}\otimes\left(\sum_{i=0}^{K-1} (g_0^{-1})_{ji}\ket{\Phi_i}\right) \\
         &=\sum_{i=0}^{K-1}\ket{i}\otimes\left(\sum_{j=0}^{K-1} (g_0^{-1})_{ji}^\top\ket{\Phi_j}\right),
    \end{align*}
    which is equivalent to
    $
(g_1,\dots,g_{n}).\ket{\Phi_i}=(g_0^{-1})^\top.\ket{\Phi_i}
    $
    for all $0\leq i\leq K-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:lem1}
    Suppose $U_1,U_2\in\SO_m$ such that $U_1BU_2^\top$ is diagonal whenever $B\in\CC^{m\times m}$ is diagonal. Then $U_1=PB_1$ and $U_2=PB_2$ for some permutation matrix $P$ and diagonal matrices $B_1,B_2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Let $B=\text{diag}(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_4)$ such that the squared eigenvalues $\lambda^2_i$ are distinct. If $U_1BU_2^\top$ is diagonal, so is $U_1B^2 U_1^\top$. Since conjugation preserves eigenvalues, we must have
    \[
    U_1 B^2 U_1^\top=\text{diag}(\lambda_{\sigma(1)}^2,\dots,\lambda_{\sigma(m)}^2)=P_\sigma B^2 P_\sigma^\top,
    \]
    where $P_\sigma$ is the permutation matrix corresponding to $\sigma\in\mathfrak{S}_m$. Then $P_\sigma^\top U_1$ commutes with $B^2$, hence preserves the eigenspaces of $B^2$. By choice, every eigenspace of $B^2$ is 1-dimensional. Then $P_\sigma^\top U_1$ is diagonal. We conclude that $U_1=P_\sigma B$ for some diagonal $B$. The same argument applies to $U_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:GHZ3}
    The group of $U\in\SO_4^{\times 3}$ such that $U.\ket{GHZ(3)}=\ket{GHZ(3)}$ consists of all triples $(PB_1, PB_2, PB_3)$ such that $P\in \SO_4$ is a positive permutation matrix, each $B_i\in \SO_4$, each $B_i$ is diagonal, and $B_1 B_2 B_3=I$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    In the following, let $\mathfrak{t}$ be the space of $4\times 4$ complex diagonal matrices and let
    \[
    N(\mathfrak{t})=\{g\in\mathfrak \GL_4:g\mathfrak{t}g^{-1}=\mathfrak{t}\}
    \]
    be the normalizer of $\mathfrak{t}\subset\mathfrak{gl}_4$ as a Cartan subalgebra.
    Suppose $U=(U_1,U_2,U_3)\in \SO_4^{\times 3}$ such that $U.\ket{GHZ(3)}=\ket{GHZ(3)}$. We identify the $\SO_4^{\times 2}$-modules $\CC^4\otimes\CC^4$ and $\CC^{4\times 4}$. By \Cref{prop:stab}, $U=(\mu(U_2,U_3),U_2,U_3)$, where $(U_2,U_3)\in N(\mathfrak{t})$ and $\mu:N(\mathfrak{t})\to\GL(\mathfrak{t})$ is the natural representation (since $U_1=\mu(U_2,U_3)$ is orthogonal, it is equal to its own inverse transpose). By \Cref{lem:lem1}, $U_2=PB_2$ and $U_3=PB_3$ for some permutation matrix $P$ and diagonal matrices $B_2,B_3$. By symmetry of $\ket{GHZ(3)}$, $U_1=PB_1$ for some diagonal $B_1$. Now it should be clear from the definition of $\ket{GHZ(3)}$ that $B_1 B_2 B_3=I$. Finally, we note that the permutation matrix $P$ and diagonal matrices $B_i$ are all in $\SO_4$. If $P$ is negative, then each $B_i$ is negative, contradicting $B_1 B_2 B_3=I$. If some $B_i$ is negative, then $P$ must also be negative since $PB_i\in \SO_4$. This gives the same contradiction.
\end{proof}


