            then 
            $g_1g_2 =\pm  \mathrm{I}$, and hence $g_2 = g_1^{-1}$. As the inverse $\widetilde{g_1}^{-1}$ of $\widetilde{g_1}$ is the unique lift of $g_1^{-1}$ to $\overline{\Hyp_0}$, we have $\widetilde{g_1}\widetilde{g_2} 
            =\widetilde{g_1}\widetilde{g_1}^{-1}= \mathrm I$ in $\univcover$, hence $n = 0$. This implies that $\ev\Big(\overline{\Hyp}\times \overline{\Hyp}\Big)$ and $\{z^{\pm 1}\}$ are disjoint.

            Suppose that $\ev(g_1, g_2) \in \Par_1^+\cup \Par_{-1}^-$ for some $g_1, g_2\in \overline{\Hyp}$. 
