        e^\lambda & 0 \\
        0 & e^{-\lambda}
        \end{bmatrix}$ for some $\lambda>0$; and by a re-parametrization if necessary, we can assume that 
        $$g_{1,t}=\pm \begin{bmatrix}
        e^{\lambda t} & 0 \\
        0 & e^{-\lambda t}
        \end{bmatrix}$$
        for all $t\in [0,1].$ Similarly, we can assume that 
        $$g_{2,t}=\pm \abcd\begin{bmatrix}
