        \widetilde{\phi(c_2)}\in \Par_0$, and hence $\chi(\phi) = (2,2,2)$. 
        By proposition \ref{prop_char} (1), $\phi$ is reducible, and hence up to a $\psl-$conjugation is a representation into the Borel subgroup of $\psl$. That is, up to conjugation, 
        $$\phi(c_1) = 
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
            1 & t_1\\
            0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix} \quad \text{and}\quad \phi(c_2) = 
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
            1 & t_2\\
            0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}$$ 
        for some $t_1, t_2\in \mathbb{R}\setminus \{0\}$, which  implies that $\phi$ is abelian.
    \end{proof}

