         Then by Lemma 
         \ref{lem_inthyptorus}, we obtain a path $\{\phi_t\}\interval$ in $\mathrm{Hom}(\pi_1(\Sigma_{1,2}), \psl)$
         starting from $\phi=\phi_0$ with  $\{\phi_t(c_i)\}\interval$ staying within     the conjugacy class of $\phi(c_i)$ for $i = 1,2$ and $\phi_1(d)$ being hyperbolic.  
         Since $\phi_t(c_i)$ is conjugate to 
         $\phi(c_i)$ for all $t\in [0,1]$ and $i = 1,2,$
         the sign of $\phi_t$ remains invariant as $t$ varies.  
         This also implies that $\path$ is in $\HP(\Sigma_{1,2})$, so that
         the relative Euler class of $\phi_t$ is well-defined for all $t,$ and hence is a constant along the path. 
         Thus, $\path$ is a path within $\HPns(\Sigma_{1,2})$ with $\phi_1$ sending $d$ to a hyperbolic element. 
         It ensures that the relative Euler classes  $e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(P)})$ 
         and $e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(T)})$ are well-defined.
        Then by Theorem \ref{thm_goldman}, 
         $-1\leqslant e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(T)}) \leqslant 1;$ and by Theorem \ref{thm_pants2}, 
         $e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(P)}) = \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2)$.  
         Finally, the additivity property of the relative Euler class yields 
         the desired inequality. 
         Likewise, if          $\HP^{s'}_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ is non-empty, we first deform a representation
         $\phi \in \HP^{s'}_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ within $\HP^{s'}_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ to $\phi_1$ as follows: 
         After
         applying Proposition \ref{prop_NAhyp} to $\phi$ if necessary, Lemma 
         \ref{lem_inthyppants} implies that there exists a path $\{\phi_t\}\interval$ in $\mathrm{Hom}(\pi_1(\Sigma_{0,4}), \psl)$ starting from $\phi=\phi_0$ with  $\{\phi_t(c_i)\}\interval$ staying within     the conjugacy class of $\phi(c_i)$ for $i = 1,2$ and $\phi_1(d)$ being hyperbolic.  
         The rest of the proof closely follows that of
         $\HPns(\Sigma_{1,2})$;
         since $\phi_t(c_i)$ is conjugate to 
         $\phi(c_i)$ for all $t\in [0,1]$ and $1\leqslant i\leqslant 4$,
         one can conclude that $\path$ lies in $\HP^{s'}_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$, and then the additivity of the relative Euler class with Theorem \ref{thm_goldman} and \ref{thm_pants2} shows that $\frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) - 1 \leqslant n = m+m' \leqslant \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + 1$. 

        %Connectedness
         It remains to show that any non-empty $\HPns(\Sigma_{1,2})$ and $\HP^{s'}_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ are connected. 
         Since the proofs for $\HPns(\Sigma_{1,2})$ and $\HP^{s'}_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ are identical, we present the proof of the connectedness of $\HPns(\Sigma_{1,2})$. 
         Let $\phi,\psi \in \HPns(\Sigma_{1,2})$.
         We may assume that both $\phi(d)$ and $\psi(d)$ are hyperbolic, as we can replace $\phi$ by $\phi_1$ defined in the previous paragraph if necessary, and similarly for $\psi$. 
         Then the relative Euler class is well-defined for each of the restrictions $\phi|_{\pi_1(P)}$ and $\phi|_{\pi_1(T)}$, as well as for $\psi|_{\pi_1(P)}$ and $\psi|_{\pi_1(T)}$. By the additivity, we obtain
         $$n = e(\phi) = e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P)})
         + e(\phi|_{\pi_1(T)})
         = 
         e(\psi|_{\pi_1(P)})
         + e(\psi|_{\pi_1(T)}) = e(\psi).$$ 
        As discussed in the previous paragraph, Theorem \ref{thm_pants2} implies that
         $e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P)}) = e(\psi|_{\pi_1(P)}) = \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2)$.
        Therefore, we also obtain $e(\phi|_{\pi_1(T)}) = e(\psi|_{\pi_1(T)})$, and then Corollary \ref{cor_sameEuler} shows that there exists a path in $\HPns(\Sigma_{1,2})$ connecting $\phi$ and $\psi$. 

        %The other signs
         Up to this point, we have proved the theorem for all possible signs $s = (s_1, s_2)$ and $s' = (s_1,s_2,0,0)$ \big($s_1, s_2\in \{\pm 1\}$\big) that satisfies \eqref{type2}. We complete the proof by addressing the remaining possible signs, namely: 
         $
         s' = (s_1,0,s_2,0), 
         (s_1,0,0,s_2), 
         (0,s_1,s_2,0), 
         (0,s_1,0,s_2)$, 
         and $(0,0,s_1,s_2)$. 
         For the case $s' = (0,0,s_1,s_2)$, 
         the argument remains unchanged after reindexing the subsurfaces $P_1\cup P_2$ to $P_2\cup P_1$. 
         For the other signs, we need different pants decomposition of $\pantstwo$ to use the same argument in the proof. 
         For $s' = (0,s_1,s_2,0)$, 
         define a pants decomposition 
         $\pantstwo = P'_1\cup P'_2$, where $\pi_1(P'_1)$ is generated by $c_2$ and $c_3$, and $\pi_1(P'_2)$ is generated by $c_4$ and $c_1$. 
         \big(Note that
         $\phi(c_2c_3c_4c_1) = \phi(c_1)^{-1}\phi(c_1c_2c_3c_4)\phi(c_1) = \pm\mathrm{I}$.\big) 
         Using a decomposition curve 
     $d' = c_4c_1 = (c_2c_3)^{-1}$ instead of $d$, the result follows by the same argument. For $s' =  (s_1,0,0,s_2)$, reindex these subsurfaces to $P'_2\cup P'_1$. 
     For $s' = (s_1,0,s_2,0)$,
         define a pants decomposition 
         $\pantstwo = P''_1\cup P''_2$, where $\pi_1(P''_1)$ is generated by $c_3$ and $c_1$, and $\pi_1(P''_2)$ is generated by $c_2$ and $c_3c_4c_3^{-1}$. 
         Note that $c_3c_4c_3^{-1}$ is conjugate to $c_4-$hence represents the same puncture, and
         $\phi\big(c_3c_1c_2(c_3c_4c_3^{-1})\big)
         = \phi(c_3)\phi(c_1c_2c_3c_4)\phi(c_3)^{-1} = \pm\mathrm{I}$.
         Using a decomposition curve 
     $d'' = c_2(c_3c_4c_3^{-1}) = (c_3c_1)^{-1}$ instead of $d$, the result follows by the same argument. For $s' =  (0,s_1,0,s_2)$, reindex these subsurfaces to $P''_2\cup P''_1$. Then we can use the same argument for each case for the proof.
    \end{proof}

    \subsection{\textcolor{red}{Representations with three parabolic boundary components}}\label{subsection_type3}
        Now the surface is required to have at least three punctures, so we only consider 
        $\HP^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$.
        Here we 
        prove Theorem 
        \ref{thm_main4} 
        for the case where 
        $\chi(\Sigma) = -2$ 
        and 
        $p_+(s) + p_-(s) = 3$.
    \begin{theorem}\label{thm_type3}
        Let $n\in \mathbb{Z}$, $s\in\{-1,0,+1\}^4$ with $
            p_+(s) + p_-(s) = 3. $
        $\HPns(\pantstwo)$ is non-empty if and only if 
        $$-2 + p_+(s)\leqslant n \leqslant 2 - p_-(s).$$ 
        Moreover, each non-empty $\HPns(\pantstwo)$ is connected.
    \end{theorem}
    \begin{proof}
         Here we present the proof for the case  
         $s = (s_1, s_2,+1,0)$, where $s_1, s_2 \in \{\pm 1\}$. The other cases for $s$ is obtained by permutation of components and reversing the signs of nonzero components of 
         $
         s = (s_1,s_2,+1,0)$. The signs obtained by reversing signs can be verified by applying Theorem \ref{prop_pglpsl},
         and the proof is analogous for each permutation of components,
         as explained in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm_type1_nonempty} and \ref{thm_type2}.

         Let $s = (s_1, s_2,+1,0)$.
         As in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm_type2}, we rewrite the inequality in an equivalent form:
         $$\frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2)\leqslant n \leqslant \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + 1.$$
         One can verify that both inequalities are 
         $
         \begin{cases}
            1 \leqslant n \leqslant 2, & s_1 = s_2 = +1\\
            0 \leqslant n \leqslant 1, & s_1 = +1, s_2 = -1
            \mbox{ or } s_1 = -1, s_2 = +1\\
            -1 \leqslant n \leqslant 0, & s_1 = s_2 = -1.\\
         \end{cases}
         $
         \\
         \\
         We will show that $\HPns(\pantstwo)$ is non-empty if and only if 
         %Inequality
         $\frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) \leqslant n \leqslant \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + 1$, and connected if non-empty.

         % Inequality => non-empty
         We begin by proving that, if $\frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) \leqslant n \leqslant \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + 1$, then  $\HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$ is non-empty.
         Let $m = \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2)$ and $m' = n - m$. We will construct a representation $\phi$ in $\HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$ by finding and gluing two representations $\phi_1 \in \HP_m^{(s_1,s_2,0)}(P_1)$ 
          and $\phi_2 \in \HP^{(+1,0,0)}_{m'}(P_2)$ along the common boundary $d$. 
          We first find $\phi_1-$note that the components of its sign,
         $S_1(\phi_1), S_2(\phi_1)$, and $S_3(\phi_1)$, are determined by the types  in $\psl$ of 
         $\phi_1(c_1), \phi_1(c_2)$, and $\phi_1(d)$, respectively.
         Theorem \ref{thm_pants2} ensures the
         existence of a representation $\phi_1$ in 
         $\HP_m^{(s_1,s_2,0)}(P_1)$. We now find $\phi_2$ which agrees with $\phi_1$ at $d$.
         Given that 
         $\phi_1(d)$ is hyperbolic, there exists a unique lift
         $\widetilde{\phi_1(d)}$ in $\Hyp_{m'}$. The bound of $n$ implies that $0\leqslant m' \leqslant 1$, and then by Theorem \ref{thm_pants2}, $\HP^{(+1,0,0)}_{m'}(P_2)$ is non-empty. Moreover, by Corollary \ref{cor_evimage}, 
         %the evaluation map $\ev: \HP^{(+1,0,0)}_{m'}(P_2)\to \Hyp_{m'}$ defined by the lifted commutator is surjective onto $\Hyp_{m'}$, i.e., 
         there exists a representation
         $\phi_2\in \HP^{(+1,0,0)}_{m'}(P_2)$ such that $\ev(\phi_2) = \widetilde{\phi_1(d)}$.
         Finally, since 
          $\phi_2(d) = \phi_2(c_3)\phi_2(c_4) = \phi_1(d)$, gluing $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ along the boundary $d$ defines a representation of $\pi_1(\Sigma_{0,4})$.
          One can verify that $S(\phi) = (s_1,s_2) = s$, since $\phi(c_1) = \phi_1(c_1) \in\Par^{sgn(s_1)}$ and $\phi(c_2) = \phi_1(c_2) \in\Par^{sgn(s_2)}$. Moreover, the additivity of relative Euler class(cf. Proposition \ref{prop_additivity}) implies that $e(\phi) = e(\phi_1) + e(\phi_2) = 
          m + m' = n$, therefore 
          $\phi \in \HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$. 

         % non-empty => Inequality
         Now suppose that $\HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$ is non-empty for  $n\in\mathbb Z$ and  $s = (s_1,s_2).$ 
         We will prove that $n$ is bounded by 
         $\frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) - 1 \leqslant n \leqslant \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + 1$ by using the additivity  of the relative Euler class. 
         %to the restrictions of the representation on $\pi_1(P)$ and $\pi_1(T)$. 
         %However, to make this possible, the relative Euler classes of these restrictions must be well-defined. Let $\phi \in \HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$.  
         Since the relative Euler classes 
        $e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_1)})$ and $e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_2)})$ are defined only when  $\phi$ sends all boundary components of $P_1$ and $P_2$ ($c_1, c_2, c_3, c_4$ and $d$)  to non-elliptic elements of $\psl,$ we first need to deform $\phi$ within $\HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$ to a representation that sends $d$ to a  hyperbolic element. 
         To achieve this, we assume without loss of generality that 
         $\phi(\pi_1(P_1))$ and $\phi(\pi_1(P_2))$ are non-abelian. (If $\phi$ does not satisfy this property, we replace $\phi$ by its deformation obtained from Proposition \ref{prop_NAhyp}.) 
         Then we can apply Lemma 
         \ref{lem_inthyppants} to find a path $\{\phi_t\}\interval$ in $\mathrm{Hom}(\pi_1(\Sigma_{1,2}), \psl)$,
         starting from $\phi=\phi_0$ with  $\{\phi_t(c_i)\}\interval$ staying within the conjugacy class of $\phi(c_i)$ for $1\leqslant i\leqslant 4$, and $\phi_1(d)$ being hyperbolic.  
         Since $\phi_t(c_i)$ is conjugate to 
         $\phi(c_i)$ for all $t\in [0,1]$ and $1\leqslant i\leqslant 4$,
         the sign of $\phi_t$ remains invariant as $t$ varies.  
         This also implies that $\path$ is in $\HP(\Sigma_{0,4})$ so that
         the relative Euler class of $\phi_t$ is well-defined for all $t,$ and hence is a constant along the path. 
         Thus, $\path$ is a path within $\HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$ with $\phi_1$ sending $d$ to a hyperbolic element. 
         It ensures that the relative Euler classes  $e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(P_1)})$ 
         and $e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(P_2)})$ are well-defined.
        Then by Theorem \ref{thm_pants2}, 
         $e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(P)}) = \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2);$ and by Theorem \ref{thm_pants2}, 
         $0\leqslant e(\phi_1|_{\pi_1(P_2)}) \leqslant 1$.  
         Finally, the additivity property of the relative Euler class yields 
         the desired inequality. 

        %Connectedness
         It remains to show that any non-empty $\HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$ is connected. 
         Let $\phi,\psi \in \HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$.
         We may assume that both $\phi(d)$ and $\psi(d)$ are hyperbolic, as we can replace $\phi$ by $\phi_1$ defined in the previous paragraph if necessary, and similarly for $\psi$. 
         Then the relative Euler class is well-defined for each of the restrictions $\phi|_{\pi_1(P_1)}$ and $\phi|_{\pi_1(P_2)}$, as well as for $\psi|_{\pi_1(P_1)}$ and $\psi|_{\pi_1(P_2)}$. By the additivity, we obtain
         $$n = e(\phi) = e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_1)})
         + e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_2)})
         = 
         e(\psi|_{\pi_1(P_1)})
         + e(\psi|_{\pi_1(P_2)}) = e(\psi).$$ 
        As discussed in the previous paragraph, Theorem \ref{thm_pants2} implies that
         $e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P)}) = e(\psi|_{\pi_1(P)}) = \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2)$.
        Therefore, we also obtain $e(\phi|_{\pi_1(T)}) = e(\psi|_{\pi_1(T)})$, and then Corollary \ref{cor_sameEuler} completes the proof by showing that there exists a path in $\HPns(\Sigma_{0,4})$ connecting $\phi$ and $\psi$.
    \end{proof}

    \subsection{\textcolor{red}{Representations with four parabolic boundary components}}\label{subsection_type4}
    It remains to address the case where 
        $\chi(\Sigma) = -2$ 
        and 
        $p_+(s) + p_-(s) = 4$, which corresponds to the characterization of the connected components of 
        $\mathcal{R}(\pantstwo)$. 
        There is a difference from the previous cases in the non-emptiness condition, which arises from the \emph{exceptional cases}:
    \begin{eqnarray*}
        &\mbox{Exc} 1. & n = 0 \ \mbox{ and }\  
        p_+(s) = 1,
        \mbox{ or } n = 0 \ \mbox{ and }\ p_-(s) = 1\\
        &\mbox{Exc} 2. & n = 1 \ \mbox{ and }\  
        p_-(s) = 0, 
        \mbox{ or } n = -1 \ \mbox{ and }\ p_+(s) = 0.
    \end{eqnarray*}
    For these two cases, the inequality $-2 + p_+(s)\leqslant n \leqslant 2 - p_-(s)$ does not hold. 
    However, the corresponding space $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ is non-empty in these cases, and form additional connected components in $\mathcal{R}(\Sigma)$. These additional components will be addressed in Section 7; for now, we focus on the non-exceptional cases. 
    The result below can also be found in \cite{yang}, which characterizes the connected components of the character variety that consists of $\psl$-orbits of irreducible type-preserving representations.
    \begin{theorem}\label{thm_type4}
        Let $n\in \mathbb{Z}$, $s\in\{\pm 1\}^4$, and assume that $(n,s)$ does not correspond to an exceptional case.
        $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\pantstwo)$ is non-empty if and only if 
        $$-2 + p_+(s)\leqslant n \leqslant 2 - p_-(s) .$$
        Moreover, each non-empty $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\pantstwo)$ is connected.
    \end{theorem}
    \begin{proof}
        By substituting $p_-(s)$ with $4 - p_+(s)$, this inequality simplifies to the equality $n = p_+(s) -2$. Moreover, the definition of 
        $p_+(s)$ and $p_-(s)$ implies that $p_+(s)-p_-(s) = \sum_{i=1}^4 s_i$ for $s = (s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4)$, and therefore we have 
        $$\frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + \frac{1}{2}(s_3+s_4)
        = \frac{1}{2}\big(p_+(s)-p_-(s)\big) = \frac{1}{2}\big(2p_+(s)-4\big) = p_+(s) - 2.$$
        Therefore, we show that $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\pantstwo)$ is non-empty if and only if 
         $n = \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + \frac{1}{2}(s_3+s_4)$.

        For such $n$, the proof of non-emptiness of $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ closely follows that of Theorem \ref{thm_type3}. For 
        $m = \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2)$ and 
        $m' = \frac{1}{2}(s_3+s_4)$, 
        $\HP_m^{(s_1,s_2,0)}(P_1)$ and $\HP^{(s_3,s_4,0)}_{m'}(P_2)$ are non-empty by Theorem \ref{thm_pants2}.
        Choose a representation $\phi_1$ in $\HP_m^{(s_1,s_2,0)}(P_1)$, and define $\widetilde{\phi_1(d)}$ as a unique lift in $\Hyp_{m'}$. 
        Then by Corollary \ref{cor_evimage}, %$\ev: \HP^{(s_3,s_4,0)}_{m'}(P_2)\to \Hyp_{m'}$, defined by the lifted commutator, is surjective onto $\Hyp_{m'}$. This implies that 
        there exists a $\phi_2\in \HP^{(s_3,s_4,0)}_{m'}(P_2)$ such that $\ev(\phi_2) = \widetilde{\phi_1(d)}$. Gluing $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ along $d$ gives a representation in $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$.

        Now suppose that $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ is non-empty for  $n\in\mathbb Z$ and  $s = (s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4)$, where $(n,s)$ is not exceptional. 
        If $\phi\in \mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ maps $d$ to a hyperbolic element,
        then Theorem \ref{thm_pants2} with the additivity of the relative Euler class implies that $n = 
        e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_1)})
        + e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_2)})= \frac{1}{2}(s_1+s_2) + \frac{1}{2}(s_3+s_4)$. Moreover, by Corollary \ref{cor_sameEuler}, 
        any two representations $\phi, \psi\in \mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ for which $\phi(d)$ and $\psi(d)$ are hyperbolic are connected by a path in $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$. 
        Therefore, it remains to show that every $\phi\in \mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$ can be deformed within $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\pantstwo)$ to $\phi'$, where $\phi'$ maps a non-peripheral curve to a hyperbolic element. 

        We first consider the case where $p_+(s) = 4$, i.e., $s = (+1,+1,+1,+1)$. By Theorem \ref{thm_nonabel}, we can deform $\phi$ to $\phi'$ within $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$, where $\phi'\big(\pi_1(P_1)\big)$ and $\phi'\big(\pi_1(P_2)\big)$ are non-abelian. We prove that $\phi'(d)$ is hyperbolic. 
       Observe that $\phi'(c_1)$ and $\phi'(c_2)$ are both in $\Par^+$, which implies that $\phi'(d) = \phi'(c_1)\phi'(c_2) \neq \pm\mathrm{I}$. Suppose that $\phi'(d)$ is parabolic.
        Recall that the sign of 
        $\phi'|_{\pi_1(P_1)}$ is determined by the types of $\phi'(c_1), \phi'(c_2)$, and $\phi'(d)$ in $\psl$, while the sign of 
        $\phi'|_{\pi_1(P_2)}$ is determined by the types of $\phi'(c_3), \phi'(c_4)$, and $\phi'(d)^{-1}$. Thus, either
        $\phi'|_{\pi_1(P_1)}$ or $\phi'|_{\pi_1(P_2)}$ has sign $(+1,+1,+1)$ and therefore has relative Euler class 1, while the other has sign $(+1,+1,-1)$ (up to permutation of components) with relative Euler class 0.(See Theorem \ref{thm_pants}.) 
        Without loss of generality, suppose that $\phi'|_{\pi_1(P_1)} \in \mathcal{R}^{(+1,+1,-1)}_0(P_1)$. By Lemma \ref{lem_R0pants}, $\phi'|_{\pi_1(P_1)}$ is abelian, contradicting the given assumption for $\phi'$. Therefore, $\phi'(d)$ must be either hyperbolic or elliptic. 
        The result follows by Theorem \ref{thm_Exc2} in Section 7, which shows that if $\phi'(d)$ is elliptic, then $\phi' \in \mathcal{R}^s_1(\Sigma)$; which corresponds to case Exc2. 
        The proof for $s = (-1,-1,-1,-1)$ is analogous.

        Now we consider the remaining cases, where both $p_+(s)$ and $p_-(s)$ are nonzero. We prove this for the case $s_1 = \pm 1$ and $s_2 = \mp 1$, which ensures that $\phi(c_1)\in \Par^\pm$ and $\phi(c_2)\in \Par^\mp$. 
        %In the case where $p_+(s) = 3$, one of $\pi_1(P_1)$ and $\pi_1(P_2)$ is generated by two peripheral elements that $\phi$ maps to parabolic elements of opposite signs; assume without loss of generality that this holds for $\pi_1(P_1)$. If $\phi\big(\pi_1(P_1)\big)$ and $\phi\big(\pi_1(P_2)\big)$ are abelian, then $e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_1)})= e(\phi|_{\pi_1(P_2)}) = 0$, and the additivity of relative Euler class implies that $e(\phi) = 0$; which corresponds to the case Exc1. Hence, at least one restriction of $\phi$ must be non-abelian. By applying Proposition \ref{prop_NAhyp}, we can deform $\phi$ to $\phi'$ within $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$, which is non-abelian on both restrictions. 
        By Theorem \ref{thm_nonabel}, we can deform $\phi$ to $\phi'$ within $\mathcal{R}^s_n(\Sigma_{0,4})$, where $\phi'\big(\pi_1(P_1)\big)$ and $\phi'\big(\pi_1(P_2)\big)$ are non-abelian.
        Up to $\psl-$conjugation, $\phi'\big(\pi_1(P_1)\big)$ is generated by 
        $\phi'(c_1) = \pm 
        \begin{bmatrix}
         1 & \pm 1 \\
         0 &  1
     \end{bmatrix}$, and 
        $\phi'(c_2) = \pm \abcd$ with $a+d = 2$. 
     Since $\phi'(c_1)$ and $\phi'(c_2)$ do not commute, we have $c \neq 0$, and in fact $c>0$ by
     Lemma \ref{lem_offdiag}. 
     The computation shows that
     $$\phi'(d) = \phi'(c_2^{-1}c_1^{-1}) 
     = \pm \inverse\begin{bmatrix}
         1 & \mp 1 \\
         0 &  1
     \end{bmatrix}  = 
     \pm \begin{bmatrix}
         d & -b \mp d \\
         -c & a \pm c
     \end{bmatrix}.$$ 
     By Lemma \ref{lem_offdiag}, we have $c>0$ when $\phi(c_2)\in \Par^-$, and $c<0$ when $\phi(c_2)\in \Par^+$.
     Hence, $\phi'(d)$ has a lift to $\SL$ with trace $a+d\pm c >2$, and therefore $\phi'(d)$ is hyperbolic. 
     The case where $\phi'(c_1)\in \Parm$ and $\phi'(c_2)\in \Parp$ follows similarly, by computing 
     $$\phi'(d) = \phi'(c_1c_2)^{-1} 
     = \pm \inverse\begin{bmatrix}
         1 & \mp 1 \\
         0 &  1
     \end{bmatrix} = 
     \pm \begin{bmatrix}
         d & -b+d \\
         -c & a - c 
    \end{bmatrix}.$$ with $c<0$. 
     If $\phi'(c_1)$ and $\phi'(c_2)$ have the same sign, 
     use a pants decomposition 
         $\pantstwo = P'_1\cup P'_2$, where $\pi_1(P'_1) = \langle c_2, c_3 \rangle$ and $\pi_1(P'_2) = \langle c_4, c_1 \rangle$. Since $p_\pm(s) > 0$, $\phi$ maps one of the pairs $(c_2,c_3)$ and $(c_4,c_1)$ to parabolic elements of opposite signs.
         We deform $\phi$ to $\phi'$ so that $\phi'\big(\pi_1(P'_1)\big)$ and $\phi'\big(\pi_1(P'_2)\big)$ are non-abelian, and we conclude by the computation above that $d' = c_4c_1 = (c_2c_3)^{-1}$ maps to a hyperbolic element.
    \end{proof}

