            hence lies in $\overline{\Hyp_0}\cup \overline{\Hyp_s}\cup \Ell_s$ as it is the connected component 
            of $\univcover\setminus \big(\Ell_{-s}\cup \Ell_{2s}\big)$ that contains $\widetilde{g_{1,0}g_{2,0}} = \mathrm I$. 
            Since $\widetilde{g_{1,1}g_{2,1}} = \widetilde{g_1}\widetilde{g_2}$ is elliptic by the assumption, we have $\widetilde{g_{1,1}g_{2,1}}\in \Ell_s$. %Similarly, if $m = -1$, since $-\pi<\theta<0$ ensures  $(\widetilde{A_tB_t})_{21}>0$, Lemma \ref{lem_offdiag_Ell} implies that $\widetilde{(A_1B_1)}$ lies in $\Ell_{-1}$.
            \medskip

            Next, to show 
            $\Ell_s\subset \ev\big(\Par^{sgn(s)} \times\Par^{sgn(s)} \big)\cap \big(\bigcup_{k\in \mathbb{Z}\setminus \{0\}}\Ell_k\big)$, 
    we consider $$g_1=\pm \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & s \\
        0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}\quad\text{and}\quad g_2
        = \pm \begin{bmatrix}
            1 & 0 \\
            -s\lambda & 1
        \end{bmatrix}$$ for some $\lambda >0$. 
        Then $\ev(g_1,g_2) = \widetilde{g_1}\widetilde{g_2}$ projects to the matrix 
         $$A_\theta = \begin{bmatrix}
        1-\lambda & s \\
        -s\lambda & 1
        \end{bmatrix}$$
           in $\SL$. %with $\tr(A_\theta)=2 - \lambda.$ 
             %defines a continuous one-parameter family $\bigg\{\begin{bmatrix}
            %    1 - \lambda - \lambda^2
