        for all $t\in [1,2]$; since $y_1, y_2\in (-2,2)$,
        $y_t = (2-t)y_1+(t-1)y_2 \in (-2,2)$, and hence $\psi_t(c_2)\in \Ell$ for all $t\in [1,2]$; and since $|z|<2,$ $\psi_t(c_3) = \big(\psi_t(c_1)\psi_t(c_2)\big)^{-1}\in \Ell$ for all $t\in [1,2]$. This implies that 
        the path $\{\psi_t\}_{t\in [1,2]}$ lies in $\PE^s(\pants)$.

    Next, we construct the path $\{\rho_t\}_{t\in [0,1]}$ connecting $\psi_2$ to $\phi_2$.
