{\bf For non-emptyness, write down explicitly  one representation for each component. To be added.}
        First, we establish that the pre-images of $\Par_0^+$ and $\Par_0^-$ are non-empty. 
        Consider two reducible representations of $\pi_1(\Sigma_{1,1})$
        $$
        \phi = 
        \bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & -1 \\
        0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}, 
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        2 & 0 \\
        0 & \frac{1}{2}
        \end{bmatrix}
        \bigg), 
        \phi'=
        \bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 0\\
        -1 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}, 
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{1}{2} & 0 \\
        0 & 2
        \end{bmatrix}
        \bigg)
        $$
        identified with 
        $(\phi(a), \phi(b))$ and 
        $(\phi'(a), \phi'(b))$ respectively, 
        where $a, b$ are the generators of $\pi_1(\Sigma_{1,1})\cong \mathbf{F}_2$.
        Note that $\phi$ and $\phi'$
        are conjugate to each other by 
        $\begin{bmatrix}
        0 & 1 \\
        1 & 0
        \end{bmatrix}\in \GL$. (cf. Proposition \ref{prop_pglpsl} in Appendix.) 
        Here we show that 
        $\ev(\phi)\in \Par_0^+$ and 
        $\ev(\phi')\in \Par_0^-$.
        The lifted commutator of 
        $(\phi(a), \phi(b))$ 
        projects down to 
        $\begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 3 \\
        0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}$ in $\SL$, 
        which has trace 2. Note that 
        an element in $\Par_n$ projects down into a one-parameter subgroup in $\SL$ (hence has trace 2) when $n$ is even, and descends to its $-I$-product (hence has trace $-2$) when $n$ is odd.
        Thus, the lifted commutator $\ev(\phi)$
        lies in $\Par_n$ where $n$ is even, hence in $\Par_0$.
        Moreover, Lemma \ref{lem_offdiag} implies that the lifted commutator 
        $
        \ev(\phi) = \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 3 \\
        0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}$ has positive sign, 
        hence the image $\ev(\phi)$ lies in $\Par^+_0$. Likewise, the image $\ev(\phi')$ descends to $ \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 0 \\
        3 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}$ in $\SL$,
        which is negative parabolic with trace $2$. Therefore, $\ev(\phi')$ is in 
        $\Par_0^-$. 

        We now demonstrate the non-emptiness of the pre-images of $\Par_1^-$ and $\Par_{-1}^+$ by
        considering the representations
            $$
        \psi = 
        \bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        2 & 0 \\
        0 & \frac{1}{2}
        \end{bmatrix}, 
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{5}{3} & \frac{4}{3} \\
        \frac{4}{3} & \frac{5}{3}
        \end{bmatrix}
        \bigg), 
        \psi' =
        \bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{1}{2} & 0 \\
        0 & 2
        \end{bmatrix}, 
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{5}{3} & \frac{4}{3} \\
        \frac{4}{3} & \frac{5}{3}
        \end{bmatrix}
        \bigg).
        $$
        Note that $\psi$ and $\psi'$ are $\GL$-conjugate to each other as in the case of $\Par_0^+$ and $\Par_0^-$.
        The lifted commutator
        $\ev(\psi) = 
        \begin{bmatrix}
        -\frac{13}{3} & \frac{20}{3} \\
        -\frac{5}{3} & \frac{7}{3}
        \end{bmatrix}$ has trace $-2$ 
        with negative sign, which implies that 
        $\ev(\phi)$ is in $\Par_n$ where $n$ is odd - hence either $\Par_1$ or $\Par_{-1}$. It follows that $\ev(\psi)$ lies in 
        $\Par^-_{1}$,
        since $\Par^-_{-1}$ is not in the image of $\ev$. 
        One can also observe that $\ev(\psi')$ lies in $\Par_{-1}^+$ from the trace and the sign of the lifted commutator
        $\begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{7}{3} & -\frac{5}{3} \\
         \frac{20}{3} & -\frac{13}{3}
        \end{bmatrix}$ .
        \\\\\\




            First we prove the connectedness. Let $\phi, \psi\in \Rns$. 
            If $n = 0$, the representations have character $\chi(\phi) = \chi(\psi) = (2,2,2)$, which implies that $\phi$ is abelian.\cite{goldman} $\phi$ is conjugate to an element in the Borel subgroup of $\psl$, so we can deform $\phi$ to $\phi' = g\phi g^{-1} = 
            \bigg(
            \pm
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & \xi \\ 0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \pm
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & \eta \\ 0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \bigg)$ by a conjugation path $g_t\phi g_t^{-1}$ ($g_t: \pm\mathrm{I}\to g$). Similarly, deform 
            $\psi$ to $\psi' = 
            \bigg(
            \pm
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & \xi' \\ 0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \pm
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & \eta' \\ 0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix}\bigg)$.
            $s(\phi) = s(\psi)$ implies 
            $sgn(\xi) = sgn(\xi')$, 
            $sgn(\eta) = sgn(\eta')$, and
            $sgn(\xi + \eta) = sgn(\xi' + \eta')$ hence the path \\
            \begin{align*}
                 \phi_t = 
                \bigg(
                \pm
                \begin{bmatrix}
                      1 & \xi+(\xi'-\xi)t \\ 0 & 1
                \end{bmatrix},
                \pm
                \begin{bmatrix}
                      1 & \eta+(\eta'-\eta)t \\ 0 & 1
                \end{bmatrix}
                \bigg)   
            \end{align*}
            connects $\phi'$ and $\psi'$ keeping the sign for each peripheral element.\\
            If $n = \pm 1$, both representations are in 
            $\chi^{-1}(2,2,-2)$, which is a $\GL$-orbit since $\kappa(2,2,-2)\neq 2$. (See Proposition \ref{prop_char}.) 
            Since the $\GL$-component not equal to $\SL$ permutes $e^{-1}(1)$ to $e^{-1}(-1)$ (See Proposition \ref{prop_pglpsl} in Appendix), $\phi$ and $\psi$ are $\psl$-conjugate under the assumption $e(\phi) = e(\psi)$. This completes the proof of connectedness. Moreover, there is only one possible sign $s$ for $n = 1$ since the component $\mathcal{R}_1(\Sigma)$ is a $\psl$-conjugacy class. (Same for $n = -1$.)

            Now we verify the number of components in $\R$. 
           Each component in $\mathcal{R}_{0}(\Sigma)$ has Borel representative 
            $\phi = 
            \bigg(
            \pm
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & \xi \\ 0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \pm
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & \eta \\ 0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \bigg)$, where $S(\phi) = (sgn(\xi), sgn(\eta), sgn(-\xi - \eta))$. 
            Such sign may have any triple of $\pm 1$ except $s = (+1,+1,+1)$ and $s = (-1,-1,-1)$. Hence $e^{-1}(0)$ has six components.
            \\
            If $\phi, \psi\in \mathcal{R}_{\pm 1}(\Sigma)$, we have $\chi(\phi) = \chi(\psi) = (2,2,-2)$. 
            Consider the lift 
            \begin{align*}
                  \tphicone = 
                \begin{bmatrix}
                    a & b \\
                    c & d
                \end{bmatrix},  
                \tphictwo = 
                \begin{bmatrix}
                    -d & -d+b \\
                    c & c-a
                \end{bmatrix}
            \end{align*}
            so that $\tphicone\tphictwo = \parpm$. 
            $2 = \tr\tphictwo = - d + (c - a) = c - \tr\tphicone = c - 2$, hence we have $c = 4 >0$. 
            By Lemma \ref{lem_offdiag}, we see that both $\tphicone$ and $\tphictwo$ are negative. Likewise, 
            $\tphicone\tphictwo = \parmm$ implies that $\tphicone$ and $\tphictwo$ both have positive sign. It means that $S^{-1}(+1,+1,+1)$ and $S^{-1}(-1,-1,-1)$ are the components of $\mathcal{R}_{1}(S)\cup \mathcal{R}_{-1}(S)$. 
            It can also be explained by considering $\mathcal{R}_{\pm 1}(S)$ as space of holonomy representations of three-hole sphere.

            It remains to determine which sign each $\mathcal{R}_{1}(S)$ and $\mathcal{R}_{-1}(S)$ has. Note that the evaluation map is defined by
            $\ev$$(\phi) = \ev$$(A,B) = \widetilde{A}\widetilde{B}\in \Par$$_n$, where $\widetilde{A}, \widetilde{B}$ are the unique lifts in $\Par_0$ and $n$ is the Euler class of $\phi$.
            $\ev(\mathcal{R}_{1}(S))$ lies in $\Par^{-}_1$ since $\Par^{+}_1$ is not in the image of $\ev$.
            Hence $AB$ must be negative for representation $\phi = (A,B)$ to have Euler class $1$, hence has sign  
            $S(\phi) = (+1,+1,+1)$ (Recall that the third component is the sign of $(AB)^{-1}$). Likewise, 
            the representation is in $\mathcal{R}_{-1}(S)$ if and only if it has
            sign $(-1,-1,-1)$ since
            $\ev(\mathcal{R}_{-1}(S))\subset \Par^+_{-1}$.
            \end{proof}











For (1). To show that $\mathcal{R}_n^{(s_1, s_2,0)}
            (\Sigma_{0,3})$ is non-empty for $n = \frac{s_1+s_2}{2}$, under the identification of the $\psl$-representations of $\pi_1(\Sigma_{0,3})$ and pairs of elements of $\psl,$ we have  
         $\Bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
