        -5 & 0
        \end{bmatrix}
        \Bigg)\in \mathcal{R}_1^{(+1,+1,0)}
            (\Sigma_{0,3})$. 
            It remains to show that $n = \frac{s_1+s_2}{2}$ whenever $\mathcal{R}_n^{(s_1, s_2,0)}(\Sigma_{0,3})$ is non-empty. 
            Let $\phi$ be a representation in $\mathcal{R}_n^{(s_1, s_2,0)}(\Sigma_{0,3})$.
            For the lifts of its image
            $\tphicone, \tphictwo\in \Par_0$, the product
            $\tphicone\tphictwo$ lies in $\Hyp_n$. 
            Recall that the product of $\widetilde{\phi(c_1)}$ and 
            $\widetilde{\phi(c_2)}$ in the universal covering is defined as follows: 
            Each of $\phi(c_1)$ and $\phi(c_2)$ lies in a one-parameter subgroup of $\psl$. Let $\{\pm A_t\}\interval$ 
            be a one-parameter subgroup with $\pm A_0 = \pm\mathrm{I}$ and $\pm A_1 = \phi(c_1)$. Similarly, 
            let $\{\pm B_t\}\interval$ 
            be a one-parameter subgroup with $\pm B_0 = \pm\mathrm{I}$ and $\pm B_1 = \phi(c_2)$. 
            The product of these two paths for each $t \in [0,1]$ defines a new path $\{\pm A_tB_t\}\interval$ 
            in $\psl$, 
            and there exists a unique lift of the path 
            $\{\widetilde{A_t}\widetilde{B_t}\}_{t\in [0,1]}$ in $\univcover$ 
            where $\widetilde{A_0}\widetilde{B_0}$ is identity. 
            $\tphicone\tphictwo$ is defined by its endpoint $\widetilde{A_1}\widetilde{B_1}$. 
            By tracking this lifted path, we will determine the location of $\tphicone\tphictwo$ and find the value of $n$ for given $s_1$ and $s_2$.

           \textcolor{red}{Without loss of generality, we can assume that $\pm A_t$ lies in the Borel subgroup, as it is conjugate to an element in the Borel subgroup. Let 
            $\pm A_t = \pm 
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & s_1t \\
                0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix}$ to simplify the computation. }Then $\pm B_t$ is of the form 
            $\pm \abcd
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & s_2t \\
                0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \inverse$ with some $\pm \abcd \in \psl$.
            By lifting the path 
            $\{\pm A_tB_t\}\interval$, we obtain the following path in $\univcover$\\
            \begin{eqnarray}
            \{\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t\}_{t\in [0,1]} = 
            \Bigg\{
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 - s_2tac - s_1s_2t^2c^2 
                & s_1t + s_1s_2 t^2 ac + s_2t a^2\\
                -s_2tc^2 
                & 1 + s_2tac
            \end{bmatrix}
            \Bigg\}_{t\in [0,1]} \nonumber
            \end{eqnarray} 
            with trace 
            $\tr(\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t) = 2 - s_1s_2t^2c^2$. 
            (Recall that the trace of an element in $\univcover$ is defined as the trace of its projection to $\SL$.)

            If either 
            $s_1 = -s_2 = +1$ or 
            $s_1 = -s_2 = -1$, we have $\tr(\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t) = 2 + t^2c^2$.
            $\phi$ is not abelian since $\phi(a)$ is parabolic while $\phi(a)\phi(b)$ is hyperbolic, which implies $c\neq 0$.
            Hence
            $t^2c^2$ is positive for all $t>0$, i.e., $\tr(\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t)  >2$ for all $t\in (0, 1]$.
            It means that the path passes only through hyperbolic elements after starting at the identity. 
            Since $\Hyp_n$ contains $\pm\mathrm{I}$ in its closure only if $n = 0$, 
            $\widetilde{A}_t \widetilde{B}_t$ stays inside $\Hyp_0$, i.e., $ \tphicone \tphictwo \in \Hyp_0$. We conclude that 
            $n = \frac{1 + (-1)}{2} = 0$.

            If either $s_1 = s_2 = +1$ or $s_1 = s_2 = -1$, we have the trace $\tr(\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t) = 2 - t^2c^2$.
            Here we see that $|c|>2$ 
            since the path ends up being hyperbolic ($|\tr(\widetilde{A}_1\widetilde{B}_1)| = |2 - c^2| > 2$),
            and conclude that the path has endpoint with $\tr(\widetilde{A}_1\widetilde{B}_1)<-2$, 
            i.e., $n$ is odd. 
            Moreover, the set 
            $\{t\in [0,1]: \tr(\widetilde{A_t}\widetilde{B_t}) \in (-2,2)\}$ is connected, which concludes that $\widetilde{A}_1\widetilde{B}_1$ is in  $\Hyp_{\pm 1}$.

            It remains to determine whether $n = +1$ or $n = -1$ for each sign $s_1 = s_2\in \{\pm 1\}$. First we see the case $s_1 = s_2 = +1$. 
            Note that the path in $\univcover$ connecting the identity to $\Hyp_1$ must meet $\Par_1^-$ unless passing $z$ in the center of $\univcover$, 
            while the path connecting the identity to an element in $\Hyp_{-1}$ must meet $\Par_{-1}^+$ unless passing $z^{-1}$. 
            We have $\tr(\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t) = -2$ at $t = t_P = 2/|c|$, 
            and the projection of $\widetilde{A}_{t_P}
            \widetilde{B}_{t_P}$ to $\SL$ has an $21$-entry 
            $\big(A_{t_P}
            B_{t_P}\big)_{21} = -s_2t_Pc^2 = -1\times\dfrac{2}{|c|}\times c^2 = -2|c|<0$. 
            Then $\widetilde{A}_{t_P}\widetilde{B}_{t_P}$ is negative parabolic by Lemma \ref{lem_offdiag}, hence the path does not meet $\Par_{-1}^+$. Therefore, the endpoint $\widetilde{A}_{1}\widetilde{B}_1$ lies in $\Hyp_1$, which implies $n = \frac{1 + 1}{2} = 1$. 
            The case $s_1 = s_2 = -1$ is similar; one can deduce that $\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t$ passes $\Par_{-1}^+$ from 
            $\big(A_{t_P}
            B_{t_P}\big)_{21} = -s_2t_Pc^2 = 2|c|>0$ when $t = 2/|c|$, and conclude that the path terminates in $\Hyp_{-1}$, i.e., 
            $n = \frac{(-1) + (-1)}{2} = -1$.
            \\

      For (2). To show that $\mathcal{R}_n^{(0,s,0)}
            (\Sigma_{0,3})$ is non-empty for $n = 0$ or $n = s$, under the identification of the $\psl$-representations of $\pi_1(\Sigma_{0,3})$ and pairs of elements of $\psl,$ we have  
         $\Bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        \frac{1}{2} & 0 \\
        0 & 2
        \end{bmatrix},
        \pm 
        \begin{bmatrix}
         5 & -4 \\
        4 & -3
        \end{bmatrix}
        \Bigg)\in \mathcal{R}_{-1}^{(0,-1,0)}
            (\Sigma_{0,3}),\\
            $ 
       $\Bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        2 & 0 \\
        0 & \frac{1}{2}
        \end{bmatrix},
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
         1 & 0\\
         1 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \Bigg)\in \mathcal{R}_0^{(0,-1,0)}
            (\Sigma_{0,3}),$ 
        $\Bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        2 & 0 \\
        0 & \frac{1}{2}
        \end{bmatrix},
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
         1 & 1 \\
        0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \Bigg) \in \mathcal{R}_0^{(0,+1,0)}
            (\Sigma_{0,3})$  and\\
         $\Bigg(
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
        2 & 0 \\
        0 & \frac{1}{2}
        \end{bmatrix},
        \pm \begin{bmatrix}
         -3 & 4 \\
        -4 & 5
        \end{bmatrix}
        \Bigg)\in \mathcal{R}_1^{(0,+1,0)}
            (\Sigma_{0,3})$. 
            Now suppose that $\mathcal{R}_n^{(0,s,0)}(\Sigma_{0,3})$ is non-empty for some 
            $n\in \mathbb{Z}\setminus \{0\}$ and $s\in \{\pm 1\}$. 
            In order to complete the proof, it suffices to show that 
            $n = s$.
            Let $\phi$ be a representation in $\mathcal{R}_n^{(0,s,0)}(\Sigma_{0,3})$.
            For the lifts of image $\tphicone \in \Hyp_0$ and
            $\tphictwo\in \Par_0$, the product
            $\tphicone\tphictwo$ lies in $\Hyp_n$. 
            As in the proof of (1), we find a unique lift of path $\{\pm A_tB_t\}_{t\in [0,1]}$ where
            $\pm A_t = 
            \pm \begin{bmatrix}
                e^{\lambda t} & 0 \\
                0 & e^{-\lambda t}
            \end{bmatrix} (\lambda >0)$ and  
            $\pm B_t = 
            \pm \abcd
            \begin{bmatrix}
                1 & st \\
                0 & 1
            \end{bmatrix}\inverse
            $. (We let 
            $\pm A_t = \pm \begin{bmatrix}
                e^{\lambda t} & 0 \\
                0 & e^{-\lambda t}
            \end{bmatrix}$ to simplify the computation as it is conjugate to one.)
            Then we obtain the path
            \begin{eqnarray}
                    \{\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t\}_{t\in [0,1]} = 
            \Bigg\{
            \begin{bmatrix}
                e^{\lambda t} (1 - ac st) &
                e^{\lambda t} a^2 st\\
                -e^{-\lambda t} c^2 st
                & e^{- \lambda t} (1 + ac st)
            \end{bmatrix}
            \Bigg\}_{t\in [0,1]}\nonumber
            \end{eqnarray}
            with trace 
            $\tr(\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t) = 
            (e^{\lambda t} + e^{-\lambda t})
            - acst (e^{\lambda t} - e^{-\lambda t}).$\\
            Since the path  $\{\widetilde{A}_t\widetilde{B}_t\}\interval$ terminates in $\Hyp_n$ where $n \neq 0$, there exists a $t_P\in (0,1)$ which satisfies $\tr\big(\widetilde{A}_{t_P}\widetilde{B}_{t_P}\big) = -2$. The projection of  $\widetilde{A}_{t_P}\widetilde{B}_{t_P}$ to $\SL$ has $12$-entry 
            $e^{\lambda t} a^2 st_P$ and 
            $21$-entry $-e^{-\lambda t} c^2 st_P$, where at least one of them is nonzero since the condition $a = c = 0$ is impossible for a matrix $\abcd$ in $\SL$. 
            Hence, Lemma \ref{lem_offdiag} implies that $\widetilde{A}_{t_P}\widetilde{B}_{t_P}$ is negative parabolic if  
            $s = 1$ (since we have either $e^{\lambda t} a^2 t_P >0 $  or
            $-e^{-\lambda t} c^2 t_P <0$), and positive parabolic if  
            $s = -1$ (since we have either $-e^{\lambda t} a^2 t_P <0 $ or
            $e^{-\lambda t} c^2 t_P >0$.) 
            It means that $\widetilde{A}_{t}\widetilde{B}_{t}$ must not meet 
            either $\Par^+_{-1}$ nor $\Par^+_1$ when $s = 1$, which means the endpoint must lie in $\Hyp_1$ and $n = s = 1$. Likewise, one can verify that $\widetilde{A}_{t}\widetilde{B}_{t}$ does not intersect 
            either $\Par^-_{-1}$ nor $\Par^-_1$ when $s = -1$, which implies that the path ends in $\Hyp_{-1}$ and $n = s = -1$. 
