International trade plays a crucial role in the economic growth of countries, yet it also serves as a pathway for spreading economic crises~\cite{Rivera-Batiz2004, Brakman2001, 2002ForbesAre, 2005AbeysingheTrade, 2010KaliFinancial}. The pattern of crisis spreading cannot be independent of the organization of international trade, making the international trade network a major area of study in network science~\cite{2021BardosciaThe, 2003SerranoTopology, 2004GarlaschelliFitness-Dependent, 2007HidalgoThe,  2010HeStructure, 2012FronczakStatistical, 2015AlmogA, 2015CiminiSystemic, 2016SaraccoDetecting, 2017BarbierUrn,   2021ChoiSkewness, 2022AlvesThe}. Understanding the interplay between structure and dynamics~\cite{2010GarasWorldwide, 2011LeeImpact, 2013FotiStability, 2014ContrerasPropagation, 2016LeeStrength, 2018AskariDynamics, 2019StarniniThe, 2020CoquideCrisis, 2022KimAnalysis, 2024KangThe} may be of practical importance,  helping governments and entrepreneurs to design and tune investment and resource allocation across numerous potential trade partners and industrial sectors. 

During the global economic recession in 2008--2009, the world trade suffered a decline by about $15$ percent, exceeding the decrease in the world gross domestic product (GDP)~\cite{Bems2013}. Even during normal periods, a substantial number of individual trades experience a decline in volume. Such a global decline and collective collapses may emerge from local declines and their interactions over intricate connections among individual trades. Many studies~\cite{2010GarasWorldwide, 2011LeeImpact, 2013FotiStability, 2014ContrerasPropagation, 2016LeeStrength, 2018AskariDynamics, 2019StarniniThe, 2020CoquideCrisis, 2022KimAnalysis, 2024KangThe} have explored the propagation of economic fluctuations, revealing e.g., that even non-major economies can become influential spreaders of crises~\cite{2010GarasWorldwide}, that not only the gross macroeconomic capacities of individual countries but also their connectivity profiles within the global economic network are crucial~\cite{2011LeeImpact}, 
and  how shocks propagate in the international trade-investment multiplex network~\cite{2019StarniniThe}. 

However, practical insights from these studies have often been limited, primarily due to their failure to sufficiently incorporate empirically identified features into models, thereby obscuring the core principles underlying collective fluctuations in the global economy including international trade. Furthermore, the structure and dynamics of international trade have largely been explored using trade {\it networks} constructed from either a country-centric or product-centric perspective, which offers only a coarse-grained view. Given available data-sets~\cite{2021UNcomtrade} that provide trade volumes for distinct product categories between exporting and importing countries, international trade can be represented at a finer scale by adopting a hypergraph approach, which is widely used across various complex systems to effectively capture the polyadic relationships among constituent elements~\cite{1989BergeHypergraphs, 2009GhoshalRandom, 2009ZlaticHypergraph, 2010ZhangA, 2020BattistonNetworks, 2021BattistonThe, 2022MajhiDynamics, 2022YiStructure}. In the international trade hypergraph (ITH)~\cite{2022YiStructure}, each hyperedge is a  three-vertex set representing an individual trade, connecting two country vertices---one as the exporter and the other as the importer---and a product category vertex, and its weight indicates the corresponding monetary volume of trade. This hypergraph framework enables a full characterization of local and global fluctuations, and uncovers the influence of the structural organization of international trade---at an unprecedented level of detail. 

