\subsection*{Building and characterizing ITH}
\label{Sec:ITH}
We construct the ITH [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Empirical}(a)], where vertices represent countries or product categories, defined by the two-digit code of the standard international trade classification (SITC) Rev. 3~\cite{SITCrev3}, and a hyperedge $h$ represents an individual trade comprising one exporting country, one importing country, and one product category with its weight $w$ given by the annual monetary volume of the corresponding trade. The ITH for year $t$ is built using the annual trade volumes $\{w_h(t-1)\}$ from the previous year and thus represents a snapshot as of January 1st of year $t$. It undergoes changes during year $t$, such as appearance or disappearance of hyperedges or vertices and fluctuations in hyperedge weights, which are captured in the updated trade volumes $\{w_h(t)\}$ reflecting changes from $\{w_h(t-1)\}$. These volumes $\{w_h(t)\}$ are recorded as of December 31st in year $t$ and used to construct the ITH for year $t+1$. We use trade volume provided by the UN Comtrade database from 2004 to 2018~\cite{2021UNcomtrade} for our analysis. See Materials and Methods. %for details. 

To characterize how these hyperedges, representing individual trades, are organized in the ITH, we consider the adjacency of hyperedges. We define two hyperedges as adjacent if they share one or more vertices and introduce the weighted adjacency matrix $A_{hh^\prime} = 0,1,2$, or $3$~\footnote{For certain pairs $(h,h')$, we find $A_{hh'}=3$, indicating that they share two countries swapping the roles of an exporter and an importer for the same product.} representing the number of vertices shared between $h$ and $h'$~\cite{2024KimHigherOrder}. We also define the degree $k_h\equiv \sum_{h^\prime} A_{hh^\prime}$ of hyperedge as the total number of neighboring hyperedges weighted by the number of shared vertices. Throughout the paper, `degree' refers to the degree of a hyperedge.

Over the observed period, the numbers of countries and that of products (product categories) remain nearly constant at about $170$ and $66$ respectively, as shown in Fig.~S1 in Supplementary Information (SI). The number of hyperedges ($N_\text{h}$) increases from $450,000$ to $540,000$ implying a world-wide growing activity in international trade. The detailed basic features of ITH, including the distributions of hyperedges' weights and degrees, are also shown in SI.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{Local properties of collapsed trades: clustering and negative correlation with trade volume}
\label{Sec:CollapsedHyperedges}
We define a hyperedge $h$ as collapsed if the annual logarithmic change of its weight (trade volume) is sufficiently negative, i.e., less than or equal to a threshold, set at $-1$, 
\begin{equation}
g(t) \equiv \log \frac{w(t)}{w(t-1)}\leq -1,
\label{eq:decline}
\end{equation}
where $w(t-1)$ and $w(t)$ are the weight in year $t-1$ and $t$, respectively~\footnote{We examine the changes during year $t$ in trades that had nonzero volumes in the previous year and are thus represented in the ITH for year $t$.}.
Note that $g(t)$ is broadly distributed (Fig.~S1) and some trades even disappear in year $t$, exhibiting $ g(t)\rightarrow -\infty$.
Let us denote the fraction of collapsed hyperedges by $C$. We find $C \approx 0.3$ under the threshold in Eq.~\ref{eq:decline} (Fig.~S1) with an ephemeral increase in 2009 when the global crisis occurred. The fraction $C$ can vary depending on the threshold in Eq.~\ref{eq:decline}, but the main results that we will present remain qualitatively consistent.

First remarkable observation is that collapsed trades are not uniformly distributed in the ITH but rather clustered. We measured the average proportions of collapsed neighbors around collapsed ($\tilde{c}_\text{collapsed}$) and normal ($\tilde{c}_\text{normal}$) hyperedges. Their  difference can be used as a measure of the degree of clustering of the collapsed hyperedges; it is zero if the collapsed hyperedges were randomly distributed, and greater than zero if they tend to cluster together. Figure~\ref{Fig:Empirical}(b) shows that $\tilde{c}_\text{collapsed}>\tilde{c}_\text{normal}$ throughout the observed period indicating that the collapsed hyperedges tend to be more clustered than random. 

Next, collapse occurs not uniformly but inhomogeneously across hyperedges, highly dependent on their weights. In Fig.~\ref{Fig:Empirical}(c), we show that the fraction of collapsed hyperedges $c(w/\langle w\rangle)$ among hyperedges of given normalized weight $w/\langle w\rangle$, where $\langle \cdot \rangle = N_\text{h}^{-1} \sum_{h} (\cdot)$ denotes the average over all hyperedges, decreases algebraically with $w/\langle w\rangle$ as
\begin{equation}
c\left(\frac{w}{\langle w\rangle}\right) \sim \left(\frac{w}{\langle w\rangle}\right)^{-\zeta}
\label{eq:zeta}
\end{equation}
for large $w/\langle w\rangle$. The exponent $\zeta$ stays between 0.24 to 0.29 except for the year 2009 coinciding the global economic crisis, in which $\zeta \approx 0.17$ [Inset of Fig.~\ref{Fig:Empirical}(c)]. 
The algebraic decay remains robust, though $\zeta$ changes, when different thresholds are used (Fig.~S2). Additionally, we remark that the proportion $\tilde{c}_\text{collapsed}(\frac{w}{\langle w\rangle})$ of collapsed neighbors around collapsed hyperedges of given normalized weight $\frac{w}{\langle w\rangle}$ appears to be almost independent of $\frac{w}{\langle w\rangle}$, as shown in Fig.~S1.

This phenomenon may be related to the narrower distribution of annual logarithmic change $g$ for trades with larger volumes as reported for company growth~\cite{stanley_scaling_1996}. To understand its origin, one might assume that the annual volume $w$ of an individual trade in a given year is the sum of $N = \lceil w/w_{0} \rceil$ {\it independent} microscopic transactions, each of volume $w_{0}$ in that year and subject to a fluctuation from $w_{0}$ to $(w_{0}+\Delta w_{0})$ in the next year, where $\Delta w_0$ is a random variable following a Gaussian distribution with mean $\langle \Delta w_{0} \rangle$ and  standard deviation $\sigma_{\Delta w_{0}}$. This assumption then suggests that the annual change of the volume of the considered trade from $w$ to $(w+\Delta w)$ also follows a Gaussian distribution with mean $N\langle \Delta w_{0} \rangle$ and  standard deviation $N^{1/2} \sigma_{\Delta w_{0}}$~\cite{2011FischerA}. See SI for details. Using this Gaussian assumption, one may calculate the proportion of collapsed hyperedges among those of normalized weight, shown as the dashed line in Fig.~\ref{Fig:Empirical}(c). Notably, the empirical proportion $c(w/\langle w\rangle)$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:zeta} decays with $w/\langle w\rangle$  much more slowly than predicted under the Gaussian assumption; Large-weight hyperedges are more prone to collapse than would be predicted by the random fluctuations of their many constituent microscopic transactions, suggesting correlated fluctuations.

These empirical findings indicate that trade collapses do not occur individually or independently but in interconnected and correlated ways. The emergence of such  collective, correlated collapses and global decline may stem from dynamic interactions among individual trades.  In the following, we explore how a large number of collapsed trades can emerge in the ITH, and how the organization of the ITH influences this process. To achieve this, we introduce a minimal model of collapse propagation, which reveals how the identified empirical features contribute to the stability of the ITH.

