\subsection*{Collapse propagation model}
\label{Sec:Model}
To investigate how collapse propagates within the ITH, we hypothesize that the volume of an individual trade may be significantly reduced due to the influence of adjacent trades that already experience such a decline, through disruptions at their shared vertices---whether an exporting country, an importing country, a product, or any combination of them. As a result of this `infection' process, collapse may spread. Once collapse of a trade is completed, the trade is no longer infective. Given such similarity to the spread of an infectious disease~\cite{2015Pastor-SatorrasEpidemic}, we employ a susceptible-infective-recovered-like epidemic model, as depicted in Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(a). In the model, every hyperedge is in one of three states: normal or susceptible (S), infective (I), and collapsed (C). An infective hyperedge represents a trade currently undergoing collapse (declining in volume) and can infect adjacent susceptible hyperedges, i.e., transmit collapse to neighboring normal trades---those which have not yet experienced collapse. Such `infection' of collapse can be driven by product shortages or deteriorating relations between countries. Each infective hyperedge transitions to the C state at a uniform rate $\gamma$, after which it can no longer transmit collapse. The process continues until no infective hyperedges remain. The outbreak size,  defined as the fraction of the collapsed hyperedges in the final state, where no hyperedges are infective, can then be compared with the empirical fraction of collapsed trades $C$ for each year. This hyperedge-centered model may provide a more refined picture than the conventional vertex-centered models, as information from multiple individual trades is aggregated into the state of a country or product category  in the latter.

The crucial ingredient of our model is that the infection rate $\beta_{h'h}$ of an infective hyperedge $h$ to transmit collapse to a susceptible neighbor hyperedge $h^\prime$ is not uniform but depends on the weight $w_{h'}$ of the target  as [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(a)]
\begin{equation}
\beta_{h^\prime h} = \beta A_{h^\prime h} \frac{w^{\alpha}_{h^\prime}}{\langle w^{\alpha} \rangle},
\label{eq:beta}
\end{equation}
with $\beta$ a constant characteristic infection rate. The exponent $\alpha$ introduces a weight-dependent bias in hyperedge infection; with $\alpha<0$, larger-weight hyperedges  have a lower probability of collapse. We set $\beta_{h'h}$ to be independent of the source's weight $w_h$ motivated by the empirical analysis result (Fig.~S1). Also, based on the assumption that infection transmission occurs via shared vertices, we set the infection rate to grow with the number of shared vertices represented by $A_{h'h}$. 

The Monte Carlo simulation of this model on the empirical ITH, starting with a randomly chosen single infective hyperedge each year, shows that the outbreak size $C$ becomes non-zero when the rescaled infection strength $\lambda$ defined by 
\begin{equation}
    \lambda\equiv \frac{\beta}{\gamma}  \frac{\langle k^2\rangle}{\langle k\rangle},
    \label{eq:lambdadef}
\end{equation}
with $\langle k^n\rangle$ the $n$th moment of the degree distribution, 
exceeds a threshold $\lambda_\text{c}$. 

In Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(b), we plot the proportions of collapsed hyperedges $C$ in the final state as a function of $\lambda$ when $\alpha = \alpha_{*}$ and when $\alpha = 0$ to see the influence of the inhomogeneous infection rate compared to the homogeneous case.
Here, $\alpha_{*}$ is defined as $\alpha$ at which the $\zeta$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:zeta}) of empirical data and the $\zeta$ of the Monte Carlo simulation match closest [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(c)].
To obtain simulation results comparable to the empirical data, we estimate $\lambda$ value for each $\alpha$ and year such that the proportion of collapsed hyperedges matches between simulations and empirical data. The estimated values of $\lambda$ under  $\alpha = \alpha_{*}$ and $\alpha=0$ are denoted by $\lambda_*$ and $\lambda_0$, respectively [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(d)].

The organization of collapsed hyperedges under $\alpha=\alpha_{*}$ and $\lambda=\lambda_{*}$ appears significantly different from that under  $\alpha=0$ and $\lambda=\lambda_{0}$ as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(e-g). The mean proportions of collapsed neighbors around collapsed ($\tilde{c}_{\text{collapsed}}$) and normal ($\tilde{c}_{\text{normal}}$) hyperedges, whose difference indicates the tendency of collapsed hyperedges to cluster [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(e,f)], as well as the decay of the proportion of collapsed hyperedges 
with weight [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(g)] are more accurately reproduced and better match the empirical data when using $\alpha=\alpha_{*}$ than when using $\alpha=0$.

Beyond reproducing these key empirical observations, our model also provides analytic solutions by extending a mean-field approach for inhomogeneous infection models~\cite{2011BrittonA, 2017MaImportance, 2020NeipelPower-law} to our hypergraph epidemic framework. These solutions help identify two key factors, one structural and one dynamical, that can either suppress or promote the global spread of collapse within the ITH, offering quantitative insights into the mechanisms that stabilize international trade.

