\subsection*{Structural correlation and dynamical inhomogeneity: Key factors influencing collective collapse}
\label{Sec:Correlation}

Given potential variations of the infection strength $\lambda$ over time, a larger (smaller) outbreak-threshold $\lambda_\text{c}$ indicates that a global spread of collapse is less (more) probable, serving as a measure of the robustness of the ITH. Here we employ a mean-field approach to the collapse propagation model to obtain analytically the threshold $\lambda_c$ and identify the factors dictating $\lambda_\text{c}$.

The probability $i_h$ of a hyperedge $h$ to be in the I state changes with time due to infection from its neighbors ($h'$) and transition to the C state as $d i_h/dt =  \sum_{h'} \beta_{hh'}i_{h'} s_h - \gamma i_h$ with $s_h$ the probability to be in the S state.  Approximating the probability $i_{h'}$ of a neighbor $h'$ to be in the I state by an average  or a mean-field $\tilde{i} \equiv \sum_{h'} k_{h'} i_{h'}/\sum_{h'} k_{h'}$, we can represent $i_h$ and $s_h$ in terms of $\tilde{i}$ (see SI). In the early-time regime when there are only a small number of infective hyperedges  and thus $s_h\simeq 1$,  the mean-field $\tilde{i}$ evolves with time as 
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d \tilde{i}}{dt} \simeq \gamma \tilde{i}\left( 
    \frac{\sum_h k_h\frac{\beta k_h}{\gamma} \frac{w_h^\alpha }{\langle w^\alpha \rangle}}{\sum_h k_h} -1 \right)
 \label{eq:reproduction}
\end{equation}
yielding $\tilde{i}\simeq e^{\gamma (R_0-1)t}$ with the reproduction number $R_0\equiv \frac{\lambda}{\lambda_\text{c}}$ and  the threshold  $\lambda_\text{c}$ given by
\begin{equation}
\lambda_\text{c} \equiv \frac{ \langle k^2\rangle \langle w^\alpha\rangle}{\langle
k^2w^\alpha\rangle}.
\label{eq:lambdac}
\end{equation}
Thus an initial local collapse can spread if $R_0>1$ or equivalently $\lambda>\lambda_\text{c}$. As elaborated in SI, one can also obtain the outbreak size $C$ in the final state self-consistently, which is non-zero when $\lambda>\lambda_\text{c}$ with $\lambda_c$ of Eq.~\ref{eq:lambdac} in agreement with the simulation results as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(b). 

Both the Monte Carlo simulation and mean-field-theoretical results, shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig:Model}(b), consistently indicate that as $\alpha$ takes on larger negative values, it not only suppresses the outbreak size~\cite{2011BrittonA, 2017MaImportance, 2020NeipelPower-law}, but also increases the threshold $\lambda_\text{c}$ for the empirical ITH. This means that a global outbreak is suppressed over a broad range of $\lambda$ and even when it occurs, its size is smaller for given $\lambda>\lambda_c$, implying that an inhomogeneous infection rate, characterized by a negative $\alpha$, helps mitigate the emergence of collective collapse. From this perspective, we may consider the smaller value of $\zeta$ in 2009 compared to other years [inset of Fig.~\ref{Fig:Empirical}(c)] as a hallmark of the global recession in the year. A natural question then arises: why does negative $\alpha$ enhance the stability of the ITH? 

Rearranging Eq.~\ref{eq:lambdac}, we obtain 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\lambda_\text{c}}-1 = \frac{\langle (k^2 - \langle k^2\rangle)(w^\alpha - \langle w^\alpha\rangle ) \rangle}{\langle k^2 \rangle \langle w^\alpha \rangle},
\label{eq:lambdac2}
\end{equation}
which includes a normalized covariance of $k^2$ and $w^\alpha$, revealing the effect of structural correlation. It is obvious that $\lambda_\text{c}=1$ with $\alpha=0$ or no degree-weight correlation. 
When $\alpha<0$, as implicated empirically, a positive (negative) correlation between $k$ and $w$ will lead to the normalized covariance of $k^2$ and $w^\alpha$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:lambdac2} being negative (positive), making $\lambda_\text{c}$ larger (smaller) than $1$. To support these analytic predictions, we construct randomized ITHs without degree-weight correlations by randomly shuffling the weights of hyperedges in the empirical ITHs.  We find that, for the randomized ITHs,  $\lambda_\text{c}$ is fixed at $1$, independent of $\alpha$  [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Correlation}(a)]. In the empirical ITHs, a positive degree-weight correlation exists [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Correlation}(b)], similar to that observed in trade networks~\cite{2008BhattacharyaThe, 2009FagioloWorld-trade}, resulting in higher values of $\lambda_{\text{c}}$ and smaller outbreak sizes for given $\lambda$, compared to the randomized ITHs [Fig.~\ref{Fig:Correlation}(a)].

These results suggest that the combination of a negative correlation between collapse probability and trade volume ($\alpha<0$) and a positive degree-weight correlation synergistically serves to mitigate the spread of collapse from local to global scales. This can be intuitively understood as follows:  with $\alpha<0$ and a positive degree-weight correlation, a local collapse---most likely to occur in hyperedges of small weight---tends to spread to only a smaller number of neighbors, thereby reducing the reproduction number $R_0=\lambda/\lambda_\text{c}$ compared to  ITHs without a degree-weight correlation or a negative $\alpha$. Therefore our study reveals that heterogeneous infection dynamics and structural correlation can jointly, while cannot do so alone,  strengthen the stability of the ITH. 

