\section*{Conclusion}
\label{Sec:Conclusion}

Here we have studied fluctuations in international trade, focusing on the significant decline in trade volume, which we refer to as `collapse', at the level of individual trades. Taking a hypergraph approach, we found that such collapsed trades (hyperedges) are not uniformly distributed but tend to be clustered in the ITH. Furthermore, the proportion of collapsed trades decays algebraically with their volume (weight). To investigate the implications of these findings for the emergence of global-scale collapse, we introduced a collapse propagation model with an inhomogeneous infection rate, motivated by the key empirical features that we identified. Through the mean-field theory and Monte Carlo simulations, we discovered that the combined effect of the structural correlation between a hyperedge's degree and weight, and the dynamical inhomogeneity in infection rate, can either facilitate or suppress the global spread of collapse. Most importantly, the real-world ITH exhibits a positive degree-weight correlation and a collapse probability decreasing with hyperedge weight, which synergistically suppress the global outbreak of collapse. 

Our results illuminate how structure and dynamics can be reshaped to improve stability.  For example, in the ITH, further  increasing  $\alpha$ negatively or enhancing the positive degree-weight correlation may help strengthen stability, although  managing such global properties requires further investigation. The origins  of the dynamical inhomogeneity and structural correlations within the ITH have yet to be fully understood. Future research could explore correlations between microscopic transactions,
%that contribute to an annual trade, 
possibly accounting for why the proportion of collapsed trade decays more slowly than expected if these transactions were independent. Datasets that capture microscopic trades on shorter time scales could also provide a more detailed information on the collapse dynamics in the ITH~\cite{2022DiemQuantifying, 2024ChakrabortyInequality}. Additionally, analyzing extended datasets over longer time periods may reveal how the structure of the ITH evolves, shedding light on the evolutionary origins of the structural correlation.

We have identified a distinct scaling of $c(w/\langle w\rangle)$ during the global crisis in 2009 compared to other `normal' periods and demonstrated within our theoretical framework that this leads to lower outbreak thresholds, undermining the stability of the ITH. A unifying framework for understanding the nature of trade fluctuations---from microscopic to macroscopic scales,  in both normal and crisis periods---can help prepare for and prevent future crises. Finally, studying the characteristics of positive fluctuations, which may signal the growth of trade, along with the negative fluctuations focused on in this paper, will complement understanding the dynamics of the international trade system.


