\section{Background material} \label{sec:background}

In this section, we introduce several definitions that will be used extensively throughout the manuscript. These are organized into two categories: (i) definitions and results from graph theory, and (ii) definitions and results from the literature on the integrality gap.


%%%%%%%%%% Preliminaries from graph theory
\subsection{Preliminaries from graph theory}

Before giving the list of definitions we use in this work, let us clarify that, to avoid confusion, given a graph $G = (V, E)$, we use the term \emph{node} to refer to the elements of $V$; the term \emph{vertex} is reserved exclusively for the extreme points of $\psep{n}$.
%
Furthermore, we distinguish between the terms \emph{cost} and \emph{weight}: the former is used for the objective of \tsp\ or \sep; the latter denotes the components $x_{ij}$ of a feasible solution, as they can be considered weights of the support graph.

\begin{definition}[Graph isomorphism]
    Let $G = (V_G, E_G)$ and $H = (V_H, E_H)$ be two undirected graphs with weight functions $\chi_G : E_G \rightarrow \R$ and $\chi_H : E_H \rightarrow \R$ respectively. We say that $G$ and $H$ are \emph{isomorphic} if there exists a bijection $\phi : V_G \rightarrow V_H$ such that for all $i, j \in V$, \ $ij \in E_G$ if and only if $\phi(i) \phi(j) \in E_H$, and $\chi_G(ij) = \chi_H(\phi(i)\phi(j))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Vertex isomorphism]
    Two vertices $\bm{x}, \bm{x}' \in \psep{n}$ are isomorphic if their support graphs are isomorphic. 
\end{definition}

We now make a clear distinction between the two following definitions, as it will be crucial throughout this work.

\begin{definition}[Hamiltonian walk] \label{def:walk}
    Let $G$ be an undirected graph. A \emph{Hamiltonian walk} is a closed walk in $G$ that visits every node at least once, possibly traversing the same edge multiple times.
    For a given walk $\bm{w}$, let $w_{ij} \in \N$ denote the \emph{multiplicity} of $ij$ in $\bm{w}$, that is, the number of times the walk $\bm{w}$ uses the edge $ij$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hamiltonian tour]
    Let $G$ be an undirected graph. A \emph{Hamiltonian tour} is a closed walk in $G$ that visits every vertex exactly once (hence no edge is traversed multiple times).
    For a given tour $\bm{t}$, let $t_{ij} \in \{ 0, 1 \}$ denote whether $\bm{t}$ uses or not the edge $ij$.
\end{definition}

With abuse of notation, we use the same literal to denote both the walk $\bm{w}$ \ (respectively the tour $\bm{t}$) \ and its \emph{characteristic vector}, that is, the vector of $w_{ij}$ \ (respectively $t_{ij}$) \ entries.

Throughout this work, we simplify this terminology and use the terms \emph{walk} and \emph{tour} to denote only Hamiltonian walks and Hamiltonian tours, respectively.
If the underlying graph $G$ is not specified, we assume it is the complete one.

\begin{remark} \label{rem:walk_mult}
    Since we have interest only in walks which are the shortest possible, we assume that every edge $ij$ of a walk $\bm{w}$ is traversed at most twice (i.e. $w_{ij} \in \{ 0, 1, 2 \}$):
    Indeed, it is well known (see, e.g. \cite{art:CorFonNad:TSPGraph}) that, when an edge is used strictly more than twice by a walk, it is possible to get rid of two copies of that edge to obtain a shorter walk.
    It is important to notice that, imposing the condition $w_{ij} \in \{ 0, 1, 2 \}$ on all edges $ij$, the number of walks on a graph becomes finite.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Metric completion] \label{def:metric_completion}
    Let $G=(V, E)$ be a graph with $|V|=n$, and let $\bm{c} \in \R^E$ be a cost that satisfies the triangle inequalities on $E$.
    The \emph{metric completion} of $\bm{c}$ is the cost $\bm{c}^*$ on the complete graph $K_n$ defined as
    $$
    c^*_{ij} =
    \begin{cases}
        c_{ij} & \text{if } ij \in E, \\
        p(i,j) & \text{if } ij \notin E,
    \end{cases}
    $$
    where $p(i, j)$ is the cost of the shortest path between $i$ and $j$ in $G$ with respect to $\bm{c}$.
\end{definition}


%%%%%%%%%% Preliminaries from the literature on the integrality gap
\subsection{Preliminaries from the literature on the integrality gap}

In the investigation of the integrality gap, another granularity may be considered:
the \emph{integrality gap of a given dimension} $n$, defined as
$$
\alpha_n := \sup_{\bm{c} \text{ metric on $n$ nodes}} \frac{\tsp(\bm{c})}{\sep(\bm{c})} \ .
$$
The study of the integrality gap $\alpha$ can be divided into the study of $\alpha_n$ and recovered as \ $\alpha = \sup_{n \in \N} \alpha_n$.

Benoit and Boyd, in~\cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP}, partition the integrality gap even further, introducing the following concept.

\begin{definition}[\gap\ of a vertex] \label{def:vgap}
    Let $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$ be a vertex. The \emph{\gap\ of $\bm{x}$} is
    $$
    \gap(\bm{x}) := \sup \left\{
    \frac{\tsp(\bm{c})}{\sep(\bm{c})} \ \mid \ \bm{c} \text{ metric }, \ \bm{x} \in \arg\min \sep(\bm{c})
    \right\} \ .
    $$
\end{definition}

The authors show that
$$
\alpha_n = \max_{\bm{x} \text{ vertex of } \psep{n}} \gap(\bm{x})
$$
and they an LP named $\opt(\bm{x})$ to compute the inverse of $\gap(\bm{x})$.
%
The interested reader may find all the details in~\cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP}.

We build our work upon these concepts, adapting them to suit our purposes.
Other definitions and results from \cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP} are extensively used in this paper and are presented below.

\begin{definition}[$1$-edge, $1$-path, from~\cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP}]
    Let $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$ and let $e$ be an edge of $K_n$; $e$ is called \emph{$1$-edge} of $\bm{x}$ if \ $x_e = 1$.
    When $\bm{x}$ is fractional (that is, not a tour), we call \emph{$1$-path} of $\bm{x}$ a maximal path of $1$-edges in the support graph $G_{\bm{x}}$; the nodes of degree $2$ in the $1$-path are called \emph{internal nodes}, the two remaining nodes are called \emph{end nodes}.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Theorem 3.4 of \cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP}] \label{thm:3_1paths}
    Let $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$ be a fractional vertex.
    Then $\bm{x}$ has at least three distinct $1$-paths.
\end{theorem}








We now introduce a construction presented in~\cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP} and give it a name using the authors' initials.

\begin{definition}[\bb-move] \label{def:BBmove}
    Let $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$ and let $ab$ be one of its $1$-edges.
    We call \emph{\bb-move} the construction of a new point $\bm{x}' \in \psep{n+1}$ defined as follows, where $w$ is the new node added:
    $$
    x'_e = \left\{  
    \begin{array}{ll}  
        0    & \text{if } \ e = ab \\  
        1    & \text{if } \ e \in \{ aw, wb \} \\  
        0    & \text{if } \ e \in \delta(w) \setminus \{ aw, wb \} \\
        x_e  & \text{otherwise}
    \end{array} \right.  
    $$
    We denote this construction by \ $\bb(\bm{x}, ab) := \bm{x}'$. \
    When it is clear from the context or not strictly necessary, we omit the edge $ab$ in the notation: \ $\bb(\bm{x})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Theorem 3.2 in \cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP}] \label{thm:bb_move}
    Let $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$ and let $ab$ be one of its $1$-edges.
Then $\bb(\bm{x}, ab)$ is a vertex of $\psep{n+1}$ if and only if $\bm{x}$ is a vertex of $\psep{n}$.
\end{theorem}

In other words, inserting a node in a $1$-path is an operation that maps vertices to vertices. 
The inverse operation, namely removing one internal node from a $1$-path, is also a vertex-preserving operation.
%
Interestingly, the authors do not mention anything about how this operation impacts the value of $\gap(\bm{x})$.
In \Cref{sec:lifting_opt}, we will discuss this point and provide some insights within a relaxed framework.

Finally, the following result from \cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP} will also be useful for our work: it bounds the number of edges in the support graph of a vertex in relation to the number of nodes $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Theorem 3.1 of \cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP}] \label{thm:max_edges}
    Let $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$ be a vertex.
    Then \ $|E_{\bm{x}}| \leq 2n - 3$. 
\end{theorem}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% Families of vertices with a given number of edges
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Families of vertices with a given number of edges} \label{sec:families}

In this section, we will study the vertices of $\psep{n}$ considering, in their support graph, the relation between the number of edges and the number of nodes.
%
We start by stating the following simple Lemma.

\begin{lemma} \label{lemma:min_edges}
    Let $\bm{x}$ be a fractional vertex of $\psep{n}$.
    Then \ $|E_{\bm{x}}| \geq n + 3$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Consider three distinct $1$-paths of $\bm{x}$ (they exist by \Cref{thm:3_1paths}) and let $\tilde{V}$ be the set of their end nodes, so that \ $|\tilde{V}| = 6$ \ and \ $\deg(v) \geq 3 \ \ \forall v \in \tilde{V}$. \
    Then
    $$
    |E_{\bm{x}}| = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{v \in V_n} \deg(v)
    = \frac{1}{2} \bigg( \sum_{v \in \tilde{V}} \deg(v)
    + \sum_{v \notin \tilde{V}} \deg(v) \bigg)
    \geq \frac{1}{2} \big( \ 6 \!\cdot\! 3 + (n-6) \!\cdot\! 2 \ \big)
    = n + 3.
    $$
\end{proof}

Our aim now is to describe, for a given $k$, all possible fractional vertices with $n$ nodes and $n + k$ edges.
%
For this purpose, we define the families of vertices $\cF_k$:
$$
\cF_k := \{ \bm{x} \text{ fractional vertex} \ \mid \
\bm{x} \in \psep{n} \text{ for some } n, \
|E_{\bm{x}}| = n + k \} \ .
$$
%
Notice that \Cref{lemma:min_edges} implies $k \geq 3$, that is $\cF_1 = \cF_2 = \emptyset$.
%
We also observe that a \bb-move add $1$ node and $1$ edge,
therefore, in virtue of \Cref{thm:bb_move}, the application of a \bb-move to a vertex \ $\bm{x}\in \psep{n}$ \ with \ $|E_{\bm{x}}| = n + k$ \ yields a vertex \ $\bm{x'}\in \psep{n+1}$ \ with \ $|E_{\bm{x'}}| = (n+1) + k$.
In other words, a vertex is in $\cF_k$ if and only if its image under a \bb-move is in $\cF_k$ as well. 
%
This brings us to define the \emph{ancestors}, the vertices of $\cF_k$ with the shortest possible $1$-paths (i.e., $1$-paths composed of a single $1$-edge).

\begin{definition}[Ancestor of order $k$]
    An \emph{ancestor of order $k$} is a vertex $\bm{x}$ of $\cF_k$ with no node of degree $2$ (i.e., no internal node in the $1$-paths).
    We denote the set of ancestors of order $k$ as
    $$
    \cA_k := \{ \bm{x} \in \cF_k \mid \bm{x} \text{ has no node of degree } 2 \} \ .
    $$
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Successor]
    Given a vertex $\bm{x}$, we call \emph{successor} of $\bm{x}$ any vertex $\bm{x}'$ obtained by sequential applications of the \bb-move.
\end{definition}

We can thus recover the whole family $\cF_k$ by taking the elements of $\cA_k$ and replacing any $1$-edge with a $1$-path of arbitrary length; $\cF_k$ may be seen as the set of successors of $\cA_k$.

At this point, to give a complete description of $\cF_k$, it only remains to retrieve all the elements of $\cA_k$.
The following lemma helps us in this direction.

\begin{lemma}\label{th:kbound}
    Let $\bm{x}$ be an ancestor in $\cA_k$ \ and let $n$ be its number of nodes.
    Then \ $k + 3 \leq n \leq 2k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Theorem~\ref{thm:max_edges} gives the inequality \ $n + k = |E_{\bm{x}}| \leq 2n - 3$ \ which leads to $k + 3 \leq n$.
    The upper bound on $n$ is derived from the fact that the minimum degree of the nodes of $\bm{x}$ is $3$, thus \
    $n + k = |E_{\bm{x}}| = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{v \in V_n} \deg(v) \geq \frac{1}{2} \cdot 3 \, n$ \
    which simplifies to $n \leq 2k$.
\end{proof}

Therefore, all the elements of $\cA_k$ can be recovered from the lists of vertices of $\psep{n}$, with $n$ up to $2k$ (provided we have them at our disposal).
We simply need to scroll through the lists of vertices of $\psep{n}$ for all the $n = k \!+\! 3, \dots, 2k$, and extract the ones with no node of degree $2$ and with exactly $n + k$ edges.
Gathering all of them, we get $\cA_k$.

Since, at the time being, an exhaustive list of fractional vertices (up to isomorphism) is already available for $n$ up to $12$ (\cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP}, \cite{art:BoyEll:ExtrSEP}), it is also possible to completely determine $\cA_3, \cA_4, \cA_5, \cA_6$ (up to isomorphism) by \Cref{th:kbound}. 
%
For instance, in \Cref{fig:vertices_A4} we can see all the ancestors in $\cA_4$: all the vertices of $\cF_4$ are either of these shapes or with the $1$-edges replaced with $1$-paths of arbitrary length. As $k = 4$, these ancestors can be extracted from the lists of vertices of $\psep{7}$ and $\psep{8}$.

