% Computational results
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Computational results} \label{sec:comp_results}

Our pipeline starts by retrieving the lists of ancestors $\cA_k$ \ for $k = 3, 4, 5, 6$. 
%
As finding new ways for generating them directly is out of the scope of this work, we extract them from the lists of vertices already available from \cite{art:BenBoy:IGSmallTSP, art:BoyEll:ExtrSEP}%
\footnote{\url{https://www.site.uottawa.ca/~sylvia/subtourvertices/index.htm}, last visited 04.06.2025.},
as explained in \Cref{sec:families}.

For each vertex $\bm{x} \in \cA_k$, the \gap-Bounding algorithm may be applied.
We report that, for some ancestors, a straightforward application of the \gb\ algorithm returned a value higher than $\nicefrac{4}{3}$.
%
To improve the algorithm and make it return tighter bounds, we exploit \Cref{lemma:bound_ancestors} and design an enhanced version, called for simplicity \gbe\ (enhanced).
%
The \gbe\ algorithm, on input a vertex $\bm{x}$, iteratively applies \gb\ on the successors of $\bm{x}$ until a desired bound is found, as described in \Cref{alg:GBe_algo}.

\begin{algorithm}
    \caption{\gap-Bounding algorithm, enhanced} \label{alg:GBe_algo}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
        \STATE \textsc{input}: \
            $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$ vertex, \
            $\alpha$ desired bound, \
            $I$ maximum number of iterations \\[5pt]
        \STATE $i \ \gets \ 0$
        \STATE $\beta \ \gets \ \gb(\bm{x})$
        \WHILE{$\beta > \alpha$ \ and \ $i \leq I$}
            \STATE $i \ \gets \ i + 1$
            \STATE $\bm{x} \ \gets \ \bb(\bm{x}, e)$
                \qquad \qquad (where $e$ is a $1$-edge of $\bm{x}$)
            \STATE $\beta \ \gets \ \min \{ \beta, \ \gb(\bm{x}) \}$
        \ENDWHILE
        \STATE Return: $\beta$
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:GBe_algo}
    For every $\alpha$ desired bound and every $I$ maximum number of iterations., the \gap-Bounding algorithm enhanced, on input a vertex $\bm{x} \in \psep{n}$,
    returns a value $\gbe(\bm{x}, \alpha, I)$ which is an upper bound for the gap of all the successors $\bm{x}'$ of $\bm{x}$: \
    $\gap(\bm{x}') \leq \gbe(\bm{x}, \alpha, I)$.
    Moreover, the bound returned by \gbe\ is tighter or equal to the one returned by \gb: \
    $\gbe(\bm{x}, \alpha, I) \leq \gb(\bm{x})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    The theorem simply follows from the observation that the \gb\ algorithm is called at least once along the execution of \Cref{alg:GBe_algo} (line 3), and applying \Cref{thm:GB_algo} and \Cref{lemma:bound_ancestors}.
    The enhancement $\gbe(\bm{x}, \alpha, I) \leq \gb(\bm{x})$ is achieved simply because, in the while-loop (lines 4-8), the returned value $\beta$ can only decrease (line 7).
\end{proof}

Notice that there is no theoretical guarantee that the \gb\ algorithm applied to successors gives better results; indeed, it may happen that it does not.
The idea of iterating the applications of \bb-moves simply arose from the fact that we thought it reasonable that more "information" (more nodes, more edges, more dual variables) would lead to a more detailed analysis and a tighter bound.
%
Eventually, the application of the \gbe\ algorithm led us to accomplish our objective.

\begin{theorem}
    $\gap(\bm{x}) \leq \frac{4}{3}$ \ for all the vertices of $\bm{x} \in \cF_k$ \ with $k = 3, 4, 5, 6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    The proof is the computational verification that the \gbe\ algorithm applied on the ancestors of $\cA_k$ (considered up to isomorphism), with desired bound $\nicefrac{4}{3}$ and a finite number of maximum iterations (in our implementation, $10$ was enough), always returns a value smaller than or equal to $\nicefrac{4}{3}$.
\end{proof}

The \gb\ algorithm has been implemented in Python, employing the use of the commercial software Gurobi \cite{gurobi} to solve the LPs.
%
Our code is available at \url{https://github.com/tulliovilla/IG_for_TSP_with_few_edges}.
%
The computation is relatively lightweight and can be easily performed on a standard laptop.
%
More details are available in \Cref{sec:impl_details}.


